  {"topic":"people-behaviour","entries":[{"id":"peo-behaviour-000001","topic":"people-behaviour","intent":"definition","concept":"why do we procrastinate","keywords":["why do we procrastinate","delay","lazy","start","task","avoid","deadline","emotion","palampok","pig-ugma","procrastination","laziness","distinction","matters","confused","treated"],"patterns":["what is why do we procrastinate","why do we procrastinate","explain why do we procrastinate","definition of why do we procrastinate","why do we procrastinate meaning","how do i stop procrastinating","am i lazy or procrastinating","ano ang palampok","unsa ang pig-ugma"],"answer":"Procrastination is not laziness, and the distinction matters because the two are usually confused and treated with the same useless advice. A lazy person does not want to do the thing. Someone procrastinating wants to do it, would rather not be doing it right now, and cannot start, which is a different problem with a different cause. The most defensible account is that the task is emotionally unpleasant rather than difficult, and starting requires doing the unpleasant part immediately, so a person keeps trading a definite small discomfort now for an uncertain one later. This is why the usual advice about deadlines is not the answer: a deadline increases the unpleasantness and therefore the avoidance. What tends to work is making the first step small enough that the resistance does not engage, and treating a finished bad version as something that can be improved, which changes the question from whether to start into how much further to go.","variations":["A lazy person does not want to do the thing. Procrastination is not laziness, and the distinction matters because the two are usually confused and treated with the same useless advice. Someone procrastinating wants to do it, would rather not be doing it right now, and cannot start, which is a different problem with a different cause. The most defensible account is that the task is emotionally unpleasant rather than difficult, and starting requires doing the unpleasant part immediately, so a person keeps trading a definite small discomfort now for an uncertain one later. This is why the usual advice about deadlines is not the answer: a deadline increases the unpleasantness and therefore the avoidance. What tends to work is making the first step small enough that the resistance does not engage, and treating a finished bad version as something that can be improved, which changes the question from whether to start into how much further to go.","Someone procrastinating wants to do it, would rather not be doing it right now, and cannot start, which is a different problem with a different cause. Procrastination is not laziness, and the distinction matters because the two are usually confused and treated with the same useless advice. A lazy person does not want to do the thing. The most defensible account is that the task is emotionally unpleasant rather than difficult, and starting requires doing the unpleasant part immediately, so a person keeps trading a definite small discomfort now for an uncertain one later. This is why the usual advice about deadlines is not the answer: a deadline increases the unpleasantness and therefore the avoidance. What tends to work is making the first step small enough that the resistance does not engage, and treating a finished bad version as something that can be improved, which changes the question from whether to start into how much further to go."],"tl":"palampok","bis":"pig-ugma"},{"id":"peo-behaviour-000002","topic":"people-behaviour","intent":"definition","concept":"lying","keywords":["lying","honest","truth","deceive","omit","misdirect","belief","social","magsaysay","people","mostly","omission","misdirection","rather","saying"],"patterns":["what is lying","lying","explain lying","definition of lying","lying meaning","why do people lie","is lying always bad","how to tell if someone is lying","ano ang magsaysay","unsa ang magsaysay"],"answer":"People lie mostly by omission and by misdirection rather than by saying the opposite of the truth, which is why a person who has not fabricated anything may still be dishonest. The reason lying is common is that it reliably works in the short run: the benefit is immediate and the cost is usually deferred or never collected, and a social context where being pleasant matters more than being accurate pushes people towards the softer option. It is also not always deliberate, because a person who believes something is more often wrong than a person who is lying about it, and self-report is unreliable on this point in both directions. The useful distinction for a listener is between a claim that is mistaken, which can be corrected with information, and one that is constructed, which cannot, and the sign is whether the story changes when new detail arrives.","variations":["The reason lying is common is that it reliably works in the short run: the benefit is immediate and the cost is usually deferred or never collected, and a social context where being pleasant matters more than being accurate pushes people towards the softer option. People lie mostly by omission and by misdirection rather than by saying the opposite of the truth, which is why a person who has not fabricated anything may still be dishonest. It is also not always deliberate, because a person who believes something is more often wrong than a person who is lying about it, and self-report is unreliable on this point in both directions. The useful distinction for a listener is between a claim that is mistaken, which can be corrected with information, and one that is constructed, which cannot, and the sign is whether the story changes when new detail arrives.","It is also not always deliberate, because a person who believes something is more often wrong than a person who is lying about it, and self-report is unreliable on this point in both directions. People lie mostly by omission and by misdirection rather than by saying the opposite of the truth, which is why a person who has not fabricated anything may still be dishonest. The reason lying is common is that it reliably works in the short run: the benefit is immediate and the cost is usually deferred or never collected, and a social context where being pleasant matters more than being accurate pushes people towards the softer option. The useful distinction for a listener is between a claim that is mistaken, which can be corrected with information, and one that is constructed, which cannot, and the sign is whether the story changes when new detail arrives."],"tl":"magsaysay","bis":"magsaysay"}],"lang":"english","group":"people","sub":"behaviour","title":"Human behaviour","groupTitle":"People","__file":"people/behaviour.json"},
  {"topic":"philippines-government","entries":[{"id":"phi-government-000001","topic":"philippines-government","intent":"definition","concept":"edsa revolution","keywords":["edsa revolution","aquino","1986","election","protest","marcos","nation","peaceful","edsa","first","people","power","revolution"],"patterns":["what is edsa revolution","edsa revolution","explain edsa revolution","definition of edsa revolution","edsa revolution meaning","what is edsa","what happened in edsa 1986","who was corazon aquino","ano ang edsa","unsa ang edsa"],"answer":"EDSA in 1986 was the first People Power revolution, and what ended it was not a battle so much as a decision by a large number of people to stop supporting a government. The immediate cause was the alleged fraud in a snap election and the assassination of Benigno Aquino, with the election itself held afterwards under conditions most people did not accept. The distinguishing feature of 1986 was that the military did not end it: Corazon Aquino was sworn in at a military base and then asked the crowd to go home, and the transition held partly because the opposition did not claim the victory by force. The people power name came from the crowds, and it distinguishes it from EDSA II in 2001, which removed a president through a court ruling rather than a mass action. The wider significance is that it established the habit of peaceful transfer in a country where that had not been the norm, and that habit is what the 1986 Constitution was written to protect.","variations":["The immediate cause was the alleged fraud in a snap election and the assassination of Benigno Aquino, with the election itself held afterwards under conditions most people did not accept. EDSA in 1986 was the first People Power revolution, and what ended it was not a battle so much as a decision by a large number of people to stop supporting a government. The distinguishing feature of 1986 was that the military did not end it: Corazon Aquino was sworn in at a military base and then asked the crowd to go home, and the transition held partly because the opposition did not claim the victory by force. The people power name came from the crowds, and it distinguishes it from EDSA II in 2001, which removed a president through a court ruling rather than a mass action. The wider significance is that it established the habit of peaceful transfer in a country where that had not been the norm, and that habit is what the 1986 Constitution was written to protect.","The distinguishing feature of 1986 was that the military did not end it: Corazon Aquino was sworn in at a military base and then asked the crowd to go home, and the transition held partly because the opposition did not claim the victory by force. EDSA in 1986 was the first People Power revolution, and what ended it was not a battle so much as a decision by a large number of people to stop supporting a government. The immediate cause was the alleged fraud in a snap election and the assassination of Benigno Aquino, with the election itself held afterwards under conditions most people did not accept. The people power name came from the crowds, and it distinguishes it from EDSA II in 2001, which removed a president through a court ruling rather than a mass action. The wider significance is that it established the habit of peaceful transfer in a country where that had not been the norm, and that habit is what the 1986 Constitution was written to protect."],"tl":"edsa","bis":"edsa"},{"id":"phi-government-000002","topic":"philippines-government","intent":"definition","concept":"martial law","keywords":["martial law","marcos","1972","1981","suspension","military","constitution","president","martial","civil","government","favour"],"patterns":["what is martial law","martial law","explain martial law","definition of martial law","martial law meaning","when was martial law declared in the philippines","is martial law still possible","ano ang martial law","unsa ang martial law"],"answer":"Martial law is the suspension of civil government in favour of military authority, and the Philippines has declared it twice, in 1972 and in 1981. Ferdinand Marcos placed the whole country under it in September 1972, justifying it by an alleged communist rebellion that did not justify it, and ruled from 1972 until 1986 under a constitution that gave him sweeping powers and an assembly that was largely controlled. The 1981 declaration was a political move rather than an emergency measure, an attempt to keep the powers he had been using while stepping down under the pressure of the constitution's requirement, and it lasted a little over three years. The lasting effect is legal rather than political: the 1986 Constitution specifically lists martial law among the powers a president may never again use, so that a repeat is now constitutionally impossible rather than merely unlikely.","variations":["Ferdinand Marcos placed the whole country under it in September 1972, justifying it by an alleged communist rebellion that did not justify it, and ruled from 1972 until 1986 under a constitution that gave him sweeping powers and an assembly that was largely controlled. Martial law is the suspension of civil government in favour of military authority, and the Philippines has declared it twice, in 1972 and in 1981. The 1981 declaration was a political move rather than an emergency measure, an attempt to keep the powers he had been using while stepping down under the pressure of the constitution's requirement, and it lasted a little over three years. The lasting effect is legal rather than political: the 1986 Constitution specifically lists martial law among the powers a president may never again use, so that a repeat is now constitutionally impossible rather than merely unlikely.","The 1981 declaration was a political move rather than an emergency measure, an attempt to keep the powers he had been using while stepping down under the pressure of the constitution's requirement, and it lasted a little over three years. Martial law is the suspension of civil government in favour of military authority, and the Philippines has declared it twice, in 1972 and in 1981. Ferdinand Marcos placed the whole country under it in September 1972, justifying it by an alleged communist rebellion that did not justify it, and ruled from 1972 until 1986 under a constitution that gave him sweeping powers and an assembly that was largely controlled. The lasting effect is legal rather than political: the 1986 Constitution specifically lists martial law among the powers a president may never again use, so that a repeat is now constitutionally impossible rather than merely unlikely."],"tl":"martial law","bis":"martial law"},{"id":"phi-government-000003","topic":"philippines-government","intent":"definition","concept":"spain and the philippines","keywords":["spain and the philippines","legazpi","spain","mexico","barangay","catholic","trade","1565","1898","espanya","colonised","philippines","three","centuries"],"patterns":["what is spain and the philippines","spain and the philippines","explain spain and the philippines","definition of spain and the philippines","spain and the philippines meaning","why did spain colonize the philippines","when did spaniard rule the philippines","how long was spanish colonial rule","ano ang espanya","unsa ang espanya"],"answer":"Spain colonised the Philippines for over three centuries, from 1565 when Legazpi arrived to 1898 when the Spanish-American war ended Spanish rule, and what was taken was not only territory. The motive was trade, specifically the route to China, and the settlement was administered as part of that route rather than as a colony valued for itself. Three centuries produced a great deal: the institution of the barangay as the local unit, a legal system based on Spanish civil law, the Roman Catholic Church as the only institution that functioned across the islands, and a written language built on a Latin alphabet, which is why English does not occupy the same place here as it does in most former colonies. The cost was concentrated: the trade was administered from Mexico and the resources went outward, and taxation fell hardest on the people who had the least. It is worth being precise that the population did not simply fall from Spanish rule, which is a common claim that does not match the demographic evidence, and that resistance was continuous rather than beginning in 1896.","variations":["The motive was trade, specifically the route to China, and the settlement was administered as part of that route rather than as a colony valued for itself. Spain colonised the Philippines for over three centuries, from 1565 when Legazpi arrived to 1898 when the Spanish-American war ended Spanish rule, and what was taken was not only territory. Three centuries produced a great deal: the institution of the barangay as the local unit, a legal system based on Spanish civil law, the Roman Catholic Church as the only institution that functioned across the islands, and a written language built on a Latin alphabet, which is why English does not occupy the same place here as it does in most former colonies. The cost was concentrated: the trade was administered from Mexico and the resources went outward, and taxation fell hardest on the people who had the least. It is worth being precise that the population did not simply fall from Spanish rule, which is a common claim that does not match the demographic evidence, and that resistance was continuous rather than beginning in 1896.","Three centuries produced a great deal: the institution of the barangay as the local unit, a legal system based on Spanish civil law, the Roman Catholic Church as the only institution that functioned across the islands, and a written language built on a Latin alphabet, which is why English does not occupy the same place here as it does in most former colonies. Spain colonised the Philippines for over three centuries, from 1565 when Legazpi arrived to 1898 when the Spanish-American war ended Spanish rule, and what was taken was not only territory. The motive was trade, specifically the route to China, and the settlement was administered as part of that route rather than as a colony valued for itself. The cost was concentrated: the trade was administered from Mexico and the resources went outward, and taxation fell hardest on the people who had the least. It is worth being precise that the population did not simply fall from Spanish rule, which is a common claim that does not match the demographic evidence, and that resistance was continuous rather than beginning in 1896."],"tl":"espanya","bis":"espanya"}],"lang":"english","group":"philippines","sub":"government","title":"Government","groupTitle":"The Philippines","__file":"philippines/government.json"},
  {"topic":"philippines-transport","entries":[{"id":"phi-transport-000001","topic":"philippines-transport","intent":"definition","concept":"manila traffic","keywords":["manila traffic","edsa","mrt","lrt","cars","road","commute","congestion","trapiko","traffic","metro","manila","heavy","number","vehicles"],"patterns":["what is manila traffic","manila traffic","explain manila traffic","definition of manila traffic","manila traffic meaning","why is traffic heavy in manila","how do i avoid traffic in manila","is manila traffic bad","ano ang trapiko","unsa ang trapiko"],"answer":"Traffic in Metro Manila is heavy because the number of vehicles grew much faster than the road network, and the roads were built for a much smaller city. EDSA, the main east-west corridor, was laid out as a circumferential road for a capital of a few hundred thousand and now carries traffic across a metropolis of millions, and widening it is expensive enough that it has been discussed rather than done. The other half of the cause is land use: the cities spread outwards along the roads, so the commuting distance grew at the same time as the number of cars, which is the pattern that makes a road network worse than its capacity suggests. The public transport response has been rail on the MRT and LRT lines, which move far more people per hour in a corridor than a road can, and the fare integration between them is the part that was designed to make transfers bearable. A useful distinction for a newcomer is that congestion is not the same as average speed everywhere, since a road that is free-flowing at dawn can be stationary at eight.","variations":["EDSA, the main east-west corridor, was laid out as a circumferential road for a capital of a few hundred thousand and now carries traffic across a metropolis of millions, and widening it is expensive enough that it has been discussed rather than done. Traffic in Metro Manila is heavy because the number of vehicles grew much faster than the road network, and the roads were built for a much smaller city. The other half of the cause is land use: the cities spread outwards along the roads, so the commuting distance grew at the same time as the number of cars, which is the pattern that makes a road network worse than its capacity suggests. The public transport response has been rail on the MRT and LRT lines, which move far more people per hour in a corridor than a road can, and the fare integration between them is the part that was designed to make transfers bearable. A useful distinction for a newcomer is that congestion is not the same as average speed everywhere, since a road that is free-flowing at dawn can be stationary at eight.","The other half of the cause is land use: the cities spread outwards along the roads, so the commuting distance grew at the same time as the number of cars, which is the pattern that makes a road network worse than its capacity suggests. Traffic in Metro Manila is heavy because the number of vehicles grew much faster than the road network, and the roads were built for a much smaller city. EDSA, the main east-west corridor, was laid out as a circumferential road for a capital of a few hundred thousand and now carries traffic across a metropolis of millions, and widening it is expensive enough that it has been discussed rather than done. The public transport response has been rail on the MRT and LRT lines, which move far more people per hour in a corridor than a road can, and the fare integration between them is the part that was designed to make transfers bearable. A useful distinction for a newcomer is that congestion is not the same as average speed everywhere, since a road that is free-flowing at dawn can be stationary at eight."],"tl":"trapiko","bis":"trapiko"},{"id":"phi-transport-000002","topic":"philippines-transport","intent":"definition","concept":"jeepney","keywords":["jeepney","jeep","franchise","route","fare","passenger","military","cooperative","bodabil","hita","converted","fitted"],"patterns":["what is jeepney","jeepney","explain jeepney","definition of jeepney","jeepney meaning","what is a jeepney","why is it called a jeepney","jeepney fare","ano ang hita","unsa ang hita"],"answer":"A jeepney is a converted US military jeep fitted out as a passenger vehicle, and the design is a direct result of what was available after the war. The surplus jeeps were cheap, already had an engine and a chassis, and were large enough to carry passengers on a back, so the body that became standard was built around that rather than designed from the start. The route-sharing system that followed, where a jeepney runs a fixed route but a passenger can ask to get off along the way, is a genuinely Filipino adaptation and is why the vehicle is central to daily life rather than merely a leftover. They are regulated under the franchise system run by transport cooperatives, and the modern rate and the number of authorised units are the practical arguments in every fare dispute. The name is the origin: jeep plus -ney, the old word for a public vehicle.","variations":["The surplus jeeps were cheap, already had an engine and a chassis, and were large enough to carry passengers on a back, so the body that became standard was built around that rather than designed from the start. A jeepney is a converted US military jeep fitted out as a passenger vehicle, and the design is a direct result of what was available after the war. The route-sharing system that followed, where a jeepney runs a fixed route but a passenger can ask to get off along the way, is a genuinely Filipino adaptation and is why the vehicle is central to daily life rather than merely a leftover. They are regulated under the franchise system run by transport cooperatives, and the modern rate and the number of authorised units are the practical arguments in every fare dispute. The name is the origin: jeep plus -ney, the old word for a public vehicle.","The route-sharing system that followed, where a jeepney runs a fixed route but a passenger can ask to get off along the way, is a genuinely Filipino adaptation and is why the vehicle is central to daily life rather than merely a leftover. A jeepney is a converted US military jeep fitted out as a passenger vehicle, and the design is a direct result of what was available after the war. The surplus jeeps were cheap, already had an engine and a chassis, and were large enough to carry passengers on a back, so the body that became standard was built around that rather than designed from the start. They are regulated under the franchise system run by transport cooperatives, and the modern rate and the number of authorised units are the practical arguments in every fare dispute. The name is the origin: jeep plus -ney, the old word for a public vehicle."],"tl":"hita","bis":"hita"}],"lang":"english","group":"philippines","sub":"transport","title":"Transport","groupTitle":"The Philippines","__file":"philippines/transport.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-algorithms","entries":[{"id":"pro-algorithms-000001","topic":"programming-algorithms","intent":"definition","concept":"big o notation","keywords":["big o notation","big","asymptotic","growth","notation","complexity","time","linear","logarithmic","bound","saying","running","memory","requirement"],"patterns":["what is big o notation","big o notation","explain big o notation","definition of big o notation","big o notation meaning","o big n meaning","is big o the actual runtime","ano ang malaki ang O","unsa ang big o"],"answer":"Big O notation is the way of saying how a running time or memory requirement grows as the input gets larger, and it deliberately throws away constant factors so that two different implementations of the same idea can be compared. An algorithm is O of n where n is the input size when doubling the input roughly doubles the work, linear; when it takes n squared, quadratic; when the cost halves each time the input doubles, logarithmic; and when it does not depend on the input at all, constant. The letter is not a number of operations, it is a bound, and saying an algorithm is O of n log n leaves open whether the constant is 2 or 200. Dropping the constant is what makes the comparison useful and also what makes the notation wrong if you rely on it for small inputs, since a quadratic algorithm with a tiny constant can beat a linear one for a thousand elements. It is an upper bound, so O of n technically also admits something slower, and the tight form is written with theta when you have proven the bound from both sides.","variations":["An algorithm is O of n where n is the input size when doubling the input roughly doubles the work, linear; when it takes n squared, quadratic; when the cost halves each time the input doubles, logarithmic; and when it does not depend on the input at all, constant. Big O notation is the way of saying how a running time or memory requirement grows as the input gets larger, and it deliberately throws away constant factors so that two different implementations of the same idea can be compared. The letter is not a number of operations, it is a bound, and saying an algorithm is O of n log n leaves open whether the constant is 2 or 200. Dropping the constant is what makes the comparison useful and also what makes the notation wrong if you rely on it for small inputs, since a quadratic algorithm with a tiny constant can beat a linear one for a thousand elements. It is an upper bound, so O of n technically also admits something slower, and the tight form is written with theta when you have proven the bound from both sides.","The letter is not a number of operations, it is a bound, and saying an algorithm is O of n log n leaves open whether the constant is 2 or 200. Big O notation is the way of saying how a running time or memory requirement grows as the input gets larger, and it deliberately throws away constant factors so that two different implementations of the same idea can be compared. An algorithm is O of n where n is the input size when doubling the input roughly doubles the work, linear; when it takes n squared, quadratic; when the cost halves each time the input doubles, logarithmic; and when it does not depend on the input at all, constant. Dropping the constant is what makes the comparison useful and also what makes the notation wrong if you rely on it for small inputs, since a quadratic algorithm with a tiny constant can beat a linear one for a thousand elements. It is an upper bound, so O of n technically also admits something slower, and the tight form is written with theta when you have proven the bound from both sides."],"tl":"malaki ang O","bis":"big o"},{"id":"pro-algorithms-000002","topic":"programming-algorithms","intent":"definition","concept":"algorithm","keywords":["algorithm","complexity","efficient","performance","step","method","solving","problem","people"],"patterns":["what is algorithm","algorithm","explain algorithm","definition of algorithm","algorithm meaning","what is an algorithm","what is time complexity"],"answer":"An algorithm is a step-by-step method for solving a problem. When people compare algorithms they usually mean how the work grows as the input grows, written as Big O notation. An O(n) loop visits each item once; an O(n squared) loop compares every item to every other, so ten times the input means a hundred times the work. Choosing the right complexity is often the whole optimisation.","variations":["When people compare algorithms they usually mean how the work grows as the input grows, written as Big O notation. An algorithm is a step-by-step method for solving a problem. An O(n) loop visits each item once; an O(n squared) loop compares every item to every other, so ten times the input means a hundred times the work. Choosing the right complexity is often the whole optimisation.","An O(n) loop visits each item once; an O(n squared) loop compares every item to every other, so ten times the input means a hundred times the work. An algorithm is a step-by-step method for solving a problem. When people compare algorithms they usually mean how the work grows as the input grows, written as Big O notation. Choosing the right complexity is often the whole optimisation."]}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"algorithms","title":"Algorithms","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/algorithms.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-apis","entries":[{"id":"pro-apis-000001","topic":"programming-apis","intent":"definition","concept":"api","keywords":["api","endpoint","rest","request","response","json","defined","program","another","something","without","knowing"],"patterns":["what is api","api","explain api","definition of api","api meaning","what is an api","ano ang api","unsa ang api"],"answer":"An API is a defined way for one program to ask another program for something, without knowing how the other one works. You send a request to an address, often with parameters, and get back a response, usually JSON. A weather app calling a weather service is using an API.","variations":["You send a request to an address, often with parameters, and get back a response, usually JSON. An API is a defined way for one program to ask another program for something, without knowing how the other one works. A weather app calling a weather service is using an API.","A weather app calling a weather service is using an API. An API is a defined way for one program to ask another program for something, without knowing how the other one works. You send a request to an address, often with parameters, and get back a response, usually JSON."],"tl":"api","bis":"api","langs":{"tagalog":"Ang API ay ang tiyak na paraan para magtanong ang isang programa sa isa pang programa, nang hindi alam kung paano gumagana ang isa sa loob. Ipinapadala mo ang isang request - verb, address, at kadalasan ay ilang data - at bumabalik ang response, karaniwang structured data tulad ng JSON. Ang REST API ay gumagamit ng mga HTTP verb nang may Saysa: GET ay nagbabasa, POST ay gumagawa, PUT ay nagsasikapit, DELETE ay nag-aalis. Ang layunin ng abstraction na ito ay maaaring palitan ng isang service ang buong backend nito nang hindi mapansin ng app mo, hangga't hindi nagbabago ang kasunduan. Ang dapat tandaan: ang API ay kasunduan, hindi panukala - ang rate limit, kinakailangang field at error code ay totoo, at ang dokumentasyon lang ang tanging awtoridad.","bisaya":"Ang API mao ang defined nga paagi para maka-panalo og usa ka programa sa usa pa nga programa, nga wala nga basi kung unsaon nga mga sulod niya. Mipadala ka og request - verb, address, ug usually pipila ka pa og data - ug mobalik ang response, kasagaran structured data sametang JSON. Ang REST API naggamit sa meaningful nga mga HTTP verb: GET magsulat, POST maghimo, PUT mipul replaces, DELETE magsalay. Ang intent sa abstraction mao ang mahimong palitan kasa ug usa ka service ang tanan nga backend niya nga wala gikan ikaw ang app, basta dii ogma ang contract. Ang imo pa tanan mao ang API contract, dili og suggestion - ang rate limits, required fields ug error codes real gyud, ug ang documentation lang ang authority."},"confidence":null},{"id":"pro-apis-000002","topic":"programming-apis","intent":"definition","concept":"http","keywords":["http","request","response","get","post","rest","protocol","built","stateless"],"patterns":["what is http","http","explain http","definition of http","http meaning","what does a 404 mean","difference between 4xx and 5xx","ano ang HTTP","unsa ang HTTP"],"answer":"HTTP is the request and response protocol the web is built on, and it is stateless, meaning each request carries everything the server needs and the server remembers nothing between them. A GET reads, a POST creates, PUT replaces and DELETE removes, and the verbs carry meaning rather than being decoration. Status codes are grouped: 2xx succeeded, 3xx redirects, 4xx the client is wrong, 5xx the server is wrong. That grouping is the fastest way to triage a failure, because it tells you which side to look at before you read the body.","variations":["A GET reads, a POST creates, PUT replaces and DELETE removes, and the verbs carry meaning rather than being decoration. HTTP is the request and response protocol the web is built on, and it is stateless, meaning each request carries everything the server needs and the server remembers nothing between them. Status codes are grouped: 2xx succeeded, 3xx redirects, 4xx the client is wrong, 5xx the server is wrong. That grouping is the fastest way to triage a failure, because it tells you which side to look at before you read the body.","Status codes are grouped: 2xx succeeded, 3xx redirects, 4xx the client is wrong, 5xx the server is wrong. HTTP is the request and response protocol the web is built on, and it is stateless, meaning each request carries everything the server needs and the server remembers nothing between them. A GET reads, a POST creates, PUT replaces and DELETE removes, and the verbs carry meaning rather than being decoration. That grouping is the fastest way to triage a failure, because it tells you which side to look at before you read the body."],"tl":"HTTP","bis":"HTTP"},{"id":"pro-apis-000003","topic":"programming-apis","intent":"definition","concept":"cookies","keywords":["cookies","cookie","session","httponly","samesite","secure","tracking","small","value","pair","server","asks"],"patterns":["what is cookies","cookies","explain cookies","definition of cookies","cookies meaning","what is a cookie","what does samesite do","are cookies safe","ano ang cookie","unsa ang cookie"],"answer":"A cookie is a small key-value pair a server asks the browser to store and send back with later requests to the same domain, which is how a site remembers you. The important properties are scope, since a cookie only goes back to the domain that set it, and flags, where HttpOnly keeps it away from JavaScript, Secure restricts it to HTTPS, and SameSite governs whether it rides along on cross-site requests. SameSite is the main defence against cross-site request forgery, and a session cookie with neither Secure nor SameSite is among the most common serious web vulnerabilities there is.","variations":["The important properties are scope, since a cookie only goes back to the domain that set it, and flags, where HttpOnly keeps it away from JavaScript, Secure restricts it to HTTPS, and SameSite governs whether it rides along on cross-site requests. A cookie is a small key-value pair a server asks the browser to store and send back with later requests to the same domain, which is how a site remembers you. SameSite is the main defence against cross-site request forgery, and a session cookie with neither Secure nor SameSite is among the most common serious web vulnerabilities there is.","SameSite is the main defence against cross-site request forgery, and a session cookie with neither Secure nor SameSite is among the most common serious web vulnerabilities there is. A cookie is a small key-value pair a server asks the browser to store and send back with later requests to the same domain, which is how a site remembers you. The important properties are scope, since a cookie only goes back to the domain that set it, and flags, where HttpOnly keeps it away from JavaScript, Secure restricts it to HTTPS, and SameSite governs whether it rides along on cross-site requests."],"tl":"cookie","bis":"cookie"},{"id":"pro-apis-000004","topic":"programming-apis","intent":"definition","concept":"local storage","keywords":["local storage","indexeddb","persistent","origin","quota","localstorage","browser","storage","string","value","pairs"],"patterns":["what is local storage","local storage","explain local storage","definition of local storage","local storage meaning","localstorage vs sessionstorage","is localstorage secure","how much can localstorage hold","ano ang local storage","unsa ang local storage"],"answer":"localStorage is browser storage for string key-value pairs that persists across sessions and is scoped to an origin, and it holds about five to ten megabytes. The critical property is that it is synchronous, so every access blocks the main thread, which is why it is wrong for anything on a hot path such as a per-keystroke save. Anything confidential must not go in it, because it is readable by any script on the page and is not encrypted. sessionStorage is the same but cleared when the tab closes, and IndexedDB is the asynchronous answer for larger or structured data.","variations":["The critical property is that it is synchronous, so every access blocks the main thread, which is why it is wrong for anything on a hot path such as a per-keystroke save. localStorage is browser storage for string key-value pairs that persists across sessions and is scoped to an origin, and it holds about five to ten megabytes. Anything confidential must not go in it, because it is readable by any script on the page and is not encrypted. sessionStorage is the same but cleared when the tab closes, and IndexedDB is the asynchronous answer for larger or structured data.","Anything confidential must not go in it, because it is readable by any script on the page and is not encrypted. localStorage is browser storage for string key-value pairs that persists across sessions and is scoped to an origin, and it holds about five to ten megabytes. The critical property is that it is synchronous, so every access blocks the main thread, which is why it is wrong for anything on a hot path such as a per-keystroke save. sessionStorage is the same but cleared when the tab closes, and IndexedDB is the asynchronous answer for larger or structured data."],"tl":"local storage","bis":"local storage"},{"id":"pro-apis-000005","topic":"programming-apis","intent":"definition","concept":"http status codes","keywords":["http status codes","200","404","401","429","500","error","means","request","succeeded","something","created","success"],"patterns":["what is http status codes","http status codes","explain http status codes","definition of http status codes","http status codes meaning","what status code should i return","difference between 401 and 403","what is a 429","ano ang status code","unsa ang status code"],"answer":"200 means the request succeeded and 201 that something was created, while 204 is a success with no body, which is the correct answer to a DELETE. A 301 is a permanent redirect and a 302 a temporary one, and search engines treat the difference seriously. 400 is a malformed request, 401 means not authenticated, and 403 means authenticated but not allowed, which are different problems with different fixes. 429 is rate limited and the correct response is to back off and retry, and 500 upwards means the server broke, so the bug is not in the client.","variations":["A 301 is a permanent redirect and a 302 a temporary one, and search engines treat the difference seriously. 200 means the request succeeded and 201 that something was created, while 204 is a success with no body, which is the correct answer to a DELETE. 400 is a malformed request, 401 means not authenticated, and 403 means authenticated but not allowed, which are different problems with different fixes. 429 is rate limited and the correct response is to back off and retry, and 500 upwards means the server broke, so the bug is not in the client.","400 is a malformed request, 401 means not authenticated, and 403 means authenticated but not allowed, which are different problems with different fixes. 200 means the request succeeded and 201 that something was created, while 204 is a success with no body, which is the correct answer to a DELETE. A 301 is a permanent redirect and a 302 a temporary one, and search engines treat the difference seriously. 429 is rate limited and the correct response is to back off and retry, and 500 upwards means the server broke, so the bug is not in the client."],"tl":"status code","bis":"status code"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"apis","title":"APIs","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/apis.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-c","entries":[{"id":"pro-c-000001","topic":"programming-c","intent":"definition","concept":"c language","keywords":["c language","pointer","ffi","C","compiled","level","language","invented","bell","labs"],"patterns":["what is c language","c language","explain c language","definition of c language","c language meaning","what is c programming","is c hard to learn","what is c language used for","ano ang C","unsa ang C"],"answer":"C is a compiled, low-level language invented at Bell Labs in 1972 and still the layer almost every other language is written on top of. It gives you manual control over memory, raw pointers and direct access to the hardware, and in exchange it offers almost no safety: there are no classes, no automatic memory management and no bounds checking, so a mistake in one routine can corrupt memory that belongs to a completely different part of the program. Its simplicity is a feature in systems work, because an operating system, a driver or a compiler can be written in it without a runtime underneath. C is also the lingua franca for foreign function interfaces, so almost every language can call into C code, and that is why native extensions everywhere are written in it. The standard is maintained by the ISO and the current widely supported revision is C23, though C17 and C11 remain common.","variations":["It gives you manual control over memory, raw pointers and direct access to the hardware, and in exchange it offers almost no safety: there are no classes, no automatic memory management and no bounds checking, so a mistake in one routine can corrupt memory that belongs to a completely different part of the program. C is a compiled, low-level language invented at Bell Labs in 1972 and still the layer almost every other language is written on top of. Its simplicity is a feature in systems work, because an operating system, a driver or a compiler can be written in it without a runtime underneath. C is also the lingua franca for foreign function interfaces, so almost every language can call into C code, and that is why native extensions everywhere are written in it. The standard is maintained by the ISO and the current widely supported revision is C23, though C17 and C11 remain common.","Its simplicity is a feature in systems work, because an operating system, a driver or a compiler can be written in it without a runtime underneath. C is a compiled, low-level language invented at Bell Labs in 1972 and still the layer almost every other language is written on top of. It gives you manual control over memory, raw pointers and direct access to the hardware, and in exchange it offers almost no safety: there are no classes, no automatic memory management and no bounds checking, so a mistake in one routine can corrupt memory that belongs to a completely different part of the program. C is also the lingua franca for foreign function interfaces, so almost every language can call into C code, and that is why native extensions everywhere are written in it. The standard is maintained by the ISO and the current widely supported revision is C23, though C17 and C11 remain common."],"tl":"C","bis":"C"},{"id":"pro-c-000002","topic":"programming-c","intent":"definition","concept":"pointer","keywords":["pointer","address","dereference","variable","stores","memory","rather"],"patterns":["what is pointer","pointer","explain pointer","definition of pointer","pointer meaning","what is a pointer in c","why are pointers dangerous","what is dereferencing","ano ang pointer","unsa ang pointer"],"answer":"A pointer is a variable that stores a memory address rather than a value, which is C's central mechanism for referring to data without copying it. You declare one with the asterisk, for example int *p, and it can then point at a variable, at a position inside an array, or at nothing at all when its value is null. Two operations do the real work: the dereference operator reads or writes what the address points at, and the address-of operator takes the address of a variable so it can be pointed at. Because the language cannot check that the address is still valid, a pointer left dangling after the memory it referred to is freed, or a pointer cast from the wrong type, is undefined behaviour, and the compiler is entitled to assume your program never does it. This is why a garbage-collected language gives you pointers in a form it can monitor and plain C does not, and why the same buffer overrun in C can overwrite unrelated variables without any error message.","variations":["You declare one with the asterisk, for example int *p, and it can then point at a variable, at a position inside an array, or at nothing at all when its value is null. A pointer is a variable that stores a memory address rather than a value, which is C's central mechanism for referring to data without copying it. Two operations do the real work: the dereference operator reads or writes what the address points at, and the address-of operator takes the address of a variable so it can be pointed at. Because the language cannot check that the address is still valid, a pointer left dangling after the memory it referred to is freed, or a pointer cast from the wrong type, is undefined behaviour, and the compiler is entitled to assume your program never does it. This is why a garbage-collected language gives you pointers in a form it can monitor and plain C does not, and why the same buffer overrun in C can overwrite unrelated variables without any error message.","Two operations do the real work: the dereference operator reads or writes what the address points at, and the address-of operator takes the address of a variable so it can be pointed at. A pointer is a variable that stores a memory address rather than a value, which is C's central mechanism for referring to data without copying it. You declare one with the asterisk, for example int *p, and it can then point at a variable, at a position inside an array, or at nothing at all when its value is null. Because the language cannot check that the address is still valid, a pointer left dangling after the memory it referred to is freed, or a pointer cast from the wrong type, is undefined behaviour, and the compiler is entitled to assume your program never does it. This is why a garbage-collected language gives you pointers in a form it can monitor and plain C does not, and why the same buffer overrun in C can overwrite unrelated variables without any error message."],"tl":"pointer","bis":"pointer"},{"id":"pro-c-000003","topic":"programming-c","intent":"definition","concept":"memory leak","keywords":["memory leak","allocation","free","valgrind","release","memory","leak","program","allocated","longer","never"],"patterns":["what is memory leak","memory leak","explain memory leak","definition of memory leak","memory leak meaning","what is a memory leak","how do i fix a memory leak","does c have memory leaks","ano ang memory leak","unsa ang memory leak"],"answer":"A memory leak is memory a program allocated, no longer needs, and never handed back, so the process holds it for as long as it runs. In C the allocation is manual, so the matching release is manual too, and every place that acquires memory is a place that can forget to release it. A leak is not a crash by itself; the symptom is that memory use climbs over hours or days until the operating system runs out and kills the process, which is why it usually shows up in long-running servers rather than short command line tools. It is also a security concern, because a process that leaks predictably can be pushed past its limits on purpose. Tools like valgrind and the address sanitizers exist because the compiler cannot find these on its own, and the practical defences are to keep the allocation and the release in the same scope and to prefer owning structures that release themselves.","variations":["In C the allocation is manual, so the matching release is manual too, and every place that acquires memory is a place that can forget to release it. A memory leak is memory a program allocated, no longer needs, and never handed back, so the process holds it for as long as it runs. A leak is not a crash by itself; the symptom is that memory use climbs over hours or days until the operating system runs out and kills the process, which is why it usually shows up in long-running servers rather than short command line tools. It is also a security concern, because a process that leaks predictably can be pushed past its limits on purpose. Tools like valgrind and the address sanitizers exist because the compiler cannot find these on its own, and the practical defences are to keep the allocation and the release in the same scope and to prefer owning structures that release themselves.","A leak is not a crash by itself; the symptom is that memory use climbs over hours or days until the operating system runs out and kills the process, which is why it usually shows up in long-running servers rather than short command line tools. A memory leak is memory a program allocated, no longer needs, and never handed back, so the process holds it for as long as it runs. In C the allocation is manual, so the matching release is manual too, and every place that acquires memory is a place that can forget to release it. It is also a security concern, because a process that leaks predictably can be pushed past its limits on purpose. Tools like valgrind and the address sanitizers exist because the compiler cannot find these on its own, and the practical defences are to keep the allocation and the release in the same scope and to prefer owning structures that release themselves."],"tl":"memory leak","bis":"memory leak"},{"id":"pro-c-000004","topic":"programming-c","intent":"definition","concept":"malloc","keywords":["malloc","heap","calloc","realloc","free","function","reserves","block","memory"],"patterns":["what is malloc","malloc","explain malloc","definition of malloc","malloc meaning","what does malloc do","what is the difference between malloc and calloc","do i have to free malloc","ano ang malloc","unsa ang memory allocation"],"answer":"malloc is the C function that reserves a block of memory from the heap and returns a pointer to its start, and it is the entry point for every manually managed allocation in the language. Its signature takes a size in bytes and returns a null pointer on failure rather than a valid address, which is why the idiomatic C checks the result before using it. The size argument is the classic trap: the function has no idea what type you meant, so passing the wrong count for a struct writes past the end of the block and produces undefined behaviour. The related family matters as well, calloc takes a count and a size and zeroes the memory, realloc moves a block to a new size, and free releases it exactly once. Because free does not clear the pointer, a stale copy left behind is a dangling pointer and the single most common source of use-after-free bugs in real C code.","variations":["Its signature takes a size in bytes and returns a null pointer on failure rather than a valid address, which is why the idiomatic C checks the result before using it. malloc is the C function that reserves a block of memory from the heap and returns a pointer to its start, and it is the entry point for every manually managed allocation in the language. The size argument is the classic trap: the function has no idea what type you meant, so passing the wrong count for a struct writes past the end of the block and produces undefined behaviour. The related family matters as well, calloc takes a count and a size and zeroes the memory, realloc moves a block to a new size, and free releases it exactly once. Because free does not clear the pointer, a stale copy left behind is a dangling pointer and the single most common source of use-after-free bugs in real C code.","The size argument is the classic trap: the function has no idea what type you meant, so passing the wrong count for a struct writes past the end of the block and produces undefined behaviour. malloc is the C function that reserves a block of memory from the heap and returns a pointer to its start, and it is the entry point for every manually managed allocation in the language. Its signature takes a size in bytes and returns a null pointer on failure rather than a valid address, which is why the idiomatic C checks the result before using it. The related family matters as well, calloc takes a count and a size and zeroes the memory, realloc moves a block to a new size, and free releases it exactly once. Because free does not clear the pointer, a stale copy left behind is a dangling pointer and the single most common source of use-after-free bugs in real C code."],"tl":"malloc","bis":"memory allocation"},{"id":"pro-c-000005","topic":"programming-c","intent":"definition","concept":"undefined behavior","keywords":["undefined behavior","sanitizer","optimizer","miscompile","undefined","behaviour","standard's","saying","program","left"],"patterns":["what is undefined behavior","undefined behavior","explain undefined behavior","definition of undefined behavior","undefined behavior meaning","what is undefined behaviour","why does undefined behaviour cause random bugs","how do i detect undefined behaviour","ano ang undefined behaviour","unsa ang undefined behaviour"],"answer":"Undefined behaviour is the C standard's way of saying that a program has left the range where the language guarantees any result at all, and the compiler is free to assume it never happens. Reading an array past its end, dereferencing a null pointer, overflowing a signed integer or modifying a variable twice between sequence points all count, and the consequence is that the optimiser may reorder, delete or fabricate code on the assumption the situation never arises. That is why undefined behaviour does not merely produce a wrong number: it can cause code far away from the mistake to stop running, and the bug appears to move between builds for no visible reason. Sanitizers such as address sanitizer and undefined behaviour sanitizer instrument the program to report the exact operation at run time, which is the practical way to find it, because the compiler itself will not warn about most of these cases.","variations":["Reading an array past its end, dereferencing a null pointer, overflowing a signed integer or modifying a variable twice between sequence points all count, and the consequence is that the optimiser may reorder, delete or fabricate code on the assumption the situation never arises. Undefined behaviour is the C standard's way of saying that a program has left the range where the language guarantees any result at all, and the compiler is free to assume it never happens. That is why undefined behaviour does not merely produce a wrong number: it can cause code far away from the mistake to stop running, and the bug appears to move between builds for no visible reason. Sanitizers such as address sanitizer and undefined behaviour sanitizer instrument the program to report the exact operation at run time, which is the practical way to find it, because the compiler itself will not warn about most of these cases.","That is why undefined behaviour does not merely produce a wrong number: it can cause code far away from the mistake to stop running, and the bug appears to move between builds for no visible reason. Undefined behaviour is the C standard's way of saying that a program has left the range where the language guarantees any result at all, and the compiler is free to assume it never happens. Reading an array past its end, dereferencing a null pointer, overflowing a signed integer or modifying a variable twice between sequence points all count, and the consequence is that the optimiser may reorder, delete or fabricate code on the assumption the situation never arises. Sanitizers such as address sanitizer and undefined behaviour sanitizer instrument the program to report the exact operation at run time, which is the practical way to find it, because the compiler itself will not warn about most of these cases."],"tl":"undefined behaviour","bis":"undefined behaviour"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"c","title":"C","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/c.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-concepts","entries":[{"id":"pro-concepts-000001","topic":"programming-concepts","intent":"definition","concept":"variable","keywords":["variable","assignment","scope","let","const","named","container","value","something","refer"],"patterns":["what is variable","variable","explain variable","definition of variable","variable meaning","what is a variable","variables in programming"],"answer":"A variable is a named container for a value. You put something in it, refer to it by name later, and can change what is inside. In modern JavaScript you declare with let, which can be reassigned, or const, which cannot. Scope decides how long the name stays visible, and getting scope wrong is one of the most common sources of bugs.","variations":["You put something in it, refer to it by name later, and can change what is inside. A variable is a named container for a value. In modern JavaScript you declare with let, which can be reassigned, or const, which cannot. Scope decides how long the name stays visible, and getting scope wrong is one of the most common sources of bugs.","In modern JavaScript you declare with let, which can be reassigned, or const, which cannot. A variable is a named container for a value. You put something in it, refer to it by name later, and can change what is inside. Scope decides how long the name stays visible, and getting scope wrong is one of the most common sources of bugs."]},{"id":"pro-concepts-000002","topic":"programming-concepts","intent":"definition","concept":"function","keywords":["function","argument","parameter","return","reusable","block","code","takes","inputs"],"patterns":["what is function","function","explain function","definition of function","function meaning","what is a function","functions explained"],"answer":"A function is a reusable block of code that takes inputs, does something, and returns a result. The inputs are parameters, the output is the return value. Functions exist so you write something once and reuse it, and so each piece of a program does one job you can name and test on its own.","variations":["The inputs are parameters, the output is the return value. A function is a reusable block of code that takes inputs, does something, and returns a result. Functions exist so you write something once and reuse it, and so each piece of a program does one job you can name and test on its own.","Functions exist so you write something once and reuse it, and so each piece of a program does one job you can name and test on its own. A function is a reusable block of code that takes inputs, does something, and returns a result. The inputs are parameters, the output is the return value."]},{"id":"pro-concepts-000003","topic":"programming-concepts","intent":"definition","concept":"frontend","keywords":["frontend","backend","fullstack","client","server","runs","browser","user","sees","layout"],"patterns":["what is frontend","frontend","explain frontend","definition of frontend","frontend meaning","difference between frontend and backend","what is backend"],"answer":"The frontend is what runs in the browser and that the user sees: layout, buttons, styling. The backend runs on a server the user never sees - it holds the data, checks permissions, does the real work, and sends results back. Splitting them lets the same backend serve a web app, a mobile app and an API at once.","variations":["The backend runs on a server the user never sees - it holds the data, checks permissions, does the real work, and sends results back. The frontend is what runs in the browser and that the user sees: layout, buttons, styling. Splitting them lets the same backend serve a web app, a mobile app and an API at once.","Splitting them lets the same backend serve a web app, a mobile app and an API at once. The frontend is what runs in the browser and that the user sees: layout, buttons, styling. The backend runs on a server the user never sees - it holds the data, checks permissions, does the real work, and sends results back."]},{"id":"pro-concepts-000004","topic":"programming-concepts","intent":"definition","concept":"type system","keywords":["type system","types","typing","static","dynamic","compiler","safety","type","system","rules","values","program","hold"],"patterns":["what is type system","type system","explain type system","definition of type system","type system meaning","what is a type system","static vs dynamic typing","should i use typescript","ano ang type system","unsa ang type system"],"answer":"A type system is a set of rules about what values a program may hold and what can be done with them, and in statically typed languages it is a compile-time discipline rather than a runtime one. Its value is that whole categories of bug become impossible rather than merely unlikely. The honest cost is verbosity and a real learning curve, and the honest benefit is that a typed codebase is navigable because a function's contract is written down.","variations":["Its value is that whole categories of bug become impossible rather than merely unlikely. A type system is a set of rules about what values a program may hold and what can be done with them, and in statically typed languages it is a compile-time discipline rather than a runtime one. The honest cost is verbosity and a real learning curve, and the honest benefit is that a typed codebase is navigable because a function's contract is written down.","The honest cost is verbosity and a real learning curve, and the honest benefit is that a typed codebase is navigable because a function's contract is written down. A type system is a set of rules about what values a program may hold and what can be done with them, and in statically typed languages it is a compile-time discipline rather than a runtime one. Its value is that whole categories of bug become impossible rather than merely unlikely."],"tl":"type system","bis":"type system"},{"id":"pro-concepts-000005","topic":"programming-concepts","intent":"definition","concept":"object","keywords":["object","dictionary","key","value","property","structure","collection","related","values","bundled","name"],"patterns":["what is object","object","explain object","definition of object","object meaning","what is an object in programming","difference between an object and an array","ano ang object","unsa ang object"],"answer":"An object is a collection of related values bundled under one name, and it is the basic unit of organisation in most languages. In JavaScript objects are also the basis for arrays, functions and dates, which is why the language is sometimes described as having one data structure. The practical consequence is that almost nothing is deeply immutable, so a function that mutates what you passed it is a common source of bugs.","variations":["In JavaScript objects are also the basis for arrays, functions and dates, which is why the language is sometimes described as having one data structure. An object is a collection of related values bundled under one name, and it is the basic unit of organisation in most languages. The practical consequence is that almost nothing is deeply immutable, so a function that mutates what you passed it is a common source of bugs.","The practical consequence is that almost nothing is deeply immutable, so a function that mutates what you passed it is a common source of bugs. An object is a collection of related values bundled under one name, and it is the basic unit of organisation in most languages. In JavaScript objects are also the basis for arrays, functions and dates, which is why the language is sometimes described as having one data structure."],"tl":"object","bis":"object"},{"id":"pro-concepts-000006","topic":"programming-concepts","intent":"definition","concept":"loop","keywords":["loop","iteration","while","for","infinite","repeats","work","until","condition","fails"],"patterns":["what is loop","loop","explain loop","definition of loop","loop meaning","what is a loop","what is an off by one error","what is an infinite loop","ano ang loop","unsa ang loop"],"answer":"A loop repeats work until a condition fails, and the interesting part is always the exit condition. A loop whose condition never becomes false is an infinite loop, and one that stops one iteration early is an off-by-one, which is why the boundary is worth stating in terms of the first invalid value rather than the last valid one. The most common bug is mutating the thing being iterated, which behaves differently in every language.","variations":["A loop whose condition never becomes false is an infinite loop, and one that stops one iteration early is an off-by-one, which is why the boundary is worth stating in terms of the first invalid value rather than the last valid one. A loop repeats work until a condition fails, and the interesting part is always the exit condition. The most common bug is mutating the thing being iterated, which behaves differently in every language.","The most common bug is mutating the thing being iterated, which behaves differently in every language. A loop repeats work until a condition fails, and the interesting part is always the exit condition. A loop whose condition never becomes false is an infinite loop, and one that stops one iteration early is an off-by-one, which is why the boundary is worth stating in terms of the first invalid value rather than the last valid one."],"tl":"loop","bis":"loop"},{"id":"pro-concepts-000007","topic":"programming-concepts","intent":"definition","concept":"recursion","keywords":["recursion","tree","function","solves","problem","calling","itself"],"patterns":["what is recursion","recursion","explain recursion","definition of recursion","recursion meaning","what is a base case","what is a stack overflow","ano ang recursion","unsa ang recursion"],"answer":"Recursion is a function that solves a problem by calling itself on a smaller version of the same problem, and every recursion needs a base case that stops it. The call stack is what makes it work, since each call pushes a frame popped when it returns, and the practical limit is how deep that stack can go. Recursion is clearest for problems that split, like trees and sorting, and usually worse than a loop for plain iteration.","variations":["The call stack is what makes it work, since each call pushes a frame popped when it returns, and the practical limit is how deep that stack can go. Recursion is a function that solves a problem by calling itself on a smaller version of the same problem, and every recursion needs a base case that stops it. Recursion is clearest for problems that split, like trees and sorting, and usually worse than a loop for plain iteration.","Recursion is clearest for problems that split, like trees and sorting, and usually worse than a loop for plain iteration. Recursion is a function that solves a problem by calling itself on a smaller version of the same problem, and every recursion needs a base case that stops it. The call stack is what makes it work, since each call pushes a frame popped when it returns, and the practical limit is how deep that stack can go."],"tl":"recursion","bis":"recursion"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"concepts","title":"Programming concepts","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/concepts.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-cpp","entries":[{"id":"pro-cpp-000001","topic":"programming-cpp","intent":"definition","concept":"cplusplus","keywords":["cplusplus","template","compiled","constexpr","edition","C++","extension","adds","classes","templates","automatic","memory"],"patterns":["what is cplusplus","cplusplus","explain cplusplus","definition of cplusplus","cplusplus meaning","what is c++","is c++ hard","what is c++ used for","ano ang C++","unsa ang C++"],"answer":"C++ is an extension of C that adds classes, templates, automatic memory management and a much larger standard library, and it is the language behind most video games, desktop applications and much of the machine learning tooling. It is multi-paradigm rather than purely object-oriented, so you can write in an object style, a functional style or plain procedural code within the same program, and since it compiles to native machine code the performance ceiling is high when a bottleneck is reached. The cost of that power is complexity: a beginner can be overwhelmed by the number of features, and even experienced programmers spend years learning which ones to avoid. It is a compiled language, so the build step and header files are part of the workflow, and the language keeps changing, with new editions standardised roughly every three years.","variations":["It is multi-paradigm rather than purely object-oriented, so you can write in an object style, a functional style or plain procedural code within the same program, and since it compiles to native machine code the performance ceiling is high when a bottleneck is reached. C++ is an extension of C that adds classes, templates, automatic memory management and a much larger standard library, and it is the language behind most video games, desktop applications and much of the machine learning tooling. The cost of that power is complexity: a beginner can be overwhelmed by the number of features, and even experienced programmers spend years learning which ones to avoid. It is a compiled language, so the build step and header files are part of the workflow, and the language keeps changing, with new editions standardised roughly every three years.","The cost of that power is complexity: a beginner can be overwhelmed by the number of features, and even experienced programmers spend years learning which ones to avoid. C++ is an extension of C that adds classes, templates, automatic memory management and a much larger standard library, and it is the language behind most video games, desktop applications and much of the machine learning tooling. It is multi-paradigm rather than purely object-oriented, so you can write in an object style, a functional style or plain procedural code within the same program, and since it compiles to native machine code the performance ceiling is high when a bottleneck is reached. It is a compiled language, so the build step and header files are part of the workflow, and the language keeps changing, with new editions standardised roughly every three years."],"tl":"C++","bis":"C++"},{"id":"pro-cpp-000002","topic":"programming-cpp","intent":"definition","concept":"template","keywords":["template","generics","instantiation","blueprint","generates","whole","family","functions"],"patterns":["what is template","template","explain template","definition of template","template meaning","what is a template in c++","why are template errors so long","are templates slower","ano ang template","unsa ang template"],"answer":"A C++ template is a blueprint that generates a whole family of functions or classes from a single source definition, which is how the language achieves generic programming without a separate generic syntax. When you write a template on a type parameter and then call it with an int and a string, the compiler produces two separate real implementations, one for each type, and the original definition is not code you can call on its own. The consequence is that template errors often surface at the point of use rather than at the point of definition, with messages running to dozens of lines deep through the standard library, which is the single most complained-about aspect of the language. Templates also increase compile time and binary size, because the body is duplicated for every instantiation, and the idiom of putting all template definitions in a header exists only because the compiler must see them. The same mechanism powers the containers in the standard library, which is why a vector of one element type performs as well as an array.","variations":["When you write a template on a type parameter and then call it with an int and a string, the compiler produces two separate real implementations, one for each type, and the original definition is not code you can call on its own. A C++ template is a blueprint that generates a whole family of functions or classes from a single source definition, which is how the language achieves generic programming without a separate generic syntax. The consequence is that template errors often surface at the point of use rather than at the point of definition, with messages running to dozens of lines deep through the standard library, which is the single most complained-about aspect of the language. Templates also increase compile time and binary size, because the body is duplicated for every instantiation, and the idiom of putting all template definitions in a header exists only because the compiler must see them. The same mechanism powers the containers in the standard library, which is why a vector of one element type performs as well as an array.","The consequence is that template errors often surface at the point of use rather than at the point of definition, with messages running to dozens of lines deep through the standard library, which is the single most complained-about aspect of the language. A C++ template is a blueprint that generates a whole family of functions or classes from a single source definition, which is how the language achieves generic programming without a separate generic syntax. When you write a template on a type parameter and then call it with an int and a string, the compiler produces two separate real implementations, one for each type, and the original definition is not code you can call on its own. Templates also increase compile time and binary size, because the body is duplicated for every instantiation, and the idiom of putting all template definitions in a header exists only because the compiler must see them. The same mechanism powers the containers in the standard library, which is why a vector of one element type performs as well as an array."],"tl":"template","bis":"template"},{"id":"pro-cpp-000003","topic":"programming-cpp","intent":"definition","concept":"smart pointer","keywords":["smart pointer","ownership","leak","destructor","smart","pointer","class","template","owns","object"],"patterns":["what is smart pointer","smart pointer","explain smart pointer","definition of smart pointer","smart pointer meaning","what is a smart pointer","should i use shared ptr","what is the difference between unique ptr and shared ptr","ano ang smart pointer","unsa ang smart pointer"],"answer":"A smart pointer is a class template in C++ that owns the object it points at and manages its lifetime automatically, and it is the answer to nearly every question about when to delete something. A unique_ptr models strict single ownership: it holds the object exclusively, it is movable but not copyable, and when it goes out of scope the object is destroyed. A shared_ptr models shared ownership with a reference count, so the object dies when the last shared_ptr holding it does, and a weak_ptr refers to a shared_ptr without adding to the count, which is what you use to break a reference cycle. The rule of thumb is to default to a unique_ptr and treat the existence of a shared_ptr as a design smell, because a cycle between two shared_ptrs leaks both objects permanently and no destructor will ever run. The older raw pointer syntax is still the easiest way to hand a non-owning observer, and the mistake is writing delete at all in new code.","variations":["A unique_ptr models strict single ownership: it holds the object exclusively, it is movable but not copyable, and when it goes out of scope the object is destroyed. A smart pointer is a class template in C++ that owns the object it points at and manages its lifetime automatically, and it is the answer to nearly every question about when to delete something. A shared_ptr models shared ownership with a reference count, so the object dies when the last shared_ptr holding it does, and a weak_ptr refers to a shared_ptr without adding to the count, which is what you use to break a reference cycle. The rule of thumb is to default to a unique_ptr and treat the existence of a shared_ptr as a design smell, because a cycle between two shared_ptrs leaks both objects permanently and no destructor will ever run. The older raw pointer syntax is still the easiest way to hand a non-owning observer, and the mistake is writing delete at all in new code.","A shared_ptr models shared ownership with a reference count, so the object dies when the last shared_ptr holding it does, and a weak_ptr refers to a shared_ptr without adding to the count, which is what you use to break a reference cycle. A smart pointer is a class template in C++ that owns the object it points at and manages its lifetime automatically, and it is the answer to nearly every question about when to delete something. A unique_ptr models strict single ownership: it holds the object exclusively, it is movable but not copyable, and when it goes out of scope the object is destroyed. The rule of thumb is to default to a unique_ptr and treat the existence of a shared_ptr as a design smell, because a cycle between two shared_ptrs leaks both objects permanently and no destructor will ever run. The older raw pointer syntax is still the easiest way to hand a non-owning observer, and the mistake is writing delete at all in new code."],"tl":"smart pointer","bis":"smart pointer"},{"id":"pro-cpp-000004","topic":"programming-cpp","intent":"definition","concept":"raii","keywords":["raii","destructor","cleanup","lifetime","resource","acquisition","initialization","shortened","idiom"],"patterns":["what is raii","raii","explain raii","definition of raii","raii meaning","why is raii important","what is scope guard","ano ang raii","unsa ang raii"],"answer":"Resource acquisition is initialization, usually shortened to RAII, is the C++ idiom in which every resource beyond memory is tied to the lifetime of an object so that releasing it cannot be forgotten. An object that owns a file, a lock, a socket or a transaction releases it in its destructor, and because destructors run when scope ends for any reason, including an exception or an early return, the cleanup happens automatically. This is the general principle behind smart pointers, and it is why std::lock_guard replaced manual lock and unlock pairs that could be skipped when an exception was thrown between them. The pattern is easy to state and easy to get subtly wrong: releasing in a copy constructor or assignment operator, for instance, breaks the guarantee and creates a double release, and knowing which operations acquire and which release is part of understanding the idiom. Its real payoff is that cleanup correctness stops being something you review line by line.","variations":["An object that owns a file, a lock, a socket or a transaction releases it in its destructor, and because destructors run when scope ends for any reason, including an exception or an early return, the cleanup happens automatically. Resource acquisition is initialization, usually shortened to RAII, is the C++ idiom in which every resource beyond memory is tied to the lifetime of an object so that releasing it cannot be forgotten. This is the general principle behind smart pointers, and it is why std::lock_guard replaced manual lock and unlock pairs that could be skipped when an exception was thrown between them. The pattern is easy to state and easy to get subtly wrong: releasing in a copy constructor or assignment operator, for instance, breaks the guarantee and creates a double release, and knowing which operations acquire and which release is part of understanding the idiom. Its real payoff is that cleanup correctness stops being something you review line by line.","This is the general principle behind smart pointers, and it is why std::lock_guard replaced manual lock and unlock pairs that could be skipped when an exception was thrown between them. Resource acquisition is initialization, usually shortened to RAII, is the C++ idiom in which every resource beyond memory is tied to the lifetime of an object so that releasing it cannot be forgotten. An object that owns a file, a lock, a socket or a transaction releases it in its destructor, and because destructors run when scope ends for any reason, including an exception or an early return, the cleanup happens automatically. The pattern is easy to state and easy to get subtly wrong: releasing in a copy constructor or assignment operator, for instance, breaks the guarantee and creates a double release, and knowing which operations acquire and which release is part of understanding the idiom. Its real payoff is that cleanup correctness stops being something you review line by line."],"tl":"raii","bis":"raii"},{"id":"pro-cpp-000005","topic":"programming-cpp","intent":"definition","concept":"object oriented programming","keywords":["object oriented programming","class","instance","encapsulation","polymorphism","composition","inheritance","abstraction","object","oriented","programming","organising","program","around"],"patterns":["what is object oriented programming","object oriented programming","explain object oriented programming","definition of object oriented programming","object oriented programming meaning","inheritance vs composition","what is polymorphism","ano ang object oriented programming","unsa ang object oriented programming"],"answer":"Object oriented programming is a way of organising a program around data that carries both state and the functions allowed to act on that state, rather than around a list of procedures. A class describes the shape of that data, an object is one concrete instance, and encapsulation means the outside world manipulates the object through its methods instead of writing its fields directly. The four ideas usually attached to it are abstraction, which hides detail behind a simple interface, inheritance, which lets one class reuse and specialise another, polymorphism, which lets one call site handle different concrete types, and composition, where a class holds another rather than deriving from it. The honest modern position is that inheritance is used far less than beginners expect: most real designs lean on composition, because deep hierarchies are expensive to change and are the main source of fragile base-class problems.","variations":["A class describes the shape of that data, an object is one concrete instance, and encapsulation means the outside world manipulates the object through its methods instead of writing its fields directly. Object oriented programming is a way of organising a program around data that carries both state and the functions allowed to act on that state, rather than around a list of procedures. The four ideas usually attached to it are abstraction, which hides detail behind a simple interface, inheritance, which lets one class reuse and specialise another, polymorphism, which lets one call site handle different concrete types, and composition, where a class holds another rather than deriving from it. The honest modern position is that inheritance is used far less than beginners expect: most real designs lean on composition, because deep hierarchies are expensive to change and are the main source of fragile base-class problems.","The four ideas usually attached to it are abstraction, which hides detail behind a simple interface, inheritance, which lets one class reuse and specialise another, polymorphism, which lets one call site handle different concrete types, and composition, where a class holds another rather than deriving from it. Object oriented programming is a way of organising a program around data that carries both state and the functions allowed to act on that state, rather than around a list of procedures. A class describes the shape of that data, an object is one concrete instance, and encapsulation means the outside world manipulates the object through its methods instead of writing its fields directly. The honest modern position is that inheritance is used far less than beginners expect: most real designs lean on composition, because deep hierarchies are expensive to change and are the main source of fragile base-class problems."],"tl":"object oriented programming","bis":"object oriented programming"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"cpp","title":"C++","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/cpp.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-csharp","entries":[{"id":"pro-csharp-000001","topic":"programming-csharp","intent":"definition","concept":"csharp","keywords":["csharp","dotnet","var","properties","delegates","C#","managed","object","oriented","language","designed","microsoft"],"patterns":["what is csharp","csharp","explain csharp","definition of csharp","csharp meaning","what is c sharp","is c# better than java","what is c# used for","ano ang C#","unsa ang C#"],"answer":"C# is a managed, object-oriented language designed by Microsoft and run on the .NET runtime, and its defining feature is that memory is managed for you: the garbage collector reclaims objects you no longer reference, so there is no free and no manual allocation. It is statically typed with type inference through the var keyword, has value types that live on the stack for small simple data and reference types on the heap, and supports properties, delegates, events and asynchronous methods with the async and await pair built into the language. It is garbage collected rather than deterministic, which means a resource such as an open file is not released the instant it goes out of scope but when the collector gets to it, so the using statement exists to make the disposal time explicit for those cases. The language is deliberately close to Java and C++ in shape while differing in details, and the real point of comparison is usually Python, since both aim at readable code and rapid development.","variations":["It is statically typed with type inference through the var keyword, has value types that live on the stack for small simple data and reference types on the heap, and supports properties, delegates, events and asynchronous methods with the async and await pair built into the language. NET runtime, and its defining feature is that memory is managed for you: the garbage collector reclaims objects you no longer reference, so there is no free and no manual allocation. It is garbage collected rather than deterministic, which means a resource such as an open file is not released the instant it goes out of scope but when the collector gets to it, so the using statement exists to make the disposal time explicit for those cases. The language is deliberately close to Java and C++ in shape while differing in details, and the real point of comparison is usually Python, since both aim at readable code and rapid development.","It is garbage collected rather than deterministic, which means a resource such as an open file is not released the instant it goes out of scope but when the collector gets to it, so the using statement exists to make the disposal time explicit for those cases. NET runtime, and its defining feature is that memory is managed for you: the garbage collector reclaims objects you no longer reference, so there is no free and no manual allocation. It is statically typed with type inference through the var keyword, has value types that live on the stack for small simple data and reference types on the heap, and supports properties, delegates, events and asynchronous methods with the async and await pair built into the language. The language is deliberately close to Java and C++ in shape while differing in details, and the real point of comparison is usually Python, since both aim at readable code and rapid development."],"tl":"C#","bis":"C#"},{"id":"pro-csharp-000002","topic":"programming-csharp","intent":"definition","concept":"linq","keywords":["linq","query","lambda","enumerable","aggregate","language","integrated","feature","write","queries"],"patterns":["what is linq","linq","explain linq","definition of linq","linq meaning","what does linq stand for","is linq slow","ano ang linq","unsa ang linq"],"answer":"Language Integrated Query is the C# feature that lets you write queries directly in the language against collections, databases and XML instead of moving between the data and a separate query engine. The core operations are from for the source, where for filtering, select for projection, orderby for sorting, then groupby, join and aggregate functions such as count and sum, and a query ends with an execution method like ToList or First that actually runs it. It comes in two surface syntaxes, the query syntax that reads like SQL and the method syntax using extension methods, and they compile to the same thing, so the choice is readability. The deeper point is deferred execution: a query is a description of work, not the work itself, and nothing happens until it is enumerated, which is why iterating a query twice re-reads the source and why modifying a collection mid-enumeration throws.","variations":["The core operations are from for the source, where for filtering, select for projection, orderby for sorting, then groupby, join and aggregate functions such as count and sum, and a query ends with an execution method like ToList or First that actually runs it. Language Integrated Query is the C# feature that lets you write queries directly in the language against collections, databases and XML instead of moving between the data and a separate query engine. It comes in two surface syntaxes, the query syntax that reads like SQL and the method syntax using extension methods, and they compile to the same thing, so the choice is readability. The deeper point is deferred execution: a query is a description of work, not the work itself, and nothing happens until it is enumerated, which is why iterating a query twice re-reads the source and why modifying a collection mid-enumeration throws.","It comes in two surface syntaxes, the query syntax that reads like SQL and the method syntax using extension methods, and they compile to the same thing, so the choice is readability. Language Integrated Query is the C# feature that lets you write queries directly in the language against collections, databases and XML instead of moving between the data and a separate query engine. The core operations are from for the source, where for filtering, select for projection, orderby for sorting, then groupby, join and aggregate functions such as count and sum, and a query ends with an execution method like ToList or First that actually runs it. The deeper point is deferred execution: a query is a description of work, not the work itself, and nothing happens until it is enumerated, which is why iterating a query twice re-reads the source and why modifying a collection mid-enumeration throws."],"tl":"linq","bis":"linq"},{"id":"pro-csharp-000003","topic":"programming-csharp","intent":"definition","concept":"nullable reference type","keywords":["nullable reference type","nullable","annotation","warning","reference","types","feature","closes","oldest"],"patterns":["what is nullable reference type","nullable reference type","explain nullable reference type","definition of nullable reference type","nullable reference type meaning","what are nullable reference types","should i enable nullable","what is the difference between null and nullable","ano ang nullable reference type","unsa ang nullable reference type"],"answer":"Nullable reference types are a C# feature that closes one of the oldest holes in the language: it lets the compiler warn you when a reference that is supposed to hold a value is null. The declaration is an annotation, a question mark after the type, and the language then treats a variable of that type as non-nullable by default, so assigning null to it or dereferencing it without a check produces a warning rather than a runtime crash. Disabling the feature per project restores the older permissive behaviour, and the annotation is also how you document to a reader what is allowed to be absent. It is a static guarantee, not a runtime one, so the annotation is a claim you are making and the compiler checks the code against that claim. The common criticism is the long tail of warnings on older codebases, and the honest defence is that a null reference exception found in production is far more expensive than a few hundred warnings while you adopt it.","variations":["The declaration is an annotation, a question mark after the type, and the language then treats a variable of that type as non-nullable by default, so assigning null to it or dereferencing it without a check produces a warning rather than a runtime crash. Nullable reference types are a C# feature that closes one of the oldest holes in the language: it lets the compiler warn you when a reference that is supposed to hold a value is null. Disabling the feature per project restores the older permissive behaviour, and the annotation is also how you document to a reader what is allowed to be absent. It is a static guarantee, not a runtime one, so the annotation is a claim you are making and the compiler checks the code against that claim. The common criticism is the long tail of warnings on older codebases, and the honest defence is that a null reference exception found in production is far more expensive than a few hundred warnings while you adopt it.","Disabling the feature per project restores the older permissive behaviour, and the annotation is also how you document to a reader what is allowed to be absent. Nullable reference types are a C# feature that closes one of the oldest holes in the language: it lets the compiler warn you when a reference that is supposed to hold a value is null. The declaration is an annotation, a question mark after the type, and the language then treats a variable of that type as non-nullable by default, so assigning null to it or dereferencing it without a check produces a warning rather than a runtime crash. It is a static guarantee, not a runtime one, so the annotation is a claim you are making and the compiler checks the code against that claim. The common criticism is the long tail of warnings on older codebases, and the honest defence is that a null reference exception found in production is far more expensive than a few hundred warnings while you adopt it."],"tl":"nullable reference type","bis":"nullable reference type"},{"id":"pro-csharp-000004","topic":"programming-csharp","intent":"definition","concept":"value type","keywords":["value type","struct","boxing","copy","aliasing","primitive","value","type","variable","holds","data","itself"],"patterns":["what is value type","value type","explain value type","definition of value type","value type meaning","what is a value type","difference between value type and reference type","what is boxing","ano ang value type","unsa ang value type"],"answer":"A value type in C# is a type whose variable holds the data itself rather than a reference to data stored elsewhere, and the difference is visible in what happens when you assign one variable to another. Copying a value type copies the contents, so the two variables afterwards are independent. Copying a reference type copies the address, so both names point at the same object and a change through one is visible through the other. The built-in value types are the primitive numeric types, char, bool, decimal, and the struct keyword for your own, and a struct is a value type by default. Passing a large struct to a method copies it, which is a real cost in a hot loop, and boxing happens when a value type is stored in object or passed as an interface, which allocates and slows the operation down. The practical rule is to keep structs small and think carefully before making a class a struct, because the aliasing that surprises people in a reference type is exactly what they would want in a value type.","variations":["Copying a value type copies the contents, so the two variables afterwards are independent. A value type in C# is a type whose variable holds the data itself rather than a reference to data stored elsewhere, and the difference is visible in what happens when you assign one variable to another. Copying a reference type copies the address, so both names point at the same object and a change through one is visible through the other. The built-in value types are the primitive numeric types, char, bool, decimal, and the struct keyword for your own, and a struct is a value type by default. Passing a large struct to a method copies it, which is a real cost in a hot loop, and boxing happens when a value type is stored in object or passed as an interface, which allocates and slows the operation down. The practical rule is to keep structs small and think carefully before making a class a struct, because the aliasing that surprises people in a reference type is exactly what they would want in a value type.","Copying a reference type copies the address, so both names point at the same object and a change through one is visible through the other. A value type in C# is a type whose variable holds the data itself rather than a reference to data stored elsewhere, and the difference is visible in what happens when you assign one variable to another. Copying a value type copies the contents, so the two variables afterwards are independent. The built-in value types are the primitive numeric types, char, bool, decimal, and the struct keyword for your own, and a struct is a value type by default. Passing a large struct to a method copies it, which is a real cost in a hot loop, and boxing happens when a value type is stored in object or passed as an interface, which allocates and slows the operation down. The practical rule is to keep structs small and think carefully before making a class a struct, because the aliasing that surprises people in a reference type is exactly what they would want in a value type."],"tl":"value type","bis":"value type"},{"id":"pro-csharp-000005","topic":"programming-csharp","intent":"definition","concept":"dotnet","keywords":["dotnet","assembly","framework","platform","runtime","class","library","toolchain","compiles"],"patterns":["what is dotnet","dotnet","explain dotnet","definition of dotnet","dotnet meaning","is dotnet the same as c#","what is the dotnet framework","ano ang dotnet","unsa ang dotnet"],"answer":"The .NET platform is the runtime, class library and toolchain that C# compiles down to, and since its third generation it is cross-platform rather than tied to Windows. Compiling produces an intermediate assembly that the common language runtime loads and executes, so the same managed bytecode can run on Windows, Linux and macOS, which is what made cross-platform desktop and server applications practical without porting the codebase. The current generation is called .NET 5 and above, and it deliberately unifies what used to be several separate products such as .NET Framework and .NET Core onto one runtime. Alongside the runtime sit the base class library, the type system, and tooling such as the dotnet command line that builds, tests and publishes. The practical consequence for a developer is that the runtime version is a deployment target you choose, not a property of the machine.","variations":["Compiling produces an intermediate assembly that the common language runtime loads and executes, so the same managed bytecode can run on Windows, Linux and macOS, which is what made cross-platform desktop and server applications practical without porting the codebase. NET platform is the runtime, class library and toolchain that C# compiles down to, and since its third generation it is cross-platform rather than tied to Windows. NET Core onto one runtime. Alongside the runtime sit the base class library, the type system, and tooling such as the dotnet command line that builds, tests and publishes. The practical consequence for a developer is that the runtime version is a deployment target you choose, not a property of the machine.","NET Core onto one runtime. NET platform is the runtime, class library and toolchain that C# compiles down to, and since its third generation it is cross-platform rather than tied to Windows. Compiling produces an intermediate assembly that the common language runtime loads and executes, so the same managed bytecode can run on Windows, Linux and macOS, which is what made cross-platform desktop and server applications practical without porting the codebase. Alongside the runtime sit the base class library, the type system, and tooling such as the dotnet command line that builds, tests and publishes. The practical consequence for a developer is that the runtime version is a deployment target you choose, not a property of the machine."],"tl":"dotnet","bis":"dotnet"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"csharp","title":"C#","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/csharp.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-css","entries":[{"id":"pro-css-000001","topic":"programming-css","intent":"definition","concept":"css","keywords":["css","stylesheet","style","cascade","language","controls","page","looks","colour","spacing"],"patterns":["what is css","css","explain css","definition of css","css meaning"],"answer":"CSS is the language that controls how a page looks: colour, spacing, font, size, and layout. It is called a cascading style sheet because rules cascade - the most specific rule wins when several target the same element. HTML says what a thing is, CSS decides what it looks like.","variations":["It is called a cascading style sheet because rules cascade - the most specific rule wins when several target the same element. CSS is the language that controls how a page looks: colour, spacing, font, size, and layout. HTML says what a thing is, CSS decides what it looks like.","HTML says what a thing is, CSS decides what it looks like. CSS is the language that controls how a page looks: colour, spacing, font, size, and layout. It is called a cascading style sheet because rules cascade - the most specific rule wins when several target the same element."]},{"id":"pro-css-000002","topic":"programming-css","intent":"definition","concept":"responsive design","keywords":["responsive design","responsive","breakpoint","viewport","layout","design","adapts","rather"],"patterns":["what is responsive design","responsive design","explain responsive design","definition of responsive design","responsive design meaning","what is mobile first","how many breakpoints should i use","ano ang responsive","unsa ang responsive"],"answer":"Responsive design is a layout that adapts to the viewport rather than one fixed size that happens to fit. The modern way is mobile-first: write the single-column layout as the base and add breakpoints upward as space appears, because the narrow case is the hardest and the widest can only improve on it. Two content-driven breakpoints beat a set of device widths, because the layout should break where the content stops fitting rather than where a phone happens to end.","variations":["The modern way is mobile-first: write the single-column layout as the base and add breakpoints upward as space appears, because the narrow case is the hardest and the widest can only improve on it. Responsive design is a layout that adapts to the viewport rather than one fixed size that happens to fit. Two content-driven breakpoints beat a set of device widths, because the layout should break where the content stops fitting rather than where a phone happens to end.","Two content-driven breakpoints beat a set of device widths, because the layout should break where the content stops fitting rather than where a phone happens to end. Responsive design is a layout that adapts to the viewport rather than one fixed size that happens to fit. The modern way is mobile-first: write the single-column layout as the base and add breakpoints upward as space appears, because the narrow case is the hardest and the widest can only improve on it."],"tl":"responsive","bis":"responsive"},{"id":"pro-css-000003","topic":"programming-css","intent":"definition","concept":"media query","keywords":["media query","breakpoint","css","media","query","applies","condition","environment","true"],"patterns":["what is media query","media query","explain media query","definition of media query","media query meaning","what is a media query","how do I write a max width query","what is @supports","ano ang media query","unsa ang media query"],"answer":"A media query applies CSS only when a condition about the environment is true, most often a viewport width, and it is the mechanism behind every responsive layout. The modern range syntax reads as intent rather than arithmetic, so a condition between two values states the band directly instead of computing min-width minus one pixel, which is where the old off-by-one hacks came from. Feature queries test for support of a property rather than for a device, which is a more honest test.","variations":["The modern range syntax reads as intent rather than arithmetic, so a condition between two values states the band directly instead of computing min-width minus one pixel, which is where the old off-by-one hacks came from. A media query applies CSS only when a condition about the environment is true, most often a viewport width, and it is the mechanism behind every responsive layout. Feature queries test for support of a property rather than for a device, which is a more honest test.","Feature queries test for support of a property rather than for a device, which is a more honest test. A media query applies CSS only when a condition about the environment is true, most often a viewport width, and it is the mechanism behind every responsive layout. The modern range syntax reads as intent rather than arithmetic, so a condition between two values states the band directly instead of computing min-width minus one pixel, which is where the old off-by-one hacks came from."],"tl":"media query","bis":"media query"},{"id":"pro-css-000004","topic":"programming-css","intent":"definition","concept":"css specificity","keywords":["css specificity","specificity","cascade","important","selector","override","decides","wins","declarations","target"],"patterns":["what is css specificity","css specificity","explain css specificity","definition of css specificity","css specificity meaning","why is my css not applying","what does important do","ano ang specificity","unsa ang specificity"],"answer":"Specificity decides which selector wins when two declarations target the same element, and it is compared as a three-column number: ids, then classes and attributes and pseudo-classes, then elements and pseudo-elements. A single id outranks any number of classes, which is why selectors built from ids cannot be overridden without important, and why nested CSS is usually the better route. The rule is worth understanding rather than memorising, because every time the answer is important the real problem is a selector that was too strong.","variations":["A single id outranks any number of classes, which is why selectors built from ids cannot be overridden without important, and why nested CSS is usually the better route. Specificity decides which selector wins when two declarations target the same element, and it is compared as a three-column number: ids, then classes and attributes and pseudo-classes, then elements and pseudo-elements. The rule is worth understanding rather than memorising, because every time the answer is important the real problem is a selector that was too strong.","The rule is worth understanding rather than memorising, because every time the answer is important the real problem is a selector that was too strong. Specificity decides which selector wins when two declarations target the same element, and it is compared as a three-column number: ids, then classes and attributes and pseudo-classes, then elements and pseudo-elements. A single id outranks any number of classes, which is why selectors built from ids cannot be overridden without important, and why nested CSS is usually the better route."],"tl":"specificity","bis":"specificity"},{"id":"pro-css-000005","topic":"programming-css","intent":"definition","concept":"flexbox","keywords":["flexbox","flex","layout","lays","along","axis","right","tool"],"patterns":["what is flexbox","flexbox","explain flexbox","definition of flexbox","flexbox meaning","flex vs grid","how do i center with flexbox","ano ang flexbox","unsa ang flexbox"],"answer":"Flexbox lays out along one axis and is the right tool for a row or a column of things: a toolbar, a nav bar, a list of cards, a centred dialog. The three properties that matter most are the direction on the container, how leftover space is distributed by justify-content, and how items are sized on the cross axis by align-items. The reason it replaced float and inline-block hacks is that it was designed for this, so the result is a fraction of the markup and none of the clearing.","variations":["The three properties that matter most are the direction on the container, how leftover space is distributed by justify-content, and how items are sized on the cross axis by align-items. Flexbox lays out along one axis and is the right tool for a row or a column of things: a toolbar, a nav bar, a list of cards, a centred dialog. The reason it replaced float and inline-block hacks is that it was designed for this, so the result is a fraction of the markup and none of the clearing.","The reason it replaced float and inline-block hacks is that it was designed for this, so the result is a fraction of the markup and none of the clearing. Flexbox lays out along one axis and is the right tool for a row or a column of things: a toolbar, a nav bar, a list of cards, a centred dialog. The three properties that matter most are the direction on the container, how leftover space is distributed by justify-content, and how items are sized on the cross axis by align-items."],"tl":"flexbox","bis":"flexbox"},{"id":"pro-css-000006","topic":"programming-css","intent":"definition","concept":"css grid","keywords":["css grid","grid","gap","layout","lays","dimensions","once","makes","right"],"patterns":["what is css grid","css grid","explain css grid","definition of css grid","css grid meaning","grid vs flexbox when to use which","how do i make a card grid","ano ang grid","unsa ang grid"],"answer":"CSS Grid lays out in two dimensions at once, which makes it the right tool for a page shell, a card grid, or a form whose labels and fields must line up in columns. Tracks are defined with grid-template-columns and rows with grid-template-rows, and the gap property sets the space between tracks so no margin is needed. Grid areas let a whole layout be described by name in one rule, which is why page-level layout is now mostly grid while components are mostly flexbox.","variations":["Tracks are defined with grid-template-columns and rows with grid-template-rows, and the gap property sets the space between tracks so no margin is needed. CSS Grid lays out in two dimensions at once, which makes it the right tool for a page shell, a card grid, or a form whose labels and fields must line up in columns. Grid areas let a whole layout be described by name in one rule, which is why page-level layout is now mostly grid while components are mostly flexbox.","Grid areas let a whole layout be described by name in one rule, which is why page-level layout is now mostly grid while components are mostly flexbox. CSS Grid lays out in two dimensions at once, which makes it the right tool for a page shell, a card grid, or a form whose labels and fields must line up in columns. Tracks are defined with grid-template-columns and rows with grid-template-rows, and the gap property sets the space between tracks so no margin is needed."],"tl":"grid","bis":"grid"},{"id":"pro-css-000007","topic":"programming-css","intent":"definition","concept":"box model","keywords":["box model","padding","margin","border","sizing","model","describes","every","element","content"],"patterns":["what is box model","box model","explain box model","definition of box model","box model meaning","what is the box model","why is my element too wide","what does box sizing border box do","ano ang box model","unsa ang box model"],"answer":"The CSS box model describes every element as content, then padding around it, then border, then margin outside it, and content-box sizing means width applies only to the content. That is the single most common source of surprise, because an element declared fully wide with padding overflows its container. The fix is box-sizing set to border-box, which makes width include padding and border, and it is worth applying to everything once at the top of a stylesheet.","variations":["That is the single most common source of surprise, because an element declared fully wide with padding overflows its container. The CSS box model describes every element as content, then padding around it, then border, then margin outside it, and content-box sizing means width applies only to the content. The fix is box-sizing set to border-box, which makes width include padding and border, and it is worth applying to everything once at the top of a stylesheet.","The fix is box-sizing set to border-box, which makes width include padding and border, and it is worth applying to everything once at the top of a stylesheet. The CSS box model describes every element as content, then padding around it, then border, then margin outside it, and content-box sizing means width applies only to the content. That is the single most common source of surprise, because an element declared fully wide with padding overflows its container."],"tl":"box model","bis":"box model"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"css","title":"CSS","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/css.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-data-structures","entries":[{"id":"pro-data-structures-000001","topic":"programming-data-structures","intent":"definition","concept":"data structure","keywords":["data structure","array","tree","graph","tradeoff","lookup","insertion","data","structure","particular","arranging","memory","operations"],"patterns":["what is data structure","data structure","explain data structure","definition of data structure","data structure meaning","what is a data structure","which data structure should i use","why does structure matter","ano ang data structure","unsa ang data structure"],"answer":"A data structure is a particular way of arranging data in memory so that some operations become cheap and others become expensive, and choosing one is usually the decision that determines whether an algorithm is fast enough. The trade is between lookup, insertion, deletion and memory, and no structure wins all four: an array gives fast indexed access and slow middle insertion, a hash table gives fast lookup and no order at all, a tree gives ordered data with logarithmic cost, and a graph models connections at the price of expensive traversal. The structure also determines the data type in a typed language, since a stack and a queue are often built-in types rather than something you write yourself. What matters in practice is matching the structure to the dominant operation rather than to the data, because a structure chosen for reads in a write-heavy program is a poor trade even though its lookup time is excellent.","variations":["The trade is between lookup, insertion, deletion and memory, and no structure wins all four: an array gives fast indexed access and slow middle insertion, a hash table gives fast lookup and no order at all, a tree gives ordered data with logarithmic cost, and a graph models connections at the price of expensive traversal. A data structure is a particular way of arranging data in memory so that some operations become cheap and others become expensive, and choosing one is usually the decision that determines whether an algorithm is fast enough. The structure also determines the data type in a typed language, since a stack and a queue are often built-in types rather than something you write yourself. What matters in practice is matching the structure to the dominant operation rather than to the data, because a structure chosen for reads in a write-heavy program is a poor trade even though its lookup time is excellent.","The structure also determines the data type in a typed language, since a stack and a queue are often built-in types rather than something you write yourself. A data structure is a particular way of arranging data in memory so that some operations become cheap and others become expensive, and choosing one is usually the decision that determines whether an algorithm is fast enough. The trade is between lookup, insertion, deletion and memory, and no structure wins all four: an array gives fast indexed access and slow middle insertion, a hash table gives fast lookup and no order at all, a tree gives ordered data with logarithmic cost, and a graph models connections at the price of expensive traversal. What matters in practice is matching the structure to the dominant operation rather than to the data, because a structure chosen for reads in a write-heavy program is a poor trade even though its lookup time is excellent."],"tl":"data structure","bis":"data structure"},{"id":"pro-data-structures-000002","topic":"programming-data-structures","intent":"definition","concept":"stack","keywords":["stack","push","pop","undo","recursion","overflow","collection","remove","makes","last","first"],"patterns":["what is stack","stack","explain stack","definition of stack","stack meaning","what is a stack","stack vs queue","what causes a stack overflow","ano ang stack","unsa ang stack"],"answer":"A stack is a collection that only lets you add and remove at one end, the top, which makes it last in first out: the most recent item added is the first one you get back. That single restriction is the whole design, and it is what makes a stack the right model for anything where the most recent thing matters most, such as undo history, the call stack that tracks which function called which, the browser back button, and matching brackets in a parser. Adding is push and removing is pop, both constant time, and the related peek returns the top without removing it. The mistake people make is reaching for a stack when they actually need a queue, since both look like a list of pending work and produce a completely different order of processing. A stack that grows without bound is also the mechanism behind a stack overflow, when a recursive function calls itself before the base case returns.","variations":["That single restriction is the whole design, and it is what makes a stack the right model for anything where the most recent thing matters most, such as undo history, the call stack that tracks which function called which, the browser back button, and matching brackets in a parser. A stack is a collection that only lets you add and remove at one end, the top, which makes it last in first out: the most recent item added is the first one you get back. Adding is push and removing is pop, both constant time, and the related peek returns the top without removing it. The mistake people make is reaching for a stack when they actually need a queue, since both look like a list of pending work and produce a completely different order of processing. A stack that grows without bound is also the mechanism behind a stack overflow, when a recursive function calls itself before the base case returns.","Adding is push and removing is pop, both constant time, and the related peek returns the top without removing it. A stack is a collection that only lets you add and remove at one end, the top, which makes it last in first out: the most recent item added is the first one you get back. That single restriction is the whole design, and it is what makes a stack the right model for anything where the most recent thing matters most, such as undo history, the call stack that tracks which function called which, the browser back button, and matching brackets in a parser. The mistake people make is reaching for a stack when they actually need a queue, since both look like a list of pending work and produce a completely different order of processing. A stack that grows without bound is also the mechanism behind a stack overflow, when a recursive function calls itself before the base case returns."],"tl":"stack","bis":"stack"},{"id":"pro-data-structures-000003","topic":"programming-data-structures","intent":"definition","concept":"queue","keywords":["queue","enqueue","dequeue","circular","priority","collection","items","added","removed","makes"],"patterns":["what is queue","queue","explain queue","definition of queue","queue meaning","what is a queue","queue vs stack","what is a priority queue","ano ang queue","unsa ang queue"],"answer":"A queue is a collection where items are added at one end and removed from the other, which makes it first in first out: the first item that arrived is the first one served. That ordering is the point, and it is what a queue is for, so the right questions are about fairness and about what happens when the items being handled take different amounts of time. Breadth first search is the textbook example, because exploring a graph level by level and choosing a queue is what keeps it from wandering into a single deep branch. In a real server a queue also buffers a burst of arriving work so that a service processing one request at a time is not overwhelmed when a thousand arrive at once. The two known variations are a circular queue, which reuses the array positions freed at the front, and a priority queue, which ignores arrival order and serves the highest priority item first, which is implemented with a heap rather than a plain array.","variations":["That ordering is the point, and it is what a queue is for, so the right questions are about fairness and about what happens when the items being handled take different amounts of time. A queue is a collection where items are added at one end and removed from the other, which makes it first in first out: the first item that arrived is the first one served. Breadth first search is the textbook example, because exploring a graph level by level and choosing a queue is what keeps it from wandering into a single deep branch. In a real server a queue also buffers a burst of arriving work so that a service processing one request at a time is not overwhelmed when a thousand arrive at once. The two known variations are a circular queue, which reuses the array positions freed at the front, and a priority queue, which ignores arrival order and serves the highest priority item first, which is implemented with a heap rather than a plain array.","Breadth first search is the textbook example, because exploring a graph level by level and choosing a queue is what keeps it from wandering into a single deep branch. A queue is a collection where items are added at one end and removed from the other, which makes it first in first out: the first item that arrived is the first one served. That ordering is the point, and it is what a queue is for, so the right questions are about fairness and about what happens when the items being handled take different amounts of time. In a real server a queue also buffers a burst of arriving work so that a service processing one request at a time is not overwhelmed when a thousand arrive at once. The two known variations are a circular queue, which reuses the array positions freed at the front, and a priority queue, which ignores arrival order and serves the highest priority item first, which is implemented with a heap rather than a plain array."],"tl":"queue","bis":"queue"},{"id":"pro-data-structures-000004","topic":"programming-data-structures","intent":"definition","concept":"linked list","keywords":["linked list","node","contiguous","traversal","cache","insertion","linked","list","sequence","nodes","stores"],"patterns":["what is linked list","linked list","explain linked list","definition of linked list","linked list meaning","what is a linked list","linked list vs array","what is a doubly linked list","ano ang linked list","unsa ang linked list"],"answer":"A linked list is a sequence of nodes where each node stores a value and the address of the next node, rather than a single contiguous block of memory like an array. That layout is the whole trade: inserting or deleting at a known position is constant time because you just relink the neighbours, but finding the element at position n is linear because you have to walk the chain, and there is no random access. It also allocates one small block per node, which costs more memory and more cache misses than an array, so the array is faster in practice for almost every ordinary program. The variants exist to serve different access patterns: a singly linked list points one way, a doubly linked list adds a backward pointer so traversal runs both directions, and a circular list links the last node back to the first. The structure still matters in a language without a built-in dynamic array, and it is the right choice for a work queue where removal from the front is frequent.","variations":["That layout is the whole trade: inserting or deleting at a known position is constant time because you just relink the neighbours, but finding the element at position n is linear because you have to walk the chain, and there is no random access. A linked list is a sequence of nodes where each node stores a value and the address of the next node, rather than a single contiguous block of memory like an array. It also allocates one small block per node, which costs more memory and more cache misses than an array, so the array is faster in practice for almost every ordinary program. The variants exist to serve different access patterns: a singly linked list points one way, a doubly linked list adds a backward pointer so traversal runs both directions, and a circular list links the last node back to the first. The structure still matters in a language without a built-in dynamic array, and it is the right choice for a work queue where removal from the front is frequent.","It also allocates one small block per node, which costs more memory and more cache misses than an array, so the array is faster in practice for almost every ordinary program. A linked list is a sequence of nodes where each node stores a value and the address of the next node, rather than a single contiguous block of memory like an array. That layout is the whole trade: inserting or deleting at a known position is constant time because you just relink the neighbours, but finding the element at position n is linear because you have to walk the chain, and there is no random access. The variants exist to serve different access patterns: a singly linked list points one way, a doubly linked list adds a backward pointer so traversal runs both directions, and a circular list links the last node back to the first. The structure still matters in a language without a built-in dynamic array, and it is the right choice for a work queue where removal from the front is frequent."],"tl":"linked list","bis":"linked list"},{"id":"pro-data-structures-000005","topic":"programming-data-structures","intent":"definition","concept":"hash table","keywords":["hash table","bucket","collision","rehashing","chaining","hash","table","stores","value","pairs","looks"],"patterns":["what is hash table","hash table","explain hash table","definition of hash table","hash table meaning","what is a hash table","how does hashing work","what is a hash collision","ano ang hash table","unsa ang hash table"],"answer":"A hash table stores key and value pairs and looks an entry up in roughly constant time by running the key through a hash function that turns it into an index into an array of buckets. If two keys land in the same bucket that is a collision, and it is resolved either by chaining, where each bucket holds a list, or by open addressing, where the algorithm probes for the next free slot. The average case is fast, and the whole subject of database index design is how to keep it that way, because the table degrades sharply when every key hashes to the same bucket. A load factor above roughly 0.7 makes collisions common enough to matter, so implementations grow the table and rehash everything when the ratio passes a threshold. The interface is simple, the ordering is not, so iterating a hash table gives no useful order, and any algorithm that needs sorted output has to sort the keys separately.","variations":["If two keys land in the same bucket that is a collision, and it is resolved either by chaining, where each bucket holds a list, or by open addressing, where the algorithm probes for the next free slot. A hash table stores key and value pairs and looks an entry up in roughly constant time by running the key through a hash function that turns it into an index into an array of buckets. The average case is fast, and the whole subject of database index design is how to keep it that way, because the table degrades sharply when every key hashes to the same bucket. 7 makes collisions common enough to matter, so implementations grow the table and rehash everything when the ratio passes a threshold. The interface is simple, the ordering is not, so iterating a hash table gives no useful order, and any algorithm that needs sorted output has to sort the keys separately.","The average case is fast, and the whole subject of database index design is how to keep it that way, because the table degrades sharply when every key hashes to the same bucket. A hash table stores key and value pairs and looks an entry up in roughly constant time by running the key through a hash function that turns it into an index into an array of buckets. If two keys land in the same bucket that is a collision, and it is resolved either by chaining, where each bucket holds a list, or by open addressing, where the algorithm probes for the next free slot. 7 makes collisions common enough to matter, so implementations grow the table and rehash everything when the ratio passes a threshold. The interface is simple, the ordering is not, so iterating a hash table gives no useful order, and any algorithm that needs sorted output has to sort the keys separately."],"tl":"hash table","bis":"hash table"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"data-structures","title":"Data structures","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/data-structures.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-debugging","entries":[{"id":"pro-debugging-000001","topic":"programming-debugging","intent":"howto","concept":"bug","keywords":["bug","error","debug","fix","broken","crash","reliable","first","reproduce","reliably","cannot","trigger"],"patterns":["what is bug","bug","explain bug","definition of bug","bug meaning","not working","how do i fix a bug","debugging","my code does not work"],"answer":"A reliable way to find a bug: first reproduce it reliably, because a bug you cannot trigger on demand is a bug you cannot confirm you fixed. Then read the actual error message rather than skimming it - it usually names the line. Then bisect: remove half the code until the problem disappears. Finally change one thing at a time. Adding print statements or a debugger breakpoint beats guessing.","variations":["Then read the actual error message rather than skimming it - it usually names the line. A reliable way to find a bug: first reproduce it reliably, because a bug you cannot trigger on demand is a bug you cannot confirm you fixed. Then bisect: remove half the code until the problem disappears. Finally change one thing at a time. Adding print statements or a debugger breakpoint beats guessing.","Then bisect: remove half the code until the problem disappears. A reliable way to find a bug: first reproduce it reliably, because a bug you cannot trigger on demand is a bug you cannot confirm you fixed. Then read the actual error message rather than skimming it - it usually names the line. Finally change one thing at a time. Adding print statements or a debugger breakpoint beats guessing."]},{"id":"pro-debugging-000002","topic":"programming-debugging","intent":"definition","concept":"testing","keywords":["testing","integration","regression","means","checking","code","useful","division"],"patterns":["what is testing","testing","explain testing","definition of testing","testing meaning","what is unit testing","what is regression testing","how much test coverage is enough","ano ang testing","unsa ang testing"],"answer":"Testing means checking that code does what it should, and the useful division is by what you are checking for. A unit test checks one piece in isolation and is fast, an integration test checks that pieces work together, and an end-to-end test drives the real thing as a user would. The value is not catching every bug, because tests never do, but in making a change safe, since a failing test names the behaviour that broke.","variations":["A unit test checks one piece in isolation and is fast, an integration test checks that pieces work together, and an end-to-end test drives the real thing as a user would. Testing means checking that code does what it should, and the useful division is by what you are checking for. The value is not catching every bug, because tests never do, but in making a change safe, since a failing test names the behaviour that broke.","The value is not catching every bug, because tests never do, but in making a change safe, since a failing test names the behaviour that broke. Testing means checking that code does what it should, and the useful division is by what you are checking for. A unit test checks one piece in isolation and is fast, an integration test checks that pieces work together, and an end-to-end test drives the real thing as a user would."],"tl":"testing","bis":"testing"},{"id":"pro-debugging-000003","topic":"programming-debugging","intent":"definition","concept":"debugging","keywords":["debugging","breakpoint","reproduce","bisect","print","log","finding","code","expected","hard","expectation"],"patterns":["what is debugging","debugging","explain debugging","definition of debugging","debugging meaning","how do i debug code","what is a breakpoint","why use logs instead of a debugger","ano ang debugging","unsa ang debugging"],"answer":"Debugging is finding out why code does not do what you expected, and the hard part is usually that the expectation was wrong rather than the code. The reliable method is to narrow it: reproduce it, bisect by commenting things out, and check the actual value at the point of failure rather than reasoning about what it should be. Print statements beat a debugger when the bug is intermittent, because a debugger changes the timing that caused it.","variations":["The reliable method is to narrow it: reproduce it, bisect by commenting things out, and check the actual value at the point of failure rather than reasoning about what it should be. Debugging is finding out why code does not do what you expected, and the hard part is usually that the expectation was wrong rather than the code. Print statements beat a debugger when the bug is intermittent, because a debugger changes the timing that caused it.","Print statements beat a debugger when the bug is intermittent, because a debugger changes the timing that caused it. Debugging is finding out why code does not do what you expected, and the hard part is usually that the expectation was wrong rather than the code. The reliable method is to narrow it: reproduce it, bisect by commenting things out, and check the actual value at the point of failure rather than reasoning about what it should be."],"tl":"debugging","bis":"debugging"},{"id":"pro-debugging-000004","topic":"programming-debugging","intent":"definition","concept":"logging","keywords":["logging","error","warning","info","structured","search","debug","output","log","attaching","explanatory","text","program","letting"],"patterns":["what is logging","logging","explain logging","definition of logging","logging meaning","how to log properly","difference between logging and debugging","ano ang log","unsa ang logging"],"answer":"Logging is attaching explanatory text to a program and letting it be written out while the program runs, and the alternative is relying on a debugger, which is faster for a single mistake and useless for a program running on a machine you cannot reach. The three levels matter and are usually named error, warning and info, and the discipline is deciding which one a message is: an error is something that broke, a warning is something suspicious that did not, and info is ordinary progress. The common beginner mistake is logging everything at one level, so a real problem is buried in noise, or conversely labelling every message an error so nobody reads them. Machine-readable structured output, where each line is a key and value rather than a sentence, is what makes logs searchable at scale, and it is the difference between finding one user's failure in a million requests and not. A related decision is how much personal data to put in a log, since logs are copied around and retained far longer than anyone intends.","variations":["The three levels matter and are usually named error, warning and info, and the discipline is deciding which one a message is: an error is something that broke, a warning is something suspicious that did not, and info is ordinary progress. Logging is attaching explanatory text to a program and letting it be written out while the program runs, and the alternative is relying on a debugger, which is faster for a single mistake and useless for a program running on a machine you cannot reach. The common beginner mistake is logging everything at one level, so a real problem is buried in noise, or conversely labelling every message an error so nobody reads them. Machine-readable structured output, where each line is a key and value rather than a sentence, is what makes logs searchable at scale, and it is the difference between finding one user's failure in a million requests and not. A related decision is how much personal data to put in a log, since logs are copied around and retained far longer than anyone intends.","The common beginner mistake is logging everything at one level, so a real problem is buried in noise, or conversely labelling every message an error so nobody reads them. Logging is attaching explanatory text to a program and letting it be written out while the program runs, and the alternative is relying on a debugger, which is faster for a single mistake and useless for a program running on a machine you cannot reach. The three levels matter and are usually named error, warning and info, and the discipline is deciding which one a message is: an error is something that broke, a warning is something suspicious that did not, and info is ordinary progress. Machine-readable structured output, where each line is a key and value rather than a sentence, is what makes logs searchable at scale, and it is the difference between finding one user's failure in a million requests and not. A related decision is how much personal data to put in a log, since logs are copied around and retained far longer than anyone intends."],"tl":"log","bis":"logging"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"debugging","title":"Debugging","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/debugging.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-everyday-why","entries":[{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000001","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"dreaming","keywords":["dreaming","sleep","brain","memory","rem","image","paralysis","meaning","panaginip","happens","particular","stage"],"patterns":["what is dreaming","dreaming","explain dreaming","definition of dreaming","dreaming meaning","why do we dream","what is a dream","bakit nga nangidream","ano ang panaginip","unsa ang panaginip"],"answer":"Dreaming happens during a particular stage of sleep in which the brain is active and the body is nearly paralysed, and what it is doing is not nothing. The most defensible account is that it is processing, mixing recent experience with existing memory, which is why a dream is usually familiar rather than strange, and why a person who lost a limb in a car crash may dream of it for years. The imagery comes from the same visual processing that waking perception uses, which is why the brain dreams in pictures rather than in words even for people who think in words. The paralysis is a real feature rather than a fault, and it stops the body acting out a dream. Whether the content carries meaning is the part nobody agrees on: a remembered dream can be tied to something going on, but the reliability of that link is low, and a dream is a poor basis for a decision.","variations":["The most defensible account is that it is processing, mixing recent experience with existing memory, which is why a dream is usually familiar rather than strange, and why a person who lost a limb in a car crash may dream of it for years. Dreaming happens during a particular stage of sleep in which the brain is active and the body is nearly paralysed, and what it is doing is not nothing. The imagery comes from the same visual processing that waking perception uses, which is why the brain dreams in pictures rather than in words even for people who think in words. The paralysis is a real feature rather than a fault, and it stops the body acting out a dream. Whether the content carries meaning is the part nobody agrees on: a remembered dream can be tied to something going on, but the reliability of that link is low, and a dream is a poor basis for a decision.","The imagery comes from the same visual processing that waking perception uses, which is why the brain dreams in pictures rather than in words even for people who think in words. Dreaming happens during a particular stage of sleep in which the brain is active and the body is nearly paralysed, and what it is doing is not nothing. The most defensible account is that it is processing, mixing recent experience with existing memory, which is why a dream is usually familiar rather than strange, and why a person who lost a limb in a car crash may dream of it for years. The paralysis is a real feature rather than a fault, and it stops the body acting out a dream. Whether the content carries meaning is the part nobody agrees on: a remembered dream can be tied to something going on, but the reliability of that link is low, and a dream is a poor basis for a decision."],"tl":"panaginip","bis":"panaginip"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000002","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"yawning","keywords":["yawning","oxygen","brain","cool","contagious","mirror","reflex","tired","bobo","deepens","intake","cools"],"patterns":["what is yawning","yawning","explain yawning","definition of yawning","yawning meaning","why do we yawn","why is yawning contagious","bakit nga bobo ako","ano ang bobo","unsa ang bobo"],"answer":"Yawning is the reflex that deepens the intake of oxygen and cools the brain, and the cooling part is the more interesting of the two explanations. The brain generates a lot of heat while thinking, and a yawn draws in a large volume of cooler air, which the blood carries away, and the drop is measurable at the surface of the brain. The other proposed function is arousal: yawning clusters around transitions, so it appears before a stressful or boring task and may be a way of shifting attention. The reason yawning is contagious is that it is one of the few things people mirror automatically, along with flinching, and the mechanism is thought to be a cue for the mirror system rather than a shared feeling. It says more about physiology than about tiredness in any individual case, which is why yawning in a meeting is not reliable evidence that anyone is bored.","variations":["The brain generates a lot of heat while thinking, and a yawn draws in a large volume of cooler air, which the blood carries away, and the drop is measurable at the surface of the brain. Yawning is the reflex that deepens the intake of oxygen and cools the brain, and the cooling part is the more interesting of the two explanations. The other proposed function is arousal: yawning clusters around transitions, so it appears before a stressful or boring task and may be a way of shifting attention. The reason yawning is contagious is that it is one of the few things people mirror automatically, along with flinching, and the mechanism is thought to be a cue for the mirror system rather than a shared feeling. It says more about physiology than about tiredness in any individual case, which is why yawning in a meeting is not reliable evidence that anyone is bored.","The other proposed function is arousal: yawning clusters around transitions, so it appears before a stressful or boring task and may be a way of shifting attention. Yawning is the reflex that deepens the intake of oxygen and cools the brain, and the cooling part is the more interesting of the two explanations. The brain generates a lot of heat while thinking, and a yawn draws in a large volume of cooler air, which the blood carries away, and the drop is measurable at the surface of the brain. The reason yawning is contagious is that it is one of the few things people mirror automatically, along with flinching, and the mechanism is thought to be a cue for the mirror system rather than a shared feeling. It says more about physiology than about tiredness in any individual case, which is why yawning in a meeting is not reliable evidence that anyone is bored."],"tl":"bobo","bis":"bobo"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000003","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"blinking","keywords":["blinking","eye","tears","wet","dust","cornea","reflex","dry","mata","keeps","surface","clear","several","jobs"],"patterns":["what is blinking","blinking","explain blinking","definition of blinking","blinking meaning","why do we blink","why are my eyes tired","bakit nga mamiya ako","ano ang mata","unsa ang mata"],"answer":"Blinking keeps the surface of the eye wet and clear, and it does several jobs at once, which is why it is hard to go long without blinking. The eyelids spread tears across the cornea so it stays moist, and that moisture is what the surface cells need in order to survive rather than drying out. They also sweep away dust and grit, which is the part people notice when the eye feels gritty. The reflex is not voluntary in the way a deliberate blink is, and it is driven by drying as much as by anything else, so a screen and a dry room both raise the rate without the person noticing. The average person blinks perhaps ten to twenty times a minute, and the rate falls sharply when the eyes are focused on a screen, which is one reason a long day of screen work leaves the eyes tired rather than strained in any muscular sense.","variations":["The eyelids spread tears across the cornea so it stays moist, and that moisture is what the surface cells need in order to survive rather than drying out. Blinking keeps the surface of the eye wet and clear, and it does several jobs at once, which is why it is hard to go long without blinking. They also sweep away dust and grit, which is the part people notice when the eye feels gritty. The reflex is not voluntary in the way a deliberate blink is, and it is driven by drying as much as by anything else, so a screen and a dry room both raise the rate without the person noticing. The average person blinks perhaps ten to twenty times a minute, and the rate falls sharply when the eyes are focused on a screen, which is one reason a long day of screen work leaves the eyes tired rather than strained in any muscular sense.","They also sweep away dust and grit, which is the part people notice when the eye feels gritty. Blinking keeps the surface of the eye wet and clear, and it does several jobs at once, which is why it is hard to go long without blinking. The eyelids spread tears across the cornea so it stays moist, and that moisture is what the surface cells need in order to survive rather than drying out. The reflex is not voluntary in the way a deliberate blink is, and it is driven by drying as much as by anything else, so a screen and a dry room both raise the rate without the person noticing. The average person blinks perhaps ten to twenty times a minute, and the rate falls sharply when the eyes are focused on a screen, which is one reason a long day of screen work leaves the eyes tired rather than strained in any muscular sense."],"tl":"mata","bis":"mata"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000004","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"magnetism","keywords":["magnetism","magnet","iron","field","pole","attract","compass","electron","attracts","magnetic","around"],"patterns":["what is magnetism","magnetism","explain magnetism","definition of magnetism","magnetism meaning","how do magnets work","why does a magnet attract iron","bakit nga humahawak ang magnet","ano ang magnet","unsa ang magnet"],"answer":"A magnet attracts iron because the magnetic field around it is produced by the motion of electric charge, and that is the whole mechanism. In an electron, moving charge means spinning, and the spin of a group of electrons in a material lines up with an external field, so the material develops its own field in the same direction, and a field in the same direction as the original attracts. This is why a magnet attracts iron, nickel and cobalt and does nothing to most other materials, which have their electron spins paired and therefore no net field. The two poles are a feature of the field rather than a pair of separate things, and cutting a magnet in half produces two smaller magnets each with both poles, which is the clearest demonstration that the pole is part of the field shape. The Earth itself has a field from the iron in its core, which is what makes a compass work and which also deflects most of the solar wind.","variations":["In an electron, moving charge means spinning, and the spin of a group of electrons in a material lines up with an external field, so the material develops its own field in the same direction, and a field in the same direction as the original attracts. A magnet attracts iron because the magnetic field around it is produced by the motion of electric charge, and that is the whole mechanism. This is why a magnet attracts iron, nickel and cobalt and does nothing to most other materials, which have their electron spins paired and therefore no net field. The two poles are a feature of the field rather than a pair of separate things, and cutting a magnet in half produces two smaller magnets each with both poles, which is the clearest demonstration that the pole is part of the field shape. The Earth itself has a field from the iron in its core, which is what makes a compass work and which also deflects most of the solar wind.","This is why a magnet attracts iron, nickel and cobalt and does nothing to most other materials, which have their electron spins paired and therefore no net field. A magnet attracts iron because the magnetic field around it is produced by the motion of electric charge, and that is the whole mechanism. In an electron, moving charge means spinning, and the spin of a group of electrons in a material lines up with an external field, so the material develops its own field in the same direction, and a field in the same direction as the original attracts. The two poles are a feature of the field rather than a pair of separate things, and cutting a magnet in half produces two smaller magnets each with both poles, which is the clearest demonstration that the pole is part of the field shape. The Earth itself has a field from the iron in its core, which is what makes a compass work and which also deflects most of the solar wind."],"tl":"magnet","bis":"magnet"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000005","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"fire hot","keywords":["fire hot","combustion","oxid","fuel","oxygen","reaction","energy","flame","sunog","fire","chemical","releases"],"patterns":["what is fire hot","fire hot","explain fire hot","definition of fire hot","fire hot meaning","why is fire hot","what is combustion","bakit nga init ang sunog","ano ang sunog","unsa ang sunog"],"answer":"Fire is hot because combustion is a chemical reaction that releases energy, and the reason the energy appears as heat is that the reaction happens faster than the heat can leave. Fire is a sustained oxidation, meaning fuel combining with oxygen, and the arrangement that matters is a large surface meeting the air over a small area so that heat goes into the fuel rather than into the air. That is the whole reason a candle works and a lump of coal does not: the wick has a high surface to volume ratio and keeps the fuel in contact with fresh air at the point where it is hottest. The heat is the energy of the chemical bonds being rearranged, and the visible flame is light from the excited gas, not the heat at all. A flame is also usefully self-limiting, since it consumes the fuel and the oxygen in its own vicinity and goes out when either runs out.","variations":["Fire is a sustained oxidation, meaning fuel combining with oxygen, and the arrangement that matters is a large surface meeting the air over a small area so that heat goes into the fuel rather than into the air. Fire is hot because combustion is a chemical reaction that releases energy, and the reason the energy appears as heat is that the reaction happens faster than the heat can leave. That is the whole reason a candle works and a lump of coal does not: the wick has a high surface to volume ratio and keeps the fuel in contact with fresh air at the point where it is hottest. The heat is the energy of the chemical bonds being rearranged, and the visible flame is light from the excited gas, not the heat at all. A flame is also usefully self-limiting, since it consumes the fuel and the oxygen in its own vicinity and goes out when either runs out.","That is the whole reason a candle works and a lump of coal does not: the wick has a high surface to volume ratio and keeps the fuel in contact with fresh air at the point where it is hottest. Fire is hot because combustion is a chemical reaction that releases energy, and the reason the energy appears as heat is that the reaction happens faster than the heat can leave. Fire is a sustained oxidation, meaning fuel combining with oxygen, and the arrangement that matters is a large surface meeting the air over a small area so that heat goes into the fuel rather than into the air. The heat is the energy of the chemical bonds being rearranged, and the visible flame is light from the excited gas, not the heat at all. A flame is also usefully self-limiting, since it consumes the fuel and the oxygen in its own vicinity and goes out when either runs out."],"tl":"sunog","bis":"sunog"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000006","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"green grass","keywords":["green grass","chlorophyll","absorb","reflect","light","leaf","yellow","photosynthesis","damo","grass","green","built"],"patterns":["what is green grass","green grass","explain green grass","definition of green grass","green grass meaning","why is grass green","why are leaves green","bakit nga green ang damo","ano ang damo","unsa ang damo"],"answer":"Grass is green because chlorophyll is built to absorb light rather than to look green, which is the whole explanation and the thing that surprises people. A green pigment looks green because it reflects green light back and takes the red and blue instead, and that arrangement is a chemical solution to a physical problem: a plant has to catch the energy in sunlight, and the most efficient way is to absorb as much of it as possible. It absorbs best in the blue and red ends of the spectrum, which is where sunlight carries most of its power, and what is left over to be reflected is the middle. The colour is not decorative at all, and the argument runs the other way too: a plant with no chlorophyll would be starved of energy, which is why the leaves of a plant deprived of light turn yellow.","variations":["A green pigment looks green because it reflects green light back and takes the red and blue instead, and that arrangement is a chemical solution to a physical problem: a plant has to catch the energy in sunlight, and the most efficient way is to absorb as much of it as possible. Grass is green because chlorophyll is built to absorb light rather than to look green, which is the whole explanation and the thing that surprises people. It absorbs best in the blue and red ends of the spectrum, which is where sunlight carries most of its power, and what is left over to be reflected is the middle. The colour is not decorative at all, and the argument runs the other way too: a plant with no chlorophyll would be starved of energy, which is why the leaves of a plant deprived of light turn yellow.","It absorbs best in the blue and red ends of the spectrum, which is where sunlight carries most of its power, and what is left over to be reflected is the middle. Grass is green because chlorophyll is built to absorb light rather than to look green, which is the whole explanation and the thing that surprises people. A green pigment looks green because it reflects green light back and takes the red and blue instead, and that arrangement is a chemical solution to a physical problem: a plant has to catch the energy in sunlight, and the most efficient way is to absorb as much of it as possible. The colour is not decorative at all, and the argument runs the other way too: a plant with no chlorophyll would be starved of energy, which is why the leaves of a plant deprived of light turn yellow."],"tl":"damo","bis":"damo"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000007","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"moon round","keywords":["moon round","phase","orbit","sphere","full","quarter","light","revolve","round","bulan","moon","looks","surprises","people","time"],"patterns":["what is moon round","moon round","explain moon round","definition of moon round","moon round meaning","why is the moon round","what are the moon phases","why does the moon look full","ano ang bulan","unsa ang bulan"],"answer":"The Moon looks round because it is round, and the part that surprises people is that it is round all the time. What changes is how much of the lit half faces the Earth, and the phases are a viewing geometry rather than anything happening to the Moon. At new Moon the lit side faces away from us, at first quarter we see half, at full the lit side faces us directly, and at last quarter the other half. Because the Moon orbits once every 27 days while Earth travels round the Sun, the cycle from one full Moon to the next is about 29 and a half days, which is why the phases do not line up exactly with a calendar month. The shape is a sphere rather than a disc because it formed from the same material as the rest of the solar system, and the near side keeps facing us because the Moon rotates once per orbit, which is a coincidence worth noticing and not a requirement of the physics.","variations":["What changes is how much of the lit half faces the Earth, and the phases are a viewing geometry rather than anything happening to the Moon. The Moon looks round because it is round, and the part that surprises people is that it is round all the time. At new Moon the lit side faces away from us, at first quarter we see half, at full the lit side faces us directly, and at last quarter the other half. Because the Moon orbits once every 27 days while Earth travels round the Sun, the cycle from one full Moon to the next is about 29 and a half days, which is why the phases do not line up exactly with a calendar month. The shape is a sphere rather than a disc because it formed from the same material as the rest of the solar system, and the near side keeps facing us because the Moon rotates once per orbit, which is a coincidence worth noticing and not a requirement of the physics.","At new Moon the lit side faces away from us, at first quarter we see half, at full the lit side faces us directly, and at last quarter the other half. The Moon looks round because it is round, and the part that surprises people is that it is round all the time. What changes is how much of the lit half faces the Earth, and the phases are a viewing geometry rather than anything happening to the Moon. Because the Moon orbits once every 27 days while Earth travels round the Sun, the cycle from one full Moon to the next is about 29 and a half days, which is why the phases do not line up exactly with a calendar month. The shape is a sphere rather than a disc because it formed from the same material as the rest of the solar system, and the near side keeps facing us because the Moon rotates once per orbit, which is a coincidence worth noticing and not a requirement of the physics."],"tl":"bulan","bis":"bulan"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000008","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"leaf colour change","keywords":["leaf colour change","autumn","fall","chlorophyll","carotenoid","anthocyanin","yellow","red","season","dahon","leaves","change","colour","green","pigment"],"patterns":["what is leaf colour change","leaf colour change","explain leaf colour change","definition of leaf colour change","leaf colour change meaning","why do leaves change colour","why do leaves turn yellow","why do leaves fall","ano ang dahon","unsa ang dahon"],"answer":"Leaves change colour because chlorophyll, the green pigment that does the work of catching sunlight, breaks down as the light shortens, and what colour a leaf turns reveals what was in it underneath the whole time. The yellow is carotenoids, which are present all year and simply hidden by the green. The red and purple are anthocyanins, which are made fresh in autumn rather than uncovered, so that colour is a new substance rather than an old one showing through. The reason trees spend the energy on them is that they are protective: they shield the leaf from the cold and the bright light of a low sun, which is a mild hazard in winter, and they are part of what the tree does before dropping the leaf. The timing is triggered by falling temperature and shortening day length rather than by cold itself, which is why a tree in the tropics may not change colour much at all.","variations":["The yellow is carotenoids, which are present all year and simply hidden by the green. Leaves change colour because chlorophyll, the green pigment that does the work of catching sunlight, breaks down as the light shortens, and what colour a leaf turns reveals what was in it underneath the whole time. The red and purple are anthocyanins, which are made fresh in autumn rather than uncovered, so that colour is a new substance rather than an old one showing through. The reason trees spend the energy on them is that they are protective: they shield the leaf from the cold and the bright light of a low sun, which is a mild hazard in winter, and they are part of what the tree does before dropping the leaf. The timing is triggered by falling temperature and shortening day length rather than by cold itself, which is why a tree in the tropics may not change colour much at all.","The red and purple are anthocyanins, which are made fresh in autumn rather than uncovered, so that colour is a new substance rather than an old one showing through. Leaves change colour because chlorophyll, the green pigment that does the work of catching sunlight, breaks down as the light shortens, and what colour a leaf turns reveals what was in it underneath the whole time. The yellow is carotenoids, which are present all year and simply hidden by the green. The reason trees spend the energy on them is that they are protective: they shield the leaf from the cold and the bright light of a low sun, which is a mild hazard in winter, and they are part of what the tree does before dropping the leaf. The timing is triggered by falling temperature and shortening day length rather than by cold itself, which is why a tree in the tropics may not change colour much at all."],"tl":"dahon","bis":"dahon"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000009","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"salty sea","keywords":["salty sea","salt","sodium","river","weathering","mineral","evaporate","ocean","asin","salty","rivers","washing","dissolved","minerals","land"],"patterns":["what is salty sea","salty sea","explain salty sea","definition of salty sea","salty sea meaning","why is the sea salty","why is salt in the ocean","bakit nga asin ang dagat","ano ang asin","unsa ang asin"],"answer":"The sea is salty because rivers have been washing dissolved minerals off the land and into the ocean for billions of years, and the water leaves but the salt stays. Rain falls on rock, picks up sodium, calcium, magnesium and other ions in a process called weathering, and carries them downhill into the sea. Water then evaporates back into the sky and falls again as rain, and the cycle repeats, so the ocean is a basin that has been accumulating for a very long time. Salt does not leave the way water does, because it is not volatile, so the concentration has built up until it reaches an equilibrium between what arrives and what is taken out in shells and sea spray. That is also why the sea is salty rather than a unique accident, and why a lake that drains periodically is fresh while the ocean, which does not, is not.","variations":["Rain falls on rock, picks up sodium, calcium, magnesium and other ions in a process called weathering, and carries them downhill into the sea. The sea is salty because rivers have been washing dissolved minerals off the land and into the ocean for billions of years, and the water leaves but the salt stays. Water then evaporates back into the sky and falls again as rain, and the cycle repeats, so the ocean is a basin that has been accumulating for a very long time. Salt does not leave the way water does, because it is not volatile, so the concentration has built up until it reaches an equilibrium between what arrives and what is taken out in shells and sea spray. That is also why the sea is salty rather than a unique accident, and why a lake that drains periodically is fresh while the ocean, which does not, is not.","Water then evaporates back into the sky and falls again as rain, and the cycle repeats, so the ocean is a basin that has been accumulating for a very long time. The sea is salty because rivers have been washing dissolved minerals off the land and into the ocean for billions of years, and the water leaves but the salt stays. Rain falls on rock, picks up sodium, calcium, magnesium and other ions in a process called weathering, and carries them downhill into the sea. Salt does not leave the way water does, because it is not volatile, so the concentration has built up until it reaches an equilibrium between what arrives and what is taken out in shells and sea spray. That is also why the sea is salty rather than a unique accident, and why a lake that drains periodically is fresh while the ocean, which does not, is not."],"tl":"asin","bis":"asin"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000010","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"volcano","keywords":["volcano","magma","lava","pressure","gas","crater","ash","erup","bulkan","erupts","rock","inside","molten"],"patterns":["what is volcano","volcano","explain volcano","definition of volcano","volcano meaning","why do volcanoes erupt","what is a volcano","how do volcanoes erupt","ano ang bulkan","unsa ang bulkan"],"answer":"A volcano erupts because the rock inside it is molten and under pressure, and the pressure is the part that is not obvious. Magma rises because it is less dense than the rock above it, and the gases dissolved in it come out of solution as it decompresses, which is what makes the gas expand. A rising column can then be trapped under a solid cap, so pressure builds until it exceeds the strength of the rock and the rock gives way in the least resistant direction available. This is why eruptions are usually preceded by small quakes as the magma moves, and why a volcano that has been quiet may be the one most worth watching. The two major types are different for a reason: a stratovolcano alternates layers of erupted material and has the viscous magma that traps gas and builds pressure, while a shield volcano runs mostly fluid basalt, spreads out and rarely produces a violent blast.","variations":["Magma rises because it is less dense than the rock above it, and the gases dissolved in it come out of solution as it decompresses, which is what makes the gas expand. A volcano erupts because the rock inside it is molten and under pressure, and the pressure is the part that is not obvious. A rising column can then be trapped under a solid cap, so pressure builds until it exceeds the strength of the rock and the rock gives way in the least resistant direction available. This is why eruptions are usually preceded by small quakes as the magma moves, and why a volcano that has been quiet may be the one most worth watching. The two major types are different for a reason: a stratovolcano alternates layers of erupted material and has the viscous magma that traps gas and builds pressure, while a shield volcano runs mostly fluid basalt, spreads out and rarely produces a violent blast.","A rising column can then be trapped under a solid cap, so pressure builds until it exceeds the strength of the rock and the rock gives way in the least resistant direction available. A volcano erupts because the rock inside it is molten and under pressure, and the pressure is the part that is not obvious. Magma rises because it is less dense than the rock above it, and the gases dissolved in it come out of solution as it decompresses, which is what makes the gas expand. This is why eruptions are usually preceded by small quakes as the magma moves, and why a volcano that has been quiet may be the one most worth watching. The two major types are different for a reason: a stratovolcano alternates layers of erupted material and has the viscous magma that traps gas and builds pressure, while a shield volcano runs mostly fluid basalt, spreads out and rarely produces a violent blast."],"tl":"bulkan","bis":"bulkan"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000011","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"ice slippery","keywords":["ice slippery","melt","film","water","surface","skate","friction","pressure","yelo","slippery","thin","forms","above"],"patterns":["what is ice slippery","ice slippery","explain ice slippery","definition of ice slippery","ice slippery meaning","why is ice slippery","is ice slippery because of water","why do skates work","ano ang yelo","unsa ang yelo"],"answer":"Ice is slippery because a thin film of water forms on it well above the point where it would be a drop, and the water is the point rather than a side effect. Below about zero degrees a surface of ice has a disordered outer layer of molecules that never quite locks in place, and a person skating or walking on it is sliding on that layer rather than on the solid structure. At around minus seven the surface itself begins to melt and that thin film gets thicker, which is why ice is less slippery near the melting point than when it is colder. A pressure that melts the ice under a skate explains the other part: the blade or the foot is the only thing keeping it in contact, and the whole technique is a way of balancing on that spot. Grit makes it worse rather than better, which is why street ice feels more dangerous than clean ice.","variations":["Below about zero degrees a surface of ice has a disordered outer layer of molecules that never quite locks in place, and a person skating or walking on it is sliding on that layer rather than on the solid structure. Ice is slippery because a thin film of water forms on it well above the point where it would be a drop, and the water is the point rather than a side effect. At around minus seven the surface itself begins to melt and that thin film gets thicker, which is why ice is less slippery near the melting point than when it is colder. A pressure that melts the ice under a skate explains the other part: the blade or the foot is the only thing keeping it in contact, and the whole technique is a way of balancing on that spot. Grit makes it worse rather than better, which is why street ice feels more dangerous than clean ice.","At around minus seven the surface itself begins to melt and that thin film gets thicker, which is why ice is less slippery near the melting point than when it is colder. Ice is slippery because a thin film of water forms on it well above the point where it would be a drop, and the water is the point rather than a side effect. Below about zero degrees a surface of ice has a disordered outer layer of molecules that never quite locks in place, and a person skating or walking on it is sliding on that layer rather than on the solid structure. A pressure that melts the ice under a skate explains the other part: the blade or the foot is the only thing keeping it in contact, and the whole technique is a way of balancing on that spot. Grit makes it worse rather than better, which is why street ice feels more dangerous than clean ice."],"tl":"yelo","bis":"yelo"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000012","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"hiccups","keywords":["hiccups","diaphragm","breath","spasm","sound","hiccup","rhythm","persistent","followed","sudden","closure"],"patterns":["what is hiccups","hiccups","explain hiccups","definition of hiccups","hiccups meaning","why do we get hiccups","how do you stop a hiccup","what causes hiccups","ano ang hiccup","unsa ang hiccup"],"answer":"A hiccup is a spasm of the diaphragm followed by the sudden closure of the vocal cords, and the sound is produced by that second part rather than by the muscle. Nobody has established a single cause, and the many that have been proposed agree on the mechanism: something irritates the diaphragm or the breathing control, and the signal arrives out of step. The most common triggers are eating too fast, drinking too much carbonated liquid, alcohol, and a sudden temperature change on the surface of the chest, which is why folk remedies involve drinking water upside down or being startled. Those remedies work for the same reason the problem resolves itself: they interrupt the rhythm, and a hiccup that has been running for a few minutes will usually stop when the pattern is broken. Persistent hiccups lasting more than a couple of days are a different matter and are a sign of something worth looking into rather than a joke.","variations":["Nobody has established a single cause, and the many that have been proposed agree on the mechanism: something irritates the diaphragm or the breathing control, and the signal arrives out of step. A hiccup is a spasm of the diaphragm followed by the sudden closure of the vocal cords, and the sound is produced by that second part rather than by the muscle. The most common triggers are eating too fast, drinking too much carbonated liquid, alcohol, and a sudden temperature change on the surface of the chest, which is why folk remedies involve drinking water upside down or being startled. Those remedies work for the same reason the problem resolves itself: they interrupt the rhythm, and a hiccup that has been running for a few minutes will usually stop when the pattern is broken. Persistent hiccups lasting more than a couple of days are a different matter and are a sign of something worth looking into rather than a joke.","The most common triggers are eating too fast, drinking too much carbonated liquid, alcohol, and a sudden temperature change on the surface of the chest, which is why folk remedies involve drinking water upside down or being startled. A hiccup is a spasm of the diaphragm followed by the sudden closure of the vocal cords, and the sound is produced by that second part rather than by the muscle. Nobody has established a single cause, and the many that have been proposed agree on the mechanism: something irritates the diaphragm or the breathing control, and the signal arrives out of step. Those remedies work for the same reason the problem resolves itself: they interrupt the rhythm, and a hiccup that has been running for a few minutes will usually stop when the pattern is broken. Persistent hiccups lasting more than a couple of days are a different matter and are a sign of something worth looking into rather than a joke."],"tl":"hiccup","bis":"hiccup"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000013","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"taste","keywords":["taste","tongue","sweet","sour","bitter","salty","savoury","smell","receptor","lasa","chemistry","rather","sense","organ","forgotten"],"patterns":["what is taste","taste","explain taste","definition of taste","taste meaning","how do we taste food","how many tastes can we taste","why cant i taste when i have a cold","ano ang lasa","unsa ang taste"],"answer":"Taste is chemistry rather than a sense organ on its own, and the part that is often forgotten is that much of what people call taste is smell. The tongue carries receptors for the five basic tastes, which are sweet, sour, salty, bitter and savoury, and every one of them is a chemical dissolving on the surface and being detected. Everything else, which is most of what a person means by taste, comes from the aroma travelling up to the nose from the back of the mouth, and this is why food with a blocked nose tastes like almost nothing. Those five are not a complete list of what a tongue can detect: there is also umami for glutamate and a fat sensation, and the fat one is a real finding rather than a popular one. The reason a person cannot taste anything when they have a cold is the smell, not the taste, which is worth knowing because it is a quick and free way to check the two systems.","variations":["The tongue carries receptors for the five basic tastes, which are sweet, sour, salty, bitter and savoury, and every one of them is a chemical dissolving on the surface and being detected. Taste is chemistry rather than a sense organ on its own, and the part that is often forgotten is that much of what people call taste is smell. Everything else, which is most of what a person means by taste, comes from the aroma travelling up to the nose from the back of the mouth, and this is why food with a blocked nose tastes like almost nothing. Those five are not a complete list of what a tongue can detect: there is also umami for glutamate and a fat sensation, and the fat one is a real finding rather than a popular one. The reason a person cannot taste anything when they have a cold is the smell, not the taste, which is worth knowing because it is a quick and free way to check the two systems.","Everything else, which is most of what a person means by taste, comes from the aroma travelling up to the nose from the back of the mouth, and this is why food with a blocked nose tastes like almost nothing. Taste is chemistry rather than a sense organ on its own, and the part that is often forgotten is that much of what people call taste is smell. The tongue carries receptors for the five basic tastes, which are sweet, sour, salty, bitter and savoury, and every one of them is a chemical dissolving on the surface and being detected. Those five are not a complete list of what a tongue can detect: there is also umami for glutamate and a fat sensation, and the fat one is a real finding rather than a popular one. The reason a person cannot taste anything when they have a cold is the smell, not the taste, which is worth knowing because it is a quick and free way to check the two systems."],"tl":"lasa","bis":"taste"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000014","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"onion tears","keywords":["onion tears","onion","sulphur","enzyme","gas","acid","chop","cry","knife","sibuyas","chopping","makes","eyes","water","cutting"],"patterns":["what is onion tears","onion tears","explain onion tears","definition of onion tears","onion tears meaning","why do onions make you cry","how to stop crying when cutting onion","why do onions sting","ano ang sibuyas","unsa ang sibuyas"],"answer":"Chopping an onion makes your eyes water because cutting it breaks open cells and releases an enzyme and a compound that were kept apart inside them. The enzyme converts a sulphur-containing molecule in the cell into a gas, propanethial oxide, and the gas reaches the eye and reacts with the moisture there to make a mild acid. The eye does not know what acid is doing there, and the reflex it produces is the same one that produces tears to wash a real irritant out, so the burning and the watering are one response rather than a sad film. The practical consequences are all about getting the gas to not reach the eye. Cold reduces the enzyme activity, a sharp knife ruptures fewer cells at once rather than crushing them, cutting near the root end where the concentration is highest makes less of it, and a wet blade carries the liquid away instead of aerosolising it. A very hot pan briefly overwhelms the reaction before it starts, which is the only genuinely absurd piece of cooking advice that works.","variations":["The enzyme converts a sulphur-containing molecule in the cell into a gas, propanethial oxide, and the gas reaches the eye and reacts with the moisture there to make a mild acid. Chopping an onion makes your eyes water because cutting it breaks open cells and releases an enzyme and a compound that were kept apart inside them. The eye does not know what acid is doing there, and the reflex it produces is the same one that produces tears to wash a real irritant out, so the burning and the watering are one response rather than a sad film. The practical consequences are all about getting the gas to not reach the eye. Cold reduces the enzyme activity, a sharp knife ruptures fewer cells at once rather than crushing them, cutting near the root end where the concentration is highest makes less of it, and a wet blade carries the liquid away instead of aerosolising it. A very hot pan briefly overwhelms the reaction before it starts, which is the only genuinely absurd piece of cooking advice that works.","The eye does not know what acid is doing there, and the reflex it produces is the same one that produces tears to wash a real irritant out, so the burning and the watering are one response rather than a sad film. Chopping an onion makes your eyes water because cutting it breaks open cells and releases an enzyme and a compound that were kept apart inside them. The enzyme converts a sulphur-containing molecule in the cell into a gas, propanethial oxide, and the gas reaches the eye and reacts with the moisture there to make a mild acid. The practical consequences are all about getting the gas to not reach the eye. Cold reduces the enzyme activity, a sharp knife ruptures fewer cells at once rather than crushing them, cutting near the root end where the concentration is highest makes less of it, and a wet blade carries the liquid away instead of aerosolising it. A very hot pan briefly overwhelms the reaction before it starts, which is the only genuinely absurd piece of cooking advice that works."],"tl":"sibuyas","bis":"sibuyas"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000015","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"sweating","keywords":["sweating","sweat","cool","evaporat","humid","heat","salt","temperature","pawis","cooling","system","works","water","evaporates"],"patterns":["what is sweating","sweating","explain sweating","definition of sweating","sweating meaning","why do we sweat","why is sweating cold","bakit nga pawis","ano ang pawis","unsa ang pawis"],"answer":"We sweat because sweat is a cooling system, and it only works if the water evaporates. A hot body makes liquid sweat, carries it to the surface and lets it evaporate, and the energy needed to do that leaves the body, which is the entire mechanism. This is why humidity matters so much: in dry air sweat evaporates and you cool, while in humid air the air is already carrying so much water that it cannot take much more, so the sweat stays on the skin and drips without cooling anything. That is the whole explanation for why a humid day feels worse than a dry one at the same temperature. Salt in the sweat is what makes it a problem for someone with a condition that limits sweating, and it is why plain water is not a substitute for sweat lost over a long period.","variations":["A hot body makes liquid sweat, carries it to the surface and lets it evaporate, and the energy needed to do that leaves the body, which is the entire mechanism. We sweat because sweat is a cooling system, and it only works if the water evaporates. This is why humidity matters so much: in dry air sweat evaporates and you cool, while in humid air the air is already carrying so much water that it cannot take much more, so the sweat stays on the skin and drips without cooling anything. That is the whole explanation for why a humid day feels worse than a dry one at the same temperature. Salt in the sweat is what makes it a problem for someone with a condition that limits sweating, and it is why plain water is not a substitute for sweat lost over a long period.","This is why humidity matters so much: in dry air sweat evaporates and you cool, while in humid air the air is already carrying so much water that it cannot take much more, so the sweat stays on the skin and drips without cooling anything. We sweat because sweat is a cooling system, and it only works if the water evaporates. A hot body makes liquid sweat, carries it to the surface and lets it evaporate, and the energy needed to do that leaves the body, which is the entire mechanism. That is the whole explanation for why a humid day feels worse than a dry one at the same temperature. Salt in the sweat is what makes it a problem for someone with a condition that limits sweating, and it is why plain water is not a substitute for sweat lost over a long period."],"tl":"pawis","bis":"pawis"},{"id":"pro-everyday-why-000016","topic":"programming-everyday-why","intent":"definition","concept":"why do we sleep","keywords":["why do we sleep","rest","cycle","stage","memory","deep","repair","fatigue","tulog","katulog","sleep","simply","absence","being","awake","reason"],"patterns":["what is why do we sleep","why do we sleep","explain why do we sleep","definition of why do we sleep","why do we sleep meaning","how much sleep do i need","bakit nga kailangan matulog","ano ang tulog","unsa ang katulog"],"answer":"Sleep is not simply the absence of being awake, and the reason is that the body uses the time for maintenance and the brain uses it for something specific. Physically it repairs tissue, consolidates what was learned during the day into something that lasts, and runs through a cycle of stages rather than being one uniform state, with the deepest stage early in the night and the lightest towards morning. That is why a person who sleeps six hours in one block feels more tired than one who sleeps six hours in two, and why the last hour of the night is worth less than the first. Sleep feels necessary because being awake for a long time degrades attention and reaction time measurably, and that is not something a person can decide to push through, which is why sleep deprivation does so much damage compared with hunger.","variations":["Physically it repairs tissue, consolidates what was learned during the day into something that lasts, and runs through a cycle of stages rather than being one uniform state, with the deepest stage early in the night and the lightest towards morning. Sleep is not simply the absence of being awake, and the reason is that the body uses the time for maintenance and the brain uses it for something specific. That is why a person who sleeps six hours in one block feels more tired than one who sleeps six hours in two, and why the last hour of the night is worth less than the first. Sleep feels necessary because being awake for a long time degrades attention and reaction time measurably, and that is not something a person can decide to push through, which is why sleep deprivation does so much damage compared with hunger.","That is why a person who sleeps six hours in one block feels more tired than one who sleeps six hours in two, and why the last hour of the night is worth less than the first. Sleep is not simply the absence of being awake, and the reason is that the body uses the time for maintenance and the brain uses it for something specific. Physically it repairs tissue, consolidates what was learned during the day into something that lasts, and runs through a cycle of stages rather than being one uniform state, with the deepest stage early in the night and the lightest towards morning. Sleep feels necessary because being awake for a long time degrades attention and reaction time measurably, and that is not something a person can decide to push through, which is why sleep deprivation does so much damage compared with hunger."],"tl":"tulog","bis":"katulog"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"everyday-why","title":"Everyday why","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/everyday-why.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-git","entries":[{"id":"pro-git-000001","topic":"programming-git","intent":"definition","concept":"branch","keywords":["branch","reference","commit","merge","tracking","feature","sangay","version","control","movable","name","pointing"],"patterns":["what is branch","branch","explain branch","definition of branch","branch meaning","what is a branch in git","how do i create a branch","what is the default branch","ano ang sangay","unsa ang branch"],"answer":"A branch in version control is a movable name pointing at a commit, and the point that confuses beginners is that a branch is not a copy of the code and not a separate folder. Creating one is instantaneous and costs nothing, because it is a reference, and switching to it changes the working directory to the state that reference points at. The default branch is the one that receives merges and is the branch you should always be able to fall back to. Feature work belongs on a named branch so that it can be reviewed and merged separately, and the workflow people call branch-per-issue is simply one branch per piece of work. Branches can also point at other branches, which is called tracking, and the pairing is what makes git pull on that branch update the copy on the server as well. A branch that was never merged is not deleted by a delete command without a flag, and the flag is a real safety net: it means the commits are not gone, just unreachable.","variations":["Creating one is instantaneous and costs nothing, because it is a reference, and switching to it changes the working directory to the state that reference points at. A branch in version control is a movable name pointing at a commit, and the point that confuses beginners is that a branch is not a copy of the code and not a separate folder. The default branch is the one that receives merges and is the branch you should always be able to fall back to. Feature work belongs on a named branch so that it can be reviewed and merged separately, and the workflow people call branch-per-issue is simply one branch per piece of work. Branches can also point at other branches, which is called tracking, and the pairing is what makes git pull on that branch update the copy on the server as well. A branch that was never merged is not deleted by a delete command without a flag, and the flag is a real safety net: it means the commits are not gone, just unreachable.","The default branch is the one that receives merges and is the branch you should always be able to fall back to. A branch in version control is a movable name pointing at a commit, and the point that confuses beginners is that a branch is not a copy of the code and not a separate folder. Creating one is instantaneous and costs nothing, because it is a reference, and switching to it changes the working directory to the state that reference points at. Feature work belongs on a named branch so that it can be reviewed and merged separately, and the workflow people call branch-per-issue is simply one branch per piece of work. Branches can also point at other branches, which is called tracking, and the pairing is what makes git pull on that branch update the copy on the server as well. A branch that was never merged is not deleted by a delete command without a flag, and the flag is a real safety net: it means the commits are not gone, just unreachable."],"tl":"sangay","bis":"branch"},{"id":"pro-git-000002","topic":"programming-git","intent":"definition","concept":"rebase","keywords":["rebase","history","commit","merge","hash","linear","i-rebase","replays","commits","branch","another"],"patterns":["what is rebase","rebase","explain rebase","definition of rebase","rebase meaning","merge vs rebase","is rebase safe","ano ang i-rebase","unsa ang rebase"],"answer":"Rebase replays the commits of one branch on top of another, so the history becomes a straight line of your own work sitting on top of the branch you started from. The result looks cleaner and produces a much easier review, which is why it is recommended for work that has not been shared with anyone. The cost is that the commit hashes are rewritten, since each commit has a new parent, so a rebased branch is not interchangeable with the original and rebasing something already pushed to a shared branch forces everyone else to redo their work. Merge is the alternative: it keeps both histories and records a single commit joining them, which is honest about the fact that two lines of work existed and needs no coordination. The practical rule is to merge shared work and rebase your own, and to stop rebasing a branch that is already public rather than trying to repair it afterwards.","variations":["The result looks cleaner and produces a much easier review, which is why it is recommended for work that has not been shared with anyone. Rebase replays the commits of one branch on top of another, so the history becomes a straight line of your own work sitting on top of the branch you started from. The cost is that the commit hashes are rewritten, since each commit has a new parent, so a rebased branch is not interchangeable with the original and rebasing something already pushed to a shared branch forces everyone else to redo their work. Merge is the alternative: it keeps both histories and records a single commit joining them, which is honest about the fact that two lines of work existed and needs no coordination. The practical rule is to merge shared work and rebase your own, and to stop rebasing a branch that is already public rather than trying to repair it afterwards.","The cost is that the commit hashes are rewritten, since each commit has a new parent, so a rebased branch is not interchangeable with the original and rebasing something already pushed to a shared branch forces everyone else to redo their work. Rebase replays the commits of one branch on top of another, so the history becomes a straight line of your own work sitting on top of the branch you started from. The result looks cleaner and produces a much easier review, which is why it is recommended for work that has not been shared with anyone. Merge is the alternative: it keeps both histories and records a single commit joining them, which is honest about the fact that two lines of work existed and needs no coordination. The practical rule is to merge shared work and rebase your own, and to stop rebasing a branch that is already public rather than trying to repair it afterwards."],"tl":"i-rebase","bis":"rebase"},{"id":"pro-git-000003","topic":"programming-git","intent":"definition","concept":"git","keywords":["git","commit","branch","merge","repository","version","control","system","records","history","project"],"patterns":["what is git","git","explain git","definition of git","git meaning","what is a git branch","how do i use git","ano ang git","unsa ang git"],"answer":"Git is a version control system, so it records the history of a project and lets you see what changed, when and by whom. A commit is one snapshot, and a branch is a movable label pointing at a commit, which is why branching is cheap enough to do per feature. The habit that matters is committing and merging often, because a branch left alone for a month is a conflict nobody wants.","variations":["A commit is one snapshot, and a branch is a movable label pointing at a commit, which is why branching is cheap enough to do per feature. Git is a version control system, so it records the history of a project and lets you see what changed, when and by whom. The habit that matters is committing and merging often, because a branch left alone for a month is a conflict nobody wants.","The habit that matters is committing and merging often, because a branch left alone for a month is a conflict nobody wants. Git is a version control system, so it records the history of a project and lets you see what changed, when and by whom. A commit is one snapshot, and a branch is a movable label pointing at a commit, which is why branching is cheap enough to do per feature."],"tl":"git","bis":"git"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"git","title":"Git","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/git.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-github","entries":[{"id":"pro-github-000001","topic":"programming-github","intent":"definition","concept":"github","keywords":["github","issue","repository","licence","place","host","repositories","collaborate","around"],"patterns":["what is github","github","explain github","definition of github","github meaning","what is a pull request","is github the same as git","ano ang github","unsa ang github"],"answer":"GitHub is a place to host git repositories and collaborate around them, and it is not the same thing as git itself. The useful parts beyond storage are pull requests, which let a change be reviewed before it lands, and issues, where work gets discussed. Being hosted on GitHub does not make something open source, and the licence file is what decides that.","variations":["The useful parts beyond storage are pull requests, which let a change be reviewed before it lands, and issues, where work gets discussed. GitHub is a place to host git repositories and collaborate around them, and it is not the same thing as git itself. Being hosted on GitHub does not make something open source, and the licence file is what decides that.","Being hosted on GitHub does not make something open source, and the licence file is what decides that. GitHub is a place to host git repositories and collaborate around them, and it is not the same thing as git itself. The useful parts beyond storage are pull requests, which let a change be reviewed before it lands, and issues, where work gets discussed."],"tl":"github","bis":"github"},{"id":"pro-github-000002","topic":"programming-github","intent":"definition","concept":"pull request","keywords":["pull request","review","merge","branch","diff","checks","conflicts","approve","pull","request","proposal","another"],"patterns":["what is pull request","pull request","explain pull request","definition of pull request","pull request meaning","what is a pull request","how does a pull request work","why is my merge button disabled","ano ang pull request","unsa ang pull request"],"answer":"A pull request is a proposal to merge one branch into another, and it is the centre of how work gets reviewed on GitHub rather than being merely a way to send code. Opening one lets anyone with access see the exact lines that changed, comment on them, and approve or request changes, so the conversation happens on the code itself rather than in a message. The person who opens it is the author, the person who merges is the maintainer, and the two are often different people, which is the whole point of the arrangement. The checks that matter are the status checks the project has configured, since a build or a test run that fails blocks the merge, and reviewing is a separate requirement from a passing build. A common beginner question is why a merge button is greyed out, and the answer is usually one of these: failing checks, an approval that has not happened yet, or a conflict where the same lines changed on both sides.","variations":["Opening one lets anyone with access see the exact lines that changed, comment on them, and approve or request changes, so the conversation happens on the code itself rather than in a message. A pull request is a proposal to merge one branch into another, and it is the centre of how work gets reviewed on GitHub rather than being merely a way to send code. The person who opens it is the author, the person who merges is the maintainer, and the two are often different people, which is the whole point of the arrangement. The checks that matter are the status checks the project has configured, since a build or a test run that fails blocks the merge, and reviewing is a separate requirement from a passing build. A common beginner question is why a merge button is greyed out, and the answer is usually one of these: failing checks, an approval that has not happened yet, or a conflict where the same lines changed on both sides.","The person who opens it is the author, the person who merges is the maintainer, and the two are often different people, which is the whole point of the arrangement. A pull request is a proposal to merge one branch into another, and it is the centre of how work gets reviewed on GitHub rather than being merely a way to send code. Opening one lets anyone with access see the exact lines that changed, comment on them, and approve or request changes, so the conversation happens on the code itself rather than in a message. The checks that matter are the status checks the project has configured, since a build or a test run that fails blocks the merge, and reviewing is a separate requirement from a passing build. A common beginner question is why a merge button is greyed out, and the answer is usually one of these: failing checks, an approval that has not happened yet, or a conflict where the same lines changed on both sides."],"tl":"pull request","bis":"pull request"},{"id":"pro-github-000003","topic":"programming-github","intent":"definition","concept":"fork","keywords":["fork","copy","repository","contribute","clone","upstream","access","sync","somebody","else's","site"],"patterns":["what is fork","fork","explain fork","definition of fork","fork meaning","what is a fork on github","what is the difference between fork and clone","how do i contribute to a project","ano ang fork","unsa ang fork"],"answer":"A fork is your own copy of somebody else's repository on the same site, and it is the mechanism that lets someone contribute to a project they do not have write access to. You fork, make your change on your fork, and then open a pull request from your fork to the original, which is why the contribution path works without anyone having to add you as a collaborator. The alternative is for the maintainer to grant access directly, and the reason forks are preferred is scale: a popular project cannot grant access to every person who wants to try a small fix. A fork is also useful for experimenting without touching the original, and a common beginner mistake is to make the change on the original repository instead of on the fork, which then produces a pull request that a maintainer has to ask you to move. A fork falls behind the original over time, so it is pulled down before starting new work if it was created a while ago.","variations":["You fork, make your change on your fork, and then open a pull request from your fork to the original, which is why the contribution path works without anyone having to add you as a collaborator. A fork is your own copy of somebody else's repository on the same site, and it is the mechanism that lets someone contribute to a project they do not have write access to. The alternative is for the maintainer to grant access directly, and the reason forks are preferred is scale: a popular project cannot grant access to every person who wants to try a small fix. A fork is also useful for experimenting without touching the original, and a common beginner mistake is to make the change on the original repository instead of on the fork, which then produces a pull request that a maintainer has to ask you to move. A fork falls behind the original over time, so it is pulled down before starting new work if it was created a while ago.","The alternative is for the maintainer to grant access directly, and the reason forks are preferred is scale: a popular project cannot grant access to every person who wants to try a small fix. A fork is your own copy of somebody else's repository on the same site, and it is the mechanism that lets someone contribute to a project they do not have write access to. You fork, make your change on your fork, and then open a pull request from your fork to the original, which is why the contribution path works without anyone having to add you as a collaborator. A fork is also useful for experimenting without touching the original, and a common beginner mistake is to make the change on the original repository instead of on the fork, which then produces a pull request that a maintainer has to ask you to move. A fork falls behind the original over time, so it is pulled down before starting new work if it was created a while ago."],"tl":"fork","bis":"fork"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"github","title":"GitHub","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/github.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-html","entries":[{"id":"pro-html-000001","topic":"programming-html","intent":"definition","concept":"html","keywords":["html","hypertext","markup","tag","language","gives","page","structure","styling"],"patterns":["what is html","html","explain html","definition of html","html meaning"],"answer":"HTML is the language that gives a web page its structure. It is not styling and not behaviour - it is the skeleton: headings, paragraphs, images, links, forms. Every page you have ever opened is built out of HTML tags such as h1 for a heading or p for a paragraph. CSS handles how it looks, JavaScript handles what it does.","variations":["It is not styling and not behaviour - it is the skeleton: headings, paragraphs, images, links, forms. HTML is the language that gives a web page its structure. Every page you have ever opened is built out of HTML tags such as h1 for a heading or p for a paragraph. CSS handles how it looks, JavaScript handles what it does.","Every page you have ever opened is built out of HTML tags such as h1 for a heading or p for a paragraph. HTML is the language that gives a web page its structure. It is not styling and not behaviour - it is the skeleton: headings, paragraphs, images, links, forms. CSS handles how it looks, JavaScript handles what it does."]},{"id":"pro-html-000002","topic":"programming-html","intent":"definition","concept":"dom","keywords":["dom","tree","node","mutation","reflow","layout","browser's","live","representation","document","object"],"patterns":["what is dom","dom","explain dom","definition of dom","dom meaning","what is the dom","why is my dom manipulation slow","what is layout thrashing","ano ang DOM","unsa ang DOM"],"answer":"The DOM is the browser's live tree representation of the document, and it is a live object rather than a snapshot, so changing a node updates the screen and observing a change fires a mutation. Scripting the DOM is how a page becomes an application. The expensive operation is repeatedly reading layout properties, because the browser must recalculate geometry, so interleaving a read and a write in a loop causes layout thrashing; batching all reads then all writes avoids it.","variations":["Scripting the DOM is how a page becomes an application. The DOM is the browser's live tree representation of the document, and it is a live object rather than a snapshot, so changing a node updates the screen and observing a change fires a mutation. The expensive operation is repeatedly reading layout properties, because the browser must recalculate geometry, so interleaving a read and a write in a loop causes layout thrashing; batching all reads then all writes avoids it.","The expensive operation is repeatedly reading layout properties, because the browser must recalculate geometry, so interleaving a read and a write in a loop causes layout thrashing; batching all reads then all writes avoids it. The DOM is the browser's live tree representation of the document, and it is a live object rather than a snapshot, so changing a node updates the screen and observing a change fires a mutation. Scripting the DOM is how a page becomes an application."],"tl":"DOM","bis":"DOM"},{"id":"pro-html-000003","topic":"programming-html","intent":"definition","concept":"semantic html","keywords":["semantic html","semantics","accessible","markup","accessibility","semantik","semantic","html","means","choosing","element","meaning"],"patterns":["what is semantic html","semantic html","explain semantic html","definition of semantic html","semantic html meaning","what does semantic html mean","why use semantic html","does semantic html help seo","ano ang semantik","unsa ang semantik"],"answer":"Semantic HTML means choosing the element for its meaning rather than for how it looks, so a button is a button and a navigation is a nav. It costs nothing at runtime, and it is what gives you keyboard focus, screen reader announcements and browser behaviour for free. The classic test is turning off the CSS: a well-built page is still a readable document in the right order, while a div soup becomes an undifferentiated wall.","variations":["It costs nothing at runtime, and it is what gives you keyboard focus, screen reader announcements and browser behaviour for free. Semantic HTML means choosing the element for its meaning rather than for how it looks, so a button is a button and a navigation is a nav. The classic test is turning off the CSS: a well-built page is still a readable document in the right order, while a div soup becomes an undifferentiated wall.","The classic test is turning off the CSS: a well-built page is still a readable document in the right order, while a div soup becomes an undifferentiated wall. Semantic HTML means choosing the element for its meaning rather than for how it looks, so a button is a button and a navigation is a nav. It costs nothing at runtime, and it is what gives you keyboard focus, screen reader announcements and browser behaviour for free."],"tl":"semantik","bis":"semantik"},{"id":"pro-html-000004","topic":"programming-html","intent":"definition","concept":"accessibility","keywords":["accessibility","a11y","aria","keyboard","contrast","means","page","works","everyone","large"],"patterns":["what is accessibility","accessibility","explain accessibility","definition of accessibility","accessibility meaning","do i need aria labels","how do i test keyboard navigation","ano ang accessibility","unsa ang accessibility"],"answer":"Accessibility means the page works for everyone, and a large part of it is nearly free: use real elements, keep the DOM order matching the visual order, label every control, make focus visible, and never remove the outline without replacing it. Beyond that, contrast matters, text must be resizable, and nothing may be conveyed by colour alone. The most useful habit is testing with the keyboard only, because anything you cannot reach with Tab you cannot operate without a mouse, and a screen reader will read the page in DOM order whether or not that is the order you designed for.","variations":["Beyond that, contrast matters, text must be resizable, and nothing may be conveyed by colour alone. Accessibility means the page works for everyone, and a large part of it is nearly free: use real elements, keep the DOM order matching the visual order, label every control, make focus visible, and never remove the outline without replacing it. The most useful habit is testing with the keyboard only, because anything you cannot reach with Tab you cannot operate without a mouse, and a screen reader will read the page in DOM order whether or not that is the order you designed for.","The most useful habit is testing with the keyboard only, because anything you cannot reach with Tab you cannot operate without a mouse, and a screen reader will read the page in DOM order whether or not that is the order you designed for. Accessibility means the page works for everyone, and a large part of it is nearly free: use real elements, keep the DOM order matching the visual order, label every control, make focus visible, and never remove the outline without replacing it. Beyond that, contrast matters, text must be resizable, and nothing may be conveyed by colour alone."],"tl":"accessibility","bis":"accessibility"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"html","title":"HTML","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/html.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-java","entries":[{"id":"pro-java-000001","topic":"programming-java","intent":"definition","concept":"java","keywords":["java","bytecode","jvm","platform","portable","compiled","class","language","designed","code"],"patterns":["what is java","java","explain java","definition of java","java meaning","is java compiled or interpreted","why is java platform independent","ano ang Java","unsa ang Java"],"answer":"Java is a compiled, class-based language designed so that code written once runs on any machine with a Java Virtual Machine, which is the origin of the phrase write once, run anywhere. Code is compiled to bytecode rather than to machine instructions, so the JVM handles memory, garbage collection and portability, and the same .class file runs unmodified on Windows, Linux or macOS. It is statically typed, meaning every variable has a declared type checked before the program runs, and that is why Java errors about type mistakes at compile time instead of halfway through execution. It is also deliberately slower to start than a language compiled straight to native code, and that trade is the whole bargain: you give up a little speed and get a much smaller class of runtime bugs.","variations":["class file runs unmodified on Windows, Linux or macOS. Java is a compiled, class-based language designed so that code written once runs on any machine with a Java Virtual Machine, which is the origin of the phrase write once, run anywhere. It is statically typed, meaning every variable has a declared type checked before the program runs, and that is why Java errors about type mistakes at compile time instead of halfway through execution. It is also deliberately slower to start than a language compiled straight to native code, and that trade is the whole bargain: you give up a little speed and get a much smaller class of runtime bugs.","It is statically typed, meaning every variable has a declared type checked before the program runs, and that is why Java errors about type mistakes at compile time instead of halfway through execution. Java is a compiled, class-based language designed so that code written once runs on any machine with a Java Virtual Machine, which is the origin of the phrase write once, run anywhere. class file runs unmodified on Windows, Linux or macOS. It is also deliberately slower to start than a language compiled straight to native code, and that trade is the whole bargain: you give up a little speed and get a much smaller class of runtime bugs."],"tl":"Java","bis":"Java"},{"id":"pro-java-000002","topic":"programming-java","intent":"definition","concept":"jvm","keywords":["jvm","heap","java","virtual","machine","runtime","actually","executes"],"patterns":["what is jvm","jvm","explain jvm","definition of jvm","jvm meaning","what is the jvm","how does java run on different operating systems","what does the jvm do","ano ang JVM","unsa ang JVM"],"answer":"The Java Virtual Machine is the runtime that actually executes Java bytecode, and it sits between your compiled .class files and the operating system. When the JVM starts it verifies the bytecode for safety, allocates memory, resolves references to classes on demand, and hands the result to the processor through a just-in-time compiler that turns hot methods into native code while the program runs. That just-in-time step is why Java performance approaches compiled languages after the program has been running for a while, and why a short script pays a startup cost that a long-running service does not. Each operating system ships its own JVM build, which is exactly how one bytecode file can execute everywhere.","variations":["When the JVM starts it verifies the bytecode for safety, allocates memory, resolves references to classes on demand, and hands the result to the processor through a just-in-time compiler that turns hot methods into native code while the program runs. class files and the operating system. That just-in-time step is why Java performance approaches compiled languages after the program has been running for a while, and why a short script pays a startup cost that a long-running service does not. Each operating system ships its own JVM build, which is exactly how one bytecode file can execute everywhere.","That just-in-time step is why Java performance approaches compiled languages after the program has been running for a while, and why a short script pays a startup cost that a long-running service does not. class files and the operating system. When the JVM starts it verifies the bytecode for safety, allocates memory, resolves references to classes on demand, and hands the result to the processor through a just-in-time compiler that turns hot methods into native code while the program runs. Each operating system ships its own JVM build, which is exactly how one bytecode file can execute everywhere."],"tl":"JVM","bis":"JVM"},{"id":"pro-java-000003","topic":"programming-java","intent":"definition","concept":"garbage collection","keywords":["garbage collection","memory","reachability","heap","reference","leak","generation","garbage","collection","automatic","reclamation","running"],"patterns":["what is garbage collection","garbage collection","explain garbage collection","definition of garbage collection","garbage collection meaning","how does garbage collection work","what causes a memory leak","does java have memory problems","ano ang garbage collection","unsa ang memory management"],"answer":"Garbage collection is the automatic reclamation of memory that a running program no longer needs, and it removes an entire class of bug in which a program forgets to release memory it has finished with. The collector watches which objects are still reachable from variables and stack frames, and anything unreachable becomes eligible for reclamation; if a reference still points at an object nobody else can see, that object is never collected, which is a memory leak even though the collector is running correctly. Modern collectors such as those in Java and Go split the heap into generations so that most short-lived objects, which are the majority, are reclaimed cheaply in a young space and never incur the cost of a full collection. The practical consequence is that you stop thinking about free and reallocating memory during ordinary application logic, and the remaining leak in Java is almost always an accidentally retained reference rather than a forgotten free.","variations":["The collector watches which objects are still reachable from variables and stack frames, and anything unreachable becomes eligible for reclamation; if a reference still points at an object nobody else can see, that object is never collected, which is a memory leak even though the collector is running correctly. Garbage collection is the automatic reclamation of memory that a running program no longer needs, and it removes an entire class of bug in which a program forgets to release memory it has finished with. Modern collectors such as those in Java and Go split the heap into generations so that most short-lived objects, which are the majority, are reclaimed cheaply in a young space and never incur the cost of a full collection. The practical consequence is that you stop thinking about free and reallocating memory during ordinary application logic, and the remaining leak in Java is almost always an accidentally retained reference rather than a forgotten free.","Modern collectors such as those in Java and Go split the heap into generations so that most short-lived objects, which are the majority, are reclaimed cheaply in a young space and never incur the cost of a full collection. Garbage collection is the automatic reclamation of memory that a running program no longer needs, and it removes an entire class of bug in which a program forgets to release memory it has finished with. The collector watches which objects are still reachable from variables and stack frames, and anything unreachable becomes eligible for reclamation; if a reference still points at an object nobody else can see, that object is never collected, which is a memory leak even though the collector is running correctly. The practical consequence is that you stop thinking about free and reallocating memory during ordinary application logic, and the remaining leak in Java is almost always an accidentally retained reference rather than a forgotten free."],"tl":"garbage collection","bis":"memory management"},{"id":"pro-java-000004","topic":"programming-java","intent":"definition","concept":"exception handling","keywords":["exception handling","try","catch","finally","throw","exception","handling","java","structured","dealing","failure"],"patterns":["what is exception handling","exception handling","explain exception handling","definition of exception handling","exception handling meaning","difference between checked and unchecked exceptions","how do try catch work","ano ang exception handling","unsa ang exception handling"],"answer":"Exception handling in Java is a structured way of dealing with failure: code that might fail is wrapped in a try block, and the catch clauses that follow decide what to do about each type of failure, while finally runs regardless of outcome. Exceptions represent abnormal conditions and are objects, which means they can carry a message and a stack trace and can be thrown across method boundaries without every intermediate method having to know anything about them. A checked exception must be declared or handled, which forces the author to think about failure at every call site, and an unchecked runtime exception such as a null pointer reference signals a genuine programming mistake rather than an expected condition. The common criticism is that catch blocks that print and continue are worse than crashing, because they hide the fault and leave the program in an undefined state.","variations":["Exceptions represent abnormal conditions and are objects, which means they can carry a message and a stack trace and can be thrown across method boundaries without every intermediate method having to know anything about them. Exception handling in Java is a structured way of dealing with failure: code that might fail is wrapped in a try block, and the catch clauses that follow decide what to do about each type of failure, while finally runs regardless of outcome. A checked exception must be declared or handled, which forces the author to think about failure at every call site, and an unchecked runtime exception such as a null pointer reference signals a genuine programming mistake rather than an expected condition. The common criticism is that catch blocks that print and continue are worse than crashing, because they hide the fault and leave the program in an undefined state.","A checked exception must be declared or handled, which forces the author to think about failure at every call site, and an unchecked runtime exception such as a null pointer reference signals a genuine programming mistake rather than an expected condition. Exception handling in Java is a structured way of dealing with failure: code that might fail is wrapped in a try block, and the catch clauses that follow decide what to do about each type of failure, while finally runs regardless of outcome. Exceptions represent abnormal conditions and are objects, which means they can carry a message and a stack trace and can be thrown across method boundaries without every intermediate method having to know anything about them. The common criticism is that catch blocks that print and continue are worse than crashing, because they hide the fault and leave the program in an undefined state."],"tl":"exception handling","bis":"exception handling"},{"id":"pro-java-000005","topic":"programming-java","intent":"definition","concept":"maven","keywords":["maven","pom","dependency","repository","lifecycle","artifact","jar","build","management","tool","java"],"patterns":["what is maven","maven","explain maven","definition of maven","maven meaning","what is pom xml","is maven better than gradle","ano ang Maven","unsa ang Maven"],"answer":"Maven is a build and dependency management tool for Java, and its central idea is a pom.xml file that declares what a project depends on rather than shipping libraries alongside the source. When you declare a dependency, Maven resolves the version, downloads it and every library it needs into a local repository, and the versions of transitive dependencies are reconciled by mediation rules so that a whole project ends up with one consistent tree. Its lifecycle is a fixed sequence of phases and by convention the artifact name and version carry the coordinates that other projects use to depend on this one. The practical reason it exists is that a project with thirty jars on the classpath is impossible to reproduce by hand, and Maven replaced that with one declarative file. Gradle took the opposite approach with a programmable build script, and the real difference is that Maven is configuration while Gradle is code.","variations":["When you declare a dependency, Maven resolves the version, downloads it and every library it needs into a local repository, and the versions of transitive dependencies are reconciled by mediation rules so that a whole project ends up with one consistent tree. xml file that declares what a project depends on rather than shipping libraries alongside the source. Its lifecycle is a fixed sequence of phases and by convention the artifact name and version carry the coordinates that other projects use to depend on this one. The practical reason it exists is that a project with thirty jars on the classpath is impossible to reproduce by hand, and Maven replaced that with one declarative file. Gradle took the opposite approach with a programmable build script, and the real difference is that Maven is configuration while Gradle is code.","Its lifecycle is a fixed sequence of phases and by convention the artifact name and version carry the coordinates that other projects use to depend on this one. xml file that declares what a project depends on rather than shipping libraries alongside the source. When you declare a dependency, Maven resolves the version, downloads it and every library it needs into a local repository, and the versions of transitive dependencies are reconciled by mediation rules so that a whole project ends up with one consistent tree. The practical reason it exists is that a project with thirty jars on the classpath is impossible to reproduce by hand, and Maven replaced that with one declarative file. Gradle took the opposite approach with a programmable build script, and the real difference is that Maven is configuration while Gradle is code."],"tl":"Maven","bis":"Maven"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"java","title":"Java","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/java.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-javascript","entries":[{"id":"pro-javascript-000001","topic":"programming-javascript","intent":"definition","concept":"javascript","keywords":["javascript","js","ecmascript","programming","language","runs","browsers","makes"],"patterns":["what is javascript","javascript","explain javascript","definition of javascript","javascript meaning","what is js","ano ang javascript","unsa ang javascript"],"answer":"JavaScript is the programming language that runs in web browsers. It is what makes a page interactive - a button that reacts, a form that validates, a list that updates without reloading. It also runs on servers through Node.js, and on phones and desktops. Nearly every website you use has JavaScript in it.","variations":["It is what makes a page interactive - a button that reacts, a form that validates, a list that updates without reloading. JavaScript is the programming language that runs in web browsers. js, and on phones and desktops. Nearly every website you use has JavaScript in it.","js, and on phones and desktops. JavaScript is the programming language that runs in web browsers. It is what makes a page interactive - a button that reacts, a form that validates, a list that updates without reloading. Nearly every website you use has JavaScript in it."],"tl":"javascript","bis":"javascript","langs":{"tagalog":"Ang JavaScript ay ang programming language na tumatakbo sa mga web browser, at mula pa sa Node.js tumatakbo rin ito sa mga server. Dynamically typed ito: hindi ini-declare ang mga variable na may partikular na uri, at ang maling uri ay nagkakamali sa oras ng paggamit, hindi bago pa tumakbo ang programa. Hindi ito kaugnay ng Java sa kabila ng katulad na pangalan - ang Java ay nina-compile sa bytecode at strongly typed, samantalang ang JavaScript ay nai-interpret at mas maluwag. Halos lahat ng website na inyong nagamit ay may JavaScript, kaya ito ang pinakamaraming ginagamit na wika sa mundo.","bisaya":"Ang JavaScript mao ang programming language nga nga rana sa mga web browser, ug tungod sa Node.js nga rana pud siya sa mga server. Dynamically typed siya: ang mga variable dili gideklara nga may specific nga type, ug ang ECF nga wrong nga type mao gyud nga ma-error sa panahon nga gamiton, dili sa wala pa nga nakat-running ang programa. Dili siya related sa Java notwithstanding ang similar nga ngalan - ang Java gi-compile ngadto sa bytecode ug strongly typed, samtang ang JavaScript gi-interpret ug mas loose. Sa mga website nga naa ka gyud nga nagamit, naa siya - kana man nga pinakagami nga language sa kalibutan."},"confidence":null},{"id":"pro-javascript-000002","topic":"programming-javascript","intent":"definition","concept":"browser rendering","keywords":["browser rendering","browser","rendering","layout","paint","engine","turns","html","javascript","pixels","sequence"],"patterns":["what is browser rendering","browser rendering","explain browser rendering","definition of browser rendering","browser rendering meaning","how does a browser render a page","what is the critical rendering path","what blocks the main thread","ano ang browser","unsa ang browser"],"answer":"A browser turns HTML, CSS and JavaScript into pixels, and the sequence is roughly parse, build the DOM, build the CSS object model, combine them into a render tree, then layout, then paint, then composite. Knowing that order explains most performance work, because layout and paint are the expensive phases and optimising the wrong one moves nothing. Rendering is largely main-thread work, so a long task blocks everything including scrolling and input, which is why offloading to a worker or yielding to the event loop is the fix.","variations":["Knowing that order explains most performance work, because layout and paint are the expensive phases and optimising the wrong one moves nothing. A browser turns HTML, CSS and JavaScript into pixels, and the sequence is roughly parse, build the DOM, build the CSS object model, combine them into a render tree, then layout, then paint, then composite. Rendering is largely main-thread work, so a long task blocks everything including scrolling and input, which is why offloading to a worker or yielding to the event loop is the fix.","Rendering is largely main-thread work, so a long task blocks everything including scrolling and input, which is why offloading to a worker or yielding to the event loop is the fix. A browser turns HTML, CSS and JavaScript into pixels, and the sequence is roughly parse, build the DOM, build the CSS object model, combine them into a render tree, then layout, then paint, then composite. Knowing that order explains most performance work, because layout and paint are the expensive phases and optimising the wrong one moves nothing."],"tl":"browser","bis":"browser"},{"id":"pro-javascript-000003","topic":"programming-javascript","intent":"definition","concept":"frontend frameworks","keywords":["frontend frameworks","framework","react","vue","svelte","angular","frontend","component","main","choices"],"patterns":["what is frontend frameworks","frontend frameworks","explain frontend frameworks","definition of frontend frameworks","frontend frameworks meaning","what framework should i use for frontend","react or vue","is react better than vue","ano ang framework","unsa ang framework"],"answer":"The main frontend choices are React, Vue, Svelte and Angular, and the honest answer is that they solve the same problem and differ in what they ask of you. React has the largest ecosystem and the most job listings, Vue has the gentlest learning curve, Svelte compiles away most of its own runtime and ships the smallest bundles, and Angular is a full framework with built-in structure that suits large teams. There is no correct one for a beginner, because picking by ecosystem size is a real strategy for getting work and picking by learning curve is a real strategy for finishing a first project. What matters more than the choice is that you learn the ideas underneath it, since every one of them is a component model plus reactive state plus a build step.","variations":["React has the largest ecosystem and the most job listings, Vue has the gentlest learning curve, Svelte compiles away most of its own runtime and ships the smallest bundles, and Angular is a full framework with built-in structure that suits large teams. The main frontend choices are React, Vue, Svelte and Angular, and the honest answer is that they solve the same problem and differ in what they ask of you. There is no correct one for a beginner, because picking by ecosystem size is a real strategy for getting work and picking by learning curve is a real strategy for finishing a first project. What matters more than the choice is that you learn the ideas underneath it, since every one of them is a component model plus reactive state plus a build step.","There is no correct one for a beginner, because picking by ecosystem size is a real strategy for getting work and picking by learning curve is a real strategy for finishing a first project. The main frontend choices are React, Vue, Svelte and Angular, and the honest answer is that they solve the same problem and differ in what they ask of you. React has the largest ecosystem and the most job listings, Vue has the gentlest learning curve, Svelte compiles away most of its own runtime and ships the smallest bundles, and Angular is a full framework with built-in structure that suits large teams. What matters more than the choice is that you learn the ideas underneath it, since every one of them is a component model plus reactive state plus a build step."],"tl":"framework","bis":"framework"},{"id":"pro-javascript-000004","topic":"programming-javascript","intent":"definition","concept":"closure","keywords":["closure","scope","binding","let","var","memory","garbage","capture","javascript","pairing","function","variables","captured"],"patterns":["what is closure","closure","explain closure","definition of closure","closure meaning","what is a closure in javascript","why does my loop counter give the wrong value","closure vs scope","ano ang closure","unsa ang closure"],"answer":"A closure in JavaScript is the pairing of a function with the variables it captured from the surrounding scope, and those variables stay alive after the function that created it has returned. That is the whole mechanism, and it is what lets a function remember where it was written even after the call it was defined inside has finished, which is the basis of most callback-driven code. The effect people notice first is on loops: a variable declared outside a loop and modified on each pass is a single shared variable, so every function created during that loop reads the final value rather than its own, and declaring the variable inside the loop with let fixes it because each pass then gets a fresh binding. Closures also cost memory, since anything a closure references cannot be released while the closure is still reachable, so a long-lived function holding a large object keeps that object alive.","variations":["That is the whole mechanism, and it is what lets a function remember where it was written even after the call it was defined inside has finished, which is the basis of most callback-driven code. A closure in JavaScript is the pairing of a function with the variables it captured from the surrounding scope, and those variables stay alive after the function that created it has returned. The effect people notice first is on loops: a variable declared outside a loop and modified on each pass is a single shared variable, so every function created during that loop reads the final value rather than its own, and declaring the variable inside the loop with let fixes it because each pass then gets a fresh binding. Closures also cost memory, since anything a closure references cannot be released while the closure is still reachable, so a long-lived function holding a large object keeps that object alive.","The effect people notice first is on loops: a variable declared outside a loop and modified on each pass is a single shared variable, so every function created during that loop reads the final value rather than its own, and declaring the variable inside the loop with let fixes it because each pass then gets a fresh binding. A closure in JavaScript is the pairing of a function with the variables it captured from the surrounding scope, and those variables stay alive after the function that created it has returned. That is the whole mechanism, and it is what lets a function remember where it was written even after the call it was defined inside has finished, which is the basis of most callback-driven code. Closures also cost memory, since anything a closure references cannot be released while the closure is still reachable, so a long-lived function holding a large object keeps that object alive."],"tl":"closure","bis":"closure"},{"id":"pro-javascript-000005","topic":"programming-javascript","intent":"definition","concept":"promises","keywords":["promises","async","then","catch","awaited","state","pending","promise","object","representing","value","available","exists"],"patterns":["what is promises","promises","explain promises","definition of promises","promises meaning","what is a promise in javascript","what is the difference between promise and callback","async await explained","ano ang promise","unsa ang promise"],"answer":"A promise is an object representing a value that is not available yet, and it exists because asynchronous operations cannot return a result directly without blocking everything else while they wait. A promise is in one of three states: pending while the work is running, fulfilled once it succeeds with a value, and rejected once it fails with a reason, and once it leaves pending it never changes again, which immutability is what makes it safe to pass around. The consuming side is then, which registers what to do on success, and catch, which registers what to do on failure. The rule that matters most is that callbacks registered with then do not run immediately but on a later turn of the event loop, so code after a then still runs before the callback, which is the source of most ordering surprises. The newer syntax wraps it in an async function, where await turns a promise back into something that looks sequential, and the benefit is that errors can be thrown and caught normally rather than through a chain.","variations":["A promise is in one of three states: pending while the work is running, fulfilled once it succeeds with a value, and rejected once it fails with a reason, and once it leaves pending it never changes again, which immutability is what makes it safe to pass around. A promise is an object representing a value that is not available yet, and it exists because asynchronous operations cannot return a result directly without blocking everything else while they wait. The consuming side is then, which registers what to do on success, and catch, which registers what to do on failure. The rule that matters most is that callbacks registered with then do not run immediately but on a later turn of the event loop, so code after a then still runs before the callback, which is the source of most ordering surprises. The newer syntax wraps it in an async function, where await turns a promise back into something that looks sequential, and the benefit is that errors can be thrown and caught normally rather than through a chain.","The consuming side is then, which registers what to do on success, and catch, which registers what to do on failure. A promise is an object representing a value that is not available yet, and it exists because asynchronous operations cannot return a result directly without blocking everything else while they wait. A promise is in one of three states: pending while the work is running, fulfilled once it succeeds with a value, and rejected once it fails with a reason, and once it leaves pending it never changes again, which immutability is what makes it safe to pass around. The rule that matters most is that callbacks registered with then do not run immediately but on a later turn of the event loop, so code after a then still runs before the callback, which is the source of most ordering surprises. The newer syntax wraps it in an async function, where await turns a promise back into something that looks sequential, and the benefit is that errors can be thrown and caught normally rather than through a chain."],"tl":"promise","bis":"promise"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"javascript","title":"JavaScript","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/javascript.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-nodejs","entries":[{"id":"pro-nodejs-000001","topic":"programming-nodejs","intent":"definition","concept":"nodejs","keywords":["nodejs","runtime","v8","npm","server","Node.js","node","javascript","outside","browser"],"patterns":["what is nodejs","nodejs","explain nodejs","definition of nodejs","nodejs meaning","what is node js","is node js a language","should i use node js","ano ang Node.js","unsa ang Node.js"],"answer":"Node.js is a runtime that lets JavaScript run outside the browser, on a server or a command line, and that shift is the whole reason JavaScript became a language for backend work. It is built on V8, the same JavaScript engine Chrome uses, wrapped in a standard library that includes an event loop, file system access and networking. Because the runtime is single-threaded with asynchronous input and output, it handles many concurrent connections without a thread per request, which is excellent for I/O bound work such as databases and APIs and a poor fit for CPU heavy work such as image processing. The package ecosystem on the npm registry is the largest of any language, and the common directory of missing-lockfile and left-pad incidents from 2016 are still the standard warning about depending on it.","variations":["It is built on V8, the same JavaScript engine Chrome uses, wrapped in a standard library that includes an event loop, file system access and networking. js is a runtime that lets JavaScript run outside the browser, on a server or a command line, and that shift is the whole reason JavaScript became a language for backend work. Because the runtime is single-threaded with asynchronous input and output, it handles many concurrent connections without a thread per request, which is excellent for I/O bound work such as databases and APIs and a poor fit for CPU heavy work such as image processing. The package ecosystem on the npm registry is the largest of any language, and the common directory of missing-lockfile and left-pad incidents from 2016 are still the standard warning about depending on it.","Because the runtime is single-threaded with asynchronous input and output, it handles many concurrent connections without a thread per request, which is excellent for I/O bound work such as databases and APIs and a poor fit for CPU heavy work such as image processing. js is a runtime that lets JavaScript run outside the browser, on a server or a command line, and that shift is the whole reason JavaScript became a language for backend work. It is built on V8, the same JavaScript engine Chrome uses, wrapped in a standard library that includes an event loop, file system access and networking. The package ecosystem on the npm registry is the largest of any language, and the common directory of missing-lockfile and left-pad incidents from 2016 are still the standard warning about depending on it."],"tl":"Node.js","bis":"Node.js"},{"id":"pro-nodejs-000002","topic":"programming-nodejs","intent":"definition","concept":"event loop","keywords":["event loop","tick","promise","concurrency","microtask","event","loop","scheduling","mechanism","inside","node"],"patterns":["what is event loop","event loop","explain event loop","definition of event loop","event loop meaning","how does the event loop work","what is the event loop in node","why is node single threaded","ano ang event loop","unsa ang event loop"],"answer":"The event loop is the scheduling mechanism inside Node.js that decides what runs next, and understanding it explains almost every surprise people hit with asynchronous code. There is a call stack for synchronous work, a heap for objects, and a queue of callbacks waiting to be run. When the stack is empty the loop takes the next callback, runs it to completion, and only then looks at the queue again, which is why a long synchronous block freezes everything including the event loop itself. Non-blocking operations such as reading a file or making a request register a callback and return immediately, so thousands of them can be in flight while the process uses one thread. The consequence worth remembering is that code after an await or inside a callback always runs on a later tick than code that looks sequential, and a for loop with await inside runs the body in series unless you use Promise.all.","variations":["There is a call stack for synchronous work, a heap for objects, and a queue of callbacks waiting to be run. js that decides what runs next, and understanding it explains almost every surprise people hit with asynchronous code. When the stack is empty the loop takes the next callback, runs it to completion, and only then looks at the queue again, which is why a long synchronous block freezes everything including the event loop itself. Non-blocking operations such as reading a file or making a request register a callback and return immediately, so thousands of them can be in flight while the process uses one thread. all.","When the stack is empty the loop takes the next callback, runs it to completion, and only then looks at the queue again, which is why a long synchronous block freezes everything including the event loop itself. js that decides what runs next, and understanding it explains almost every surprise people hit with asynchronous code. There is a call stack for synchronous work, a heap for objects, and a queue of callbacks waiting to be run. Non-blocking operations such as reading a file or making a request register a callback and return immediately, so thousands of them can be in flight while the process uses one thread. all."],"tl":"event loop","bis":"event loop"},{"id":"pro-nodejs-000003","topic":"programming-nodejs","intent":"definition","concept":"npm","keywords":["npm","package","lockfile","dependency","registry","script","manager","ships","node","default","install"],"patterns":["what is npm","npm","explain npm","definition of npm","npm meaning","what does npm stand for","what is package json","should i commit package lock","ano ang npm","unsa ang npm"],"answer":"npm is the package manager that ships with Node.js and it is the default way to install dependencies and publish your own code. A project declares what it needs in package.json, and npm install reads that file, downloads the packages and their transitive dependencies into a node_modules directory, then records exact versions in a lockfile. The lockfile is the part that matters most in practice: without a committed package-lock.json, two developers on the same project can end up with different versions of a transitive dependency and a bug that reproduces on one machine only. Running a script from package.json is also the standard way to wire up a dev server or a test runner, so the commands a project uses are declared in the repository rather than living in a wiki. The two commands people need first are npm install to get everything and npm start to run the declared entry point.","variations":["json, and npm install reads that file, downloads the packages and their transitive dependencies into a node_modules directory, then records exact versions in a lockfile. js and it is the default way to install dependencies and publish your own code. json, two developers on the same project can end up with different versions of a transitive dependency and a bug that reproduces on one machine only. json is also the standard way to wire up a dev server or a test runner, so the commands a project uses are declared in the repository rather than living in a wiki. The two commands people need first are npm install to get everything and npm start to run the declared entry point.","json, two developers on the same project can end up with different versions of a transitive dependency and a bug that reproduces on one machine only. js and it is the default way to install dependencies and publish your own code. json, and npm install reads that file, downloads the packages and their transitive dependencies into a node_modules directory, then records exact versions in a lockfile. json is also the standard way to wire up a dev server or a test runner, so the commands a project uses are declared in the repository rather than living in a wiki. The two commands people need first are npm install to get everything and npm start to run the declared entry point."],"tl":"npm","bis":"npm"},{"id":"pro-nodejs-000004","topic":"programming-nodejs","intent":"definition","concept":"express","keywords":["express","middleware","router","request","response","minimal","framework","node","sits","built"],"patterns":["what is express","express","explain express","definition of express","express meaning","what is express middleware","is express still used","ano ang express","unsa ang express"],"answer":"Express is a minimal web framework for Node.js that sits on top of the built-in http module and removes the repetitive work of handling a request. Its core idea is a middleware chain: you register functions in order, each one receives the request, the response and a next callback, and each one either sends a response or passes control along, so authentication, logging, body parsing and routing become small composable pieces rather than one large handler. Routing is declarative, so you can attach handlers to a method and a path, and a request that matches no route falls through to a handler you defined for that case. It is deliberately small and leaves decisions such as templating, file uploads and database access to other packages, which is why it has stayed the most common starting point despite many newer competitors. The cost is that the same flexibility means security choices are entirely yours, and things like body size limits and header configuration have to be set deliberately.","variations":["Its core idea is a middleware chain: you register functions in order, each one receives the request, the response and a next callback, and each one either sends a response or passes control along, so authentication, logging, body parsing and routing become small composable pieces rather than one large handler. js that sits on top of the built-in http module and removes the repetitive work of handling a request. Routing is declarative, so you can attach handlers to a method and a path, and a request that matches no route falls through to a handler you defined for that case. It is deliberately small and leaves decisions such as templating, file uploads and database access to other packages, which is why it has stayed the most common starting point despite many newer competitors. The cost is that the same flexibility means security choices are entirely yours, and things like body size limits and header configuration have to be set deliberately.","Routing is declarative, so you can attach handlers to a method and a path, and a request that matches no route falls through to a handler you defined for that case. js that sits on top of the built-in http module and removes the repetitive work of handling a request. Its core idea is a middleware chain: you register functions in order, each one receives the request, the response and a next callback, and each one either sends a response or passes control along, so authentication, logging, body parsing and routing become small composable pieces rather than one large handler. It is deliberately small and leaves decisions such as templating, file uploads and database access to other packages, which is why it has stayed the most common starting point despite many newer competitors. The cost is that the same flexibility means security choices are entirely yours, and things like body size limits and header configuration have to be set deliberately."],"tl":"express","bis":"express"},{"id":"pro-nodejs-000005","topic":"programming-nodejs","intent":"definition","concept":"rest api","keywords":["rest api","restful","stateless","resource","architecture","representational","state","transfer","architectural","style","describes"],"patterns":["what is rest api","rest api","explain rest api","definition of rest api","rest api meaning","what is a rest api","rest vs graphql","what does rest stand for","is rest still used","ano ang rest api","unsa ang rest api"],"answer":"Representational State Transfer is the architectural style that describes a web interface built around resources that have identities, can be represented, and respond to a small fixed set of methods. The resources are nouns in a URL, a user is at a path rather than behind a get_user action, and the HTTP method carries the meaning: GET reads and must not change anything, POST creates, PUT replaces a resource entirely, PATCH updates part of it, and DELETE removes. Responses carry status codes as part of the contract, with 200 for success, 201 for something created, 400 for a malformed request, 401 for missing authentication, 404 for a resource that does not exist and 500 for a fault on the server. The style is stateless between requests, meaning each request carries everything needed to interpret it, which is what lets any server in a pool answer any of them. The name is a description of constraints rather than a protocol, so two APIs called REST can behave very differently.","variations":["The resources are nouns in a URL, a user is at a path rather than behind a get_user action, and the HTTP method carries the meaning: GET reads and must not change anything, POST creates, PUT replaces a resource entirely, PATCH updates part of it, and DELETE removes. Representational State Transfer is the architectural style that describes a web interface built around resources that have identities, can be represented, and respond to a small fixed set of methods. Responses carry status codes as part of the contract, with 200 for success, 201 for something created, 400 for a malformed request, 401 for missing authentication, 404 for a resource that does not exist and 500 for a fault on the server. The style is stateless between requests, meaning each request carries everything needed to interpret it, which is what lets any server in a pool answer any of them. The name is a description of constraints rather than a protocol, so two APIs called REST can behave very differently.","Responses carry status codes as part of the contract, with 200 for success, 201 for something created, 400 for a malformed request, 401 for missing authentication, 404 for a resource that does not exist and 500 for a fault on the server. Representational State Transfer is the architectural style that describes a web interface built around resources that have identities, can be represented, and respond to a small fixed set of methods. The resources are nouns in a URL, a user is at a path rather than behind a get_user action, and the HTTP method carries the meaning: GET reads and must not change anything, POST creates, PUT replaces a resource entirely, PATCH updates part of it, and DELETE removes. The style is stateless between requests, meaning each request carries everything needed to interpret it, which is what lets any server in a pool answer any of them. The name is a description of constraints rather than a protocol, so two APIs called REST can behave very differently."],"tl":"rest api","bis":"rest api"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"nodejs","title":"Node.js","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/nodejs.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-php","entries":[{"id":"pro-php-000001","topic":"programming-php","intent":"definition","concept":"php","keywords":["php","interpreted","extension","scripting","server","side","language","built","1995"],"patterns":["what is php","php","explain php","definition of php","php meaning","is php still relevant","what is php used for","ano ang PHP","unsa ang PHP"],"answer":"PHP is a server-side scripting language that was built in 1995 for generating web pages, and it has grown well past that origin into the language behind a large share of the web. It is interpreted, so a file is read and executed on request rather than compiled ahead of time, it is loosely typed with optional type declarations, and the language is procedural, object-oriented and functional all at once. The extension system is the reason PHP survived every prediction of its death: adding a feature is a C module loaded through an extension interface, which is how support for databases, images, encryption and dozens of other domains arrived without changing the core. The current major version is 8, which brought a large performance improvement, named arguments, match expressions and stricter typing, and a lot of advice online about PHP still describes a version that is two generations old.","variations":["It is interpreted, so a file is read and executed on request rather than compiled ahead of time, it is loosely typed with optional type declarations, and the language is procedural, object-oriented and functional all at once. PHP is a server-side scripting language that was built in 1995 for generating web pages, and it has grown well past that origin into the language behind a large share of the web. The extension system is the reason PHP survived every prediction of its death: adding a feature is a C module loaded through an extension interface, which is how support for databases, images, encryption and dozens of other domains arrived without changing the core. The current major version is 8, which brought a large performance improvement, named arguments, match expressions and stricter typing, and a lot of advice online about PHP still describes a version that is two generations old.","The extension system is the reason PHP survived every prediction of its death: adding a feature is a C module loaded through an extension interface, which is how support for databases, images, encryption and dozens of other domains arrived without changing the core. PHP is a server-side scripting language that was built in 1995 for generating web pages, and it has grown well past that origin into the language behind a large share of the web. It is interpreted, so a file is read and executed on request rather than compiled ahead of time, it is loosely typed with optional type declarations, and the language is procedural, object-oriented and functional all at once. The current major version is 8, which brought a large performance improvement, named arguments, match expressions and stricter typing, and a lot of advice online about PHP still describes a version that is two generations old."],"tl":"PHP","bis":"PHP"},{"id":"pro-php-000002","topic":"programming-php","intent":"definition","concept":"laravel","keywords":["laravel","framework","eloquent","blade","artisan","composer","mvc","queue","full","stack","provides","structure"],"patterns":["what is laravel","laravel","explain laravel","definition of laravel","laravel meaning","is laravel hard to learn","what is eloquent orm","ano ang laravel","unsa ang laravel"],"answer":"Laravel is a full-stack PHP framework that provides the structure a web application needs so you are not assembling it from unrelated packages. Its parts are an ORM called Eloquent for database work, a Blade templating engine for views, a router, a queue system, a scheduler, authentication scaffolding and an Artisan command line tool that generates code, runs migrations and clears caches. The architectural idea is convention over configuration: the framework expects a file layout and class names in a particular shape, and it then finds everything without a list of mappings, which removes a category of configuration errors at the cost of a learning curve. It follows a model-view-controller structure, so requests go to a controller, the controller talks to a model, and the result is rendered through a view. A Laravel application is also a Composer project, so the dependency file is the thing you read to know what is installed.","variations":["Its parts are an ORM called Eloquent for database work, a Blade templating engine for views, a router, a queue system, a scheduler, authentication scaffolding and an Artisan command line tool that generates code, runs migrations and clears caches. Laravel is a full-stack PHP framework that provides the structure a web application needs so you are not assembling it from unrelated packages. The architectural idea is convention over configuration: the framework expects a file layout and class names in a particular shape, and it then finds everything without a list of mappings, which removes a category of configuration errors at the cost of a learning curve. It follows a model-view-controller structure, so requests go to a controller, the controller talks to a model, and the result is rendered through a view. A Laravel application is also a Composer project, so the dependency file is the thing you read to know what is installed.","The architectural idea is convention over configuration: the framework expects a file layout and class names in a particular shape, and it then finds everything without a list of mappings, which removes a category of configuration errors at the cost of a learning curve. Laravel is a full-stack PHP framework that provides the structure a web application needs so you are not assembling it from unrelated packages. Its parts are an ORM called Eloquent for database work, a Blade templating engine for views, a router, a queue system, a scheduler, authentication scaffolding and an Artisan command line tool that generates code, runs migrations and clears caches. It follows a model-view-controller structure, so requests go to a controller, the controller talks to a model, and the result is rendered through a view. A Laravel application is also a Composer project, so the dependency file is the thing you read to know what is installed."],"tl":"laravel","bis":"laravel"},{"id":"pro-php-000003","topic":"programming-php","intent":"definition","concept":"composer","keywords":["composer","vendor","autoload","package","psr","dependency","manager","counterpart","javascript","world"],"patterns":["what is composer","composer","explain composer","definition of composer","composer meaning","what is composer json","is composer like npm","ano ang composer","unsa ang composer"],"answer":"Composer is the dependency manager for PHP and the counterpart to npm in the JavaScript world, and it exists because a PHP project of any size needs libraries it did not write. A composer.json file declares the packages the project needs and the PHP version they require, and running composer install reads that file, resolves the dependency graph, downloads everything into a vendor directory and writes a composer.lock file pinning exact versions. The lock file is the part that makes builds reproducible, and committing it is the recommended practice for applications, while a library deliberately does not commit it so its consumers can resolve their own. Composer also handles autoloading, generating the class map that lets your code reference a class without a pile of require statements at the top, and it can run scripts defined in the same file for deployment steps.","variations":["lock file pinning exact versions. Composer is the dependency manager for PHP and the counterpart to npm in the JavaScript world, and it exists because a PHP project of any size needs libraries it did not write. The lock file is the part that makes builds reproducible, and committing it is the recommended practice for applications, while a library deliberately does not commit it so its consumers can resolve their own. Composer also handles autoloading, generating the class map that lets your code reference a class without a pile of require statements at the top, and it can run scripts defined in the same file for deployment steps.","The lock file is the part that makes builds reproducible, and committing it is the recommended practice for applications, while a library deliberately does not commit it so its consumers can resolve their own. Composer is the dependency manager for PHP and the counterpart to npm in the JavaScript world, and it exists because a PHP project of any size needs libraries it did not write. lock file pinning exact versions. Composer also handles autoloading, generating the class map that lets your code reference a class without a pile of require statements at the top, and it can run scripts defined in the same file for deployment steps."],"tl":"composer","bis":"composer"},{"id":"pro-php-000004","topic":"programming-php","intent":"definition","concept":"associative array","keywords":["associative array","array","dictionary","map","foreach","associative","type","almost","everything","including"],"patterns":["what is associative array","associative array","explain associative array","definition of associative array","associative array meaning","what is an associative array in php","is php array a list","how to add key value in php array","ano ang associative array","unsa ang associative array"],"answer":"An associative array in PHP is the array type used for almost everything, including things other languages treat as objects and dictionaries, and it is the single most distinctive thing about writing PHP. It is written as key => value pairs inside square brackets, a string or integer key can address any element, and arrays here are ordered maps rather than the flat list that the name suggests elsewhere. Because it serves as a list, a dictionary and a structure at the same time, code like foreach and array_map works uniformly, and JSON decoding produces exactly this type. The sharp edge is comparison: a loose equality check asks whether two arrays have the same key and value pairs in the same order and with the same types, so arrays that look identical can compare unequal, which is why strict comparison is the safer habit. It also explains why a list in PHP can have a gap in its numeric keys, a valid and occasionally useful state that other languages do not have.","variations":["It is written as key => value pairs inside square brackets, a string or integer key can address any element, and arrays here are ordered maps rather than the flat list that the name suggests elsewhere. An associative array in PHP is the array type used for almost everything, including things other languages treat as objects and dictionaries, and it is the single most distinctive thing about writing PHP. Because it serves as a list, a dictionary and a structure at the same time, code like foreach and array_map works uniformly, and JSON decoding produces exactly this type. The sharp edge is comparison: a loose equality check asks whether two arrays have the same key and value pairs in the same order and with the same types, so arrays that look identical can compare unequal, which is why strict comparison is the safer habit. It also explains why a list in PHP can have a gap in its numeric keys, a valid and occasionally useful state that other languages do not have.","Because it serves as a list, a dictionary and a structure at the same time, code like foreach and array_map works uniformly, and JSON decoding produces exactly this type. An associative array in PHP is the array type used for almost everything, including things other languages treat as objects and dictionaries, and it is the single most distinctive thing about writing PHP. It is written as key => value pairs inside square brackets, a string or integer key can address any element, and arrays here are ordered maps rather than the flat list that the name suggests elsewhere. The sharp edge is comparison: a loose equality check asks whether two arrays have the same key and value pairs in the same order and with the same types, so arrays that look identical can compare unequal, which is why strict comparison is the safer habit. It also explains why a list in PHP can have a gap in its numeric keys, a valid and occasionally useful state that other languages do not have."],"tl":"associative array","bis":"associative array"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"php","title":"PHP","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/php.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-python","entries":[{"id":"pro-python-000001","topic":"programming-python","intent":"definition","concept":"list comprehension","keywords":["list comprehension","python","list","loop","comprehension","filter","expression","generator","lazy","iterable","listahan","construct","builds","writing"],"patterns":["what is list comprehension","list comprehension","explain list comprehension","definition of list comprehension","list comprehension meaning","what is a list comprehension","list comprehension vs for loop","is list comprehension faster","ano ang listahan","unsa ang list comprehension"],"answer":"A list comprehension is the construct that builds a list in one expression by writing the loop as part of the brackets, and it exists because the three lines of a plain for loop say one thing while the comprehension says it in one. The shape is a pair of brackets containing the result you want, followed by a for clause and optionally an if clause to filter. Square brackets give a list, curly braces give a set, and a pair of parentheses with a generator inside gives a lazy sequence, which is the version to use for something you will only partly consume. The honest reason it is faster is not that it skips steps but that the work happens inside the interpreter's own loop, in a tight path, rather than through repeated calls to a method on a list. The real argument for it is readability, and it does hurt readability when it gets nested, so the guidance is to use it when the body is a single expression and to write a real loop the moment it needs a statement, a second loop or a side effect.","variations":["The shape is a pair of brackets containing the result you want, followed by a for clause and optionally an if clause to filter. A list comprehension is the construct that builds a list in one expression by writing the loop as part of the brackets, and it exists because the three lines of a plain for loop say one thing while the comprehension says it in one. Square brackets give a list, curly braces give a set, and a pair of parentheses with a generator inside gives a lazy sequence, which is the version to use for something you will only partly consume. The honest reason it is faster is not that it skips steps but that the work happens inside the interpreter's own loop, in a tight path, rather than through repeated calls to a method on a list. The real argument for it is readability, and it does hurt readability when it gets nested, so the guidance is to use it when the body is a single expression and to write a real loop the moment it needs a statement, a second loop or a side effect.","Square brackets give a list, curly braces give a set, and a pair of parentheses with a generator inside gives a lazy sequence, which is the version to use for something you will only partly consume. A list comprehension is the construct that builds a list in one expression by writing the loop as part of the brackets, and it exists because the three lines of a plain for loop say one thing while the comprehension says it in one. The shape is a pair of brackets containing the result you want, followed by a for clause and optionally an if clause to filter. The honest reason it is faster is not that it skips steps but that the work happens inside the interpreter's own loop, in a tight path, rather than through repeated calls to a method on a list. The real argument for it is readability, and it does hurt readability when it gets nested, so the guidance is to use it when the body is a single expression and to write a real loop the moment it needs a statement, a second loop or a side effect."],"tl":"listahan","bis":"list comprehension"},{"id":"pro-python-000002","topic":"programming-python","intent":"definition","concept":"decorator","keywords":["decorator","wrapper","logging","caching","class","return","dekorador","function","takes","another","returns","replacement"],"patterns":["what is decorator","decorator","explain decorator","definition of decorator","decorator meaning","what is a decorator","how do decorators work","what does a decorator return","ano ang dekorador","unsa ang decorator"],"answer":"A decorator is a function that takes another function and returns a replacement for it, and the reason it exists is that behaviour which applies to many functions is otherwise written many times. The syntax is an at sign above the definition, so the decorated name becomes the wrapped function rather than the original one, and the wrapper receives the original as its first argument. The classic use is logging, timing, caching and authorisation, and because the wrapper runs on every call it is also a performance cost that people forget. The related form used for classes is the same idea, and applying a decorator to a class replaces the name with the result of the call rather than modifying the class in place. Two things are worth knowing before using one widely: a decorator that does not return anything silently makes the name become None, which is the most common decorator bug, and a stack of decorators runs bottom up, so reading them means starting at the one nearest the function.","variations":["The syntax is an at sign above the definition, so the decorated name becomes the wrapped function rather than the original one, and the wrapper receives the original as its first argument. A decorator is a function that takes another function and returns a replacement for it, and the reason it exists is that behaviour which applies to many functions is otherwise written many times. The classic use is logging, timing, caching and authorisation, and because the wrapper runs on every call it is also a performance cost that people forget. The related form used for classes is the same idea, and applying a decorator to a class replaces the name with the result of the call rather than modifying the class in place. Two things are worth knowing before using one widely: a decorator that does not return anything silently makes the name become None, which is the most common decorator bug, and a stack of decorators runs bottom up, so reading them means starting at the one nearest the function.","The classic use is logging, timing, caching and authorisation, and because the wrapper runs on every call it is also a performance cost that people forget. A decorator is a function that takes another function and returns a replacement for it, and the reason it exists is that behaviour which applies to many functions is otherwise written many times. The syntax is an at sign above the definition, so the decorated name becomes the wrapped function rather than the original one, and the wrapper receives the original as its first argument. The related form used for classes is the same idea, and applying a decorator to a class replaces the name with the result of the call rather than modifying the class in place. Two things are worth knowing before using one widely: a decorator that does not return anything silently makes the name become None, which is the most common decorator bug, and a stack of decorators runs bottom up, so reading them means starting at the one nearest the function."],"tl":"dekorador","bis":"decorator"},{"id":"pro-python-000003","topic":"programming-python","intent":"definition","concept":"python","keywords":["python","scripting","snake","programming","language","built","readable","uses"],"patterns":["what is python","python","explain python","definition of python","python meaning","python language"],"answer":"Python is a programming language built to be readable. It uses clear English-like syntax and indentation instead of braces, so beginners get to working code quickly. It is used for data analysis, automation, artificial intelligence, and backends. It is the most common language beginners start with.","variations":["It uses clear English-like syntax and indentation instead of braces, so beginners get to working code quickly. Python is a programming language built to be readable. It is used for data analysis, automation, artificial intelligence, and backends. It is the most common language beginners start with.","It is used for data analysis, automation, artificial intelligence, and backends. Python is a programming language built to be readable. It uses clear English-like syntax and indentation instead of braces, so beginners get to working code quickly. It is the most common language beginners start with."]}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"python","title":"Python","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/python.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-react","entries":[{"id":"pro-react-000001","topic":"programming-react","intent":"definition","concept":"react","keywords":["react","component","state","library","hooks","javascript","building","user","interfaces"],"patterns":["what is react","react","explain react","definition of react","react meaning","is react a framework or library","should i learn react","ano ang React","unsa ang React"],"answer":"React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces out of reusable components, and the idea that made it popular is one-way data flow: a component receives data through props, and the only way that data changes is through a state hook that re-renders it. That constraint is what makes React apps predictable, because there is a single owner of every piece of data and no two parts of the tree quietly disagree about it. The rendering model is a virtual document object model, a plain JavaScript description of what the interface should look like, which React compares against the previous one and updates only the real nodes that changed. The downside is that the extra layer and the build tooling add real complexity to a small project, and the first few hours are often spent on tooling rather than on the feature.","variations":["That constraint is what makes React apps predictable, because there is a single owner of every piece of data and no two parts of the tree quietly disagree about it. React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces out of reusable components, and the idea that made it popular is one-way data flow: a component receives data through props, and the only way that data changes is through a state hook that re-renders it. The rendering model is a virtual document object model, a plain JavaScript description of what the interface should look like, which React compares against the previous one and updates only the real nodes that changed. The downside is that the extra layer and the build tooling add real complexity to a small project, and the first few hours are often spent on tooling rather than on the feature.","The rendering model is a virtual document object model, a plain JavaScript description of what the interface should look like, which React compares against the previous one and updates only the real nodes that changed. React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces out of reusable components, and the idea that made it popular is one-way data flow: a component receives data through props, and the only way that data changes is through a state hook that re-renders it. That constraint is what makes React apps predictable, because there is a single owner of every piece of data and no two parts of the tree quietly disagree about it. The downside is that the extra layer and the build tooling add real complexity to a small project, and the first few hours are often spent on tooling rather than on the feature."],"tl":"React","bis":"React"},{"id":"pro-react-000002","topic":"programming-react","intent":"definition","concept":"jsx","keywords":["jsx","babel","transpiler","element","expression","compiler","syntax","extension","write","looks","html","inside"],"patterns":["what is jsx","jsx","explain jsx","definition of jsx","jsx meaning","is jsx html","do i need babel for jsx","ano ang jsx","unsa ang jsx"],"answer":"JSX is a syntax extension that lets you write what looks like HTML inside JavaScript, and it compiles to a function call that creates an element object rather than producing a string of markup. A tag becomes a function invocation, so {name} inside it substitutes a value, and a component that starts with a capital letter is treated as a custom component and called rather than emitted as a literal tag. The compiler is Babel or an equivalent tool built into most React toolchains, and the compiled output is plain JavaScript that a browser can run, which is what makes JSX an extension rather than a new language. The point worth knowing is that JSX creates a tree of plain objects, not a DOM node, so the fast comparison React performs is a comparison of JavaScript objects. Browser tools can display it and JavaScript files can contain it, which means a stray typo surfaces as a syntax error at compile time rather than as a blank page.","variations":["A tag becomes a function invocation, so {name} inside it substitutes a value, and a component that starts with a capital letter is treated as a custom component and called rather than emitted as a literal tag. JSX is a syntax extension that lets you write what looks like HTML inside JavaScript, and it compiles to a function call that creates an element object rather than producing a string of markup. The compiler is Babel or an equivalent tool built into most React toolchains, and the compiled output is plain JavaScript that a browser can run, which is what makes JSX an extension rather than a new language. The point worth knowing is that JSX creates a tree of plain objects, not a DOM node, so the fast comparison React performs is a comparison of JavaScript objects. Browser tools can display it and JavaScript files can contain it, which means a stray typo surfaces as a syntax error at compile time rather than as a blank page.","The compiler is Babel or an equivalent tool built into most React toolchains, and the compiled output is plain JavaScript that a browser can run, which is what makes JSX an extension rather than a new language. JSX is a syntax extension that lets you write what looks like HTML inside JavaScript, and it compiles to a function call that creates an element object rather than producing a string of markup. A tag becomes a function invocation, so {name} inside it substitutes a value, and a component that starts with a capital letter is treated as a custom component and called rather than emitted as a literal tag. The point worth knowing is that JSX creates a tree of plain objects, not a DOM node, so the fast comparison React performs is a comparison of JavaScript objects. Browser tools can display it and JavaScript files can contain it, which means a stray typo surfaces as a syntax error at compile time rather than as a blank page."],"tl":"jsx","bis":"jsx"},{"id":"pro-react-000003","topic":"programming-react","intent":"definition","concept":"react hook","keywords":["react hook","closure","re-render","memoization","hook","function","beginning","component","state","react"],"patterns":["what is react hook","react hook","explain react hook","definition of react hook","react hook meaning","what is a react hook","why does a hook start with use","what is the dependency array","ano ang react hook","unsa ang react hook"],"answer":"A hook is a function beginning with use that lets a function component use state and other React features, and the naming rule is not decoration: React identifies hooks by that prefix and relies on the calls happening in the same order on every render. useState returns a value and a setter, useEffect runs code after rendering for things like fetching data or subscribing to an event, and useMemo caches an expensive calculation between renders. The rule against calling a hook conditionally or inside a loop follows from how the runtime tracks them, since it matches them up by position, so skipping one on some path makes every hook after it read the wrong state. Hooks also solved the problem of sharing behaviour between components, which the older class component approach handled with inheritance and higher order components, both of which made code hard to follow. The criticism that arrived later is that hooks plus dependency arrays are easy to get subtly wrong, and forgetting a dependency is a common source of stale data.","variations":["useState returns a value and a setter, useEffect runs code after rendering for things like fetching data or subscribing to an event, and useMemo caches an expensive calculation between renders. A hook is a function beginning with use that lets a function component use state and other React features, and the naming rule is not decoration: React identifies hooks by that prefix and relies on the calls happening in the same order on every render. The rule against calling a hook conditionally or inside a loop follows from how the runtime tracks them, since it matches them up by position, so skipping one on some path makes every hook after it read the wrong state. Hooks also solved the problem of sharing behaviour between components, which the older class component approach handled with inheritance and higher order components, both of which made code hard to follow. The criticism that arrived later is that hooks plus dependency arrays are easy to get subtly wrong, and forgetting a dependency is a common source of stale data.","The rule against calling a hook conditionally or inside a loop follows from how the runtime tracks them, since it matches them up by position, so skipping one on some path makes every hook after it read the wrong state. A hook is a function beginning with use that lets a function component use state and other React features, and the naming rule is not decoration: React identifies hooks by that prefix and relies on the calls happening in the same order on every render. useState returns a value and a setter, useEffect runs code after rendering for things like fetching data or subscribing to an event, and useMemo caches an expensive calculation between renders. Hooks also solved the problem of sharing behaviour between components, which the older class component approach handled with inheritance and higher order components, both of which made code hard to follow. The criticism that arrived later is that hooks plus dependency arrays are easy to get subtly wrong, and forgetting a dependency is a common source of stale data."],"tl":"react hook","bis":"react hook"},{"id":"pro-react-000004","topic":"programming-react","intent":"definition","concept":"props","keywords":["props","properties","children","immutable","context","react","prop","value","parent","passes","child"],"patterns":["what is props","props","explain props","definition of props","props meaning","what are props in react","can you change props","what is state lifting","ano ang props","unsa ang props"],"answer":"In React a prop is a value a parent passes down to a child, and it is the only way information flows downward. Props are read-only for the child: a component that wants to change what it was given does not write to the prop, it calls a state setter, and the new value travels back up and then down again as a new prop. Children are passed the same way, as props.children when a component wraps content, and functions passed down as props are how a parent hands a child the ability to call back. The design rule is that props are immutable data describing what something is, while state is the part that changes over time, and mixing the two is what causes a component that re-renders when nothing visible changed. Because props flow one way and never upward, sharing state between distant components usually means lifting it to their closest common parent or using a context, which is the main structural decision a React application makes.","variations":["Props are read-only for the child: a component that wants to change what it was given does not write to the prop, it calls a state setter, and the new value travels back up and then down again as a new prop. In React a prop is a value a parent passes down to a child, and it is the only way information flows downward. children when a component wraps content, and functions passed down as props are how a parent hands a child the ability to call back. The design rule is that props are immutable data describing what something is, while state is the part that changes over time, and mixing the two is what causes a component that re-renders when nothing visible changed. Because props flow one way and never upward, sharing state between distant components usually means lifting it to their closest common parent or using a context, which is the main structural decision a React application makes.","children when a component wraps content, and functions passed down as props are how a parent hands a child the ability to call back. In React a prop is a value a parent passes down to a child, and it is the only way information flows downward. Props are read-only for the child: a component that wants to change what it was given does not write to the prop, it calls a state setter, and the new value travels back up and then down again as a new prop. The design rule is that props are immutable data describing what something is, while state is the part that changes over time, and mixing the two is what causes a component that re-renders when nothing visible changed. Because props flow one way and never upward, sharing state between distant components usually means lifting it to their closest common parent or using a context, which is the main structural decision a React application makes."],"tl":"props","bis":"props"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"react","title":"React","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/react.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-sql","entries":[{"id":"pro-sql-000001","topic":"programming-sql","intent":"definition","concept":"database","keywords":["database","sql","table","query","postgres","mysql","data","stores","again","later","unlike"],"patterns":["what is database","database","explain database","definition of database","database meaning","what is a database","what is sql"],"answer":"A database stores data so it can be found again later, unlike a file which has to be read end to end. A relational database keeps data in tables of rows and columns, and you ask it questions with SQL, a language designed for exactly that. The point is not speed alone but that data stays consistent when many things read and write at once.","variations":["A relational database keeps data in tables of rows and columns, and you ask it questions with SQL, a language designed for exactly that. A database stores data so it can be found again later, unlike a file which has to be read end to end. The point is not speed alone but that data stays consistent when many things read and write at once.","The point is not speed alone but that data stays consistent when many things read and write at once. A database stores data so it can be found again later, unlike a file which has to be read end to end. A relational database keeps data in tables of rows and columns, and you ask it questions with SQL, a language designed for exactly that."]},{"id":"pro-sql-000002","topic":"programming-sql","intent":"definition","concept":"sql","keywords":["sql","query","declarative","select","plan","language","asking","relational","questions"],"patterns":["what is sql","sql","explain sql","definition of sql","sql meaning","is sql a programming language","how to learn sql","ano ang sql","unsa ang sql"],"answer":"SQL is the language for asking a relational database questions in a declarative way, meaning you state the result you want rather than the steps to get it. The database works out the steps, which is the whole point and also the trap, because a perfectly clear query can still be catastrophically slow. Learning to read an execution plan matters more than memorising syntax.","variations":["The database works out the steps, which is the whole point and also the trap, because a perfectly clear query can still be catastrophically slow. SQL is the language for asking a relational database questions in a declarative way, meaning you state the result you want rather than the steps to get it. Learning to read an execution plan matters more than memorising syntax.","Learning to read an execution plan matters more than memorising syntax. SQL is the language for asking a relational database questions in a declarative way, meaning you state the result you want rather than the steps to get it. The database works out the steps, which is the whole point and also the trap, because a perfectly clear query can still be catastrophically slow."],"tl":"sql","bis":"sql"},{"id":"pro-sql-000003","topic":"programming-sql","intent":"definition","concept":"primary key","keywords":["primary key","unique","id","composite","constraint","primary","columns","uniquely","identifies","enforced"],"patterns":["what is primary key","primary key","explain primary key","definition of primary key","primary key meaning","what is a primary key","do i need a primary key","what is a composite key","ano ang primary key","unsa ang primary key"],"answer":"A primary key is the column or set of columns that uniquely identifies a row, and it is enforced rather than trusted. That enforcement is its real value, because an application that guesses a key is fine until two people guess the same one. An auto-incrementing integer is the usual choice, and a composite key using two columns is the answer when no single column is unique alone.","variations":["That enforcement is its real value, because an application that guesses a key is fine until two people guess the same one. A primary key is the column or set of columns that uniquely identifies a row, and it is enforced rather than trusted. An auto-incrementing integer is the usual choice, and a composite key using two columns is the answer when no single column is unique alone.","An auto-incrementing integer is the usual choice, and a composite key using two columns is the answer when no single column is unique alone. A primary key is the column or set of columns that uniquely identifies a row, and it is enforced rather than trusted. That enforcement is its real value, because an application that guesses a key is fine until two people guess the same one."],"tl":"primary key","bis":"primary key"},{"id":"pro-sql-000004","topic":"programming-sql","intent":"definition","concept":"foreign key","keywords":["foreign key","reference","constraint","orphan","cascade","foreign","references","primary","another","table"],"patterns":["what is foreign key","foreign key","explain foreign key","definition of foreign key","foreign key meaning","what is a foreign key","what is an orphaned row","what does on delete cascade do","ano ang foreign key","unsa ang foreign key"],"answer":"A foreign key is a column that references the primary key of another table, and it stops a row pointing at something that does not exist. Without it, the references in your data are a convention only the application enforces, so a bug in one screen quietly breaks every other. The cost is that deleting a referenced row now needs a decision about what happens to the rows pointing at it.","variations":["Without it, the references in your data are a convention only the application enforces, so a bug in one screen quietly breaks every other. A foreign key is a column that references the primary key of another table, and it stops a row pointing at something that does not exist. The cost is that deleting a referenced row now needs a decision about what happens to the rows pointing at it.","The cost is that deleting a referenced row now needs a decision about what happens to the rows pointing at it. A foreign key is a column that references the primary key of another table, and it stops a row pointing at something that does not exist. Without it, the references in your data are a convention only the application enforces, so a bug in one screen quietly breaks every other."],"tl":"foreign key","bis":"foreign key"},{"id":"pro-sql-000005","topic":"programming-sql","intent":"definition","concept":"normalization","keywords":["normalization","redundancy","denormalize","join","organises","tables","fact","stored","once"],"patterns":["what is normalization","normalization","explain normalization","definition of normalization","normalization meaning","what is denormalization","is denormalization bad","ano ang normalization","unsa ang normalization"],"answer":"Normalization organises tables so each fact is stored once, which removes contradictions where two copies disagree and makes updates safe. The first three normal forms cover repeating groups, partial dependencies and transitive dependencies. It is not free, because every join costs something at read time, so denormalising a hot read-heavy table is a deliberate choice rather than a mistake.","variations":["The first three normal forms cover repeating groups, partial dependencies and transitive dependencies. Normalization organises tables so each fact is stored once, which removes contradictions where two copies disagree and makes updates safe. It is not free, because every join costs something at read time, so denormalising a hot read-heavy table is a deliberate choice rather than a mistake.","It is not free, because every join costs something at read time, so denormalising a hot read-heavy table is a deliberate choice rather than a mistake. Normalization organises tables so each fact is stored once, which removes contradictions where two copies disagree and makes updates safe. The first three normal forms cover repeating groups, partial dependencies and transitive dependencies."],"tl":"normalization","bis":"normalization"},{"id":"pro-sql-000006","topic":"programming-sql","intent":"definition","concept":"join","keywords":["join","match","combines","rows","tables","shared"],"patterns":["what is join","join","explain join","definition of join","join meaning","difference between inner and left join","what does a left join do","why is my left join returning too many rows","ano ang join","unsa ang join"],"answer":"A join combines rows from two tables on a shared column, and the type of join decides what happens to rows that do not match. An inner join keeps only matching rows, a left join keeps everything from the left with nulls for the rest, and a full join keeps both. The left join causes most surprise, because switching an inner for a left silently multiplies rows on a many-to-one relationship.","variations":["An inner join keeps only matching rows, a left join keeps everything from the left with nulls for the rest, and a full join keeps both. A join combines rows from two tables on a shared column, and the type of join decides what happens to rows that do not match. The left join causes most surprise, because switching an inner for a left silently multiplies rows on a many-to-one relationship.","The left join causes most surprise, because switching an inner for a left silently multiplies rows on a many-to-one relationship. A join combines rows from two tables on a shared column, and the type of join decides what happens to rows that do not match. An inner join keeps only matching rows, a left join keeps everything from the left with nulls for the rest, and a full join keeps both."],"tl":"join","bis":"join"},{"id":"pro-sql-000007","topic":"programming-sql","intent":"definition","concept":"index","keywords":["index","performance","b-tree","scan","separate","structure","rows","without"],"patterns":["what is index","index","explain index","definition of index","index meaning","what is a database index","should i index this column","why is my index not used","ano ang index","unsa ang index"],"answer":"An index is a separate structure that lets the database find rows without reading the whole table, trading write speed and disk for read speed. It is worth having on columns you filter, join or sort by, and worth not having on a small table where a full scan is already cheap. The common failure is indexing a low-selectivity column, since an index on a boolean often costs more to maintain than the scan it replaces.","variations":["It is worth having on columns you filter, join or sort by, and worth not having on a small table where a full scan is already cheap. An index is a separate structure that lets the database find rows without reading the whole table, trading write speed and disk for read speed. The common failure is indexing a low-selectivity column, since an index on a boolean often costs more to maintain than the scan it replaces.","The common failure is indexing a low-selectivity column, since an index on a boolean often costs more to maintain than the scan it replaces. An index is a separate structure that lets the database find rows without reading the whole table, trading write speed and disk for read speed. It is worth having on columns you filter, join or sort by, and worth not having on a small table where a full scan is already cheap."],"tl":"index","bis":"index"},{"id":"pro-sql-000008","topic":"programming-sql","intent":"definition","concept":"transaction","keywords":["transaction","acid","atomic","isolation","commit","rollback","groups","statements","succeed","fail","together"],"patterns":["what is transaction","transaction","explain transaction","definition of transaction","transaction meaning","what is a transaction","what is acid","what is an isolation level","ano ang transaction","unsa ang transaction"],"answer":"A transaction groups statements so they all succeed or all fail together, which is what stops a half-finished transfer from existing. The properties are called ACID: atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability. The isolation level is the dial that matters in practice, because it decides whether a concurrent read can see partially applied work.","variations":["The properties are called ACID: atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability. A transaction groups statements so they all succeed or all fail together, which is what stops a half-finished transfer from existing. The isolation level is the dial that matters in practice, because it decides whether a concurrent read can see partially applied work.","The isolation level is the dial that matters in practice, because it decides whether a concurrent read can see partially applied work. A transaction groups statements so they all succeed or all fail together, which is what stops a half-finished transfer from existing. The properties are called ACID: atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability."],"tl":"transaction","bis":"transaction"},{"id":"pro-sql-000009","topic":"programming-sql","intent":"definition","concept":"nosql","keywords":["nosql","scale","mongodb","covers","families","relational","guarantees"],"patterns":["what is nosql","nosql","explain nosql","definition of nosql","nosql meaning","when to use nosql instead of sql","is mongodb better than postgres","ano ang nosql","unsa ang nosql"],"answer":"NoSQL covers database families that give up some relational guarantees in exchange for horizontal scaling and flexible structure. Document stores keep JSON-like records, key-value stores are a fast lookup table, and column and graph stores suit different access patterns. The honest tradeoff is that these scale more easily but corrupt more easily, because no schema stops a field from being missing or the wrong type.","variations":["Document stores keep JSON-like records, key-value stores are a fast lookup table, and column and graph stores suit different access patterns. NoSQL covers database families that give up some relational guarantees in exchange for horizontal scaling and flexible structure. The honest tradeoff is that these scale more easily but corrupt more easily, because no schema stops a field from being missing or the wrong type.","The honest tradeoff is that these scale more easily but corrupt more easily, because no schema stops a field from being missing or the wrong type. NoSQL covers database families that give up some relational guarantees in exchange for horizontal scaling and flexible structure. Document stores keep JSON-like records, key-value stores are a fast lookup table, and column and graph stores suit different access patterns."],"tl":"nosql","bis":"nosql"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"sql","title":"SQL","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/sql.json"},
  {"topic":"programming-vue","entries":[{"id":"pro-vue-000001","topic":"programming-vue","intent":"definition","concept":"vue","keywords":["vue","reactivity","v-model","v-if","progressive","javascript","framework","means","library","works"],"patterns":["what is vue","vue","explain vue","definition of vue","vue meaning","is vue hard to learn","vue vs react","ano ang Vue","unsa ang Vue"],"answer":"Vue is a progressive JavaScript framework, which means the same library works in three ways: as plain script tags that add reactivity to an existing page, as modules for a larger build, or as a full single file component setup. Its centre is reactive state, where declaring a value inside a reactive object or a ref automatically tracks anything that reads it and updates only what depends on it, so a template updates without manual refresh code. The template syntax is deliberately close to plain HTML with a few extra attributes like v-model for two way form binding, v-if for conditional rendering and v-for for lists, and the learning curve is short for anyone who has used HTML. The single file component convention packs the template, the script and the style for one component into one file, which many teams find easier to read than separating them. It is a smaller and gentler framework than Angular, and unlike React it is opinionated enough to give you a standard structure to follow.","variations":["Its centre is reactive state, where declaring a value inside a reactive object or a ref automatically tracks anything that reads it and updates only what depends on it, so a template updates without manual refresh code. Vue is a progressive JavaScript framework, which means the same library works in three ways: as plain script tags that add reactivity to an existing page, as modules for a larger build, or as a full single file component setup. The template syntax is deliberately close to plain HTML with a few extra attributes like v-model for two way form binding, v-if for conditional rendering and v-for for lists, and the learning curve is short for anyone who has used HTML. The single file component convention packs the template, the script and the style for one component into one file, which many teams find easier to read than separating them. It is a smaller and gentler framework than Angular, and unlike React it is opinionated enough to give you a standard structure to follow.","The template syntax is deliberately close to plain HTML with a few extra attributes like v-model for two way form binding, v-if for conditional rendering and v-for for lists, and the learning curve is short for anyone who has used HTML. Vue is a progressive JavaScript framework, which means the same library works in three ways: as plain script tags that add reactivity to an existing page, as modules for a larger build, or as a full single file component setup. Its centre is reactive state, where declaring a value inside a reactive object or a ref automatically tracks anything that reads it and updates only what depends on it, so a template updates without manual refresh code. The single file component convention packs the template, the script and the style for one component into one file, which many teams find easier to read than separating them. It is a smaller and gentler framework than Angular, and unlike React it is opinionated enough to give you a standard structure to follow."],"tl":"Vue","bis":"Vue"},{"id":"pro-vue-000002","topic":"programming-vue","intent":"definition","concept":"reactive","keywords":["reactive","proxy","ref","computed","watch","mutation","reactivity","mechanism","makes","user","interface","update"],"patterns":["what is reactive","reactive","explain reactive","definition of reactive","reactive meaning","how does vue reactivity work","what is a ref in vue","what is a computed property","ano ang reactive","unsa ang reactive"],"answer":"Reactivity in Vue is the mechanism that makes a user interface update when data changes, and the key difference from a plain variable is that Vue tracks who read a value. When you return a plain object from setup, Vue wraps it in a Proxy that records property access, and when you write to a property it notifies exactly the components whose render functions read that property. Refs are the equivalent for a single value, where reading .value inside script gives you the tracked read and the template unwraps it automatically. A computed property caches a derived value and recomputes only when its dependencies change, which is the right tool for a value that would otherwise be recalculated on every render. The reason the tracking works is contrast with a non reactive variable: a plain let in a function body is invisible to Vue, and mutating it changes nothing on screen. That is also why you are warned against destructuring a reactive object into plain variables, because the tracking link is lost.","variations":["When you return a plain object from setup, Vue wraps it in a Proxy that records property access, and when you write to a property it notifies exactly the components whose render functions read that property. Reactivity in Vue is the mechanism that makes a user interface update when data changes, and the key difference from a plain variable is that Vue tracks who read a value. value inside script gives you the tracked read and the template unwraps it automatically. A computed property caches a derived value and recomputes only when its dependencies change, which is the right tool for a value that would otherwise be recalculated on every render. The reason the tracking works is contrast with a non reactive variable: a plain let in a function body is invisible to Vue, and mutating it changes nothing on screen. That is also why you are warned against destructuring a reactive object into plain variables, because the tracking link is lost.","value inside script gives you the tracked read and the template unwraps it automatically. Reactivity in Vue is the mechanism that makes a user interface update when data changes, and the key difference from a plain variable is that Vue tracks who read a value. When you return a plain object from setup, Vue wraps it in a Proxy that records property access, and when you write to a property it notifies exactly the components whose render functions read that property. A computed property caches a derived value and recomputes only when its dependencies change, which is the right tool for a value that would otherwise be recalculated on every render. The reason the tracking works is contrast with a non reactive variable: a plain let in a function body is invisible to Vue, and mutating it changes nothing on screen. That is also why you are warned against destructuring a reactive object into plain variables, because the tracking link is lost."],"tl":"reactive","bis":"reactive"},{"id":"pro-vue-000003","topic":"programming-vue","intent":"definition","concept":"vue directive","keywords":["vue directive","v-model","v-if","v-show","v-for","v-bind","v-on","key","directive","attribute","extends","element's","behaviour","rather"],"patterns":["what is vue directive","vue directive","explain vue directive","definition of vue directive","vue directive meaning","what are vue directives","difference between v-if and v-show","why does v-for need a key","ano ang vue directive","unsa ang vue directive"],"answer":"A Vue directive is an attribute that extends an element's behaviour rather than its content, and the built-in ones cover most needs. v-model is the most used: it wires an input or textarea to a value in both directions so typing updates the data and changing the data updates the field. v-if and v-else-if and v-else remove an element from the DOM entirely when the condition is false, while v-show keeps the element and toggles a display style, and the difference matters when the element is expensive to create or holds state. v-for renders a list, taking a value and a key, and that key matters because it is how Vue tracks which element is which when the list is reordered, and using the array index there causes a well known class of state bugs. v-on handles events and v-bind passes attributes, and custom directives are written with the directive lifecycle hooks when something genuinely has no computed equivalent.","variations":["v-model is the most used: it wires an input or textarea to a value in both directions so typing updates the data and changing the data updates the field. A Vue directive is an attribute that extends an element's behaviour rather than its content, and the built-in ones cover most needs. v-if and v-else-if and v-else remove an element from the DOM entirely when the condition is false, while v-show keeps the element and toggles a display style, and the difference matters when the element is expensive to create or holds state. v-for renders a list, taking a value and a key, and that key matters because it is how Vue tracks which element is which when the list is reordered, and using the array index there causes a well known class of state bugs. v-on handles events and v-bind passes attributes, and custom directives are written with the directive lifecycle hooks when something genuinely has no computed equivalent.","v-if and v-else-if and v-else remove an element from the DOM entirely when the condition is false, while v-show keeps the element and toggles a display style, and the difference matters when the element is expensive to create or holds state. A Vue directive is an attribute that extends an element's behaviour rather than its content, and the built-in ones cover most needs. v-model is the most used: it wires an input or textarea to a value in both directions so typing updates the data and changing the data updates the field. v-for renders a list, taking a value and a key, and that key matters because it is how Vue tracks which element is which when the list is reordered, and using the array index there causes a well known class of state bugs. v-on handles events and v-bind passes attributes, and custom directives are written with the directive lifecycle hooks when something genuinely has no computed equivalent."],"tl":"vue directive","bis":"vue directive"},{"id":"pro-vue-000004","topic":"programming-vue","intent":"definition","concept":"vue router","keywords":["vue router","spa","route","pushstate","router","official","routing","library","maps","path"],"patterns":["what is vue router","vue router","explain vue router","definition of vue router","vue router meaning","how does vue router work","what is a navigation guard","ano ang vue router","unsa ang vue router"],"answer":"The Vue Router is the official routing library, and it maps the URL path to a component so a single page application can show different views without a full page load. Routes are declared as a list, each with a path, a component and optionally a name and parameters, and the router renders whichever one matches the current address. Its two modes are the history mode, which uses the pushState API and needs a server rewrite so a deep link serves index.html, and the hash mode, which puts a hash in the URL and needs no server configuration at all. Dynamic segments are declared with a colon, so a path of /user/:id matches many concrete URLs and gives the component the id as a parameter, while a catch-all segment is how a not-found view is expressed. Navigation guards are the feature people reach for first, because they let you redirect an unauthenticated user before the component mounts. The critical caution is that an unmatched path should render a deliberate not-found view rather than a blank page.","variations":["Routes are declared as a list, each with a path, a component and optionally a name and parameters, and the router renders whichever one matches the current address. The Vue Router is the official routing library, and it maps the URL path to a component so a single page application can show different views without a full page load. html, and the hash mode, which puts a hash in the URL and needs no server configuration at all. Dynamic segments are declared with a colon, so a path of /user/:id matches many concrete URLs and gives the component the id as a parameter, while a catch-all segment is how a not-found view is expressed. Navigation guards are the feature people reach for first, because they let you redirect an unauthenticated user before the component mounts. The critical caution is that an unmatched path should render a deliberate not-found view rather than a blank page.","html, and the hash mode, which puts a hash in the URL and needs no server configuration at all. The Vue Router is the official routing library, and it maps the URL path to a component so a single page application can show different views without a full page load. Routes are declared as a list, each with a path, a component and optionally a name and parameters, and the router renders whichever one matches the current address. Dynamic segments are declared with a colon, so a path of /user/:id matches many concrete URLs and gives the component the id as a parameter, while a catch-all segment is how a not-found view is expressed. Navigation guards are the feature people reach for first, because they let you redirect an unauthenticated user before the component mounts. The critical caution is that an unmatched path should render a deliberate not-found view rather than a blank page."],"tl":"vue router","bis":"vue router"}],"lang":"english","group":"programming","sub":"vue","title":"Vue","groupTitle":"Programming","__file":"programming/vue.json"},
  {"topic":"science-astronomy","entries":[{"id":"sci-astronomy-000001","topic":"science-astronomy","intent":"definition","concept":"solar system","keywords":["solar system","sun","planets","orbit","asteroid","formation","system","together","everything","held"],"patterns":["what is solar system","solar system","explain solar system","definition of solar system","solar system meaning","what is the solar system","how many planets are there","how was the solar system formed","ano ang solar system","unsa ang sistema ng sikat"],"answer":"The solar system is the Sun together with everything held in orbit around it: eight planets, their moons, dwarf planets, asteroids, comets and a large band of icy debris called the Kuiper Belt. The four inner planets are small, rocky and close in, while the four outer ones are gas or ice giants much further out, and the arrangement is a leftover of how the system formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a collapsing cloud of gas and dust. The planets orbit in nearly the same direction and in nearly the same plane, which is the fossil record of that formation, and most orbit in almost circular paths because their own gravity and the Sun's have damped out anything else. The Sun holds more than 99.8 percent of the system's mass, so the centre of mass is effectively the Sun itself and every planet simply falls around it.","variations":["6 billion years ago from a collapsing cloud of gas and dust. The solar system is the Sun together with everything held in orbit around it: eight planets, their moons, dwarf planets, asteroids, comets and a large band of icy debris called the Kuiper Belt. The planets orbit in nearly the same direction and in nearly the same plane, which is the fossil record of that formation, and most orbit in almost circular paths because their own gravity and the Sun's have damped out anything else. 8 percent of the system's mass, so the centre of mass is effectively the Sun itself and every planet simply falls around it.","The planets orbit in nearly the same direction and in nearly the same plane, which is the fossil record of that formation, and most orbit in almost circular paths because their own gravity and the Sun's have damped out anything else. The solar system is the Sun together with everything held in orbit around it: eight planets, their moons, dwarf planets, asteroids, comets and a large band of icy debris called the Kuiper Belt. 6 billion years ago from a collapsing cloud of gas and dust. 8 percent of the system's mass, so the centre of mass is effectively the Sun itself and every planet simply falls around it."],"tl":"solar system","bis":"sistema ng sikat"},{"id":"sci-astronomy-000002","topic":"science-astronomy","intent":"definition","concept":"light year","keywords":["light year","parsec","distance","scale","light","year","unit","time","travels"],"patterns":["what is light year","light year","explain light year","definition of light year","light year meaning","how far is a light year","how long is a light year in km","is a light year a unit of time","ano ang light year","unsa ang light year"],"answer":"A light year is a unit of distance, not time, and it is the distance light travels in one year, which works out to roughly 9.46 trillion kilometres. That number is worth holding onto because it is the scale at which the rest of the universe becomes sensible: the nearest star after the Sun is about 4.2 light years away, the centre of our galaxy is about 26,000, and the nearest large galaxy beyond the Milky Way is over two million. The reason astronomers use it is that a light year is defined by the speed of light, which is the same everywhere, so it gives a common ruler for distances that no ordinary unit could span. The common confusion is treating it as a duration, but saying a journey will take ten light years is nonsense in the same way as saying it is ten kilometres long.","variations":["2 light years away, the centre of our galaxy is about 26,000, and the nearest large galaxy beyond the Milky Way is over two million. 46 trillion kilometres. The reason astronomers use it is that a light year is defined by the speed of light, which is the same everywhere, so it gives a common ruler for distances that no ordinary unit could span. The common confusion is treating it as a duration, but saying a journey will take ten light years is nonsense in the same way as saying it is ten kilometres long.","The reason astronomers use it is that a light year is defined by the speed of light, which is the same everywhere, so it gives a common ruler for distances that no ordinary unit could span. 46 trillion kilometres. 2 light years away, the centre of our galaxy is about 26,000, and the nearest large galaxy beyond the Milky Way is over two million. The common confusion is treating it as a duration, but saying a journey will take ten light years is nonsense in the same way as saying it is ten kilometres long."],"tl":"light year","bis":"light year"},{"id":"sci-astronomy-000003","topic":"science-astronomy","intent":"definition","concept":"exoplanet","keywords":["exoplanet","atmosphere","star","detection","telescope","planet","orbiting","matter","existence"],"patterns":["what is exoplanet","exoplanet","explain exoplanet","definition of exoplanet","exoplanet meaning","what is an exoplanet","how do scientists find planets","what is the habitable zone","ano ang exoplanet","unsa ang exoplanet"],"answer":"An exoplanet is a planet orbiting a star other than the Sun, and they matter because their existence was not a prediction from theory so much as something nobody had ever actually seen until a few decades ago. The first confirmed detections came in the 1990s, largely from the radial velocity method, which watches a star for the small periodic wobble in its motion caused by a planet pulling on it, and later from the transit method, which watches for the regular dimming when a planet passes in front of its star. The transit method is the one people describe as a silhouette, and it is also how the atmosphere is probed, because the starlight filtered through a planet's air carries chemical fingerprints. Thousands are now known, and the surprise is the variety: gas giants, rocky worlds, and systems with several planets in tight orbits unlike our own. The open question is how common Earth-sized planets in the habitable zone actually are.","variations":["The first confirmed detections came in the 1990s, largely from the radial velocity method, which watches a star for the small periodic wobble in its motion caused by a planet pulling on it, and later from the transit method, which watches for the regular dimming when a planet passes in front of its star. An exoplanet is a planet orbiting a star other than the Sun, and they matter because their existence was not a prediction from theory so much as something nobody had ever actually seen until a few decades ago. The transit method is the one people describe as a silhouette, and it is also how the atmosphere is probed, because the starlight filtered through a planet's air carries chemical fingerprints. Thousands are now known, and the surprise is the variety: gas giants, rocky worlds, and systems with several planets in tight orbits unlike our own. The open question is how common Earth-sized planets in the habitable zone actually are.","The transit method is the one people describe as a silhouette, and it is also how the atmosphere is probed, because the starlight filtered through a planet's air carries chemical fingerprints. An exoplanet is a planet orbiting a star other than the Sun, and they matter because their existence was not a prediction from theory so much as something nobody had ever actually seen until a few decades ago. The first confirmed detections came in the 1990s, largely from the radial velocity method, which watches a star for the small periodic wobble in its motion caused by a planet pulling on it, and later from the transit method, which watches for the regular dimming when a planet passes in front of its star. Thousands are now known, and the surprise is the variety: gas giants, rocky worlds, and systems with several planets in tight orbits unlike our own. The open question is how common Earth-sized planets in the habitable zone actually are."],"tl":"exoplanet","bis":"exoplanet"},{"id":"sci-astronomy-000004","topic":"science-astronomy","intent":"definition","concept":"telescope","keywords":["telescope","aperture","lens","mirror","resolution","radio","observatory","instrument","collects","light","area","larger"],"patterns":["what is telescope","telescope","explain telescope","definition of telescope","telescope meaning","what is a telescope","how does a telescope work","why put telescopes in space","ano ang telescope","unsa ang telescope"],"answer":"A telescope is an instrument that collects light over an area larger than your eye and concentrates it, and everything else it does is a consequence of that collection. The two designs are the refracting telescope, which uses a lens, and the reflecting telescope, which uses a curved mirror, with the reflecting design dominant for anything large because a mirror can be supported from behind and a lens cannot. Beyond gathering light, resolution is the other limit: two points can be separated only if the aperture is large enough relative to the wavelength, which is why a large telescope in space can see detail a larger one on the ground cannot, since the atmosphere blurs light before it arrives. The practical categories are the optical for visible light, the radio for long wavelengths, and the space observatories such as Hubble and James Webb that avoid atmospheric distortion entirely. A modern ground instrument uses adaptive optics, adjustable mirrors that correct for the distortion in real time.","variations":["The two designs are the refracting telescope, which uses a lens, and the reflecting telescope, which uses a curved mirror, with the reflecting design dominant for anything large because a mirror can be supported from behind and a lens cannot. A telescope is an instrument that collects light over an area larger than your eye and concentrates it, and everything else it does is a consequence of that collection. Beyond gathering light, resolution is the other limit: two points can be separated only if the aperture is large enough relative to the wavelength, which is why a large telescope in space can see detail a larger one on the ground cannot, since the atmosphere blurs light before it arrives. The practical categories are the optical for visible light, the radio for long wavelengths, and the space observatories such as Hubble and James Webb that avoid atmospheric distortion entirely. A modern ground instrument uses adaptive optics, adjustable mirrors that correct for the distortion in real time.","Beyond gathering light, resolution is the other limit: two points can be separated only if the aperture is large enough relative to the wavelength, which is why a large telescope in space can see detail a larger one on the ground cannot, since the atmosphere blurs light before it arrives. A telescope is an instrument that collects light over an area larger than your eye and concentrates it, and everything else it does is a consequence of that collection. The two designs are the refracting telescope, which uses a lens, and the reflecting telescope, which uses a curved mirror, with the reflecting design dominant for anything large because a mirror can be supported from behind and a lens cannot. The practical categories are the optical for visible light, the radio for long wavelengths, and the space observatories such as Hubble and James Webb that avoid atmospheric distortion entirely. A modern ground instrument uses adaptive optics, adjustable mirrors that correct for the distortion in real time."],"tl":"telescope","bis":"telescope"},{"id":"sci-astronomy-000005","topic":"science-astronomy","intent":"definition","concept":"satellite","keywords":["satellite","orbit","moon","gps","coverage","debris","object","placed","around","another"],"patterns":["what is satellite","satellite","explain satellite","definition of satellite","satellite meaning","what is a satellite","how do satellites stay in orbit","what is a low earth orbit","ano ang satellite","unsa ang satellite"],"answer":"A satellite is an object placed into orbit around another body, and the word covers two quite different things that are easy to confuse. A natural satellite is a body that a planet holds, so the Moon is Earth's only natural satellite and the other planets have their own sets. An artificial satellite is a machine put into orbit deliberately, and it stays up because it is continually falling toward the Earth and missing it, which is the clearest way to understand orbit at all: the thing is falling permanently and the curve of its fall keeps matching the curve of the surface. A satellite in low orbit follows the ground closely and is used for imaging and for the constellations that make global positioning work, while one in a higher orbit takes longer to pass over the same point, which is what a communications satellite at about 35,000 km above the equator relies on, because at that height it moves exactly as fast as the Earth turns. A satellite is not free to place just anywhere, since the lower band is crowded with debris from old launches.","variations":["A natural satellite is a body that a planet holds, so the Moon is Earth's only natural satellite and the other planets have their own sets. A satellite is an object placed into orbit around another body, and the word covers two quite different things that are easy to confuse. An artificial satellite is a machine put into orbit deliberately, and it stays up because it is continually falling toward the Earth and missing it, which is the clearest way to understand orbit at all: the thing is falling permanently and the curve of its fall keeps matching the curve of the surface. A satellite in low orbit follows the ground closely and is used for imaging and for the constellations that make global positioning work, while one in a higher orbit takes longer to pass over the same point, which is what a communications satellite at about 35,000 km above the equator relies on, because at that height it moves exactly as fast as the Earth turns. A satellite is not free to place just anywhere, since the lower band is crowded with debris from old launches.","An artificial satellite is a machine put into orbit deliberately, and it stays up because it is continually falling toward the Earth and missing it, which is the clearest way to understand orbit at all: the thing is falling permanently and the curve of its fall keeps matching the curve of the surface. A satellite is an object placed into orbit around another body, and the word covers two quite different things that are easy to confuse. A natural satellite is a body that a planet holds, so the Moon is Earth's only natural satellite and the other planets have their own sets. A satellite in low orbit follows the ground closely and is used for imaging and for the constellations that make global positioning work, while one in a higher orbit takes longer to pass over the same point, which is what a communications satellite at about 35,000 km above the equator relies on, because at that height it moves exactly as fast as the Earth turns. A satellite is not free to place just anywhere, since the lower band is crowded with debris from old launches."],"tl":"satellite","bis":"satellite"},{"id":"sci-astronomy-000006","topic":"science-astronomy","intent":"definition","concept":"comet","keywords":["comet","tail","ice","nucleus","orbit","periodic","halley","sublimation","kometa","small","body","system","mostly"],"patterns":["what is comet","comet","explain comet","definition of comet","comet meaning","what is a comet","why does a comet tail point away from the sun","what is a periodic comet","ano ang kometa","unsa ang comet"],"answer":"A comet is a small body in the solar system, mostly ice and dust, whose orbit brings it near the Sun at intervals, and the reason it is visible at all is what happens to it there. Far from the Sun the ice stays solid and it is a dark point of light. Approaching, the heat volatilises the ice into gas, and the gas and dust stream off, forming the tail. The tail always points away from the Sun rather than away from the comet, which is counter-intuitive and was one of the first things measured once comet orbits were understood well enough. That is the clue to what the tail is: it is matter being blown off, not a streak left behind as the comet travels. A comet has been called a dirty snowball for that reason, and the solid centre, the nucleus, is only a few kilometres across. Famous ones such as Halley's have orbits measured in decades or longer, so the same one returning on a schedule is a periodic comet.","variations":["Far from the Sun the ice stays solid and it is a dark point of light. A comet is a small body in the solar system, mostly ice and dust, whose orbit brings it near the Sun at intervals, and the reason it is visible at all is what happens to it there. Approaching, the heat volatilises the ice into gas, and the gas and dust stream off, forming the tail. The tail always points away from the Sun rather than away from the comet, which is counter-intuitive and was one of the first things measured once comet orbits were understood well enough. That is the clue to what the tail is: it is matter being blown off, not a streak left behind as the comet travels. A comet has been called a dirty snowball for that reason, and the solid centre, the nucleus, is only a few kilometres across. Famous ones such as Halley's have orbits measured in decades or longer, so the same one returning on a schedule is a periodic comet.","Approaching, the heat volatilises the ice into gas, and the gas and dust stream off, forming the tail. A comet is a small body in the solar system, mostly ice and dust, whose orbit brings it near the Sun at intervals, and the reason it is visible at all is what happens to it there. Far from the Sun the ice stays solid and it is a dark point of light. The tail always points away from the Sun rather than away from the comet, which is counter-intuitive and was one of the first things measured once comet orbits were understood well enough. That is the clue to what the tail is: it is matter being blown off, not a streak left behind as the comet travels. A comet has been called a dirty snowball for that reason, and the solid centre, the nucleus, is only a few kilometres across. Famous ones such as Halley's have orbits measured in decades or longer, so the same one returning on a schedule is a periodic comet."],"tl":"kometa","bis":"comet"},{"id":"sci-astronomy-000007","topic":"science-astronomy","intent":"definition","concept":"solar flare","keywords":["solar flare","sunspot","aurora","flare","sudden","violent","release","magnetic"],"patterns":["what is solar flare","solar flare","explain solar flare","definition of solar flare","solar flare meaning","what is a solar flare","what is a coronal mass ejection","can a solar flare reach earth","ano ang solar flare","unsa ang solar flare"],"answer":"A solar flare is a sudden, violent release of magnetic energy in the Sun's atmosphere that releases electromagnetic radiation across the whole spectrum, from radio waves to X-rays, and it happens in minutes rather than hours. The mechanism is magnetic reconnection, where twisted field lines suddenly untwist and release the energy that was stored in them, and the active regions where it occurs are the sunspot groups. Near the Earth the radiation arrives in about eight minutes, so a flare is visible as soon as it happens, while the material it throws out travels more slowly and takes a day or more to arrive, which is why the two events are described separately. The practical consequences fall into three kinds: radio blackouts for radio navigation and shortwave near the daylight side, radiation storms that polar aurora reach down towards mid latitudes, and a danger to astronauts and to satellites in low orbit who need shelter or a warning. A flare is a different phenomenon from a coronal mass ejection, which is the slower and much larger ejection of gas, and the confusion is common because a powerful active region produces both.","variations":["The mechanism is magnetic reconnection, where twisted field lines suddenly untwist and release the energy that was stored in them, and the active regions where it occurs are the sunspot groups. A solar flare is a sudden, violent release of magnetic energy in the Sun's atmosphere that releases electromagnetic radiation across the whole spectrum, from radio waves to X-rays, and it happens in minutes rather than hours. Near the Earth the radiation arrives in about eight minutes, so a flare is visible as soon as it happens, while the material it throws out travels more slowly and takes a day or more to arrive, which is why the two events are described separately. The practical consequences fall into three kinds: radio blackouts for radio navigation and shortwave near the daylight side, radiation storms that polar aurora reach down towards mid latitudes, and a danger to astronauts and to satellites in low orbit who need shelter or a warning. A flare is a different phenomenon from a coronal mass ejection, which is the slower and much larger ejection of gas, and the confusion is common because a powerful active region produces both.","Near the Earth the radiation arrives in about eight minutes, so a flare is visible as soon as it happens, while the material it throws out travels more slowly and takes a day or more to arrive, which is why the two events are described separately. A solar flare is a sudden, violent release of magnetic energy in the Sun's atmosphere that releases electromagnetic radiation across the whole spectrum, from radio waves to X-rays, and it happens in minutes rather than hours. The mechanism is magnetic reconnection, where twisted field lines suddenly untwist and release the energy that was stored in them, and the active regions where it occurs are the sunspot groups. The practical consequences fall into three kinds: radio blackouts for radio navigation and shortwave near the daylight side, radiation storms that polar aurora reach down towards mid latitudes, and a danger to astronauts and to satellites in low orbit who need shelter or a warning. A flare is a different phenomenon from a coronal mass ejection, which is the slower and much larger ejection of gas, and the confusion is common because a powerful active region produces both."],"tl":"solar flare","bis":"solar flare"},{"id":"sci-astronomy-000008","topic":"science-astronomy","intent":"definition","concept":"black hole","keywords":["black hole","singularity","gravitational","collapse","spacetime","accretion","einstein","black","hole","region","space","gravity","strong"],"patterns":["what is black hole","black hole","explain black hole","definition of black hole","black hole meaning","what is a black hole","what is an event horizon","can you escape a black hole","ano ang black hole","unsa ang black hole"],"answer":"A black hole is a region of space whose gravity is strong enough that nothing, including light, can escape it, and it is what remains when a very massive star collapses at the end of its life. It is not a solid object: it has no surface, and the boundary called the event horizon is a mathematical point in space beyond which every possible path leads inward, which is why it looks like an absence rather than a thing. The defining observation is that a supermassive black hole at the centre of a galaxy bends light so strongly that it can be detected indirectly, and when material falls in it heats up and radiates, which is what makes active galactic centres so bright. If you were falling in, from outside, you would cross the horizon without noticing anything unusual and only later notice that light from outside can no longer reach you. The prediction came from Einstein's general relativity in 1915 and the first direct image was published in 2019.","variations":["It is not a solid object: it has no surface, and the boundary called the event horizon is a mathematical point in space beyond which every possible path leads inward, which is why it looks like an absence rather than a thing. A black hole is a region of space whose gravity is strong enough that nothing, including light, can escape it, and it is what remains when a very massive star collapses at the end of its life. The defining observation is that a supermassive black hole at the centre of a galaxy bends light so strongly that it can be detected indirectly, and when material falls in it heats up and radiates, which is what makes active galactic centres so bright. If you were falling in, from outside, you would cross the horizon without noticing anything unusual and only later notice that light from outside can no longer reach you. The prediction came from Einstein's general relativity in 1915 and the first direct image was published in 2019.","The defining observation is that a supermassive black hole at the centre of a galaxy bends light so strongly that it can be detected indirectly, and when material falls in it heats up and radiates, which is what makes active galactic centres so bright. A black hole is a region of space whose gravity is strong enough that nothing, including light, can escape it, and it is what remains when a very massive star collapses at the end of its life. It is not a solid object: it has no surface, and the boundary called the event horizon is a mathematical point in space beyond which every possible path leads inward, which is why it looks like an absence rather than a thing. If you were falling in, from outside, you would cross the horizon without noticing anything unusual and only later notice that light from outside can no longer reach you. The prediction came from Einstein's general relativity in 1915 and the first direct image was published in 2019."],"tl":"black hole","bis":"black hole"}],"lang":"english","group":"science","sub":"astronomy","title":"Astronomy","groupTitle":"Science","__file":"science/astronomy.json"},
  {"topic":"science-biology","entries":[{"id":"sci-biology-000001","topic":"science-biology","intent":"definition","concept":"dna","keywords":["dna","genetic","gene","rna","protein","cell","molecule","stores","hereditary","information","almost","living"],"patterns":["what is dna","dna","explain dna","definition of dna","dna meaning","what is a gene","how do genes work","ano ang dna","unsa ang dna"],"answer":"DNA is the molecule that stores hereditary information in almost all living things. It is a double helix of four bases - A, T, C and G - and the order of those bases is the instruction set. A gene is a stretch of DNA that codes for a protein, and those proteins do nearly everything a cell does. You inherit half from each parent.","variations":["It is a double helix of four bases - A, T, C and G - and the order of those bases is the instruction set. DNA is the molecule that stores hereditary information in almost all living things. A gene is a stretch of DNA that codes for a protein, and those proteins do nearly everything a cell does. You inherit half from each parent.","A gene is a stretch of DNA that codes for a protein, and those proteins do nearly everything a cell does. DNA is the molecule that stores hereditary information in almost all living things. It is a double helix of four bases - A, T, C and G - and the order of those bases is the instruction set. You inherit half from each parent."],"tl":"dna","bis":"dna","langs":{"tagalog":"Ang DNA ay ang molekulang nag-iimbak ng impormasyong minana sa halos lahat ng bagay na buhay. Ang anyo nito ay isang baligtad na hagdan: dalawang mahabang hibla na magkatikom, may paires ng base na kemikal - karaniwang isinusulat bilang A, T, C at G - na nagsasama bilang hagdan. Ang pagkakasunod ng mga base ang siyang code, at ang pagbabasa nito ay sinasabi sa cell kung ano ang gagawin at kailan. Nagbibigay ang bawat magulang ng half, kaya katulad ng magulang ang mga anak ngunit hindi sila kopya ninuman sa kanila. Ang RNA ang kaugnay na molekulang talagang bumabasa ng code na iyon at gumagawa ng bagay rito. Halos lahat nito ay pareho sa halaman, hayop at bakteriya, kaya karaniwang gumagana sa yeast cell ang bahagi ng DNA ng tao, at kaya matutukoy ng ebidensya ng DNA ang isang tao mula sa buhok.","bisaya":"Ang DNA mao ang molecule nga nag-store sa hereditary info sa tanan nga living nga butang. Ang iyang anyo mao ang twirly nga hagdan: duha ka long nga strand nga maghagkot, naa nga pairs nga chemical nga base - kasagaran gisulat nga A, T, C ug G - nga naghugot nga sama sa mga rung. Ang order sa mga base na iya ang code, ug pagbasa niini maka-sabi sa cell unsang gayahon nga imong himoon ug kanus-a. Kaayo og katugtang matud ang parents nga katugtang anak nga dili man naa gayud nga kopya. Ang RNA mao ang related nga molecule nga gyud nga mabasa sa code ug himoon ang mga butas gikan niini. Kayo gyud nga pareho niini sa mga tanom, hayop ug bacteria, kana man kay kasagaran maa gyud nga human DNA ang moduha sa yeast cell, ug kana man nga DNA evidence makadawat og usa ka tawo gikan sa buhok."},"confidence":null},{"id":"sci-biology-000002","topic":"science-biology","intent":"definition","concept":"photosynthesis","keywords":["photosynthesis","plant","chloroplast","glucose","oxygen","plants","turn","light","chemical","energy"],"patterns":["what is photosynthesis","photosynthesis","explain photosynthesis","definition of photosynthesis","photosynthesis meaning","where does photosynthesis happen","what gas does it release","ano ang photosynthesis","unsa ang photosynthesis"],"answer":"Photosynthesis is how plants turn light into chemical energy, using carbon dioxide and water to make glucose and release oxygen. It happens in the chloroplast, and the light reactions split water to supply electrons and energy while a separate cycle fixes carbon. The reason it matters is not the sugar, which is only an intermediate, but that it is the origin of almost all organic carbon in the biosphere and of the oxygen in the atmosphere.","variations":["It happens in the chloroplast, and the light reactions split water to supply electrons and energy while a separate cycle fixes carbon. Photosynthesis is how plants turn light into chemical energy, using carbon dioxide and water to make glucose and release oxygen. The reason it matters is not the sugar, which is only an intermediate, but that it is the origin of almost all organic carbon in the biosphere and of the oxygen in the atmosphere.","The reason it matters is not the sugar, which is only an intermediate, but that it is the origin of almost all organic carbon in the biosphere and of the oxygen in the atmosphere. Photosynthesis is how plants turn light into chemical energy, using carbon dioxide and water to make glucose and release oxygen. It happens in the chloroplast, and the light reactions split water to supply electrons and energy while a separate cycle fixes carbon."],"tl":"photosynthesis","bis":"photosynthesis"},{"id":"sci-biology-000003","topic":"science-biology","intent":"definition","concept":"evolution","keywords":["evolution","variation","species","adaptation","change","inherited","characteristics","population","generations"],"patterns":["what is evolution","evolution","explain evolution","definition of evolution","evolution meaning","does evolution have a purpose","how does natural selection work","ano ang evolution","unsa ang evolution"],"answer":"Evolution is change in the inherited characteristics of a population over generations, driven by heritable variation filtered by selection. It does not have a purpose, and that is the part people most often get wrong, because nothing in the mechanism is aiming at anything. It acts on existing variation rather than creating what is needed, which is why pressures in our environment produce no new adaptations in our own lifetime.","variations":["It does not have a purpose, and that is the part people most often get wrong, because nothing in the mechanism is aiming at anything. Evolution is change in the inherited characteristics of a population over generations, driven by heritable variation filtered by selection. It acts on existing variation rather than creating what is needed, which is why pressures in our environment produce no new adaptations in our own lifetime.","It acts on existing variation rather than creating what is needed, which is why pressures in our environment produce no new adaptations in our own lifetime. Evolution is change in the inherited characteristics of a population over generations, driven by heritable variation filtered by selection. It does not have a purpose, and that is the part people most often get wrong, because nothing in the mechanism is aiming at anything."],"tl":"evolution","bis":"evolution"},{"id":"sci-biology-000004","topic":"science-biology","intent":"definition","concept":"cell","keywords":["cell","nucleus","membrane","mitochondria","prokaryotic","smallest","unit","life","either"],"patterns":["what is cell","cell","explain cell","definition of cell","cell meaning","what is a cell","difference between prokaryotic and eukaryotic","what is a mitochondrion","ano ang cell","unsa ang cell"],"answer":"A cell is the smallest unit of life, and it is either prokaryotic with no nucleus or eukaryotic with one and other membrane-bound organelles. The nucleus protects the DNA and lets regulated copies be made, which is the innovation that allowed complex cells and eventually multicellular life. Mitochondria are separate organisms that were absorbed rather than invented, and they still carry their own small genome.","variations":["The nucleus protects the DNA and lets regulated copies be made, which is the innovation that allowed complex cells and eventually multicellular life. A cell is the smallest unit of life, and it is either prokaryotic with no nucleus or eukaryotic with one and other membrane-bound organelles. Mitochondria are separate organisms that were absorbed rather than invented, and they still carry their own small genome.","Mitochondria are separate organisms that were absorbed rather than invented, and they still carry their own small genome. A cell is the smallest unit of life, and it is either prokaryotic with no nucleus or eukaryotic with one and other membrane-bound organelles. The nucleus protects the DNA and lets regulated copies be made, which is the innovation that allowed complex cells and eventually multicellular life."],"tl":"cell","bis":"cell"},{"id":"sci-biology-000005","topic":"science-biology","intent":"definition","concept":"genetic","keywords":["genetic","dna","inheritance","trait","chromosome","carrier","heredity","variation","genetiko","means","relating","information","passed","parents"],"patterns":["what is genetic","genetic","explain genetic","definition of genetic","genetic meaning","what does inherited mean","what is a carrier","ano ang genetiko","unsa ang genetic"],"answer":"Genetic means relating to the information passed from parents to offspring, and the word is used for two different things that a learner often runs together: a genetic trait is a characteristic, while a genetic condition is an illness arising from a change in that information. The information itself is written in the DNA, in a sequence of four chemical letters, and instructions for building every part of an organism are laid out along it like an extremely long line of text. What makes it remarkable is how much of the variation in living things comes from changes in single letters rather than from whole genes, and how many such changes are harmless. This is why a family resemblance is a matter of probability rather than of copying: a child inherits one set from each parent, and the visible outcome is a mix influenced by many separate instructions. The practical consequence is that inherited conditions run in families in a recognisable pattern, and genetic counselling exists to estimate that probability before someone has children. It also explains why a trait can skip a generation, since a carrier may show nothing and still pass it on.","variations":["The information itself is written in the DNA, in a sequence of four chemical letters, and instructions for building every part of an organism are laid out along it like an extremely long line of text. Genetic means relating to the information passed from parents to offspring, and the word is used for two different things that a learner often runs together: a genetic trait is a characteristic, while a genetic condition is an illness arising from a change in that information. What makes it remarkable is how much of the variation in living things comes from changes in single letters rather than from whole genes, and how many such changes are harmless. This is why a family resemblance is a matter of probability rather than of copying: a child inherits one set from each parent, and the visible outcome is a mix influenced by many separate instructions. The practical consequence is that inherited conditions run in families in a recognisable pattern, and genetic counselling exists to estimate that probability before someone has children. It also explains why a trait can skip a generation, since a carrier may show nothing and still pass it on.","What makes it remarkable is how much of the variation in living things comes from changes in single letters rather than from whole genes, and how many such changes are harmless. Genetic means relating to the information passed from parents to offspring, and the word is used for two different things that a learner often runs together: a genetic trait is a characteristic, while a genetic condition is an illness arising from a change in that information. The information itself is written in the DNA, in a sequence of four chemical letters, and instructions for building every part of an organism are laid out along it like an extremely long line of text. This is why a family resemblance is a matter of probability rather than of copying: a child inherits one set from each parent, and the visible outcome is a mix influenced by many separate instructions. The practical consequence is that inherited conditions run in families in a recognisable pattern, and genetic counselling exists to estimate that probability before someone has children. It also explains why a trait can skip a generation, since a carrier may show nothing and still pass it on."],"tl":"genetiko","bis":"genetic"}],"lang":"english","group":"science","sub":"biology","title":"Biology","groupTitle":"Science","__file":"science/biology.json"},
  {"topic":"science-chemistry","entries":[{"id":"sci-chemistry-000001","topic":"science-chemistry","intent":"definition","concept":"atom","keywords":["atom","proton","neutron","electron","nucleus","smallest","unit","ordinary","matter","keeps"],"patterns":["what is atom","atom","explain atom","definition of atom","atom meaning","what is an atom","what is inside an atom","what is an atomic number","ano ang atom","unsa ang atom"],"answer":"An atom is the smallest unit of ordinary matter that keeps an element's properties, made of a nucleus of protons and neutrons surrounded by electrons. The number of protons is the atomic number and it alone defines which element it is, so changing it changes the element. The electrons determine the chemistry, and the electron shells filling in a particular pattern is the whole reason the periodic table has a shape.","variations":["The number of protons is the atomic number and it alone defines which element it is, so changing it changes the element. An atom is the smallest unit of ordinary matter that keeps an element's properties, made of a nucleus of protons and neutrons surrounded by electrons. The electrons determine the chemistry, and the electron shells filling in a particular pattern is the whole reason the periodic table has a shape.","The electrons determine the chemistry, and the electron shells filling in a particular pattern is the whole reason the periodic table has a shape. An atom is the smallest unit of ordinary matter that keeps an element's properties, made of a nucleus of protons and neutrons surrounded by electrons. The number of protons is the atomic number and it alone defines which element it is, so changing it changes the element."],"tl":"atom","bis":"atom"},{"id":"sci-chemistry-000002","topic":"science-chemistry","intent":"definition","concept":"periodic table","keywords":["periodic table","element","group","period","valence","atomic","periodic","table","arranges","elements","number"],"patterns":["what is periodic table","periodic table","explain periodic table","definition of periodic table","periodic table meaning","how is the periodic table organised","what does group number mean","why are there no gaps","ano ang periodic table","unsa ang periodic table"],"answer":"The periodic table arranges the elements by atomic number, which puts elements with similar electron structures into the same column. That is why it works as a predictor rather than a list, because an element's position tells you how it will react and the group number gives the valence electrons. The table has no gaps and no exceptions, which is a stronger claim about physics than it looks.","variations":["That is why it works as a predictor rather than a list, because an element's position tells you how it will react and the group number gives the valence electrons. The periodic table arranges the elements by atomic number, which puts elements with similar electron structures into the same column. The table has no gaps and no exceptions, which is a stronger claim about physics than it looks.","The table has no gaps and no exceptions, which is a stronger claim about physics than it looks. The periodic table arranges the elements by atomic number, which puts elements with similar electron structures into the same column. That is why it works as a predictor rather than a list, because an element's position tells you how it will react and the group number gives the valence electrons."],"tl":"periodic table","bis":"periodic table"},{"id":"sci-chemistry-000003","topic":"science-chemistry","intent":"definition","concept":"chemical bond","keywords":["chemical bond","bond","covalent","ionic","metallic","electron","molecule","atoms","being","bonded","lower","energy"],"patterns":["what is chemical bond","chemical bond","explain chemical bond","definition of chemical bond","chemical bond meaning","what is a chemical bond","difference between ionic and covalent","what is valence","ano ang chemical bond","unsa ang chemical bond"],"answer":"Atoms bond because being bonded is a lower energy state than being separate, and the type of bond is decided by how the electrons are shared or transferred. Covalent bonds share electron pairs and produce the familiar molecules, ionic bonds are a transfer that leaves charged ions, and metallic bonds are a shared sea of electrons holding a lattice together. Bond type predicts properties well, which is why it is taught before any reaction.","variations":["Covalent bonds share electron pairs and produce the familiar molecules, ionic bonds are a transfer that leaves charged ions, and metallic bonds are a shared sea of electrons holding a lattice together. Atoms bond because being bonded is a lower energy state than being separate, and the type of bond is decided by how the electrons are shared or transferred. Bond type predicts properties well, which is why it is taught before any reaction.","Bond type predicts properties well, which is why it is taught before any reaction. Atoms bond because being bonded is a lower energy state than being separate, and the type of bond is decided by how the electrons are shared or transferred. Covalent bonds share electron pairs and produce the familiar molecules, ionic bonds are a transfer that leaves charged ions, and metallic bonds are a shared sea of electrons holding a lattice together."],"tl":"chemical bond","bis":"chemical bond"},{"id":"sci-chemistry-000004","topic":"science-chemistry","intent":"definition","concept":"molecule","keywords":["molecule","atom","fixed","arrangement","water","moles","identity","substance","molekyul","atoms","joined","defining"],"patterns":["what is molecule","molecule","explain molecule","definition of molecule","molecule meaning","what is a molecule","difference between molecule and compound","how many molecules in a drop","ano ang molekyul","unsa ang molecule"],"answer":"A molecule is two or more atoms joined in a fixed arrangement, and the defining property is that a molecule has a definite size and shape, which is what separates it from the substance it forms part of. Water is the standard example: two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom joined at an angle, and because the shape is fixed the properties of water are fixed, which is why it behaves the same way everywhere rather than varying with the batch. That word is important, because a liquid such as water is not a pile of separate molecules each the same size, while in a gas the molecules are far apart and the substance has no fixed shape of its own. Molecules are also mostly far smaller than what can be seen, so a single drop contains an enormous number of them and the number is the reason chemical quantities are described in moles. A molecule is the smallest particle of a substance that keeps its identity, and splitting a water molecule gives hydrogen and oxygen, which are different substances with none of water's properties.","variations":["Water is the standard example: two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom joined at an angle, and because the shape is fixed the properties of water are fixed, which is why it behaves the same way everywhere rather than varying with the batch. A molecule is two or more atoms joined in a fixed arrangement, and the defining property is that a molecule has a definite size and shape, which is what separates it from the substance it forms part of. That word is important, because a liquid such as water is not a pile of separate molecules each the same size, while in a gas the molecules are far apart and the substance has no fixed shape of its own. Molecules are also mostly far smaller than what can be seen, so a single drop contains an enormous number of them and the number is the reason chemical quantities are described in moles. A molecule is the smallest particle of a substance that keeps its identity, and splitting a water molecule gives hydrogen and oxygen, which are different substances with none of water's properties.","That word is important, because a liquid such as water is not a pile of separate molecules each the same size, while in a gas the molecules are far apart and the substance has no fixed shape of its own. A molecule is two or more atoms joined in a fixed arrangement, and the defining property is that a molecule has a definite size and shape, which is what separates it from the substance it forms part of. Water is the standard example: two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom joined at an angle, and because the shape is fixed the properties of water are fixed, which is why it behaves the same way everywhere rather than varying with the batch. Molecules are also mostly far smaller than what can be seen, so a single drop contains an enormous number of them and the number is the reason chemical quantities are described in moles. A molecule is the smallest particle of a substance that keeps its identity, and splitting a water molecule gives hydrogen and oxygen, which are different substances with none of water's properties."],"tl":"molekyul","bis":"molecule"},{"id":"sci-chemistry-000005","topic":"science-chemistry","intent":"definition","concept":"mixture","keywords":["mixture","compound","separation","physical","solution","properties","alloy","element","combination","substances","together","joined","single"],"patterns":["what is mixture","mixture","explain mixture","definition of mixture","mixture meaning","what is a mixture","mixture vs compound","how do you separate a mixture","ano ang mixture","unsa ang mixture"],"answer":"A mixture is a combination of substances that are together but not joined, and the single test is whether each part keeps its own properties and can be separated again. Air is the everyday example, since the nitrogen and oxygen in it are mixed rather than bonded, and each one is still nitrogen or oxygen after you take it back out. That is what separates a mixture from a compound: a compound is a fixed ratio joined in a definite arrangement with properties of its own, while a mixture can vary in proportion and keeps the properties of its parts. A second difference is that mixtures are separated by physical means such as filtering, distilling or letting something settle, while a compound has to be broken chemically. The same logic explains why the melting point of a mixture is not a single clean temperature the way a pure substance is, and why alloying a metal changes its properties without producing anything new.","variations":["Air is the everyday example, since the nitrogen and oxygen in it are mixed rather than bonded, and each one is still nitrogen or oxygen after you take it back out. A mixture is a combination of substances that are together but not joined, and the single test is whether each part keeps its own properties and can be separated again. That is what separates a mixture from a compound: a compound is a fixed ratio joined in a definite arrangement with properties of its own, while a mixture can vary in proportion and keeps the properties of its parts. A second difference is that mixtures are separated by physical means such as filtering, distilling or letting something settle, while a compound has to be broken chemically. The same logic explains why the melting point of a mixture is not a single clean temperature the way a pure substance is, and why alloying a metal changes its properties without producing anything new.","That is what separates a mixture from a compound: a compound is a fixed ratio joined in a definite arrangement with properties of its own, while a mixture can vary in proportion and keeps the properties of its parts. A mixture is a combination of substances that are together but not joined, and the single test is whether each part keeps its own properties and can be separated again. Air is the everyday example, since the nitrogen and oxygen in it are mixed rather than bonded, and each one is still nitrogen or oxygen after you take it back out. A second difference is that mixtures are separated by physical means such as filtering, distilling or letting something settle, while a compound has to be broken chemically. The same logic explains why the melting point of a mixture is not a single clean temperature the way a pure substance is, and why alloying a metal changes its properties without producing anything new."],"tl":"mixture","bis":"mixture"},{"id":"sci-chemistry-000006","topic":"science-chemistry","intent":"definition","concept":"solvent","keywords":["solvent","solute","dissolve","solution","soap","polar","saturated","temperature","solente","substance","dissolves","another","being","dissolved"],"patterns":["what is solvent","solvent","explain solvent","definition of solvent","solvent meaning","what is a solvent","what is a solute","like dissolves like","ano ang solente","unsa ang solvent"],"answer":"A solvent is a substance that dissolves another, and the substance being dissolved is the solute, and together they form a solution. Water is the everyday example and dissolves a surprising amount: salt, sugar, most acids and bases, and a family of molecules called ionic compounds because they split into charged pieces in it. What it does not dissolve is the important half of the story, and this is why washing cannot remove every kind of stain. Oil does not dissolve in water, which is why a greasy pan needs soap: soap molecules have a water-friendly end and an oil-friendly end, so they bridge the two and let the oil wash away. The general rule is that like dissolves like, meaning a polar solvent dissolves polar substances and a non-polar one dissolves non-polar substances, and that single idea explains most of the cases. Temperature and the amount of solute also matter, since a saturated solution holds as much as it can at that temperature, and most solids dissolve better in a hot liquid, which is why a hot drink takes sugar faster than a cold one.","variations":["Water is the everyday example and dissolves a surprising amount: salt, sugar, most acids and bases, and a family of molecules called ionic compounds because they split into charged pieces in it. A solvent is a substance that dissolves another, and the substance being dissolved is the solute, and together they form a solution. What it does not dissolve is the important half of the story, and this is why washing cannot remove every kind of stain. Oil does not dissolve in water, which is why a greasy pan needs soap: soap molecules have a water-friendly end and an oil-friendly end, so they bridge the two and let the oil wash away. The general rule is that like dissolves like, meaning a polar solvent dissolves polar substances and a non-polar one dissolves non-polar substances, and that single idea explains most of the cases. Temperature and the amount of solute also matter, since a saturated solution holds as much as it can at that temperature, and most solids dissolve better in a hot liquid, which is why a hot drink takes sugar faster than a cold one.","What it does not dissolve is the important half of the story, and this is why washing cannot remove every kind of stain. A solvent is a substance that dissolves another, and the substance being dissolved is the solute, and together they form a solution. Water is the everyday example and dissolves a surprising amount: salt, sugar, most acids and bases, and a family of molecules called ionic compounds because they split into charged pieces in it. Oil does not dissolve in water, which is why a greasy pan needs soap: soap molecules have a water-friendly end and an oil-friendly end, so they bridge the two and let the oil wash away. The general rule is that like dissolves like, meaning a polar solvent dissolves polar substances and a non-polar one dissolves non-polar substances, and that single idea explains most of the cases. Temperature and the amount of solute also matter, since a saturated solution holds as much as it can at that temperature, and most solids dissolve better in a hot liquid, which is why a hot drink takes sugar faster than a cold one."],"tl":"solente","bis":"solvent"},{"id":"sci-chemistry-000007","topic":"science-chemistry","intent":"definition","concept":"concentration","keywords":["concentration","molarity","grams","saturated","dilute","solubility","temperature","konsentrasyon","substance","present","amount","solution","units"],"patterns":["what is concentration","concentration","explain concentration","definition of concentration","concentration meaning","what is molarity","what is the difference between concentrated and dilute","ano ang konsentrasyon","unsa ang concentration"],"answer":"Concentration is how much of a substance is present in a given amount of solution, and the units are the practical part, since the same quantity can be expressed in several ways that are not interchangeable. The everyday one is grams per litre or per hundred millilitres, which is what a medicine label states, while the precise chemistry one is moles per litre, called molarity, which is what a calculation uses. Both describe the same thing and neither is wrong, so the danger is a number being read in the wrong unit. Concentration also changes with temperature: a gas becomes less soluble in a warmer liquid, which is why a warm fizzy drink goes flat faster than a cold one, and a closed container of it holds its gas better when chilled. The two words people confuse are concentrated, meaning strong, and diluted, meaning weakened, and the act of weakening is dilution, which is a dilution calculation.","variations":["The everyday one is grams per litre or per hundred millilitres, which is what a medicine label states, while the precise chemistry one is moles per litre, called molarity, which is what a calculation uses. Concentration is how much of a substance is present in a given amount of solution, and the units are the practical part, since the same quantity can be expressed in several ways that are not interchangeable. Both describe the same thing and neither is wrong, so the danger is a number being read in the wrong unit. Concentration also changes with temperature: a gas becomes less soluble in a warmer liquid, which is why a warm fizzy drink goes flat faster than a cold one, and a closed container of it holds its gas better when chilled. The two words people confuse are concentrated, meaning strong, and diluted, meaning weakened, and the act of weakening is dilution, which is a dilution calculation.","Both describe the same thing and neither is wrong, so the danger is a number being read in the wrong unit. Concentration is how much of a substance is present in a given amount of solution, and the units are the practical part, since the same quantity can be expressed in several ways that are not interchangeable. The everyday one is grams per litre or per hundred millilitres, which is what a medicine label states, while the precise chemistry one is moles per litre, called molarity, which is what a calculation uses. Concentration also changes with temperature: a gas becomes less soluble in a warmer liquid, which is why a warm fizzy drink goes flat faster than a cold one, and a closed container of it holds its gas better when chilled. The two words people confuse are concentrated, meaning strong, and diluted, meaning weakened, and the act of weakening is dilution, which is a dilution calculation."],"tl":"konsentrasyon","bis":"concentration"},{"id":"sci-chemistry-000008","topic":"science-chemistry","intent":"definition","concept":"reaction","keywords":["reaction","equation","combustion","catalyst","conservation","synthesis","reactant","product","reaksyon","chemical","process","substances","converted","another"],"patterns":["what is reaction","reaction","explain reaction","definition of reaction","reaction meaning","what is a chemical reaction","what is a catalyst","how do you balance an equation","ano ang reaksyon","unsa ang chemical reaction"],"answer":"A chemical reaction is the process where one set of substances is converted into another, and it is a rearrangement of atoms rather than a creation or destruction of matter. The atoms on one side of an equation are the same atoms on the other, only reassembled, which is why the law of conservation of mass holds and why mass appears to be lost when gas escapes an open container. The types that matter are synthesis, where two substances combine into one, decomposition, where one breaks down, single replacement, double replacement, and combustion, which is the common one the word also names. Some are fast and energetic, like combustion, and some are slow and silent, like rusting or the browning of a cut apple, and a reaction can also be driven backwards by changing conditions. Catalysts speed a reaction without being used up, and they work by lowering the energy needed for the step rather than by adding energy, which is the part that trips people up.","variations":["The atoms on one side of an equation are the same atoms on the other, only reassembled, which is why the law of conservation of mass holds and why mass appears to be lost when gas escapes an open container. A chemical reaction is the process where one set of substances is converted into another, and it is a rearrangement of atoms rather than a creation or destruction of matter. The types that matter are synthesis, where two substances combine into one, decomposition, where one breaks down, single replacement, double replacement, and combustion, which is the common one the word also names. Some are fast and energetic, like combustion, and some are slow and silent, like rusting or the browning of a cut apple, and a reaction can also be driven backwards by changing conditions. Catalysts speed a reaction without being used up, and they work by lowering the energy needed for the step rather than by adding energy, which is the part that trips people up.","The types that matter are synthesis, where two substances combine into one, decomposition, where one breaks down, single replacement, double replacement, and combustion, which is the common one the word also names. A chemical reaction is the process where one set of substances is converted into another, and it is a rearrangement of atoms rather than a creation or destruction of matter. The atoms on one side of an equation are the same atoms on the other, only reassembled, which is why the law of conservation of mass holds and why mass appears to be lost when gas escapes an open container. Some are fast and energetic, like combustion, and some are slow and silent, like rusting or the browning of a cut apple, and a reaction can also be driven backwards by changing conditions. Catalysts speed a reaction without being used up, and they work by lowering the energy needed for the step rather than by adding energy, which is the part that trips people up."],"tl":"reaksyon","bis":"chemical reaction"}],"lang":"english","group":"science","sub":"chemistry","title":"Chemistry","groupTitle":"Science","__file":"science/chemistry.json"},
  {"topic":"science-earth-science","entries":[{"id":"sci-earth-science-000001","topic":"science-earth-science","intent":"definition","concept":"plate tectonics","keywords":["plate tectonics","subduction","ridge","tectonics","plate","theory","earth's","outer","shell"],"patterns":["what is plate tectonics","plate tectonics","explain plate tectonics","definition of plate tectonics","plate tectonics meaning","what causes earthquakes","how fast do tectonic plates move","ano ang plate tectonics","unsa ang tectonics"],"answer":"Plate tectonics is the theory that the Earth's outer shell is broken into a dozen or so large rigid plates that move over the softer layer beneath them, and it explains earthquakes, volcanoes, mountain building and continental drift with one idea. The plates move only centimetres a year, roughly the rate your fingernails grow, which sounds impossible until you consider that the Atlantic widens by about that much every year and the Himalaya are still rising. They move because the mantle convects slowly underneath, and the driving forces are the sinking of dense oceanic crust in subduction zones and the push from ridges where new crust forms. Where plates pull apart you get rifts and new ocean floor, where they slide past each other you get transform faults and the earthquake danger zones on the west coast of the Americas, and where one dives under another you get deep trenches, arcs of volcanoes, and the largest earthquakes. The theory replaced fixed continents with a moving surface, and it is the reason the same fossils and rock layers appear on opposite shores of the Atlantic.","variations":["The plates move only centimetres a year, roughly the rate your fingernails grow, which sounds impossible until you consider that the Atlantic widens by about that much every year and the Himalaya are still rising. Plate tectonics is the theory that the Earth's outer shell is broken into a dozen or so large rigid plates that move over the softer layer beneath them, and it explains earthquakes, volcanoes, mountain building and continental drift with one idea. They move because the mantle convects slowly underneath, and the driving forces are the sinking of dense oceanic crust in subduction zones and the push from ridges where new crust forms. Where plates pull apart you get rifts and new ocean floor, where they slide past each other you get transform faults and the earthquake danger zones on the west coast of the Americas, and where one dives under another you get deep trenches, arcs of volcanoes, and the largest earthquakes. The theory replaced fixed continents with a moving surface, and it is the reason the same fossils and rock layers appear on opposite shores of the Atlantic.","They move because the mantle convects slowly underneath, and the driving forces are the sinking of dense oceanic crust in subduction zones and the push from ridges where new crust forms. Plate tectonics is the theory that the Earth's outer shell is broken into a dozen or so large rigid plates that move over the softer layer beneath them, and it explains earthquakes, volcanoes, mountain building and continental drift with one idea. The plates move only centimetres a year, roughly the rate your fingernails grow, which sounds impossible until you consider that the Atlantic widens by about that much every year and the Himalaya are still rising. Where plates pull apart you get rifts and new ocean floor, where they slide past each other you get transform faults and the earthquake danger zones on the west coast of the Americas, and where one dives under another you get deep trenches, arcs of volcanoes, and the largest earthquakes. The theory replaced fixed continents with a moving surface, and it is the reason the same fossils and rock layers appear on opposite shores of the Atlantic."],"tl":"plate tectonics","bis":"tectonics"},{"id":"sci-earth-science-000002","topic":"science-earth-science","intent":"definition","concept":"water cycle","keywords":["water cycle","evaporation","condensation","precipitation","transpiration","groundwater","aquifer","freshwater","water","cycle","continuous","movement","environment","driven"],"patterns":["what is water cycle","water cycle","explain water cycle","definition of water cycle","water cycle meaning","what is the water cycle","where does rain come from","how much of earth is water","ano ang water cycle","unsa ang water cycle"],"answer":"The water cycle is the continuous movement of water through the environment, driven by the Sun's heat, and nothing is lost from it in the way people sometimes imagine. Water evaporates from oceans, lakes, soil and plants, condenses into clouds when rising air cools, falls as precipitation, and then either infiltrates into the ground, runs off across the surface into rivers, or is taken up by vegetation and returned to the atmosphere through transpiration. The ocean holds the overwhelming majority of the available freshwater locked as salt water, and the small fraction that is fresh is mostly locked in glaciers and ice caps, with rivers and lakes holding very little. That distribution is why a region can be water-rich by rainfall and still short of usable water, and it is the reason groundwater extraction can lower a water table faster than rain replaces it. Local weather is a small-scale expression of the same process: rain happens because a parcel of air carrying water vapour cooled until it could no longer hold the vapour.","variations":["Water evaporates from oceans, lakes, soil and plants, condenses into clouds when rising air cools, falls as precipitation, and then either infiltrates into the ground, runs off across the surface into rivers, or is taken up by vegetation and returned to the atmosphere through transpiration. The water cycle is the continuous movement of water through the environment, driven by the Sun's heat, and nothing is lost from it in the way people sometimes imagine. The ocean holds the overwhelming majority of the available freshwater locked as salt water, and the small fraction that is fresh is mostly locked in glaciers and ice caps, with rivers and lakes holding very little. That distribution is why a region can be water-rich by rainfall and still short of usable water, and it is the reason groundwater extraction can lower a water table faster than rain replaces it. Local weather is a small-scale expression of the same process: rain happens because a parcel of air carrying water vapour cooled until it could no longer hold the vapour.","The ocean holds the overwhelming majority of the available freshwater locked as salt water, and the small fraction that is fresh is mostly locked in glaciers and ice caps, with rivers and lakes holding very little. The water cycle is the continuous movement of water through the environment, driven by the Sun's heat, and nothing is lost from it in the way people sometimes imagine. Water evaporates from oceans, lakes, soil and plants, condenses into clouds when rising air cools, falls as precipitation, and then either infiltrates into the ground, runs off across the surface into rivers, or is taken up by vegetation and returned to the atmosphere through transpiration. That distribution is why a region can be water-rich by rainfall and still short of usable water, and it is the reason groundwater extraction can lower a water table faster than rain replaces it. Local weather is a small-scale expression of the same process: rain happens because a parcel of air carrying water vapour cooled until it could no longer hold the vapour."],"tl":"water cycle","bis":"water cycle"},{"id":"sci-earth-science-000003","topic":"science-earth-science","intent":"definition","concept":"earthquake","keywords":["earthquake","magnitude","epicentre","aftershock","sudden","release","stress","along","fault"],"patterns":["what is earthquake","earthquake","explain earthquake","definition of earthquake","earthquake meaning","what is an earthquake","how are earthquakes measured","can earthquakes be predicted","ano ang earthquake","unsa ang earthquake"],"answer":"An earthquake is the sudden release of stress along a fault line, and the danger comes less from the shaking itself than from what falls off, collapses or catches fire because of it. The strain accumulates over years as two plates grind past each other, and when the rock finally slips it releases energy as seismic waves that travel through the Earth and out to a network of sensors, which is how magnitude is estimated. Magnitude is logarithmic, so a magnitude 7 releases about thirty times more energy than a magnitude 6, and an 8 is a further tenfold increase over that. This is why the number of damaging earthquakes grows so sharply with magnitude and why the very largest ones are rare. Earthquakes cluster along plate boundaries rather than spreading evenly, which is what makes some regions, like the Pacific rim, far more exposed than others. Most are small and harmless, and the practical protection is building design rather than prediction, because the current science can identify where risk is high but not when a specific event will occur.","variations":["The strain accumulates over years as two plates grind past each other, and when the rock finally slips it releases energy as seismic waves that travel through the Earth and out to a network of sensors, which is how magnitude is estimated. An earthquake is the sudden release of stress along a fault line, and the danger comes less from the shaking itself than from what falls off, collapses or catches fire because of it. Magnitude is logarithmic, so a magnitude 7 releases about thirty times more energy than a magnitude 6, and an 8 is a further tenfold increase over that. This is why the number of damaging earthquakes grows so sharply with magnitude and why the very largest ones are rare. Earthquakes cluster along plate boundaries rather than spreading evenly, which is what makes some regions, like the Pacific rim, far more exposed than others. Most are small and harmless, and the practical protection is building design rather than prediction, because the current science can identify where risk is high but not when a specific event will occur.","Magnitude is logarithmic, so a magnitude 7 releases about thirty times more energy than a magnitude 6, and an 8 is a further tenfold increase over that. An earthquake is the sudden release of stress along a fault line, and the danger comes less from the shaking itself than from what falls off, collapses or catches fire because of it. The strain accumulates over years as two plates grind past each other, and when the rock finally slips it releases energy as seismic waves that travel through the Earth and out to a network of sensors, which is how magnitude is estimated. This is why the number of damaging earthquakes grows so sharply with magnitude and why the very largest ones are rare. Earthquakes cluster along plate boundaries rather than spreading evenly, which is what makes some regions, like the Pacific rim, far more exposed than others. Most are small and harmless, and the practical protection is building design rather than prediction, because the current science can identify where risk is high but not when a specific event will occur."],"tl":"earthquake","bis":"earthquake"},{"id":"sci-earth-science-000004","topic":"science-earth-science","intent":"definition","concept":"rock cycle","keywords":["rock cycle","igneous","sedimentary","metamorphic","magma","erosion","weathering","metamorphism","rock","cycle","description","changes","type","another"],"patterns":["what is rock cycle","rock cycle","explain rock cycle","definition of rock cycle","rock cycle meaning","what is the rock cycle","how are rocks formed","what is metamorphic rock","ano ang rock cycle","unsa ang rock cycle"],"answer":"The rock cycle is the description of how rock changes from one type to another, driven by heat, pressure and water, and it is the reason a mountain range made of granite can eventually become beach sand. The three main rock types are igneous, which crystallises from cooled magma or lava, sedimentary, which forms when existing rock is eroded, carried away, deposited in layers and compacted, and metamorphic, which is existing rock altered in the solid state by heat and pressure without melting. Any of them can become any other given the right conditions, and melting returns material to magma to start again, so the cycle has no real beginning. The common misconception is that it takes millions of years for anything to happen: the full cycle is indeed that long, but individual legs of it are fast enough to observe, and a flood can move a great deal of sediment in a day. Metamorphism is also what gives metamorphic rocks their banded appearance, since the minerals recrystallise in layers under pressure.","variations":["The three main rock types are igneous, which crystallises from cooled magma or lava, sedimentary, which forms when existing rock is eroded, carried away, deposited in layers and compacted, and metamorphic, which is existing rock altered in the solid state by heat and pressure without melting. The rock cycle is the description of how rock changes from one type to another, driven by heat, pressure and water, and it is the reason a mountain range made of granite can eventually become beach sand. Any of them can become any other given the right conditions, and melting returns material to magma to start again, so the cycle has no real beginning. The common misconception is that it takes millions of years for anything to happen: the full cycle is indeed that long, but individual legs of it are fast enough to observe, and a flood can move a great deal of sediment in a day. Metamorphism is also what gives metamorphic rocks their banded appearance, since the minerals recrystallise in layers under pressure.","Any of them can become any other given the right conditions, and melting returns material to magma to start again, so the cycle has no real beginning. The rock cycle is the description of how rock changes from one type to another, driven by heat, pressure and water, and it is the reason a mountain range made of granite can eventually become beach sand. The three main rock types are igneous, which crystallises from cooled magma or lava, sedimentary, which forms when existing rock is eroded, carried away, deposited in layers and compacted, and metamorphic, which is existing rock altered in the solid state by heat and pressure without melting. The common misconception is that it takes millions of years for anything to happen: the full cycle is indeed that long, but individual legs of it are fast enough to observe, and a flood can move a great deal of sediment in a day. Metamorphism is also what gives metamorphic rocks their banded appearance, since the minerals recrystallise in layers under pressure."],"tl":"rock cycle","bis":"rock cycle"},{"id":"sci-earth-science-000005","topic":"science-earth-science","intent":"definition","concept":"erosion","keywords":["erosion","weathering","stream","glacier","valley","topsoil","sediment","vegetation","removal","rock","soil","water","wind"],"patterns":["what is erosion","erosion","explain erosion","definition of erosion","erosion meaning","difference between erosion and weathering","what causes soil erosion","ano ang erosion","unsa ang erosion"],"answer":"Erosion is the removal of rock and soil by water, wind, ice or gravity, and it is the reason landforms change and why a river valley ends up where it is. Weathering breaks rock down where it sits, while erosion carries the material away, and the two are separate processes that are constantly confused. Water is the most effective agent: a stream cuts a V-shaped valley, and where a glacier moves through it cuts a much wider, rounder U-shaped valley, which is why glacial valleys often have their characteristic hanging shape after the ice has left. Wind erosion dominates in dry, sparsely vegetated country, where it produces yardangs and dust bowls, and the material it moves becomes the fine sediment that later forms fertile plains far away. The rate depends on slope, rainfall, vegetation cover and whether the rock is resistant, which is why a resistant ridge stands above softer ground around it. In the Philippines this is not abstract: flash floods strip topsoil off agricultural land, and the loss of the protective vegetation cover is what turns a normal storm into a disaster.","variations":["Weathering breaks rock down where it sits, while erosion carries the material away, and the two are separate processes that are constantly confused. Erosion is the removal of rock and soil by water, wind, ice or gravity, and it is the reason landforms change and why a river valley ends up where it is. Water is the most effective agent: a stream cuts a V-shaped valley, and where a glacier moves through it cuts a much wider, rounder U-shaped valley, which is why glacial valleys often have their characteristic hanging shape after the ice has left. Wind erosion dominates in dry, sparsely vegetated country, where it produces yardangs and dust bowls, and the material it moves becomes the fine sediment that later forms fertile plains far away. The rate depends on slope, rainfall, vegetation cover and whether the rock is resistant, which is why a resistant ridge stands above softer ground around it. In the Philippines this is not abstract: flash floods strip topsoil off agricultural land, and the loss of the protective vegetation cover is what turns a normal storm into a disaster.","Water is the most effective agent: a stream cuts a V-shaped valley, and where a glacier moves through it cuts a much wider, rounder U-shaped valley, which is why glacial valleys often have their characteristic hanging shape after the ice has left. Erosion is the removal of rock and soil by water, wind, ice or gravity, and it is the reason landforms change and why a river valley ends up where it is. Weathering breaks rock down where it sits, while erosion carries the material away, and the two are separate processes that are constantly confused. Wind erosion dominates in dry, sparsely vegetated country, where it produces yardangs and dust bowls, and the material it moves becomes the fine sediment that later forms fertile plains far away. The rate depends on slope, rainfall, vegetation cover and whether the rock is resistant, which is why a resistant ridge stands above softer ground around it. In the Philippines this is not abstract: flash floods strip topsoil off agricultural land, and the loss of the protective vegetation cover is what turns a normal storm into a disaster."],"tl":"erosion","bis":"erosion"}],"lang":"english","group":"science","sub":"earth-science","title":"Earth science","groupTitle":"Science","__file":"science/earth-science.json"},
  {"topic":"science-environment","entries":[{"id":"sci-environment-000001","topic":"science-environment","intent":"definition","concept":"ecosystem","keywords":["ecosystem","producer","consumer","decomposer","biodiversity","habitat","community","living","organisms","together","interact"],"patterns":["what is ecosystem","ecosystem","explain ecosystem","definition of ecosystem","ecosystem meaning","what is an ecosystem","what is the food chain","why does biodiversity matter","ano ang ecosystem","unsa ang ecosystem"],"answer":"An ecosystem is a community of living organisms together with the non-living things they interact with in the same area, and it is the basic unit of ecology because energy flow and nutrient cycling only make sense at that scale. Producers such as plants capture solar energy, consumers take it by eating, and decomposers break down what is left and return nutrients to the soil, so the same material can be reused many times. The energy itself moves in one direction and is lost as heat at every step, which is why a food chain supports far fewer levels than it might seem to, while nutrients cycle and can persist. A forest, a coral reef, a rice field and a city park are all ecosystems, and they differ in productivity, species diversity and how much of their functioning depends on outside inputs. The reason this matters practically is that an ecosystem with a wide range of species is more resilient: remove one and the others partly absorb the loss, whereas a simplified one can collapse.","variations":["Producers such as plants capture solar energy, consumers take it by eating, and decomposers break down what is left and return nutrients to the soil, so the same material can be reused many times. An ecosystem is a community of living organisms together with the non-living things they interact with in the same area, and it is the basic unit of ecology because energy flow and nutrient cycling only make sense at that scale. The energy itself moves in one direction and is lost as heat at every step, which is why a food chain supports far fewer levels than it might seem to, while nutrients cycle and can persist. A forest, a coral reef, a rice field and a city park are all ecosystems, and they differ in productivity, species diversity and how much of their functioning depends on outside inputs. The reason this matters practically is that an ecosystem with a wide range of species is more resilient: remove one and the others partly absorb the loss, whereas a simplified one can collapse.","The energy itself moves in one direction and is lost as heat at every step, which is why a food chain supports far fewer levels than it might seem to, while nutrients cycle and can persist. An ecosystem is a community of living organisms together with the non-living things they interact with in the same area, and it is the basic unit of ecology because energy flow and nutrient cycling only make sense at that scale. Producers such as plants capture solar energy, consumers take it by eating, and decomposers break down what is left and return nutrients to the soil, so the same material can be reused many times. A forest, a coral reef, a rice field and a city park are all ecosystems, and they differ in productivity, species diversity and how much of their functioning depends on outside inputs. The reason this matters practically is that an ecosystem with a wide range of species is more resilient: remove one and the others partly absorb the loss, whereas a simplified one can collapse."],"tl":"ecosystem","bis":"ecosystem"},{"id":"sci-environment-000002","topic":"science-environment","intent":"definition","concept":"biodiversity","keywords":["biodiversity","species","genetic","extinction","resilience","pollination","conservation","variety","living","area","three","levels"],"patterns":["what is biodiversity","biodiversity","explain biodiversity","definition of biodiversity","biodiversity meaning","why is biodiversity important","what causes species extinction","ano ang biodiversity","unsa ang biodiversity"],"answer":"Biodiversity is the variety of living things in an area, and it has three levels: genetic variety within a species, the number of species, and the variety of ecosystems those species occupy. All three decline together as habitat is lost, which is why the clearest cause of biodiversity decline worldwide is the conversion of natural habitat to farming, logging and urban growth. The variety is not decoration; a system with many overlapping species is more robust because they respond differently to a change in conditions, so a drought that wipes out one food source does not remove the whole chain. That resilience has a direct economic consequence, since pollination, soil formation, water purification and pest control are all services delivered by functioning ecosystems rather than by machinery. Measuring it honestly is genuinely difficult, because species go extinct without anyone recording them and many have not been named yet, so the usual global estimates carry wide error bars.","variations":["All three decline together as habitat is lost, which is why the clearest cause of biodiversity decline worldwide is the conversion of natural habitat to farming, logging and urban growth. Biodiversity is the variety of living things in an area, and it has three levels: genetic variety within a species, the number of species, and the variety of ecosystems those species occupy. The variety is not decoration; a system with many overlapping species is more robust because they respond differently to a change in conditions, so a drought that wipes out one food source does not remove the whole chain. That resilience has a direct economic consequence, since pollination, soil formation, water purification and pest control are all services delivered by functioning ecosystems rather than by machinery. Measuring it honestly is genuinely difficult, because species go extinct without anyone recording them and many have not been named yet, so the usual global estimates carry wide error bars.","The variety is not decoration; a system with many overlapping species is more robust because they respond differently to a change in conditions, so a drought that wipes out one food source does not remove the whole chain. Biodiversity is the variety of living things in an area, and it has three levels: genetic variety within a species, the number of species, and the variety of ecosystems those species occupy. All three decline together as habitat is lost, which is why the clearest cause of biodiversity decline worldwide is the conversion of natural habitat to farming, logging and urban growth. That resilience has a direct economic consequence, since pollination, soil formation, water purification and pest control are all services delivered by functioning ecosystems rather than by machinery. Measuring it honestly is genuinely difficult, because species go extinct without anyone recording them and many have not been named yet, so the usual global estimates carry wide error bars."],"tl":"biodiversity","bis":"biodiversity"},{"id":"sci-environment-000003","topic":"science-environment","intent":"definition","concept":"greenhouse effect","keywords":["greenhouse effect","methane","infrared","warming","ozone","atmosphere","radiation","greenhouse","effect","planet's","surface","caused"],"patterns":["what is greenhouse effect","greenhouse effect","explain greenhouse effect","definition of greenhouse effect","greenhouse effect meaning","what is the greenhouse effect","what causes global warming","is global warming the same as ozone","ano ang greenhouse effect","unsa ang greenhouse effect"],"answer":"The greenhouse effect is the warming of a planet's surface caused by certain gases in its atmosphere absorbing and re-emitting outgoing infrared radiation, and it is the reason the Earth is habitable rather than frozen. Sunlight arrives mostly as visible light, warms the surface, and the surface radiates back as infrared, which the greenhouse gases in the atmosphere absorb and re-emit in all directions including back downwards. What makes it a problem is the addition of gases rather than the existence of the mechanism, since natural greenhouse gases are what set the planet's roughly 15 degree average instead of the minus 18 it would otherwise be. The main gases are carbon dioxide from burning fossil fuels and cement production, methane from livestock, landfills and leaks, and nitrous oxide from agriculture. The two climate problems are distinct and often confused: the enhanced greenhouse effect is a steady warming that persists, while the ozone hole is a separate thinning of a different layer by chlorofluorocarbons. Weather is a chaotic system for the next week; climate is the statistics of weather over decades.","variations":["Sunlight arrives mostly as visible light, warms the surface, and the surface radiates back as infrared, which the greenhouse gases in the atmosphere absorb and re-emit in all directions including back downwards. The greenhouse effect is the warming of a planet's surface caused by certain gases in its atmosphere absorbing and re-emitting outgoing infrared radiation, and it is the reason the Earth is habitable rather than frozen. What makes it a problem is the addition of gases rather than the existence of the mechanism, since natural greenhouse gases are what set the planet's roughly 15 degree average instead of the minus 18 it would otherwise be. The main gases are carbon dioxide from burning fossil fuels and cement production, methane from livestock, landfills and leaks, and nitrous oxide from agriculture. The two climate problems are distinct and often confused: the enhanced greenhouse effect is a steady warming that persists, while the ozone hole is a separate thinning of a different layer by chlorofluorocarbons. Weather is a chaotic system for the next week; climate is the statistics of weather over decades.","What makes it a problem is the addition of gases rather than the existence of the mechanism, since natural greenhouse gases are what set the planet's roughly 15 degree average instead of the minus 18 it would otherwise be. The greenhouse effect is the warming of a planet's surface caused by certain gases in its atmosphere absorbing and re-emitting outgoing infrared radiation, and it is the reason the Earth is habitable rather than frozen. Sunlight arrives mostly as visible light, warms the surface, and the surface radiates back as infrared, which the greenhouse gases in the atmosphere absorb and re-emit in all directions including back downwards. The main gases are carbon dioxide from burning fossil fuels and cement production, methane from livestock, landfills and leaks, and nitrous oxide from agriculture. The two climate problems are distinct and often confused: the enhanced greenhouse effect is a steady warming that persists, while the ozone hole is a separate thinning of a different layer by chlorofluorocarbons. Weather is a chaotic system for the next week; climate is the statistics of weather over decades."],"tl":"greenhouse effect","bis":"greenhouse effect"},{"id":"sci-environment-000004","topic":"science-environment","intent":"definition","concept":"recycling","keywords":["recycling","compost","landfill","circular","contamination","waste","processing","material","back","feedstock","manufactured"],"patterns":["what is recycling","recycling","explain recycling","definition of recycling","recycling meaning","is recycling effective","what can i recycle","ano ang recycling","unsa ang recycling"],"answer":"Recycling is processing used material back into raw feedstock so it can be manufactured into new products, and its value depends on which material is involved and on doing it cheaper than extracting the primary source. Paper, glass, aluminium and steel are the materials where recycling is genuinely established, because their production is energy intensive and recovered material is close to virgin material in quality. Plastics are harder because there are many resin types that are cheap to mix and expensive to separate, so sorting matters enormously, and a resin that has no market will be landfilled. In organic waste, composting is the better route than burning, because it returns nutrients to soil rather than sending carbon straight to the atmosphere. The honest criticism of recycling is that it was promoted as the solution to a consumption problem, and the highest-value step is still reduction: less material entering the system beats recovering it later.","variations":["Paper, glass, aluminium and steel are the materials where recycling is genuinely established, because their production is energy intensive and recovered material is close to virgin material in quality. Recycling is processing used material back into raw feedstock so it can be manufactured into new products, and its value depends on which material is involved and on doing it cheaper than extracting the primary source. Plastics are harder because there are many resin types that are cheap to mix and expensive to separate, so sorting matters enormously, and a resin that has no market will be landfilled. In organic waste, composting is the better route than burning, because it returns nutrients to soil rather than sending carbon straight to the atmosphere. The honest criticism of recycling is that it was promoted as the solution to a consumption problem, and the highest-value step is still reduction: less material entering the system beats recovering it later.","Plastics are harder because there are many resin types that are cheap to mix and expensive to separate, so sorting matters enormously, and a resin that has no market will be landfilled. Recycling is processing used material back into raw feedstock so it can be manufactured into new products, and its value depends on which material is involved and on doing it cheaper than extracting the primary source. Paper, glass, aluminium and steel are the materials where recycling is genuinely established, because their production is energy intensive and recovered material is close to virgin material in quality. In organic waste, composting is the better route than burning, because it returns nutrients to soil rather than sending carbon straight to the atmosphere. The honest criticism of recycling is that it was promoted as the solution to a consumption problem, and the highest-value step is still reduction: less material entering the system beats recovering it later."],"tl":"recycling","bis":"recycling"},{"id":"sci-environment-000005","topic":"science-environment","intent":"definition","concept":"lightning","keywords":["lightning","thunder","storm","charge","ground","shelter","distance","ice","kidlat","sudden","electrical","discharge","sound"],"patterns":["what is lightning","lightning","explain lightning","definition of lightning","lightning meaning","what causes lightning","why is lightning dangerous","is it safe to shelter under a tree","ano ang kidlat","unsa ang lightning"],"answer":"Lightning is a sudden electrical discharge, and the sound of thunder is the shock wave from the same event travelling through the air, so the gap between the flash and the sound is a measure of distance. A rough version of the rule is to divide the number of seconds by three to get kilometres, which works because light crosses a kilometre in about three millionths of a second while sound covers it in nearly three seconds. A storm produces lightning when ice crystals and water droplets collide inside a cloud, separating a charge that grows until it overcomes the air's ability to resist and releases in a channel, and that channel is what is seen as the flash. The danger is not being struck by the visible bolt, which is what people picture, but the ground current that spreads sideways from the point of impact, since it kills more people than the direct strike. The safe rule indoors and in a vehicle is simply to be inside something substantial, and outdoors the advice is to avoid open ground, water and isolated tall objects, since being under a tree is one of the commonest and most dangerous mistakes.","variations":["A rough version of the rule is to divide the number of seconds by three to get kilometres, which works because light crosses a kilometre in about three millionths of a second while sound covers it in nearly three seconds. Lightning is a sudden electrical discharge, and the sound of thunder is the shock wave from the same event travelling through the air, so the gap between the flash and the sound is a measure of distance. A storm produces lightning when ice crystals and water droplets collide inside a cloud, separating a charge that grows until it overcomes the air's ability to resist and releases in a channel, and that channel is what is seen as the flash. The danger is not being struck by the visible bolt, which is what people picture, but the ground current that spreads sideways from the point of impact, since it kills more people than the direct strike. The safe rule indoors and in a vehicle is simply to be inside something substantial, and outdoors the advice is to avoid open ground, water and isolated tall objects, since being under a tree is one of the commonest and most dangerous mistakes.","A storm produces lightning when ice crystals and water droplets collide inside a cloud, separating a charge that grows until it overcomes the air's ability to resist and releases in a channel, and that channel is what is seen as the flash. Lightning is a sudden electrical discharge, and the sound of thunder is the shock wave from the same event travelling through the air, so the gap between the flash and the sound is a measure of distance. A rough version of the rule is to divide the number of seconds by three to get kilometres, which works because light crosses a kilometre in about three millionths of a second while sound covers it in nearly three seconds. The danger is not being struck by the visible bolt, which is what people picture, but the ground current that spreads sideways from the point of impact, since it kills more people than the direct strike. The safe rule indoors and in a vehicle is simply to be inside something substantial, and outdoors the advice is to avoid open ground, water and isolated tall objects, since being under a tree is one of the commonest and most dangerous mistakes."],"tl":"kidlat","bis":"lightning"},{"id":"sci-environment-000006","topic":"science-environment","intent":"definition","concept":"ozone layer","keywords":["ozone layer","ultraviolet","chlorofluorocarbons","stratosphere","montreal","shield","recover","ozone","layer","band","upper","atmosphere","molecule"],"patterns":["what is ozone layer","ozone layer","explain ozone layer","definition of ozone layer","ozone layer meaning","what is the ozone layer","what destroyed the ozone layer","is ozone a pollutant","ano ang ozone layer","unsa ang ozone layer"],"answer":"The ozone layer is a band of the upper atmosphere where ozone, a molecule made of three oxygen atoms, absorbs most of the ultraviolet radiation arriving from the Sun, and it is not the layer that gets talked about when someone mentions ozone in air pollution. That is a real trap of terminology, because ground-level ozone is a pollutant that irritates the lungs, while the stratospheric layer is a shield, and the two are chemically the same substance in completely different places with opposite effects. The layer forms when ultraviolet light splits an oxygen molecule and the free atom combines with another, which is why it needs that radiation to exist at all. Damage to it came from chlorofluorocarbons and related gases used in refrigeration and sprays, which break down in the upper atmosphere and release chlorine that destroys the ozone catalytically, so a small amount does a great deal of harm. The international response was the Montreal Protocol, widely regarded as the most successful environmental agreement so far, and the layer is on its way to recovering. The same chemicals being phased out, being a greenhouse gas, links the two problems in a way that is not intuitive.","variations":["That is a real trap of terminology, because ground-level ozone is a pollutant that irritates the lungs, while the stratospheric layer is a shield, and the two are chemically the same substance in completely different places with opposite effects. The ozone layer is a band of the upper atmosphere where ozone, a molecule made of three oxygen atoms, absorbs most of the ultraviolet radiation arriving from the Sun, and it is not the layer that gets talked about when someone mentions ozone in air pollution. The layer forms when ultraviolet light splits an oxygen molecule and the free atom combines with another, which is why it needs that radiation to exist at all. Damage to it came from chlorofluorocarbons and related gases used in refrigeration and sprays, which break down in the upper atmosphere and release chlorine that destroys the ozone catalytically, so a small amount does a great deal of harm. The international response was the Montreal Protocol, widely regarded as the most successful environmental agreement so far, and the layer is on its way to recovering. The same chemicals being phased out, being a greenhouse gas, links the two problems in a way that is not intuitive.","The layer forms when ultraviolet light splits an oxygen molecule and the free atom combines with another, which is why it needs that radiation to exist at all. The ozone layer is a band of the upper atmosphere where ozone, a molecule made of three oxygen atoms, absorbs most of the ultraviolet radiation arriving from the Sun, and it is not the layer that gets talked about when someone mentions ozone in air pollution. That is a real trap of terminology, because ground-level ozone is a pollutant that irritates the lungs, while the stratospheric layer is a shield, and the two are chemically the same substance in completely different places with opposite effects. Damage to it came from chlorofluorocarbons and related gases used in refrigeration and sprays, which break down in the upper atmosphere and release chlorine that destroys the ozone catalytically, so a small amount does a great deal of harm. The international response was the Montreal Protocol, widely regarded as the most successful environmental agreement so far, and the layer is on its way to recovering. The same chemicals being phased out, being a greenhouse gas, links the two problems in a way that is not intuitive."],"tl":"ozone layer","bis":"ozone layer"},{"id":"sci-environment-000007","topic":"science-environment","intent":"definition","concept":"deforestation","keywords":["deforestation","forest","carbon","habitat","logging","erosion","rainforest","primary","clearing","forests","common","trees","being"],"patterns":["what is deforestation","deforestation","explain deforestation","definition of deforestation","deforestation meaning","why is deforestation bad","how much forest does the philippines have","ano ang deforestation","unsa ang deforestation"],"answer":"Deforestation is the clearing of forests, and the more common form is not trees being felled but land being converted, since forest is removed to grow crops or cattle rather than for timber. It matters for three separate reasons: the trees that are removed no longer take carbon dioxide out of the air, the soil that was protected is exposed and erodes, and the habitat and rainfall cycle of the region are disrupted. Tropical forests are the most important of these because they hold a large share of the world's land-based carbon, much of it in the soil rather than the visible trees, so clearing releases carbon that can take decades to regrow. The Philippine context is specific and severe: the country was once among the most heavily forested in Asia, and the remaining forest is now largely in the uplands of Luzon, Mindanao and Palawan, with the Caramoan hillsides among the most deforestation-prone land in the world. Rates there have fallen markedly, which is the difference between a solvable policy problem and a permanent loss.","variations":["It matters for three separate reasons: the trees that are removed no longer take carbon dioxide out of the air, the soil that was protected is exposed and erodes, and the habitat and rainfall cycle of the region are disrupted. Deforestation is the clearing of forests, and the more common form is not trees being felled but land being converted, since forest is removed to grow crops or cattle rather than for timber. Tropical forests are the most important of these because they hold a large share of the world's land-based carbon, much of it in the soil rather than the visible trees, so clearing releases carbon that can take decades to regrow. The Philippine context is specific and severe: the country was once among the most heavily forested in Asia, and the remaining forest is now largely in the uplands of Luzon, Mindanao and Palawan, with the Caramoan hillsides among the most deforestation-prone land in the world. Rates there have fallen markedly, which is the difference between a solvable policy problem and a permanent loss.","Tropical forests are the most important of these because they hold a large share of the world's land-based carbon, much of it in the soil rather than the visible trees, so clearing releases carbon that can take decades to regrow. Deforestation is the clearing of forests, and the more common form is not trees being felled but land being converted, since forest is removed to grow crops or cattle rather than for timber. It matters for three separate reasons: the trees that are removed no longer take carbon dioxide out of the air, the soil that was protected is exposed and erodes, and the habitat and rainfall cycle of the region are disrupted. The Philippine context is specific and severe: the country was once among the most heavily forested in Asia, and the remaining forest is now largely in the uplands of Luzon, Mindanao and Palawan, with the Caramoan hillsides among the most deforestation-prone land in the world. Rates there have fallen markedly, which is the difference between a solvable policy problem and a permanent loss."],"tl":"deforestation","bis":"deforestation"}],"lang":"english","group":"science","sub":"environment","title":"Environment","groupTitle":"Science","__file":"science/environment.json"},
  {"topic":"science-general-science","entries":[{"id":"sci-general-science-000001","topic":"science-general-science","intent":"definition","concept":"scientific method","keywords":["scientific method","observation","experiment","reproducibility","replication","claim","bias","pagsusuri","scientific","method","procedure","makes","checkable"],"patterns":["what is scientific method","scientific method","explain scientific method","definition of scientific method","scientific method meaning","what is the scientific method","steps of scientific method","why is replication important","ano ang pagsusuri","unsa ang scientific method"],"answer":"The scientific method is the procedure that makes a claim checkable by other people, and its structure is a cycle rather than a straight line: observation, question, hypothesis, experiment, analysis, and back to the start, because a result that contradicts the hypothesis sends you round again rather than ending the enquiry. The parts that are genuinely required are that the claim is stated so it could be wrong, that the test is a fair test of that specific claim, and that the result is reported whatever it was. Publishing the method along with the result is what allows someone else to repeat it, which is why replication rather than plausibility is the real currency, and why a claim with no stated method is not yet a scientific claim at all. A method can be wrong without anyone lying, and a good course in research methods spends most of its time on the ways that happens: an unrepresentative sample, a measurement that changes what it is supposed to measure, and a conclusion that claims more than the design can support. The method does not tell you which theory is true; it tells you which claims have survived being tested.","variations":["The parts that are genuinely required are that the claim is stated so it could be wrong, that the test is a fair test of that specific claim, and that the result is reported whatever it was. The scientific method is the procedure that makes a claim checkable by other people, and its structure is a cycle rather than a straight line: observation, question, hypothesis, experiment, analysis, and back to the start, because a result that contradicts the hypothesis sends you round again rather than ending the enquiry. Publishing the method along with the result is what allows someone else to repeat it, which is why replication rather than plausibility is the real currency, and why a claim with no stated method is not yet a scientific claim at all. A method can be wrong without anyone lying, and a good course in research methods spends most of its time on the ways that happens: an unrepresentative sample, a measurement that changes what it is supposed to measure, and a conclusion that claims more than the design can support. The method does not tell you which theory is true; it tells you which claims have survived being tested.","Publishing the method along with the result is what allows someone else to repeat it, which is why replication rather than plausibility is the real currency, and why a claim with no stated method is not yet a scientific claim at all. The scientific method is the procedure that makes a claim checkable by other people, and its structure is a cycle rather than a straight line: observation, question, hypothesis, experiment, analysis, and back to the start, because a result that contradicts the hypothesis sends you round again rather than ending the enquiry. The parts that are genuinely required are that the claim is stated so it could be wrong, that the test is a fair test of that specific claim, and that the result is reported whatever it was. A method can be wrong without anyone lying, and a good course in research methods spends most of its time on the ways that happens: an unrepresentative sample, a measurement that changes what it is supposed to measure, and a conclusion that claims more than the design can support. The method does not tell you which theory is true; it tells you which claims have survived being tested."],"tl":"pagsusuri","bis":"scientific method"},{"id":"sci-general-science-000002","topic":"science-general-science","intent":"definition","concept":"variable in an experiment","keywords":["variable in an experiment","independent","dependent","confounding","randomisation","control","manipulation","correlation","variable","experiment","quantity","change","affect","result"],"patterns":["what is variable in an experiment","variable in an experiment","explain variable in an experiment","definition of variable in an experiment","variable in an experiment meaning","what is a variable in an experiment","independent vs dependent variable","what is a confounding variable","ano ang variable","unsa ang variable"],"answer":"A variable in an experiment is any quantity that can change and might affect the result, and the discipline of designing a study is mostly the discipline of deciding which ones to control. The independent variable is the one the researcher deliberately changes, the dependent variable is the outcome being measured, and everything else that could also change the outcome is a confounding variable and has to be held constant or accounted for. A study that changes a supplement and measures blood pressure has an independent variable of the supplement, a dependent variable of blood pressure, and confounders of diet, exercise, stress and time of day, and if those are not handled then a result cannot be attributed to the supplement. Randomisation is the tool that handles unmeasured confounders, because assigning participants at random balances them on average, and it is why a randomised trial is the strongest design available. The related distinction is between a variable you record and a variable you change: an observation is what you are measuring, and a manipulation is what you are doing to it, and a correlational study only ever does the first, which is why it cannot establish that one thing caused another.","variations":["The independent variable is the one the researcher deliberately changes, the dependent variable is the outcome being measured, and everything else that could also change the outcome is a confounding variable and has to be held constant or accounted for. A variable in an experiment is any quantity that can change and might affect the result, and the discipline of designing a study is mostly the discipline of deciding which ones to control. A study that changes a supplement and measures blood pressure has an independent variable of the supplement, a dependent variable of blood pressure, and confounders of diet, exercise, stress and time of day, and if those are not handled then a result cannot be attributed to the supplement. Randomisation is the tool that handles unmeasured confounders, because assigning participants at random balances them on average, and it is why a randomised trial is the strongest design available. The related distinction is between a variable you record and a variable you change: an observation is what you are measuring, and a manipulation is what you are doing to it, and a correlational study only ever does the first, which is why it cannot establish that one thing caused another.","A study that changes a supplement and measures blood pressure has an independent variable of the supplement, a dependent variable of blood pressure, and confounders of diet, exercise, stress and time of day, and if those are not handled then a result cannot be attributed to the supplement. A variable in an experiment is any quantity that can change and might affect the result, and the discipline of designing a study is mostly the discipline of deciding which ones to control. The independent variable is the one the researcher deliberately changes, the dependent variable is the outcome being measured, and everything else that could also change the outcome is a confounding variable and has to be held constant or accounted for. Randomisation is the tool that handles unmeasured confounders, because assigning participants at random balances them on average, and it is why a randomised trial is the strongest design available. The related distinction is between a variable you record and a variable you change: an observation is what you are measuring, and a manipulation is what you are doing to it, and a correlational study only ever does the first, which is why it cannot establish that one thing caused another."],"tl":"variable","bis":"variable"},{"id":"sci-general-science-000003","topic":"science-general-science","intent":"definition","concept":"quantum","keywords":["quantum","entanglement","qubit","superposition","wave","mechanics","describes","matter","energy","small"],"patterns":["what is quantum","quantum","explain quantum","definition of quantum","quantum meaning","what is quantum entanglement"],"answer":"Quantum mechanics describes matter and energy at very small scales, where particles behave in ways everyday intuition gets wrong. A quantum state can be a superposition of several possibilities at once until it is measured, and two particles can be entangled so that measuring one instantly constrains the other, however far apart. It is not a guess - it is the most precisely tested theory in physics, and it is what gives us semiconductors and lasers.","variations":["A quantum state can be a superposition of several possibilities at once until it is measured, and two particles can be entangled so that measuring one instantly constrains the other, however far apart. Quantum mechanics describes matter and energy at very small scales, where particles behave in ways everyday intuition gets wrong. It is not a guess - it is the most precisely tested theory in physics, and it is what gives us semiconductors and lasers.","It is not a guess - it is the most precisely tested theory in physics, and it is what gives us semiconductors and lasers. Quantum mechanics describes matter and energy at very small scales, where particles behave in ways everyday intuition gets wrong. A quantum state can be a superposition of several possibilities at once until it is measured, and two particles can be entangled so that measuring one instantly constrains the other, however far apart."]}],"lang":"english","group":"science","sub":"general-science","title":"General science","groupTitle":"Science","__file":"science/general-science.json"},
  {"topic":"science-physics","entries":[{"id":"sci-physics-000001","topic":"science-physics","intent":"definition","concept":"gravity","keywords":["gravity","gravitation","newton","attract","orbit","fall","mutual","attraction","anything","mass","object"],"patterns":["what is gravity","gravity","explain gravity","definition of gravity","gravity meaning","how does gravity work","why do things fall","ano ang gravity","unsa ang gravity"],"answer":"Gravity is the mutual attraction between anything with mass. The more mass an object has, and the closer it is, the stronger the pull. On Earth it is what makes things fall and what keeps you on the ground; in space it is what keeps the Moon in orbit. Newton described it as a force falling off with the square of distance, so doubling the distance quarters the pull.","variations":["The more mass an object has, and the closer it is, the stronger the pull. Gravity is the mutual attraction between anything with mass. On Earth it is what makes things fall and what keeps you on the ground; in space it is what keeps the Moon in orbit. Newton described it as a force falling off with the square of distance, so doubling the distance quarters the pull.","On Earth it is what makes things fall and what keeps you on the ground; in space it is what keeps the Moon in orbit. Gravity is the mutual attraction between anything with mass. The more mass an object has, and the closer it is, the stronger the pull. Newton described it as a force falling off with the square of distance, so doubling the distance quarters the pull."],"tl":"gravity","bis":"gravity","langs":{"tagalog":"Ang gravity ay ang pagkakawit sa pagitan ng dalawang bagay na may mass. Ito ang dahilan kung bakit bumagsak ang mga inihulog, bakit nananatiling nasa orbit ang Buwan, at bakit hindi ka lumulutang - hinihila ka ng Earth sa lahat ng oras, at hinihila mo rin ang Earth nang pantay-pantay. Sa ibabaw ng lupa, parang constant na pag-pull sa ibaba ang dating nito dahil malaki ang Earth at halos lahat ng mass nito ay nasa ilalim ng iyong paa, kaya halos hindi nagbabago ang direksyon habang gumagalaw ka. Lumalabo ito sa layo: sa dalawang beses ang layo mula sa sentro, isang-sapat ang lakas. Isang maling pagkaunawa ang tinatanggap na gravity ay umiiral lang sa ibaba - umiiral ito sa lahat ng direksyon sa pagitan ng mga mass, at iyan ang dahilan kung bakit ang orbit ay freefall.","bisaya":"Ang gravity mao ang attraction sa between sa duha ka butang nga naa nga mass. Kana man nga rason nga nga makatumba ang mga gihatay, nga nga nga orbit ang Moon, ug nga dili ka mahimong makatakaw - gi-pull ka gyud ni Earth sa tanan nga panahon, ug gi-pull ka usab ni Earth sa equal nga kantidad. Sa surface, mga kanunay nga pull down ang tanan nga naa nga feeling nato tungod sa dako ug dako pa kaayo nga Earth, ug kadto nga mass nga naa gyud sa ilawom nga imong feet, busa apil lang iya undi ang direction kon mousa ka. Mahumot kini sa kayo: kung dualha ang distansia gikan sa center, quarter lang ang kadaanan. Usa ka kasagaran nga sayop ang mga tawo: naa lang daw ang gravity sa ubos - naa gyud siya sa kada direksyon sa between sa mga mass, ug kana man nga rason nga freefall ang orbit."},"confidence":null},{"id":"sci-physics-000002","topic":"science-physics","intent":"definition","concept":"energy","keywords":["energy","power","joule","work","capacity","cause","change","conserved"],"patterns":["what is energy","energy","explain energy","definition of energy","energy meaning","energy conservation","what is power","law of conservation"],"answer":"Energy is the capacity to do work or cause change, and it is conserved: it can move between forms or objects, but it does not simply appear or vanish. Power is the rate of using it, measured in watts, so a light bulb drawing 60 watts uses 60 joules every second it is on. The forms include kinetic, heat, chemical, electrical and light.","variations":["Power is the rate of using it, measured in watts, so a light bulb drawing 60 watts uses 60 joules every second it is on. Energy is the capacity to do work or cause change, and it is conserved: it can move between forms or objects, but it does not simply appear or vanish. The forms include kinetic, heat, chemical, electrical and light.","The forms include kinetic, heat, chemical, electrical and light. Energy is the capacity to do work or cause change, and it is conserved: it can move between forms or objects, but it does not simply appear or vanish. Power is the rate of using it, measured in watts, so a light bulb drawing 60 watts uses 60 joules every second it is on."]},{"id":"sci-physics-000003","topic":"science-physics","intent":"definition","concept":"electricity","keywords":["electricity","current","voltage","resistance","ohm","conductor","kuryente","electric","flow","charge","material"],"patterns":["what is electricity","electricity","explain electricity","definition of electricity","electricity meaning","what is electrical resistance","what is ohm's law","why does a light bulb glow","ano ang electricity","unsa ang kuryente"],"answer":"Electric current is a flow of charge, and the resistance a material offers to that flow depends on its structure. Metals have free electrons that move easily and so conduct well, while materials with electrons locked into place insulate. The relationship is Ohm's law, where current is voltage divided by resistance, and the practical consequence is that heating a wire lowers its resistance, which is exactly why a bulb filament glows.","variations":["Metals have free electrons that move easily and so conduct well, while materials with electrons locked into place insulate. Electric current is a flow of charge, and the resistance a material offers to that flow depends on its structure. The relationship is Ohm's law, where current is voltage divided by resistance, and the practical consequence is that heating a wire lowers its resistance, which is exactly why a bulb filament glows.","The relationship is Ohm's law, where current is voltage divided by resistance, and the practical consequence is that heating a wire lowers its resistance, which is exactly why a bulb filament glows. Electric current is a flow of charge, and the resistance a material offers to that flow depends on its structure. Metals have free electrons that move easily and so conduct well, while materials with electrons locked into place insulate."],"tl":"electricity","bis":"kuryente"},{"id":"sci-physics-000004","topic":"science-physics","intent":"definition","concept":"momentum","keywords":["momentum","mass","velocity","vector","collision","conservation","impulse","rocket","quantity","motion","multiplied"],"patterns":["what is momentum","momentum","explain momentum","definition of momentum","momentum meaning","what is conservation of momentum","momentum vs velocity","ano ang momentum","unsa ang momentum"],"answer":"Momentum is the quantity of motion, and it is mass multiplied by velocity, so a heavy slow object and a light fast one can carry the same momentum. The reason it matters is conservation: in a closed system with no outside force, total momentum cannot change, and the everyday example is a stationary trolley that gains momentum in one direction and pushes a second trolley in the other, with the total before and after identical. This is what makes rockets work in empty space, since a rocket does not push against air, and what makes a collision dangerous even at low speed when the mass is large. Momentum is a vector, so direction is part of the value, and that is why a glancing blow does different damage from a head-on one: the component along the line of impact is the part that transfers. The conservation law for energy is separate and both apply at once, which is why a bouncing ball loses energy while the total momentum of the system stays the same.","variations":["The reason it matters is conservation: in a closed system with no outside force, total momentum cannot change, and the everyday example is a stationary trolley that gains momentum in one direction and pushes a second trolley in the other, with the total before and after identical. Momentum is the quantity of motion, and it is mass multiplied by velocity, so a heavy slow object and a light fast one can carry the same momentum. This is what makes rockets work in empty space, since a rocket does not push against air, and what makes a collision dangerous even at low speed when the mass is large. Momentum is a vector, so direction is part of the value, and that is why a glancing blow does different damage from a head-on one: the component along the line of impact is the part that transfers. The conservation law for energy is separate and both apply at once, which is why a bouncing ball loses energy while the total momentum of the system stays the same.","This is what makes rockets work in empty space, since a rocket does not push against air, and what makes a collision dangerous even at low speed when the mass is large. Momentum is the quantity of motion, and it is mass multiplied by velocity, so a heavy slow object and a light fast one can carry the same momentum. The reason it matters is conservation: in a closed system with no outside force, total momentum cannot change, and the everyday example is a stationary trolley that gains momentum in one direction and pushes a second trolley in the other, with the total before and after identical. Momentum is a vector, so direction is part of the value, and that is why a glancing blow does different damage from a head-on one: the component along the line of impact is the part that transfers. The conservation law for energy is separate and both apply at once, which is why a bouncing ball loses energy while the total momentum of the system stays the same."],"tl":"momentum","bis":"momentum"},{"id":"sci-physics-000005","topic":"science-physics","intent":"definition","concept":"wave","keywords":["wave","sound","light","frequency","amplitude","wavelength","interference","refraction","transfer","disturbance","medium","without","itself"],"patterns":["what is wave","wave","explain wave","definition of wave","wave meaning","what is a wave","what is wavelength","why does sound not travel in space","ano ang wave","unsa ang wave"],"answer":"A wave is the transfer of a disturbance through a medium without the medium itself moving permanently, which is the part that makes waves counter-intuitive: when a rope moves, the rope comes back to where it started, and what travelled is the shape. The two families are mechanical, which need something to carry the disturbance, such as sound needing air, and electromagnetic, which do not, which is why light crosses a vacuum and sound does not. The properties describe the wave: amplitude is how big the disturbance is and relates to loudness, wavelength is the distance between repeating points and relates to pitch in sound, and frequency is how many cycles pass per second. Wave speed equals frequency multiplied by wavelength, so raising the pitch without changing the medium means shortening the wavelength rather than speeding anything up. The behaviour at a boundary matters too: waves reflect, refract, diffract around obstacles and interfere with one another, and interference is why noise-cancelling headphones work by adding a wave that cancels the arriving one.","variations":["The two families are mechanical, which need something to carry the disturbance, such as sound needing air, and electromagnetic, which do not, which is why light crosses a vacuum and sound does not. A wave is the transfer of a disturbance through a medium without the medium itself moving permanently, which is the part that makes waves counter-intuitive: when a rope moves, the rope comes back to where it started, and what travelled is the shape. The properties describe the wave: amplitude is how big the disturbance is and relates to loudness, wavelength is the distance between repeating points and relates to pitch in sound, and frequency is how many cycles pass per second. Wave speed equals frequency multiplied by wavelength, so raising the pitch without changing the medium means shortening the wavelength rather than speeding anything up. The behaviour at a boundary matters too: waves reflect, refract, diffract around obstacles and interfere with one another, and interference is why noise-cancelling headphones work by adding a wave that cancels the arriving one.","The properties describe the wave: amplitude is how big the disturbance is and relates to loudness, wavelength is the distance between repeating points and relates to pitch in sound, and frequency is how many cycles pass per second. A wave is the transfer of a disturbance through a medium without the medium itself moving permanently, which is the part that makes waves counter-intuitive: when a rope moves, the rope comes back to where it started, and what travelled is the shape. The two families are mechanical, which need something to carry the disturbance, such as sound needing air, and electromagnetic, which do not, which is why light crosses a vacuum and sound does not. Wave speed equals frequency multiplied by wavelength, so raising the pitch without changing the medium means shortening the wavelength rather than speeding anything up. The behaviour at a boundary matters too: waves reflect, refract, diffract around obstacles and interfere with one another, and interference is why noise-cancelling headphones work by adding a wave that cancels the arriving one."],"tl":"wave","bis":"wave"},{"id":"sci-physics-000006","topic":"science-physics","intent":"definition","concept":"light scattering","keywords":["light scattering","rayleigh","sky","sunset","sunrise","atmosphere","wavelength","blue","red","langit","dilaw","umaga","scattering","light","sunsets","whole","effect"],"patterns":["what is light scattering","light scattering","explain light scattering","definition of light scattering","light scattering meaning","why is the sky blue","why is the sunset red","bakit nga blue ang langit","ngano man nga blue ang langit","bakit dilaw ang umaga","ano ang langit","unsa ang langit"],"answer":"Light scattering is why the sky is blue and the sunsets are red, and the whole effect comes from the size of the particles the light meets on its way. Air molecules are far smaller than the wavelength of visible light, and when light hits something that much smaller it is scattered in every direction rather than simply passing through, so blue light is redirected across the whole sky and reaches an eye anywhere on Earth. The bias towards blue is the crucial part and it is not about colour preference: it is a fourth-power relationship, so short wavelengths are scattered much more strongly than long ones, and blue is scattered several times more than red. That is why the sky overhead is blue and the light that survives a long horizontal path through the atmosphere at sunset has had its blue removed, leaving red and orange. A sunset and a midday sky are the same scattering with a different path length, which is why \"why is the sky blue\" and \"why are sunsets red\" are one answer rather than two.","variations":["Air molecules are far smaller than the wavelength of visible light, and when light hits something that much smaller it is scattered in every direction rather than simply passing through, so blue light is redirected across the whole sky and reaches an eye anywhere on Earth. Light scattering is why the sky is blue and the sunsets are red, and the whole effect comes from the size of the particles the light meets on its way. The bias towards blue is the crucial part and it is not about colour preference: it is a fourth-power relationship, so short wavelengths are scattered much more strongly than long ones, and blue is scattered several times more than red. That is why the sky overhead is blue and the light that survives a long horizontal path through the atmosphere at sunset has had its blue removed, leaving red and orange. A sunset and a midday sky are the same scattering with a different path length, which is why \"why is the sky blue\" and \"why are sunsets red\" are one answer rather than two.","The bias towards blue is the crucial part and it is not about colour preference: it is a fourth-power relationship, so short wavelengths are scattered much more strongly than long ones, and blue is scattered several times more than red. Light scattering is why the sky is blue and the sunsets are red, and the whole effect comes from the size of the particles the light meets on its way. Air molecules are far smaller than the wavelength of visible light, and when light hits something that much smaller it is scattered in every direction rather than simply passing through, so blue light is redirected across the whole sky and reaches an eye anywhere on Earth. That is why the sky overhead is blue and the light that survives a long horizontal path through the atmosphere at sunset has had its blue removed, leaving red and orange. A sunset and a midday sky are the same scattering with a different path length, which is why \"why is the sky blue\" and \"why are sunsets red\" are one answer rather than two."],"tl":"langit","bis":"langit"},{"id":"sci-physics-000007","topic":"science-physics","intent":"definition","concept":"rainbow","keywords":["rainbow","spectrum","prism","droplet","dispersion","arc","sunlight","wavelength","produced","light","refracted","dispersed"],"patterns":["what is rainbow","rainbow","explain rainbow","definition of rainbow","rainbow meaning","what is a rainbow","how is a rainbow formed","why does a rainbow have those colours","ano ang rainbow","unsa ang rainbow"],"answer":"A rainbow is a spectrum produced when light is refracted and dispersed by water droplets, and it is a separate phenomenon from the blue sky even though the same sunlight causes both. Inside each droplet the light is bent on entry, reflected off the back of the droplet, and bent again on the way out, and because the amount of bending depends on wavelength, the colours come out separated rather than mixed. The colours appear in a fixed order, red on the outside, and that order is the same reason a prism separates white light the same way. A rainbow is really a full circle centred on the point opposite the sun, and a person standing on the ground only sees the upper arc because the rest is below the horizon, which is why standing on a hill with water behind you shows more of it. Seeing one requires the sun behind the observer and water in front, and it is the single clearest demonstration that white light is made of colours.","variations":["Inside each droplet the light is bent on entry, reflected off the back of the droplet, and bent again on the way out, and because the amount of bending depends on wavelength, the colours come out separated rather than mixed. A rainbow is a spectrum produced when light is refracted and dispersed by water droplets, and it is a separate phenomenon from the blue sky even though the same sunlight causes both. The colours appear in a fixed order, red on the outside, and that order is the same reason a prism separates white light the same way. A rainbow is really a full circle centred on the point opposite the sun, and a person standing on the ground only sees the upper arc because the rest is below the horizon, which is why standing on a hill with water behind you shows more of it. Seeing one requires the sun behind the observer and water in front, and it is the single clearest demonstration that white light is made of colours.","The colours appear in a fixed order, red on the outside, and that order is the same reason a prism separates white light the same way. A rainbow is a spectrum produced when light is refracted and dispersed by water droplets, and it is a separate phenomenon from the blue sky even though the same sunlight causes both. Inside each droplet the light is bent on entry, reflected off the back of the droplet, and bent again on the way out, and because the amount of bending depends on wavelength, the colours come out separated rather than mixed. A rainbow is really a full circle centred on the point opposite the sun, and a person standing on the ground only sees the upper arc because the rest is below the horizon, which is why standing on a hill with water behind you shows more of it. Seeing one requires the sun behind the observer and water in front, and it is the single clearest demonstration that white light is made of colours."],"tl":"rainbow","bis":"rainbow"},{"id":"sci-physics-000008","topic":"science-physics","intent":"definition","concept":"friction","keywords":["friction","surface","static","kinetic","rolling","lubrication","grip","resistance","force","resisting","relative","motion","surfaces"],"patterns":["what is friction","friction","explain friction","definition of friction","friction meaning","types of friction","why is friction needed","ano ang friction","unsa ang friction"],"answer":"Friction is the force resisting the relative motion of two surfaces in contact, and it is what allows walking, gripping a handle and a car moving rather than sliding, so the same force is a problem in one place and a necessity in another. It increases with the pressing force between the surfaces and with how rough they are, and the interesting part is that it is not a single known force: a separate term for each of the three ways it acts. Static friction is what holds a box still on a slope and is adjustable up to a limit, so a gentle push moves it and a hard one does not. Kinetic friction applies once it is sliding, and rolling friction is much smaller again, which is why wheels are used at all: a ball rolling and the same ball dragged along a surface lose very different amounts of energy. The practical consequence is that most of the energy lost in a machine is friction, and lubrication and ball bearings exist to move from one kind to the more favourable one.","variations":["It increases with the pressing force between the surfaces and with how rough they are, and the interesting part is that it is not a single known force: a separate term for each of the three ways it acts. Friction is the force resisting the relative motion of two surfaces in contact, and it is what allows walking, gripping a handle and a car moving rather than sliding, so the same force is a problem in one place and a necessity in another. Static friction is what holds a box still on a slope and is adjustable up to a limit, so a gentle push moves it and a hard one does not. Kinetic friction applies once it is sliding, and rolling friction is much smaller again, which is why wheels are used at all: a ball rolling and the same ball dragged along a surface lose very different amounts of energy. The practical consequence is that most of the energy lost in a machine is friction, and lubrication and ball bearings exist to move from one kind to the more favourable one.","Static friction is what holds a box still on a slope and is adjustable up to a limit, so a gentle push moves it and a hard one does not. Friction is the force resisting the relative motion of two surfaces in contact, and it is what allows walking, gripping a handle and a car moving rather than sliding, so the same force is a problem in one place and a necessity in another. It increases with the pressing force between the surfaces and with how rough they are, and the interesting part is that it is not a single known force: a separate term for each of the three ways it acts. Kinetic friction applies once it is sliding, and rolling friction is much smaller again, which is why wheels are used at all: a ball rolling and the same ball dragged along a surface lose very different amounts of energy. The practical consequence is that most of the energy lost in a machine is friction, and lubrication and ball bearings exist to move from one kind to the more favourable one."],"tl":"friction","bis":"friction"},{"id":"sci-physics-000009","topic":"science-physics","intent":"definition","concept":"circuit","keywords":["circuit","current","voltage","resistance","ohms","battery","load","short","kurso","closed","path","electricity","flow","around"],"patterns":["what is circuit","circuit","explain circuit","definition of circuit","circuit meaning","what is an electric circuit","what is current","what is voltage","ano ang kurso","unsa ang circuit"],"answer":"A circuit is a closed path that electricity can flow around, and the closed part is not a detail, since current exists only while the loop is complete, which is why a broken switch stops everything. The three parts every circuit has are a source that provides the push, called the electromotive force, a wire that carries the charge, and a load that resists and does something with the energy. The two quantities that describe the behaviour are current, the amount of charge flowing past a point per second, and voltage, the push across a component, and they are linked by resistance through Ohm's law, so raising either the push or the resistance raises the current. The two are frequently confused because they are measured with different meters in series and in parallel, and the reason is that current is the same everywhere in a single loop while the voltage is shared between the components in proportion to their resistance. A short circuit is what happens when the resistance is bypassed, and the result is a very large current, which is why protection devices exist.","variations":["The three parts every circuit has are a source that provides the push, called the electromotive force, a wire that carries the charge, and a load that resists and does something with the energy. A circuit is a closed path that electricity can flow around, and the closed part is not a detail, since current exists only while the loop is complete, which is why a broken switch stops everything. The two quantities that describe the behaviour are current, the amount of charge flowing past a point per second, and voltage, the push across a component, and they are linked by resistance through Ohm's law, so raising either the push or the resistance raises the current. The two are frequently confused because they are measured with different meters in series and in parallel, and the reason is that current is the same everywhere in a single loop while the voltage is shared between the components in proportion to their resistance. A short circuit is what happens when the resistance is bypassed, and the result is a very large current, which is why protection devices exist.","The two quantities that describe the behaviour are current, the amount of charge flowing past a point per second, and voltage, the push across a component, and they are linked by resistance through Ohm's law, so raising either the push or the resistance raises the current. A circuit is a closed path that electricity can flow around, and the closed part is not a detail, since current exists only while the loop is complete, which is why a broken switch stops everything. The three parts every circuit has are a source that provides the push, called the electromotive force, a wire that carries the charge, and a load that resists and does something with the energy. The two are frequently confused because they are measured with different meters in series and in parallel, and the reason is that current is the same everywhere in a single loop while the voltage is shared between the components in proportion to their resistance. A short circuit is what happens when the resistance is bypassed, and the result is a very large current, which is why protection devices exist."],"tl":"kurso","bis":"circuit"}],"lang":"english","group":"science","sub":"physics","title":"Physics","groupTitle":"Science","__file":"science/physics.json"},
  {"topic":"sports-athletics","entries":[{"id":"spo-athletics-000001","topic":"sports-athletics","intent":"definition","concept":"hurdles","keywords":["hurdles","barrier","distance","speed","technique","400","running","series","barriers","spaced","intervals"],"patterns":["what is hurdles","hurdles","explain hurdles","definition of hurdles","hurdles meaning","what is a hurdles race","how high are the hurdles","why is 400m hurdles flat","ano ang hurdles","unsa ang hurdles"],"answer":"Hurdles is running over a series of barriers spaced at intervals, and the distances are set so the race is evenly divided between speed and technique rather than being a sprint with obstacles in the way. The men's 110 metres and women's 100 metres use 1.067 metre barriers, while the 400 metres hurdles use 0.914, because the longer race allows a lower barrier. The 400 metres version is run almost entirely on the flat because there is no room to jump, which makes it a different event.","variations":["914, because the longer race allows a lower barrier. Hurdles is running over a series of barriers spaced at intervals, and the distances are set so the race is evenly divided between speed and technique rather than being a sprint with obstacles in the way. The 400 metres version is run almost entirely on the flat because there is no room to jump, which makes it a different event.","The 400 metres version is run almost entirely on the flat because there is no room to jump, which makes it a different event. Hurdles is running over a series of barriers spaced at intervals, and the distances are set so the race is evenly divided between speed and technique rather than being a sprint with obstacles in the way. 914, because the longer race allows a lower barrier."],"tl":"hurdles","bis":"hurdles"},{"id":"spo-athletics-000002","topic":"sports-athletics","intent":"definition","concept":"relay","keywords":["relay","baton","leg","anchor","exchange","zone","passes","runners","running","team"],"patterns":["what is relay","relay","explain relay","definition of relay","relay meaning","what is a relay race","who is the anchor","what happens if you drop the baton","ano ang relay","unsa ang relay"],"answer":"A relay passes a baton between runners, each running one leg, and the team result is the sum of the four times rather than the best individual time. That is why the fourth runner, the anchor, is usually the strongest, since a fast first three can absorb one poor leg. The rules are strict about where the exchange happens, inside a marked zone, and handing the baton rather than throwing it, because an invalid pass disqualifies the team.","variations":["That is why the fourth runner, the anchor, is usually the strongest, since a fast first three can absorb one poor leg. A relay passes a baton between runners, each running one leg, and the team result is the sum of the four times rather than the best individual time. The rules are strict about where the exchange happens, inside a marked zone, and handing the baton rather than throwing it, because an invalid pass disqualifies the team.","The rules are strict about where the exchange happens, inside a marked zone, and handing the baton rather than throwing it, because an invalid pass disqualifies the team. A relay passes a baton between runners, each running one leg, and the team result is the sum of the four times rather than the best individual time. That is why the fourth runner, the anchor, is usually the strongest, since a fast first three can absorb one poor leg."],"tl":"relay","bis":"relay"},{"id":"spo-athletics-000003","topic":"sports-athletics","intent":"definition","concept":"long jump","keywords":["long jump","take-off","board","sand","run-up","fault","long","jump","horizontal","measured","nearest"],"patterns":["what is long jump","long jump","explain long jump","definition of long jump","long jump meaning","why is the take-off board so close","what counts as a foul","ano ang long jump","unsa ang long jump"],"answer":"The long jump is a horizontal jump measured from the take-off board to the nearest mark in the sand, and the technique is a run-up that has to be consistent and a take-off that has to be in front of the board rather than after it. The physics is that a longer take-off board distance gives a faster approach but reduces the margin for error, which is why the board position is the technical work. Most faults are called for touching the board or the sand, rather than for a short jump.","variations":["The physics is that a longer take-off board distance gives a faster approach but reduces the margin for error, which is why the board position is the technical work. The long jump is a horizontal jump measured from the take-off board to the nearest mark in the sand, and the technique is a run-up that has to be consistent and a take-off that has to be in front of the board rather than after it. Most faults are called for touching the board or the sand, rather than for a short jump.","Most faults are called for touching the board or the sand, rather than for a short jump. The long jump is a horizontal jump measured from the take-off board to the nearest mark in the sand, and the technique is a run-up that has to be consistent and a take-off that has to be in front of the board rather than after it. The physics is that a longer take-off board distance gives a faster approach but reduces the margin for error, which is why the board position is the technical work."],"tl":"long jump","bis":"long jump"},{"id":"spo-athletics-000004","topic":"sports-athletics","intent":"definition","concept":"triple jump","keywords":["triple jump","hop","step","jump","stride","landing","triple","three","linked","jumps"],"patterns":["what is triple jump","triple jump","explain triple jump","definition of triple jump","triple jump meaning","what is a triple jump","what are the three phases","why is triple jump harder","ano ang triple jump","unsa ang triple jump"],"answer":"The triple jump is three linked jumps, hop, step and jump, and the discipline is that each phase has a purpose rather than being three equal bounds. The hop takes off and absorbs on the same leg, the step extends the stride, and the final jump converts the run into height and distance. It is the harder event of the two jumps, because the athlete must commit to a landing position in the air before knowing how far the previous phase carried them.","variations":["The hop takes off and absorbs on the same leg, the step extends the stride, and the final jump converts the run into height and distance. The triple jump is three linked jumps, hop, step and jump, and the discipline is that each phase has a purpose rather than being three equal bounds. It is the harder event of the two jumps, because the athlete must commit to a landing position in the air before knowing how far the previous phase carried them.","It is the harder event of the two jumps, because the athlete must commit to a landing position in the air before knowing how far the previous phase carried them. The triple jump is three linked jumps, hop, step and jump, and the discipline is that each phase has a purpose rather than being three equal bounds. The hop takes off and absorbs on the same leg, the step extends the stride, and the final jump converts the run into height and distance."],"tl":"triple jump","bis":"triple jump"},{"id":"spo-athletics-000005","topic":"sports-athletics","intent":"definition","concept":"sprint start","keywords":["sprint start","blocks","reaction","phase","hand","sprint","start","launch","worth","time"],"patterns":["what is sprint start","sprint start","explain sprint start","definition of sprint start","sprint start meaning","what is a sprint start","why are starting blocks used","what is a false start","ano ang sprint start","unsa ang sprint start"],"answer":"A sprint start is the launch from the blocks, and it is worth more time than most athletes expect, since the difference between a good and a poor start is a fifth of a second. The three-point stance is standard: rear foot about two foot-lengths back and roughly a hand's width ahead of the front. The reaction time dominates the first phase, so a false start is penalised even when the athlete was ready, and the rules allow one false start before disqualification in most competitions.","variations":["The three-point stance is standard: rear foot about two foot-lengths back and roughly a hand's width ahead of the front. A sprint start is the launch from the blocks, and it is worth more time than most athletes expect, since the difference between a good and a poor start is a fifth of a second. The reaction time dominates the first phase, so a false start is penalised even when the athlete was ready, and the rules allow one false start before disqualification in most competitions.","The reaction time dominates the first phase, so a false start is penalised even when the athlete was ready, and the rules allow one false start before disqualification in most competitions. A sprint start is the launch from the blocks, and it is worth more time than most athletes expect, since the difference between a good and a poor start is a fifth of a second. The three-point stance is standard: rear foot about two foot-lengths back and roughly a hand's width ahead of the front."],"tl":"sprint start","bis":"sprint start"}],"lang":"english","group":"sports","sub":"athletics","title":"Athletics","groupTitle":"Sports","__file":"sports/athletics.json"},
  {"topic":"sports-badminton","entries":[{"id":"spo-badminton-000001","topic":"sports-badminton","intent":"definition","concept":"shuttlecock","keywords":["shuttlecock","shuttle","feather","plastic","cork","speed","projectile","badminton","feathers","rather"],"patterns":["what is shuttlecock","shuttlecock","explain shuttlecock","definition of shuttlecock","shuttlecock meaning","what is a shuttlecock","why is a shuttlecock called a shuttle","how fast does a shuttle travel","ano ang shuttlecock","unsa ang shuttlecock"],"answer":"A shuttlecock is the projectile used in badminton, and it is made of feathers or plastic rather than being a hollow ball, which is why it is called a shuttle. It is the lightest and fastest object in any mainstream racket sport, hitting speeds well over 400 kilometres an hour. That speed comes from the shape, since a cork nose and flared skirt let it pass through the air with far less drag than a sphere.","variations":["It is the lightest and fastest object in any mainstream racket sport, hitting speeds well over 400 kilometres an hour. A shuttlecock is the projectile used in badminton, and it is made of feathers or plastic rather than being a hollow ball, which is why it is called a shuttle. That speed comes from the shape, since a cork nose and flared skirt let it pass through the air with far less drag than a sphere.","That speed comes from the shape, since a cork nose and flared skirt let it pass through the air with far less drag than a sphere. A shuttlecock is the projectile used in badminton, and it is made of feathers or plastic rather than being a hollow ball, which is why it is called a shuttle. It is the lightest and fastest object in any mainstream racket sport, hitting speeds well over 400 kilometres an hour."],"tl":"shuttlecock","bis":"shuttlecock"},{"id":"spo-badminton-000002","topic":"sports-badminton","intent":"definition","concept":"smash","keywords":["smash","overhead","attack","jump","flat","block","shot","hard","badminton's","primary"],"patterns":["what is smash","smash","explain smash","definition of smash","smash meaning","what is a smash in badminton","how to hit a jump smash","is smash the same as spike","ano ang smash","unsa ang smash"],"answer":"A smash is an overhead shot hit down hard, and it is badminton's primary attacking stroke, the equivalent of a spike in volleyball. It is produced by a full arm swing plus a jump, and a flat smash is almost impossible to block. The common variant is the jump smash, taken from above the head while airborne, which is far more powerful and is the shot the sport's power players are known for.","variations":["It is produced by a full arm swing plus a jump, and a flat smash is almost impossible to block. A smash is an overhead shot hit down hard, and it is badminton's primary attacking stroke, the equivalent of a spike in volleyball. The common variant is the jump smash, taken from above the head while airborne, which is far more powerful and is the shot the sport's power players are known for.","The common variant is the jump smash, taken from above the head while airborne, which is far more powerful and is the shot the sport's power players are known for. A smash is an overhead shot hit down hard, and it is badminton's primary attacking stroke, the equivalent of a spike in volleyball. It is produced by a full arm swing plus a jump, and a flat smash is almost impossible to block."],"tl":"smash","bis":"smash"},{"id":"spo-badminton-000003","topic":"sports-badminton","intent":"definition","concept":"service court","keywords":["service court","serve","land","underhand","fault","service","court","area","must"],"patterns":["what is service court","service court","explain service court","definition of service court","service court meaning","what is a service court","what is a service fault in badminton","can you serve overhead","ano ang service court","unsa ang service court"],"answer":"The service court is the area where a serve must land, and it is divided by a centre line so it is not simply the whole half. Standing in the wrong court is one of the most common beginner faults and costs the rally immediately, which is why beginners serve from the sideline rather than from behind it. The service is also underhand by rule, with the shuttle hit below the waist, and there is no overhead serve in the sport.","variations":["Standing in the wrong court is one of the most common beginner faults and costs the rally immediately, which is why beginners serve from the sideline rather than from behind it. The service court is the area where a serve must land, and it is divided by a centre line so it is not simply the whole half. The service is also underhand by rule, with the shuttle hit below the waist, and there is no overhead serve in the sport.","The service is also underhand by rule, with the shuttle hit below the waist, and there is no overhead serve in the sport. The service court is the area where a serve must land, and it is divided by a centre line so it is not simply the whole half. Standing in the wrong court is one of the most common beginner faults and costs the rally immediately, which is why beginners serve from the sideline rather than from behind it."],"tl":"service court","bis":"service court"},{"id":"spo-badminton-000004","topic":"sports-badminton","intent":"definition","concept":"doubles","keywords":["doubles","pair","court","cover","formation","rotate","played","against","whole","structure","exists"],"patterns":["what is doubles","doubles","explain doubles","definition of doubles","doubles meaning","what is doubles in badminton","how to position in doubles","is doubles different from singles","ano ang doubles","unsa ang doubles"],"answer":"Doubles is played two against two, and the whole structure exists because the shuttle is too fast for one player to cover the whole court. Pairing is about complementary positions rather than two equal players, since a strong attacker on the same side as another one leaves a gap at the back. The attacking pair in a pair game is set and rotates, and their formation of attacking side by side or one behind the other is a genuine tactical choice.","variations":["Pairing is about complementary positions rather than two equal players, since a strong attacker on the same side as another one leaves a gap at the back. Doubles is played two against two, and the whole structure exists because the shuttle is too fast for one player to cover the whole court. The attacking pair in a pair game is set and rotates, and their formation of attacking side by side or one behind the other is a genuine tactical choice.","The attacking pair in a pair game is set and rotates, and their formation of attacking side by side or one behind the other is a genuine tactical choice. Doubles is played two against two, and the whole structure exists because the shuttle is too fast for one player to cover the whole court. Pairing is about complementary positions rather than two equal players, since a strong attacker on the same side as another one leaves a gap at the back."],"tl":"doubles","bis":"doubles"},{"id":"spo-badminton-000005","topic":"sports-badminton","intent":"definition","concept":"let","keywords":["let","replay","interrupted","etiquette","casual","call","rally","shuttle","landing","cord"],"patterns":["what is let","let","explain let","definition of let","let meaning","what is a let in badminton","when can you call a let","who can call a let","ano ang let","unsa ang let"],"answer":"A let is a call made when a rally is interrupted, usually by a shuttle landing on the net cord and dropping back, and it causes the point to be replayed rather than awarded. Only the side that was not obviously winning may call it, which is a rule about etiquette as much as fairness. It also happens when somebody is not ready, and in a casual game the correct procedure is to stop the rally and say let rather than to play on.","variations":["Only the side that was not obviously winning may call it, which is a rule about etiquette as much as fairness. A let is a call made when a rally is interrupted, usually by a shuttle landing on the net cord and dropping back, and it causes the point to be replayed rather than awarded. It also happens when somebody is not ready, and in a casual game the correct procedure is to stop the rally and say let rather than to play on.","It also happens when somebody is not ready, and in a casual game the correct procedure is to stop the rally and say let rather than to play on. A let is a call made when a rally is interrupted, usually by a shuttle landing on the net cord and dropping back, and it causes the point to be replayed rather than awarded. Only the side that was not obviously winning may call it, which is a rule about etiquette as much as fairness."],"tl":"let","bis":"let"}],"lang":"english","group":"sports","sub":"badminton","title":"Badminton","groupTitle":"Sports","__file":"sports/badminton.json"},
  {"topic":"sports-basketball","entries":[{"id":"spo-basketball-000001","topic":"sports-basketball","intent":"definition","concept":"dunk","keywords":["dunk","score","block","restricted","whistle","dunks","scoring","taking","ball","hoop","rather"],"patterns":["what is dunk","dunk","explain dunk","definition of dunk","dunk meaning","what is a dunk","how many points is a dunk","why are dunks restricted","ano ang dunk","unsa ang dunks"],"answer":"A dunk is scoring by taking the ball down through the hoop rather than laying it in, and it is worth two points exactly like a normal basket, so its whole value is that it cannot be blocked. That is also why it is restricted in game situations, since an uncontestable two points would break the game; early rules forbade it entirely in a certain year. The most contested version is one taken in the last seconds, where a team is gambling on the whistle not going.","variations":["That is also why it is restricted in game situations, since an uncontestable two points would break the game; early rules forbade it entirely in a certain year. A dunk is scoring by taking the ball down through the hoop rather than laying it in, and it is worth two points exactly like a normal basket, so its whole value is that it cannot be blocked. The most contested version is one taken in the last seconds, where a team is gambling on the whistle not going.","The most contested version is one taken in the last seconds, where a team is gambling on the whistle not going. A dunk is scoring by taking the ball down through the hoop rather than laying it in, and it is worth two points exactly like a normal basket, so its whole value is that it cannot be blocked. That is also why it is restricted in game situations, since an uncontestable two points would break the game; early rules forbade it entirely in a certain year."],"tl":"dunk","bis":"dunks"},{"id":"spo-basketball-000002","topic":"sports-basketball","intent":"definition","concept":"three-pointer","keywords":["three-pointer","arc","line","spacing","corner","three","pointer","shot","beyond","metres"],"patterns":["what is three-pointer","three-pointer","explain three-pointer","definition of three-pointer","three-pointer meaning","what is a three pointer","how far is the three point line","how many points is a three","ano ang three-pointer","unsa ang three"],"answer":"A three-pointer is a shot from beyond the arc, a line about 6.7 metres from the basket at the top and closer in the corners, and it is worth one more point than a two. The arc exists because the court is symmetrical: pulling the three line straight would make the corners far more valuable than the wings. The shot became central in the 1990s, and the maths that followed changed how spacing and defending work.","variations":["The arc exists because the court is symmetrical: pulling the three line straight would make the corners far more valuable than the wings. 7 metres from the basket at the top and closer in the corners, and it is worth one more point than a two. The shot became central in the 1990s, and the maths that followed changed how spacing and defending work.","The shot became central in the 1990s, and the maths that followed changed how spacing and defending work. 7 metres from the basket at the top and closer in the corners, and it is worth one more point than a two. The arc exists because the court is symmetrical: pulling the three line straight would make the corners far more valuable than the wings."],"tl":"three-pointer","bis":"three"},{"id":"spo-basketball-000003","topic":"sports-basketball","intent":"definition","concept":"rebound","keywords":["rebound","offensive","defensive","possession","miss","regaining","after","shot","missed"],"patterns":["what is rebound","rebound","explain rebound","definition of rebound","rebound meaning","what is a rebound","offensive vs defensive rebound","why is rebounding important","ano ang rebound","unsa ang rebound"],"answer":"A rebound is regaining possession after a shot that missed, and the word covers both the offensive rebound that keeps an attack alive and the defensive one that starts a break. The arithmetic favours the defence, since a miss sends everybody to the same place, which is why the best rebounding teams are usually good in transition. Box outs and positioning decide far more of them than size does.","variations":["The arithmetic favours the defence, since a miss sends everybody to the same place, which is why the best rebounding teams are usually good in transition. A rebound is regaining possession after a shot that missed, and the word covers both the offensive rebound that keeps an attack alive and the defensive one that starts a break. Box outs and positioning decide far more of them than size does.","Box outs and positioning decide far more of them than size does. A rebound is regaining possession after a shot that missed, and the word covers both the offensive rebound that keeps an attack alive and the defensive one that starts a break. The arithmetic favours the defence, since a miss sends everybody to the same place, which is why the best rebounding teams are usually good in transition."],"tl":"rebound","bis":"rebound"},{"id":"spo-basketball-000004","topic":"sports-basketball","intent":"definition","concept":"triple double","keywords":["triple double","points","rebounds","assists","ten","rare","triple","double","single","game","player","records"],"patterns":["what is triple double","triple double","explain triple double","definition of triple double","triple double meaning","what is a triple double","how many points for a triple double","is a quadruple double possible","ano ang triple double","unsa ang triple double"],"answer":"A triple double is a single game in which one player records at least ten in each of points, rebounds and assists, and it is the closest basketball has to a single-game measure of all-round contribution. The same ten-point threshold applies to blocks and steals, and a quadruple double is possible though it has never been recorded in an official game. The number was chosen to be rare enough to mean something, and a close game is worth more to a team than a big individual number.","variations":["The same ten-point threshold applies to blocks and steals, and a quadruple double is possible though it has never been recorded in an official game. A triple double is a single game in which one player records at least ten in each of points, rebounds and assists, and it is the closest basketball has to a single-game measure of all-round contribution. The number was chosen to be rare enough to mean something, and a close game is worth more to a team than a big individual number.","The number was chosen to be rare enough to mean something, and a close game is worth more to a team than a big individual number. A triple double is a single game in which one player records at least ten in each of points, rebounds and assists, and it is the closest basketball has to a single-game measure of all-round contribution. The same ten-point threshold applies to blocks and steals, and a quadruple double is possible though it has never been recorded in an official game."],"tl":"triple double","bis":"triple double"},{"id":"spo-basketball-000005","topic":"sports-basketball","intent":"definition","concept":"dribble","keywords":["dribble","bounce","move","pass","crossover","balance","ball","moving","illegal","almost"],"patterns":["what is dribble","dribble","explain dribble","definition of dribble","dribble meaning","what is a dribble","why can't you dribble in football","what is a crossover","ano ang dribble","unsa ang dribble"],"answer":"To dribble is to bounce the ball while moving, and it is illegal in almost every other sport, which makes it basketball's defining skill. Its purpose is to create an advantage in position, and a player who only dribbles to get somewhere has already given up the other option of passing. The hardest version is the crossover, where the ball is moved between legs to change direction, and it costs more in balance than it appears to.","variations":["Its purpose is to create an advantage in position, and a player who only dribbles to get somewhere has already given up the other option of passing. To dribble is to bounce the ball while moving, and it is illegal in almost every other sport, which makes it basketball's defining skill. The hardest version is the crossover, where the ball is moved between legs to change direction, and it costs more in balance than it appears to.","The hardest version is the crossover, where the ball is moved between legs to change direction, and it costs more in balance than it appears to. To dribble is to bounce the ball while moving, and it is illegal in almost every other sport, which makes it basketball's defining skill. Its purpose is to create an advantage in position, and a player who only dribbles to get somewhere has already given up the other option of passing."],"tl":"dribble","bis":"dribble"}],"lang":"english","group":"sports","sub":"basketball","title":"Basketball","groupTitle":"Sports","__file":"sports/basketball.json"},
  {"topic":"sports-fitness","entries":[{"id":"spo-fitness-000001","topic":"sports-fitness","intent":"definition","concept":"cardio","keywords":["cardio","sustained","fitness","range","easy","exercise","raises","heart","rate"],"patterns":["what is cardio","cardio","explain cardio","definition of cardio","cardio meaning","is cardio the same as aerobic","how often should i do cardio","ano ang cardio","unsa ang cardio"],"answer":"Cardio is any exercise that raises heart rate for a sustained period, and it is the category most people mean when they say they need to get fit. The measure that matters is not the heart rate at its peak but how much of the week is spent in a range where talking becomes difficult. That range is the one that changes fitness, and it is lower than most beginners assume, which is why easy sessions do more than they appear to.","variations":["The measure that matters is not the heart rate at its peak but how much of the week is spent in a range where talking becomes difficult. Cardio is any exercise that raises heart rate for a sustained period, and it is the category most people mean when they say they need to get fit. That range is the one that changes fitness, and it is lower than most beginners assume, which is why easy sessions do more than they appear to.","That range is the one that changes fitness, and it is lower than most beginners assume, which is why easy sessions do more than they appear to. Cardio is any exercise that raises heart rate for a sustained period, and it is the category most people mean when they say they need to get fit. The measure that matters is not the heart rate at its peak but how much of the week is spent in a range where talking becomes difficult."],"tl":"cardio","bis":"cardio"},{"id":"spo-fitness-000002","topic":"sports-fitness","intent":"definition","concept":"progressive overload","keywords":["progressive overload","adapt","muscle","weight","repetition","tendon","progressive","overload","principle","adapts","asked"],"patterns":["what is progressive overload","progressive overload","explain progressive overload","definition of progressive overload","progressive overload meaning","how do you apply it","is it only for weights","ano ang progressive overload","unsa ang progressive overload"],"answer":"Progressive overload is the principle that a muscle adapts only when it is asked to do more than it currently can, and it applies to almost every kind of training rather than just weightlifting. The extra can be weight, repetitions, distance, or difficulty, and it has to be small and gradual. Doing too much too fast is what produces injury rather than progress, since tendons and connective tissue adapt more slowly than muscle does.","variations":["The extra can be weight, repetitions, distance, or difficulty, and it has to be small and gradual. Progressive overload is the principle that a muscle adapts only when it is asked to do more than it currently can, and it applies to almost every kind of training rather than just weightlifting. Doing too much too fast is what produces injury rather than progress, since tendons and connective tissue adapt more slowly than muscle does.","Doing too much too fast is what produces injury rather than progress, since tendons and connective tissue adapt more slowly than muscle does. Progressive overload is the principle that a muscle adapts only when it is asked to do more than it currently can, and it applies to almost every kind of training rather than just weightlifting. The extra can be weight, repetitions, distance, or difficulty, and it has to be small and gradual."],"tl":"progressive overload","bis":"progressive overload"},{"id":"spo-fitness-000003","topic":"sports-fitness","intent":"definition","concept":"mobility","keywords":["mobility","joint","range","flexibility","loaded","compensate","move","separate"],"patterns":["what is mobility","mobility","explain mobility","definition of mobility","mobility meaning","difference between mobility and flexibility","how to improve mobility","ano ang mobility","unsa ang mobility"],"answer":"Mobility is the range a joint can move through, and it is separate from flexibility, which is the length of a muscle at rest. Strengthening a range that a joint cannot actually use is the usual mistake, since a heavy squat through a restricted ankle does not train the squat, it trains compensation. The practical work is moving each joint through its full range unloaded and slowly, which most people find uncomfortable and therefore skip, and that discomfort is the useful signal.","variations":["Strengthening a range that a joint cannot actually use is the usual mistake, since a heavy squat through a restricted ankle does not train the squat, it trains compensation. Mobility is the range a joint can move through, and it is separate from flexibility, which is the length of a muscle at rest. The practical work is moving each joint through its full range unloaded and slowly, which most people find uncomfortable and therefore skip, and that discomfort is the useful signal.","The practical work is moving each joint through its full range unloaded and slowly, which most people find uncomfortable and therefore skip, and that discomfort is the useful signal. Mobility is the range a joint can move through, and it is separate from flexibility, which is the length of a muscle at rest. Strengthening a range that a joint cannot actually use is the usual mistake, since a heavy squat through a restricted ankle does not train the squat, it trains compensation."],"tl":"mobility","bis":"mobility"},{"id":"spo-fitness-000004","topic":"sports-fitness","intent":"definition","concept":"delOAD","keywords":["delOAD","volume","fatigue","week","intensity","recover","reduced","training","popular"],"patterns":["what is deload","deload","explain deload","definition of deload","deload meaning","what is a deload week","is deloading necessary","when should you deload","ano ang deload","unsa ang deload"],"answer":"Deload is a week of reduced training volume, and it is the least popular part of a programme because it feels like going backwards while the training log still looks empty. The reason it works is that fitness and fatigue are separate: a hard week builds both, and the fitness outlasts the fatigue while the fatigue does not. Cutting the volume for a week while keeping the intensity lets the fatigue clear without losing the adaptation.","variations":["The reason it works is that fitness and fatigue are separate: a hard week builds both, and the fitness outlasts the fatigue while the fatigue does not. Deload is a week of reduced training volume, and it is the least popular part of a programme because it feels like going backwards while the training log still looks empty. Cutting the volume for a week while keeping the intensity lets the fatigue clear without losing the adaptation.","Cutting the volume for a week while keeping the intensity lets the fatigue clear without losing the adaptation. Deload is a week of reduced training volume, and it is the least popular part of a programme because it feels like going backwards while the training log still looks empty. The reason it works is that fitness and fatigue are separate: a hard week builds both, and the fitness outlasts the fatigue while the fatigue does not."],"tl":"deload","bis":"deload"},{"id":"spo-fitness-000005","topic":"sports-fitness","intent":"definition","concept":"warm-up","keywords":["warm-up","temperature","stretch","prime","pattern","force","warm","raises","primes","movement"],"patterns":["what is warm-up","warm-up","explain warm-up","definition of warm-up","warm-up meaning","what is a warm-up","should you stretch before exercise","why not stretch before sprinting","ano ang warm-up","unsa ang warm-up"],"answer":"A warm-up raises temperature and primes the movement pattern before the main effort, and it is not the same as stretching, which is a separate and much more debated activity. What actually helps is moving through the specific actions about to be performed, lightly at first, so the nervous system has run the pattern once. Static stretching held for a long time before a sprint can temporarily reduce force output, which is why the long holds are discouraged immediately before competition.","variations":["What actually helps is moving through the specific actions about to be performed, lightly at first, so the nervous system has run the pattern once. A warm-up raises temperature and primes the movement pattern before the main effort, and it is not the same as stretching, which is a separate and much more debated activity. Static stretching held for a long time before a sprint can temporarily reduce force output, which is why the long holds are discouraged immediately before competition.","Static stretching held for a long time before a sprint can temporarily reduce force output, which is why the long holds are discouraged immediately before competition. A warm-up raises temperature and primes the movement pattern before the main effort, and it is not the same as stretching, which is a separate and much more debated activity. What actually helps is moving through the specific actions about to be performed, lightly at first, so the nervous system has run the pattern once."],"tl":"warm-up","bis":"warm-up"},{"id":"spo-fitness-000006","topic":"sports-fitness","intent":"definition","concept":"hydration","keywords":["hydration","sweat","urine","electrolytes","salt","exercise","evaporating","balance","hydrated","fluid","body","takes","loses"],"patterns":["what is hydration","hydration","explain hydration","definition of hydration","hydration meaning","how much water should i drink","what is electrolytes","ano ang hydrated","unsa ang hydration"],"answer":"Hydration is the balance between the fluid a body takes in and the fluid it loses, and in hot weather or during exercise the losing side wins by default because sweat is the body's main cooling mechanism. Sweat cools by evaporating, which is why a humid day is harder on you than a dry one at the same temperature: the sweat cannot evaporate fast enough, so you sweat more and cool less. The signs of losing too much are thirst, a dark colour in the urine, headache, dizziness and tiredness, and the order matters because thirst arrives late relative to the actual loss. During exercise the practical advice is to drink ahead rather than to wait, to take in fluid every fifteen or twenty minutes, and to add salt when the session is long or the sweat is heavy, since a very long run can strip salt as well as water. The overcorrection is also real and less well known: drinking a very large amount of water in a short time can itself cause a dangerous condition, so a person should drink according to loss rather than to a rule about how much water is healthy.","variations":["Sweat cools by evaporating, which is why a humid day is harder on you than a dry one at the same temperature: the sweat cannot evaporate fast enough, so you sweat more and cool less. Hydration is the balance between the fluid a body takes in and the fluid it loses, and in hot weather or during exercise the losing side wins by default because sweat is the body's main cooling mechanism. The signs of losing too much are thirst, a dark colour in the urine, headache, dizziness and tiredness, and the order matters because thirst arrives late relative to the actual loss. During exercise the practical advice is to drink ahead rather than to wait, to take in fluid every fifteen or twenty minutes, and to add salt when the session is long or the sweat is heavy, since a very long run can strip salt as well as water. The overcorrection is also real and less well known: drinking a very large amount of water in a short time can itself cause a dangerous condition, so a person should drink according to loss rather than to a rule about how much water is healthy.","The signs of losing too much are thirst, a dark colour in the urine, headache, dizziness and tiredness, and the order matters because thirst arrives late relative to the actual loss. Hydration is the balance between the fluid a body takes in and the fluid it loses, and in hot weather or during exercise the losing side wins by default because sweat is the body's main cooling mechanism. Sweat cools by evaporating, which is why a humid day is harder on you than a dry one at the same temperature: the sweat cannot evaporate fast enough, so you sweat more and cool less. During exercise the practical advice is to drink ahead rather than to wait, to take in fluid every fifteen or twenty minutes, and to add salt when the session is long or the sweat is heavy, since a very long run can strip salt as well as water. The overcorrection is also real and less well known: drinking a very large amount of water in a short time can itself cause a dangerous condition, so a person should drink according to loss rather than to a rule about how much water is healthy."],"tl":"hydrated","bis":"hydration"},{"id":"spo-fitness-000007","topic":"sports-fitness","intent":"definition","concept":"endurance","keywords":["endurance","aerobic","stamina","heart","lungs","lactate","altitude","base","capacity","effort","going","time","quality"],"patterns":["what is endurance","endurance","explain endurance","definition of endurance","endurance meaning","endurance vs stamina","how do i build endurance","ano ang endurance","unsa ang endurance"],"answer":"Endurance is the capacity to keep an effort going over time, and it is a quality rather than a single number: it depends on how long, how hard, and at what intensity, which is why someone can have enormous endurance in one sport and none in another. Physically it rests on the aerobic system, which supplies energy using oxygen, and the limit is how fast the heart and lungs can deliver oxygen to the working muscle rather than how much oxygen is present, which is why training at altitude is a real technique. It is also limited by the muscles' own ability to clear lactate, since a sprint depends on energy made without oxygen and clearing the by-product of that is what allows a second effort. Training builds it through a larger and denser network of blood vessels, so the same effort moves less blood and costs less, and the gain is slow and specific to the kind of work done. The practical test is that an endurance base is built with long efforts, not with sprints, and a schedule that is only hard ends up producing a person who is quick and cannot finish.","variations":["Physically it rests on the aerobic system, which supplies energy using oxygen, and the limit is how fast the heart and lungs can deliver oxygen to the working muscle rather than how much oxygen is present, which is why training at altitude is a real technique. Endurance is the capacity to keep an effort going over time, and it is a quality rather than a single number: it depends on how long, how hard, and at what intensity, which is why someone can have enormous endurance in one sport and none in another. It is also limited by the muscles' own ability to clear lactate, since a sprint depends on energy made without oxygen and clearing the by-product of that is what allows a second effort. Training builds it through a larger and denser network of blood vessels, so the same effort moves less blood and costs less, and the gain is slow and specific to the kind of work done. The practical test is that an endurance base is built with long efforts, not with sprints, and a schedule that is only hard ends up producing a person who is quick and cannot finish.","It is also limited by the muscles' own ability to clear lactate, since a sprint depends on energy made without oxygen and clearing the by-product of that is what allows a second effort. Endurance is the capacity to keep an effort going over time, and it is a quality rather than a single number: it depends on how long, how hard, and at what intensity, which is why someone can have enormous endurance in one sport and none in another. Physically it rests on the aerobic system, which supplies energy using oxygen, and the limit is how fast the heart and lungs can deliver oxygen to the working muscle rather than how much oxygen is present, which is why training at altitude is a real technique. Training builds it through a larger and denser network of blood vessels, so the same effort moves less blood and costs less, and the gain is slow and specific to the kind of work done. The practical test is that an endurance base is built with long efforts, not with sprints, and a schedule that is only hard ends up producing a person who is quick and cannot finish."],"tl":"endurance","bis":"endurance"}],"lang":"english","group":"sports","sub":"fitness","title":"Fitness","groupTitle":"Sports","__file":"sports/fitness.json"},
  {"topic":"sports-football","entries":[{"id":"spo-football-000001","topic":"sports-football","intent":"definition","concept":"offside","keywords":["offside","defender","pass","player","moment","played","nearer"],"patterns":["what is offside","offside","explain offside","definition of offside","offside meaning","why am i offside","when is offside called","ano ang offside","unsa ang offside"],"answer":"A player is offside if, at the moment the ball is played to them, they are nearer the opponent's goal line than both the ball and the second-last defender. The rule exists to stop a single long pass producing an easy chance. What makes it confusing is that being offside is judged at the pass, not at the shot, so a player can run twenty yards after a through ball and still be flagged for where they were when it was played.","variations":["The rule exists to stop a single long pass producing an easy chance. A player is offside if, at the moment the ball is played to them, they are nearer the opponent's goal line than both the ball and the second-last defender. What makes it confusing is that being offside is judged at the pass, not at the shot, so a player can run twenty yards after a through ball and still be flagged for where they were when it was played.","What makes it confusing is that being offside is judged at the pass, not at the shot, so a player can run twenty yards after a through ball and still be flagged for where they were when it was played. A player is offside if, at the moment the ball is played to them, they are nearer the opponent's goal line than both the ball and the second-last defender. The rule exists to stop a single long pass producing an easy chance."],"tl":"offside","bis":"offside"},{"id":"spo-football-000002","topic":"sports-football","intent":"definition","concept":"penalty","keywords":["penalty","foul","spot","dive","awarded","inside","area","kick"],"patterns":["what is penalty","penalty","explain penalty","definition of penalty","penalty meaning","what is a penalty","where is the penalty spot","why do players dive","ano ang penalty","unsa ang penalty"],"answer":"A penalty is awarded for a foul inside the penalty area, and the kick is taken from a fixed spot twelve yards from the goal with only the goalkeeper allowed to move. The apparent unfairness is deliberate: it is the only position on the pitch where a goal is scored more often than not, because the dive narrows the angle. The rule that decides most penalties is the one about which foot or body part made contact, not how hard.","variations":["The apparent unfairness is deliberate: it is the only position on the pitch where a goal is scored more often than not, because the dive narrows the angle. A penalty is awarded for a foul inside the penalty area, and the kick is taken from a fixed spot twelve yards from the goal with only the goalkeeper allowed to move. The rule that decides most penalties is the one about which foot or body part made contact, not how hard.","The rule that decides most penalties is the one about which foot or body part made contact, not how hard. A penalty is awarded for a foul inside the penalty area, and the kick is taken from a fixed spot twelve yards from the goal with only the goalkeeper allowed to move. The apparent unfairness is deliberate: it is the only position on the pitch where a goal is scored more often than not, because the dive narrows the angle."],"tl":"penalty","bis":"penalty"},{"id":"spo-football-000003","topic":"sports-football","intent":"definition","concept":"corner","keywords":["corner","cross","header","awarded","defending","team","puts"],"patterns":["what is corner","corner","explain corner","definition of corner","corner meaning","what is a corner kick","when do you get a corner","ano ang corner","unsa ang corner"],"answer":"A corner is awarded when the defending team puts the ball over their own goal line, last touched by an attacker. It is taken from the corner of the pitch nearest the goal, which is why attacking set pieces are aimed short. The expected goal value of a corner is low, and a corner won from a good cross is usually worth more than the one from a long delivery everyone can head.","variations":["It is taken from the corner of the pitch nearest the goal, which is why attacking set pieces are aimed short. A corner is awarded when the defending team puts the ball over their own goal line, last touched by an attacker. The expected goal value of a corner is low, and a corner won from a good cross is usually worth more than the one from a long delivery everyone can head.","The expected goal value of a corner is low, and a corner won from a good cross is usually worth more than the one from a long delivery everyone can head. A corner is awarded when the defending team puts the ball over their own goal line, last touched by an attacker. It is taken from the corner of the pitch nearest the goal, which is why attacking set pieces are aimed short."],"tl":"corner","bis":"corner"},{"id":"spo-football-000004","topic":"sports-football","intent":"definition","concept":"handball","keywords":["handball","hand","arm","penalty","caution","deliberate","reckless","contact"],"patterns":["what is handball","handball","explain handball","definition of handball","handball meaning","is handball a foul","what is a handball","how strict is the handball rule","ano ang handball","unsa ang handball"],"answer":"Handball is a deliberate or reckless contact with the ball by the arm or hand, and the offence is called handball rather than a foul, which reflects how strictly it is policed. The rule is about the position of the arm rather than the intent, because a player cannot always tell a bounce off a defender's chest from one off their arm. A handball inside the area is a penalty, and a handball anywhere while scoring is a red card.","variations":["The rule is about the position of the arm rather than the intent, because a player cannot always tell a bounce off a defender's chest from one off their arm. Handball is a deliberate or reckless contact with the ball by the arm or hand, and the offence is called handball rather than a foul, which reflects how strictly it is policed. A handball inside the area is a penalty, and a handball anywhere while scoring is a red card.","A handball inside the area is a penalty, and a handball anywhere while scoring is a red card. Handball is a deliberate or reckless contact with the ball by the arm or hand, and the offence is called handball rather than a foul, which reflects how strictly it is policed. The rule is about the position of the arm rather than the intent, because a player cannot always tell a bounce off a defender's chest from one off their arm."],"tl":"handball","bis":"handball"},{"id":"spo-football-000005","topic":"sports-football","intent":"definition","concept":"substitution","keywords":["substitution","substitute","bench","squad","formation","tactics","swaps","player","another","play","competitions"],"patterns":["what is substitution","substitution","explain substitution","definition of substitution","substitution meaning","how many substitutions are allowed","when can a substitution happen","ano ang substitution","unsa ang substitution"],"answer":"A substitution swaps one player for another during play, and most competitions allow a set number rather than a rolling stream. The limit is a tactical tool as much as a physical one: three substitutions means a manager who has used them has committed to the shape on the pitch. Historically the limit was one, and the move to five was about player welfare and about broadcasters.","variations":["The limit is a tactical tool as much as a physical one: three substitutions means a manager who has used them has committed to the shape on the pitch. A substitution swaps one player for another during play, and most competitions allow a set number rather than a rolling stream. Historically the limit was one, and the move to five was about player welfare and about broadcasters.","Historically the limit was one, and the move to five was about player welfare and about broadcasters. A substitution swaps one player for another during play, and most competitions allow a set number rather than a rolling stream. The limit is a tactical tool as much as a physical one: three substitutions means a manager who has used them has committed to the shape on the pitch."],"tl":"substitution","bis":"substitution"},{"id":"spo-football-000006","topic":"sports-football","intent":"definition","concept":"world cup","keywords":["world cup","fifa","tournament","1930","brazil","knockout","world","held","every","four","years"],"patterns":["what is world cup","world cup","explain world cup","definition of world cup","world cup meaning","how often is the world cup","who has won the most world cups","ano ang world cup","unsa ang world cup"],"answer":"The FIFA World Cup is held every four years and is the oldest international football competition, first staged in 1930. Brazil is the only nation to have won it five times, and no team has won it on home soil except two. The tournament expanded from 24 to 32 teams in 1998, which lengthened it and diluted the group stage without making the knockout rounds any easier.","variations":["Brazil is the only nation to have won it five times, and no team has won it on home soil except two. The FIFA World Cup is held every four years and is the oldest international football competition, first staged in 1930. The tournament expanded from 24 to 32 teams in 1998, which lengthened it and diluted the group stage without making the knockout rounds any easier.","The tournament expanded from 24 to 32 teams in 1998, which lengthened it and diluted the group stage without making the knockout rounds any easier. The FIFA World Cup is held every four years and is the oldest international football competition, first staged in 1930. Brazil is the only nation to have won it five times, and no team has won it on home soil except two."],"tl":"world cup","bis":"world cup"}],"lang":"english","group":"sports","sub":"football","title":"Football","groupTitle":"Sports","__file":"sports/football.json"},
  {"topic":"sports-martial-arts","entries":[{"id":"spo-martial-arts-000001","topic":"sports-martial-arts","intent":"definition","concept":"judo","keywords":["judo","japanese","throw","back","ground","control","grappling","ends","exchange"],"patterns":["what is judo","judo","explain judo","definition of judo","judo meaning","what wins a judo match","can you punch in judo","ano ang judo","unsa ang judo"],"answer":"Judo is a Japanese grappling art in which a throw ends the exchange, and the aim is to put an opponent on their back rather than to hurt them, which is why holds to the neck are forbidden and why a match can be won without a single punch. Competitions are decided by a throw's completeness or by ground control measured in seconds, and the two scoring systems alternate between weight classes. A judo throw is only counted if the opponent lands on their back or side, so standing drop attacks are difficult.","variations":["Competitions are decided by a throw's completeness or by ground control measured in seconds, and the two scoring systems alternate between weight classes. Judo is a Japanese grappling art in which a throw ends the exchange, and the aim is to put an opponent on their back rather than to hurt them, which is why holds to the neck are forbidden and why a match can be won without a single punch. A judo throw is only counted if the opponent lands on their back or side, so standing drop attacks are difficult.","A judo throw is only counted if the opponent lands on their back or side, so standing drop attacks are difficult. Judo is a Japanese grappling art in which a throw ends the exchange, and the aim is to put an opponent on their back rather than to hurt them, which is why holds to the neck are forbidden and why a match can be won without a single punch. Competitions are decided by a throw's completeness or by ground control measured in seconds, and the two scoring systems alternate between weight classes."],"tl":"judo","bis":"judo"},{"id":"spo-martial-arts-000002","topic":"sports-martial-arts","intent":"definition","concept":"boxing","keywords":["boxing","punch","glove","judge","knockdown","oz","boksing","combat","sport","decides","outcome"],"patterns":["what is boxing","boxing","explain boxing","definition of boxing","boxing meaning","how is a boxing match scored","why are boxing gloves small","ano ang boxing","unsa ang boksing"],"answer":"Boxing is a combat sport in which the only thing that decides the outcome is where a punch lands, and the whole craft is making one land while none of yours do. Scoring is by counting clean blows to the head and body, judged by an official, and a knockdown does not stop the contest the way it does in some other sports. The gloves are small by design, being about 10 ounces, and the reason is historical: thin gloves force correct technique and make a punch land on the bone.","variations":["Scoring is by counting clean blows to the head and body, judged by an official, and a knockdown does not stop the contest the way it does in some other sports. Boxing is a combat sport in which the only thing that decides the outcome is where a punch lands, and the whole craft is making one land while none of yours do. The gloves are small by design, being about 10 ounces, and the reason is historical: thin gloves force correct technique and make a punch land on the bone.","The gloves are small by design, being about 10 ounces, and the reason is historical: thin gloves force correct technique and make a punch land on the bone. Boxing is a combat sport in which the only thing that decides the outcome is where a punch lands, and the whole craft is making one land while none of yours do. Scoring is by counting clean blows to the head and body, judged by an official, and a knockdown does not stop the contest the way it does in some other sports."],"tl":"boxing","bis":"boksing"},{"id":"spo-martial-arts-000003","topic":"sports-martial-arts","intent":"definition","concept":"stance","keywords":["stance","range","foot","lead","orthodox","southpaw","standing","position","exchange","fought","sets"],"patterns":["what is stance","stance","explain stance","definition of stance","stance meaning","what is a stance in boxing","orthodox vs southpaw","why does stance matter","ano ang stance","unsa ang stance"],"answer":"A stance is the standing position from which an exchange is fought, and it sets the range you can work at. In boxing there are orthodox and southpaw, differing by which foot leads, and the choice is made once and rarely changed. In kickboxing a squared stance allows both kicks and punches, while a long boxing stance does not. Getting a stance wrong costs more than any technique mistake, because every subsequent movement starts from it.","variations":["In boxing there are orthodox and southpaw, differing by which foot leads, and the choice is made once and rarely changed. A stance is the standing position from which an exchange is fought, and it sets the range you can work at. In kickboxing a squared stance allows both kicks and punches, while a long boxing stance does not. Getting a stance wrong costs more than any technique mistake, because every subsequent movement starts from it.","In kickboxing a squared stance allows both kicks and punches, while a long boxing stance does not. A stance is the standing position from which an exchange is fought, and it sets the range you can work at. In boxing there are orthodox and southpaw, differing by which foot leads, and the choice is made once and rarely changed. Getting a stance wrong costs more than any technique mistake, because every subsequent movement starts from it."],"tl":"stance","bis":"stance"},{"id":"spo-martial-arts-000004","topic":"sports-martial-arts","intent":"definition","concept":"belt","keywords":["belt","rank","black","white","colour","level","martial","visible","order"],"patterns":["what is belt","belt","explain belt","definition of belt","belt meaning","what does a black belt mean","how many belts are there in judo","do all martial arts use belts","ano ang belt","unsa ang belt"],"answer":"In a belt-based martial art a belt is a visible rank, and the colour order is almost universal: white, then coloured, then black. In judo the coloured ranks run upward through a dozen or more levels, while in aikido or karate the number and the exact colours vary by organisation. The reason the system spread from judo is that black belt is a universally legible signal of a long training, which is useful in a room full of strangers.","variations":["In judo the coloured ranks run upward through a dozen or more levels, while in aikido or karate the number and the exact colours vary by organisation. In a belt-based martial art a belt is a visible rank, and the colour order is almost universal: white, then coloured, then black. The reason the system spread from judo is that black belt is a universally legible signal of a long training, which is useful in a room full of strangers.","The reason the system spread from judo is that black belt is a universally legible signal of a long training, which is useful in a room full of strangers. In a belt-based martial art a belt is a visible rank, and the colour order is almost universal: white, then coloured, then black. In judo the coloured ranks run upward through a dozen or more levels, while in aikido or karate the number and the exact colours vary by organisation."],"tl":"belt","bis":"belt"},{"id":"spo-martial-arts-000005","topic":"sports-martial-arts","intent":"definition","concept":"round","keywords":["round","bout","rest","minute","twelve","stamina","segment","separated","period"],"patterns":["what is round","round","explain round","definition of round","round meaning","how many rounds in boxing","why are there 12 rounds","what is a round in a fight","ano ang round","unsa ang round"],"answer":"A round is one segment of a bout, separated by a rest period, and the number and length of rounds is a rule rather than a consequence of the contest. Championship boxing is twelve rounds of three minutes with one minute between, while a title fight may be scheduled for fewer, and the twelve-round format is the one that most shapes the sport's difficulty. The rest exists because the decision to continue is made with accumulated fatigue, and stamina is part of the contest.","variations":["Championship boxing is twelve rounds of three minutes with one minute between, while a title fight may be scheduled for fewer, and the twelve-round format is the one that most shapes the sport's difficulty. A round is one segment of a bout, separated by a rest period, and the number and length of rounds is a rule rather than a consequence of the contest. The rest exists because the decision to continue is made with accumulated fatigue, and stamina is part of the contest.","The rest exists because the decision to continue is made with accumulated fatigue, and stamina is part of the contest. A round is one segment of a bout, separated by a rest period, and the number and length of rounds is a rule rather than a consequence of the contest. Championship boxing is twelve rounds of three minutes with one minute between, while a title fight may be scheduled for fewer, and the twelve-round format is the one that most shapes the sport's difficulty."],"tl":"round","bis":"round"}],"lang":"english","group":"sports","sub":"martial-arts","title":"Martial arts","groupTitle":"Sports","__file":"sports/martial-arts.json"},
  {"topic":"sports-running","entries":[{"id":"spo-running-000001","topic":"sports-running","intent":"definition","concept":"marathon","keywords":["marathon","42","standardise","1904","olympic","course","maraton","kilometres","distance","arbitrary","standardised"],"patterns":["what is marathon","marathon","explain marathon","definition of marathon","marathon meaning","how long is a marathon","why is a marathon 42.195 km","is a marathon run on a track","ano ang marathon","unsa ang maraton"],"answer":"A marathon is 42.195 kilometres, and the distance is not arbitrary: it was standardised in 1904 for the Olympic Games, and the extra 195 metres were added so the course would finish exactly at the stadium. Most races now use the rounded 42 kilometres while records still count the full figure. The event is run on the road rather than the track, which is why it is one of the few races where records are possible, since no two city courses are the same.","variations":["Most races now use the rounded 42 kilometres while records still count the full figure. 195 kilometres, and the distance is not arbitrary: it was standardised in 1904 for the Olympic Games, and the extra 195 metres were added so the course would finish exactly at the stadium. The event is run on the road rather than the track, which is why it is one of the few races where records are possible, since no two city courses are the same.","The event is run on the road rather than the track, which is why it is one of the few races where records are possible, since no two city courses are the same. 195 kilometres, and the distance is not arbitrary: it was standardised in 1904 for the Olympic Games, and the extra 195 metres were added so the course would finish exactly at the stadium. Most races now use the rounded 42 kilometres while records still count the full figure."],"tl":"marathon","bis":"maraton"},{"id":"spo-running-000002","topic":"sports-running","intent":"definition","concept":"pace","keywords":["pace","kilometre","speed","manage","discipline","fast","time","taken","number","runners"],"patterns":["what is pace","pace","explain pace","definition of pace","pace meaning","what is pace in running","how is pace different from speed","what is negative split","ano ang pace","unsa ang pace"],"answer":"Pace is the time taken per kilometre, and it is the number runners actually manage themselves by rather than speed, which is easier to hold steady. Saying the pace is five minutes a kilometre is a different measurement from saying the speed is twelve kilometres an hour, and the two are inverses of each other. Race strategy is largely about pace discipline, because going out fast enough to be unable to hold is the single most common way to lose.","variations":["Saying the pace is five minutes a kilometre is a different measurement from saying the speed is twelve kilometres an hour, and the two are inverses of each other. Pace is the time taken per kilometre, and it is the number runners actually manage themselves by rather than speed, which is easier to hold steady. Race strategy is largely about pace discipline, because going out fast enough to be unable to hold is the single most common way to lose.","Race strategy is largely about pace discipline, because going out fast enough to be unable to hold is the single most common way to lose. Pace is the time taken per kilometre, and it is the number runners actually manage themselves by rather than speed, which is easier to hold steady. Saying the pace is five minutes a kilometre is a different measurement from saying the speed is twelve kilometres an hour, and the two are inverses of each other."],"tl":"pace","bis":"pace"},{"id":"spo-running-000003","topic":"sports-running","intent":"definition","concept":"taper","keywords":["taper","training","reduce","race","fatigue","fitness","reduction","days","before"],"patterns":["what is taper","taper","explain taper","definition of taper","taper meaning","what is a taper","should you train the week before a race","why does tapering feel wrong","ano ang taper","unsa ang taper"],"answer":"A taper is the reduction of training in the days before a race, and it feels wrong because the fitness is still there while the work stops. The physiology is the opposite of what people expect: accumulated fitness survives a week or two of reduced volume, while accumulated fatigue does not. Athletes who taper consistently report feeling slower in training and faster on the day, and a genuine three-week taper typically produces the best result they race all season.","variations":["The physiology is the opposite of what people expect: accumulated fitness survives a week or two of reduced volume, while accumulated fatigue does not. A taper is the reduction of training in the days before a race, and it feels wrong because the fitness is still there while the work stops. Athletes who taper consistently report feeling slower in training and faster on the day, and a genuine three-week taper typically produces the best result they race all season.","Athletes who taper consistently report feeling slower in training and faster on the day, and a genuine three-week taper typically produces the best result they race all season. A taper is the reduction of training in the days before a race, and it feels wrong because the fitness is still there while the work stops. The physiology is the opposite of what people expect: accumulated fitness survives a week or two of reduced volume, while accumulated fatigue does not."],"tl":"taper","bis":"taper"},{"id":"spo-running-000004","topic":"sports-running","intent":"definition","concept":"jog","keywords":["jog","slow","conversation","pace","fitness","run","taken","slowly","enough","hold","threshold"],"patterns":["what is jog","jog","explain jog","definition of jog","jog meaning","what is a jog","difference between jogging and running","is jogging good for you","ano ang jog","unsa ang jog"],"answer":"A jog is a run taken slowly enough that you could hold a conversation, and the threshold varies with fitness, so the same pace is a jog for one person and a run for another. English has no single agreed boundary between jogging and running, unlike some languages with a fixed term for a slow gait. The practical boundary that matters is conversational pace, and it is the recommended intensity for most of an adult week.","variations":["English has no single agreed boundary between jogging and running, unlike some languages with a fixed term for a slow gait. A jog is a run taken slowly enough that you could hold a conversation, and the threshold varies with fitness, so the same pace is a jog for one person and a run for another. The practical boundary that matters is conversational pace, and it is the recommended intensity for most of an adult week.","The practical boundary that matters is conversational pace, and it is the recommended intensity for most of an adult week. A jog is a run taken slowly enough that you could hold a conversation, and the threshold varies with fitness, so the same pace is a jog for one person and a run for another. English has no single agreed boundary between jogging and running, unlike some languages with a fixed term for a slow gait."],"tl":"jog","bis":"jog"},{"id":"spo-running-000005","topic":"sports-running","intent":"definition","concept":"sprint","keywords":["sprint","burst","speed","energy","oxygen","effect","short","near","maximum"],"patterns":["what is sprint","sprint","explain sprint","definition of sprint","sprint meaning","what is a sprint","how long is a sprint","why is sprinting exhausting","ano ang sprint","unsa ang sprint"],"answer":"A sprint is a short burst at or near maximum speed, and it lasts only as long as the body's energy supply does, which in a trained athlete is roughly ten seconds before the effect fades. The energy comes from a system that does not need oxygen, which is why sprinting is exhausting in a completely different way from a long run. A sprint effect is trained by practising at that intensity despite it feeling disproportionately bad in the moment.","variations":["The energy comes from a system that does not need oxygen, which is why sprinting is exhausting in a completely different way from a long run. A sprint is a short burst at or near maximum speed, and it lasts only as long as the body's energy supply does, which in a trained athlete is roughly ten seconds before the effect fades. A sprint effect is trained by practising at that intensity despite it feeling disproportionately bad in the moment.","A sprint effect is trained by practising at that intensity despite it feeling disproportionately bad in the moment. A sprint is a short burst at or near maximum speed, and it lasts only as long as the body's energy supply does, which in a trained athlete is roughly ten seconds before the effect fades. The energy comes from a system that does not need oxygen, which is why sprinting is exhausting in a completely different way from a long run."],"tl":"sprint","bis":"sprint"}],"lang":"english","group":"sports","sub":"running","title":"Running","groupTitle":"Sports","__file":"sports/running.json"},
  {"topic":"sports-swimming","entries":[{"id":"spo-swimming-000001","topic":"sports-swimming","intent":"definition","concept":"freestyle","keywords":["freestyle","stroke","breaststroke","flat","efficient","competitive","swimming","means"],"patterns":["what is freestyle","freestyle","explain freestyle","definition of freestyle","freestyle meaning","what is freestyle swimming","is freestyle the same as front crawl","is changing stroke allowed","ano ang freestyle","unsa ang freestyle"],"answer":"Freestyle in competitive swimming means any stroke other than breaststroke, and it is the fastest because the body is flat and the kick is small, so less energy goes into resistance. What most people picture as freestyle is actually front crawl, and the two are treated as the same event in competition. The rule that trips up beginners is that you may change stroke freely, so a swimmer can switch mid-length and it still counts as freestyle, which is why records are set on efficiency.","variations":["What most people picture as freestyle is actually front crawl, and the two are treated as the same event in competition. Freestyle in competitive swimming means any stroke other than breaststroke, and it is the fastest because the body is flat and the kick is small, so less energy goes into resistance. The rule that trips up beginners is that you may change stroke freely, so a swimmer can switch mid-length and it still counts as freestyle, which is why records are set on efficiency.","The rule that trips up beginners is that you may change stroke freely, so a swimmer can switch mid-length and it still counts as freestyle, which is why records are set on efficiency. Freestyle in competitive swimming means any stroke other than breaststroke, and it is the fastest because the body is flat and the kick is small, so less energy goes into resistance. What most people picture as freestyle is actually front crawl, and the two are treated as the same event in competition."],"tl":"freestyle","bis":"freestyle"},{"id":"spo-swimming-000002","topic":"sports-swimming","intent":"definition","concept":"lane","keywords":["lane","pool","rope","wave","reflection","marked","strips","swimmer","travels"],"patterns":["what is lane","lane","explain lane","definition of lane","lane meaning","what is a lane in swimming","why are there ropes in a pool","how many lanes in a pool","ano ang lane","unsa ang lane"],"answer":"A lane is one of the marked strips a swimmer travels down, and in a pool there are usually eight, six or ten, separated by floating ropes rather than walls. The ropes matter because they reduce what is called wave reflection, so a swimmer in a middle lane is not badly penalised by the splashing of the swimmers either side. In open water there are no lanes at all, which changes strategy completely since you cannot see who is ahead.","variations":["The ropes matter because they reduce what is called wave reflection, so a swimmer in a middle lane is not badly penalised by the splashing of the swimmers either side. A lane is one of the marked strips a swimmer travels down, and in a pool there are usually eight, six or ten, separated by floating ropes rather than walls. In open water there are no lanes at all, which changes strategy completely since you cannot see who is ahead.","In open water there are no lanes at all, which changes strategy completely since you cannot see who is ahead. A lane is one of the marked strips a swimmer travels down, and in a pool there are usually eight, six or ten, separated by floating ropes rather than walls. The ropes matter because they reduce what is called wave reflection, so a swimmer in a middle lane is not badly penalised by the splashing of the swimmers either side."],"tl":"lane","bis":"lane"},{"id":"spo-swimming-000003","topic":"sports-swimming","intent":"definition","concept":"lane rope","keywords":["lane rope","floating","wave","turn","cross","disqualification","lane","rope","barrier","separates","swimmer"],"patterns":["what is lane rope","lane rope","explain lane rope","definition of lane rope","lane rope meaning","what is a lane rope","why do swimmers push off the rope","can you cross lanes","ano ang lane rope","unsa ang lane rope"],"answer":"The lane rope is the floating barrier that separates one swimmer from the next, and it is there to absorb waves rather than to stop a determined swimmer from crossing. Swimmers push off it deliberately at every turn, which is why turning technique involves a flick rather than a stop, and crossing between lanes mid-race is a disqualification. Modern ropes are made of small discs threaded on a cable, which lets water through and takes the reflection out of the wave.","variations":["Swimmers push off it deliberately at every turn, which is why turning technique involves a flick rather than a stop, and crossing between lanes mid-race is a disqualification. The lane rope is the floating barrier that separates one swimmer from the next, and it is there to absorb waves rather than to stop a determined swimmer from crossing. Modern ropes are made of small discs threaded on a cable, which lets water through and takes the reflection out of the wave.","Modern ropes are made of small discs threaded on a cable, which lets water through and takes the reflection out of the wave. The lane rope is the floating barrier that separates one swimmer from the next, and it is there to absorb waves rather than to stop a determined swimmer from crossing. Swimmers push off it deliberately at every turn, which is why turning technique involves a flick rather than a stop, and crossing between lanes mid-race is a disqualification."],"tl":"lane rope","bis":"lane rope"},{"id":"spo-swimming-000004","topic":"sports-swimming","intent":"definition","concept":"turn","keywords":["turn","wall","somersault","flip","tenths","fold","balik","swimmer","reverses","worth","several"],"patterns":["what is turn","turn","explain turn","definition of turn","turn meaning","what is a turn in swimming","what is a flip turn","how do you turn in a pool","ano ang turn","unsa ang balik"],"answer":"A turn is how a swimmer reverses at the wall, and it is worth several tenths of a second per length, which over a distance adds up to more than the margin between most finalists. There are two kinds: the somersault, used in freestyle, and the flip turn, identical in effect but named differently by stroke. What makes a turn fast is the wall being used, with a foot planted and the body folded, so the wall pushes back instead of the water stopping the swimmer.","variations":["There are two kinds: the somersault, used in freestyle, and the flip turn, identical in effect but named differently by stroke. A turn is how a swimmer reverses at the wall, and it is worth several tenths of a second per length, which over a distance adds up to more than the margin between most finalists. What makes a turn fast is the wall being used, with a foot planted and the body folded, so the wall pushes back instead of the water stopping the swimmer.","What makes a turn fast is the wall being used, with a foot planted and the body folded, so the wall pushes back instead of the water stopping the swimmer. A turn is how a swimmer reverses at the wall, and it is worth several tenths of a second per length, which over a distance adds up to more than the margin between most finalists. There are two kinds: the somersault, used in freestyle, and the flip turn, identical in effect but named differently by stroke."],"tl":"turn","bis":"balik"},{"id":"spo-swimming-000005","topic":"sports-swimming","intent":"definition","concept":"breaststroke","keywords":["breaststroke","slow","breathe","frog","surface","rhythm","slowest","competitive","stroke","head","must"],"patterns":["what is breaststroke","breaststroke","explain breaststroke","definition of breaststroke","breaststroke meaning","why is breaststroke the slowest","what is a frog kick","ano ang breaststroke","unsa ang breaststroke"],"answer":"Breaststroke is the slowest competitive stroke and the only one where the head must rise to breathe, and that constraint is why it is slow and also why it is the stroke most often taught first. The kick is called a frog kick because both legs draw up together, and the arms pull from beneath the surface. It is also the only stroke that cannot be performed continuously without breaking the surface, which is exactly why the breathing rhythm is the whole skill.","variations":["The kick is called a frog kick because both legs draw up together, and the arms pull from beneath the surface. Breaststroke is the slowest competitive stroke and the only one where the head must rise to breathe, and that constraint is why it is slow and also why it is the stroke most often taught first. It is also the only stroke that cannot be performed continuously without breaking the surface, which is exactly why the breathing rhythm is the whole skill.","It is also the only stroke that cannot be performed continuously without breaking the surface, which is exactly why the breathing rhythm is the whole skill. Breaststroke is the slowest competitive stroke and the only one where the head must rise to breathe, and that constraint is why it is slow and also why it is the stroke most often taught first. The kick is called a frog kick because both legs draw up together, and the arms pull from beneath the surface."],"tl":"breaststroke","bis":"breaststroke"}],"lang":"english","group":"sports","sub":"swimming","title":"Swimming","groupTitle":"Sports","__file":"sports/swimming.json"},
  {"topic":"sports-tennis","entries":[{"id":"spo-tennis-000001","topic":"sports-tennis","intent":"definition","concept":"deuce","keywords":["deuce","tie","game","advantage","tied","score","inside","three"],"patterns":["what is deuce","deuce","explain deuce","definition of deuce","deuce meaning","what is deuce in tennis","why does a tennis game go to advantage","what is tiebreak","ano ang deuce","unsa ang deuce"],"answer":"Deuce is a tied score inside a game, from three-all onwards, and the game then runs to two clear points rather than to a fixed total. The reason for that rule is that a short game rewards luck, so a two-point margin makes each point matter more. In a tiebreak the same idea is applied to a whole set, where a player needs seven points with a lead of two, so it too can go past the nominal total.","variations":["The reason for that rule is that a short game rewards luck, so a two-point margin makes each point matter more. Deuce is a tied score inside a game, from three-all onwards, and the game then runs to two clear points rather than to a fixed total. In a tiebreak the same idea is applied to a whole set, where a player needs seven points with a lead of two, so it too can go past the nominal total.","In a tiebreak the same idea is applied to a whole set, where a player needs seven points with a lead of two, so it too can go past the nominal total. Deuce is a tied score inside a game, from three-all onwards, and the game then runs to two clear points rather than to a fixed total. The reason for that rule is that a short game rewards luck, so a two-point margin makes each point matter more."],"tl":"deuce","bis":"deuce"},{"id":"spo-tennis-000002","topic":"sports-tennis","intent":"definition","concept":"serve","keywords":["serve","point","fault","double","toss","return","starts","shot","server","controls","entirely"],"patterns":["what is serve","serve","explain serve","definition of serve","serve meaning","how do you serve in tennis","what is a double fault","why is the serve important","ano ang serve","unsa ang serve"],"answer":"A serve starts each point, and it is the only shot the server controls entirely before the other player has touched anything, which is why it is both the biggest weapon and the most pressured. Faults are called for failing to land the ball in the right box, and there are two before a double fault ends the point. The mechanics that matter are the toss height and the leg drive, since both come from the same push upward, and a flat power serve is much easier to return than a spin serve.","variations":["Faults are called for failing to land the ball in the right box, and there are two before a double fault ends the point. A serve starts each point, and it is the only shot the server controls entirely before the other player has touched anything, which is why it is both the biggest weapon and the most pressured. The mechanics that matter are the toss height and the leg drive, since both come from the same push upward, and a flat power serve is much easier to return than a spin serve.","The mechanics that matter are the toss height and the leg drive, since both come from the same push upward, and a flat power serve is much easier to return than a spin serve. A serve starts each point, and it is the only shot the server controls entirely before the other player has touched anything, which is why it is both the biggest weapon and the most pressured. Faults are called for failing to land the ball in the right box, and there are two before a double fault ends the point."],"tl":"serve","bis":"serve"},{"id":"spo-tennis-000003","topic":"sports-tennis","intent":"definition","concept":"grand slam","keywords":["grand slam","major","four","surface","year","career","grand","slam","winning","year's"],"patterns":["what is grand slam","grand slam","explain grand slam","definition of grand slam","grand slam meaning","what is a grand slam in tennis","how many grand slams are there","what is a career grand slam","ano ang grand slam","unsa ang grand slam"],"answer":"A grand slam is winning all four of the year's major tournaments, and it is the rarest achievement in the sport, achieved once in the men's singles and never in the women's. Each major has a different surface, which is the point: winning on clay, grass and hard court in the same year is a claim that the player can adapt rather than specialise. Career Grand Slam means all four at some point, and completing that set was last done decades ago.","variations":["Each major has a different surface, which is the point: winning on clay, grass and hard court in the same year is a claim that the player can adapt rather than specialise. A grand slam is winning all four of the year's major tournaments, and it is the rarest achievement in the sport, achieved once in the men's singles and never in the women's. Career Grand Slam means all four at some point, and completing that set was last done decades ago.","Career Grand Slam means all four at some point, and completing that set was last done decades ago. A grand slam is winning all four of the year's major tournaments, and it is the rarest achievement in the sport, achieved once in the men's singles and never in the women's. Each major has a different surface, which is the point: winning on clay, grass and hard court in the same year is a claim that the player can adapt rather than specialise."],"tl":"grand slam","bis":"grand slam"},{"id":"spo-tennis-000004","topic":"sports-tennis","intent":"definition","concept":"rally","keywords":["rally","exchange","shot","baseline","topspin","placement","sequence","shots","serves","tennis","resembles"],"patterns":["what is rally","rally","explain rally","definition of rally","rally meaning","what is a rally in tennis","why are rallies getting longer","what is baseline","ano ang rally","unsa ang rally"],"answer":"A rally is the sequence of shots between serves, and it is the part of tennis that most resembles a real sport, since the longer it goes the more it rewards placement over power. Baseline exchanges are usually the longest rallies in the modern game, because heavy topspin keeps the ball deep without needing to hit it hard. A player who wins more long rallies is usually not the better striker but the one who can vary direction and height.","variations":["Baseline exchanges are usually the longest rallies in the modern game, because heavy topspin keeps the ball deep without needing to hit it hard. A rally is the sequence of shots between serves, and it is the part of tennis that most resembles a real sport, since the longer it goes the more it rewards placement over power. A player who wins more long rallies is usually not the better striker but the one who can vary direction and height.","A player who wins more long rallies is usually not the better striker but the one who can vary direction and height. A rally is the sequence of shots between serves, and it is the part of tennis that most resembles a real sport, since the longer it goes the more it rewards placement over power. Baseline exchanges are usually the longest rallies in the modern game, because heavy topspin keeps the ball deep without needing to hit it hard."],"tl":"rally","bis":"rally"},{"id":"spo-tennis-000005","topic":"sports-tennis","intent":"definition","concept":"tiebreak","keywords":["tiebreak","set","serve","adopt","game","played","instead","long","reaches"],"patterns":["what is tiebreak","tiebreak","explain tiebreak","definition of tiebreak","tiebreak meaning","what is a tiebreak","when is a tiebreak played","how does a tiebreak work","ano ang tiebreak","unsa ang tiebreak"],"answer":"A tiebreak is a game played instead of a long set when it reaches six-all, and it is won by the first to seven points with a lead of two. The switch to a tiebreak keeps sets from running indefinitely, which is why it was adopted almost everywhere despite some players disliking it. What is unusual is that a tiebreak is served by one player for a whole point then handed over, unlike ordinary games where the serve alternates.","variations":["The switch to a tiebreak keeps sets from running indefinitely, which is why it was adopted almost everywhere despite some players disliking it. A tiebreak is a game played instead of a long set when it reaches six-all, and it is won by the first to seven points with a lead of two. What is unusual is that a tiebreak is served by one player for a whole point then handed over, unlike ordinary games where the serve alternates.","What is unusual is that a tiebreak is served by one player for a whole point then handed over, unlike ordinary games where the serve alternates. A tiebreak is a game played instead of a long set when it reaches six-all, and it is won by the first to seven points with a lead of two. The switch to a tiebreak keeps sets from running indefinitely, which is why it was adopted almost everywhere despite some players disliking it."],"tl":"tiebreak","bis":"tiebreak"}],"lang":"english","group":"sports","sub":"tennis","title":"Tennis","groupTitle":"Sports","__file":"sports/tennis.json"},
  {"topic":"sports-volleyball","entries":[{"id":"spo-volleyball-000001","topic":"sports-volleyball","intent":"definition","concept":"spike","keywords":["spike","attack","jump","power","approach","block","attacking","ball","hitting","above"],"patterns":["what is spike","spike","explain spike","definition of spike","spike meaning","what is a spike in volleyball","how do you spike","what is a setter","ano ang spike","unsa ang spike"],"answer":"A spike is attacking the ball by hitting it above the net from a jump, and it is the highest-contact and usually the most lethal stroke in the game. Its power comes from the order of operations, since the arm swing, the jump and the approach all have to peak together, and a mistimed approach makes the contact weaker no matter how hard it is hit. The block is the counter, which is why front-row players spend so much time reading the setter rather than watching the ball.","variations":["Its power comes from the order of operations, since the arm swing, the jump and the approach all have to peak together, and a mistimed approach makes the contact weaker no matter how hard it is hit. A spike is attacking the ball by hitting it above the net from a jump, and it is the highest-contact and usually the most lethal stroke in the game. The block is the counter, which is why front-row players spend so much time reading the setter rather than watching the ball.","The block is the counter, which is why front-row players spend so much time reading the setter rather than watching the ball. A spike is attacking the ball by hitting it above the net from a jump, and it is the highest-contact and usually the most lethal stroke in the game. Its power comes from the order of operations, since the arm swing, the jump and the approach all have to peak together, and a mistimed approach makes the contact weaker no matter how hard it is hit."],"tl":"spike","bis":"spike"},{"id":"spo-volleyball-000002","topic":"sports-volleyball","intent":"definition","concept":"set","keywords":["set","hitter","block","high","option","second","contact","redirects","first","towards","attack"],"patterns":["what is set","set","explain set","definition of set","set meaning","what is a set in volleyball","who is the setter","what is a good set","ano ang set","unsa ang set"],"answer":"A set is the second contact, which redirects the first contact towards the attack, and its quality decides how many options the hitter has. A good set is high and away from the net so the attacker can hit in front of the block; a bad set is too close and hands the block a free chance. It is the least glamorous skill in the sport and the one that separates a good team from a merely athletic one.","variations":["A good set is high and away from the net so the attacker can hit in front of the block; a bad set is too close and hands the block a free chance. A set is the second contact, which redirects the first contact towards the attack, and its quality decides how many options the hitter has. It is the least glamorous skill in the sport and the one that separates a good team from a merely athletic one.","It is the least glamorous skill in the sport and the one that separates a good team from a merely athletic one. A set is the second contact, which redirects the first contact towards the attack, and its quality decides how many options the hitter has. A good set is high and away from the net so the attacker can hit in front of the block; a bad set is too close and hands the block a free chance."],"tl":"set","bis":"set"},{"id":"spo-volleyball-000003","topic":"sports-volleyball","intent":"definition","concept":"rotation","keywords":["rotation","six","position","serve","block","court","rotasyon","positions","team","cycles","volleyball","decides"],"patterns":["what is rotation","rotation","explain rotation","definition of rotation","rotation meaning","what is rotation in volleyball","how many players rotate","what is outside hitter","ano ang rotation","unsa ang rotasyon"],"answer":"Rotation is the six positions a team cycles through, and it is the part of volleyball that decides serving, blocking and who plays front row. The rotation changes whenever the serve is won, so the same six players occupy different jobs depending on who last scored. Outside hitters spike from the wings while the middle blocks, and a rotation puts the strongest attacker in a back-row position exactly when the serving changes.","variations":["The rotation changes whenever the serve is won, so the same six players occupy different jobs depending on who last scored. Rotation is the six positions a team cycles through, and it is the part of volleyball that decides serving, blocking and who plays front row. Outside hitters spike from the wings while the middle blocks, and a rotation puts the strongest attacker in a back-row position exactly when the serving changes.","Outside hitters spike from the wings while the middle blocks, and a rotation puts the strongest attacker in a back-row position exactly when the serving changes. Rotation is the six positions a team cycles through, and it is the part of volleyball that decides serving, blocking and who plays front row. The rotation changes whenever the serve is won, so the same six players occupy different jobs depending on who last scored."],"tl":"rotation","bis":"rotasyon"},{"id":"spo-volleyball-000004","topic":"sports-volleyball","intent":"definition","concept":"block","keywords":["block","net","jump","spike","approach","fault","defending","jumping","arms","extended","stop"],"patterns":["what is block","block","explain block","definition of block","block meaning","what is a block in volleyball","is blocking in the net a fault","how do you read a spike","ano ang block","unsa ang block"],"answer":"A block is defending the net by jumping with arms extended to stop or deflect a spike, and it is read entirely from the attacker's approach rather than the ball. That is the skill, since the ball can be seen late but the approach is visible for a full second. A block touch that is judged over the net is a fault, so players must keep their hands inside, which removes the easy way to stop the point.","variations":["That is the skill, since the ball can be seen late but the approach is visible for a full second. A block is defending the net by jumping with arms extended to stop or deflect a spike, and it is read entirely from the attacker's approach rather than the ball. A block touch that is judged over the net is a fault, so players must keep their hands inside, which removes the easy way to stop the point.","A block touch that is judged over the net is a fault, so players must keep their hands inside, which removes the easy way to stop the point. A block is defending the net by jumping with arms extended to stop or deflect a spike, and it is read entirely from the attacker's approach rather than the ball. That is the skill, since the ball can be seen late but the approach is visible for a full second."],"tl":"block","bis":"block"},{"id":"spo-volleyball-000005","topic":"sports-volleyball","intent":"definition","concept":"libero","keywords":["libero","defensive","jersey","substitution","rally","specialist","wears","different","coloured"],"patterns":["what is libero","libero","explain libero","definition of libero","libero meaning","what is a libero","can a libero attack","what colour jersey does a libero wear","ano ang libero","unsa ang libero"],"answer":"A libero is a defensive specialist who wears a different-coloured jersey and may only play in the back row, and the restriction is the point rather than a limitation. A back-row-only player can attack much more aggressively than the rules would otherwise allow, which is why some teams use a libero who is also their best passer. The role is distinct from a specialist in other sports, because it is a substitution made mid-rally from the bench rather than a position anyone holds.","variations":["A back-row-only player can attack much more aggressively than the rules would otherwise allow, which is why some teams use a libero who is also their best passer. A libero is a defensive specialist who wears a different-coloured jersey and may only play in the back row, and the restriction is the point rather than a limitation. The role is distinct from a specialist in other sports, because it is a substitution made mid-rally from the bench rather than a position anyone holds.","The role is distinct from a specialist in other sports, because it is a substitution made mid-rally from the bench rather than a position anyone holds. A libero is a defensive specialist who wears a different-coloured jersey and may only play in the back row, and the restriction is the point rather than a limitation. A back-row-only player can attack much more aggressively than the rules would otherwise allow, which is why some teams use a libero who is also their best passer."],"tl":"libero","bis":"libero"}],"lang":"english","group":"sports","sub":"volleyball","title":"Volleyball","groupTitle":"Sports","__file":"sports/volleyball.json"},
  {"topic":"study-exam-prep","entries":[{"id":"stu-exam-prep-000001","topic":"study-exam-prep","intent":"definition","concept":"past paper","keywords":["past paper","previous","exam","format","weighting","marks","past","paper","year's","real"],"patterns":["what is past paper","past paper","explain past paper","definition of past paper","past paper meaning","what is a past paper","should you do past papers","how to use past exams","ano ang past paper","unsa ang past paper"],"answer":"A past paper is a previous year's exam, and using real ones is the most reliable preparation available because they show the actual format rather than a guess at it. They also reveal weighting, since a topic that appears every year is worth more time than a large section of the syllabus does. The mistake is treating them as tests rather than as information, so people sit one, score badly, and stop. Sitting under time pressure is worth doing, but reading where the marks actually go is worth more.","variations":["They also reveal weighting, since a topic that appears every year is worth more time than a large section of the syllabus does. A past paper is a previous year's exam, and using real ones is the most reliable preparation available because they show the actual format rather than a guess at it. The mistake is treating them as tests rather than as information, so people sit one, score badly, and stop. Sitting under time pressure is worth doing, but reading where the marks actually go is worth more.","The mistake is treating them as tests rather than as information, so people sit one, score badly, and stop. A past paper is a previous year's exam, and using real ones is the most reliable preparation available because they show the actual format rather than a guess at it. They also reveal weighting, since a topic that appears every year is worth more time than a large section of the syllabus does. Sitting under time pressure is worth doing, but reading where the marks actually go is worth more."],"tl":"past paper","bis":"past paper"},{"id":"stu-exam-prep-000002","topic":"study-exam-prep","intent":"definition","concept":"mock exam","keywords":["mock exam","mock","practice","review","ready","condition","exam","paper","real","conditions"],"patterns":["what is mock exam","mock exam","explain mock exam","definition of mock exam","mock exam meaning","what is a mock exam","is a mock exam useful","how to review a mock exam","ano ang mock exam","unsa ang mock exam"],"answer":"A mock exam is a practice paper sat under real conditions, and its value is almost entirely in what happens afterwards. The score matters less than the review, and a review means finding out why each wrong answer was wrong rather than merely that it was. A mock sat without a proper review is close to worthless, and it can be actively misleading because it produces a false sense of readiness. Two or three well-reviewed mocks are worth more than a full set of unanswered ones.","variations":["The score matters less than the review, and a review means finding out why each wrong answer was wrong rather than merely that it was. A mock exam is a practice paper sat under real conditions, and its value is almost entirely in what happens afterwards. A mock sat without a proper review is close to worthless, and it can be actively misleading because it produces a false sense of readiness. Two or three well-reviewed mocks are worth more than a full set of unanswered ones.","A mock sat without a proper review is close to worthless, and it can be actively misleading because it produces a false sense of readiness. A mock exam is a practice paper sat under real conditions, and its value is almost entirely in what happens afterwards. The score matters less than the review, and a review means finding out why each wrong answer was wrong rather than merely that it was. Two or three well-reviewed mocks are worth more than a full set of unanswered ones."],"tl":"mock exam","bis":"mock exam"},{"id":"stu-exam-prep-000003","topic":"study-exam-prep","intent":"definition","concept":"syllabus","keywords":["syllabus","exam","cover","weighting","mark","schedule","list","covers","purpose","allocate"],"patterns":["what is syllabus","syllabus","explain syllabus","definition of syllabus","syllabus meaning","what is a syllabus","is a syllabus a reading list","how to use a syllabus to revise","ano ang syllabus","unsa ang syllabus"],"answer":"A syllabus is the list of what an exam covers, and its purpose is to let you allocate time, so it is a scheduling document rather than a reading list. The weighting matters as much as the list, because a three-mark topic and a twelve-mark one are not the same amount of work. A common and expensive mistake is covering everything once rather than going deep on what is actually asked, and the fix is to read the mark scheme first, which usually describes the shape of the answer.","variations":["The weighting matters as much as the list, because a three-mark topic and a twelve-mark one are not the same amount of work. A syllabus is the list of what an exam covers, and its purpose is to let you allocate time, so it is a scheduling document rather than a reading list. A common and expensive mistake is covering everything once rather than going deep on what is actually asked, and the fix is to read the mark scheme first, which usually describes the shape of the answer.","A common and expensive mistake is covering everything once rather than going deep on what is actually asked, and the fix is to read the mark scheme first, which usually describes the shape of the answer. A syllabus is the list of what an exam covers, and its purpose is to let you allocate time, so it is a scheduling document rather than a reading list. The weighting matters as much as the list, because a three-mark topic and a twelve-mark one are not the same amount of work."],"tl":"syllabus","bis":"syllabus"},{"id":"stu-exam-prep-000004","topic":"study-exam-prep","intent":"definition","concept":"mark scheme","keywords":["mark scheme","examiner","mark","point","credit","fluency","scheme","examiner's","breakdown","answer","credited"],"patterns":["what is mark scheme","mark scheme","explain mark scheme","definition of mark scheme","mark scheme meaning","what is a mark scheme","should you read the mark scheme","how are answers marked","ano ang mark scheme","unsa ang mark scheme"],"answer":"A mark scheme is the examiner's breakdown of how an answer is credited, and it is the most underused document in any exam. It shows that the question is marked against specific points rather than for fluency, and it reveals the difference between an answer that is right and an answer that earns the marks. Many students lose marks for a correct idea expressed in the wrong form, which is visible in the scheme and invisible in the textbook.","variations":["It shows that the question is marked against specific points rather than for fluency, and it reveals the difference between an answer that is right and an answer that earns the marks. A mark scheme is the examiner's breakdown of how an answer is credited, and it is the most underused document in any exam. Many students lose marks for a correct idea expressed in the wrong form, which is visible in the scheme and invisible in the textbook.","Many students lose marks for a correct idea expressed in the wrong form, which is visible in the scheme and invisible in the textbook. A mark scheme is the examiner's breakdown of how an answer is credited, and it is the most underused document in any exam. It shows that the question is marked against specific points rather than for fluency, and it reveals the difference between an answer that is right and an answer that earns the marks."],"tl":"mark scheme","bis":"mark scheme"},{"id":"stu-exam-prep-000005","topic":"study-exam-prep","intent":"definition","concept":"cramming","keywords":["cramming","revision","retention","spike","term","cram","putting","shortest","possible","time"],"patterns":["what is cramming","cramming","explain cramming","definition of cramming","cramming meaning","does cramming work","is cramming better than spaced study","ano ang cramming","unsa ang cram"],"answer":"Cramming is putting all the revision into the shortest possible time, and the research is consistent that it works better than nothing while being worse than spaced study over the same total hours. What it does well is retrieval of facts, surface names and figures, which is often exactly what a short-answer question asks. What it does badly is retention afterwards, and it reliably produces a spike during the exam and a drop the week after. It is a legitimate tool for a specific kind of short test and a poor substitute for a term.","variations":["What it does well is retrieval of facts, surface names and figures, which is often exactly what a short-answer question asks. Cramming is putting all the revision into the shortest possible time, and the research is consistent that it works better than nothing while being worse than spaced study over the same total hours. What it does badly is retention afterwards, and it reliably produces a spike during the exam and a drop the week after. It is a legitimate tool for a specific kind of short test and a poor substitute for a term.","What it does badly is retention afterwards, and it reliably produces a spike during the exam and a drop the week after. Cramming is putting all the revision into the shortest possible time, and the research is consistent that it works better than nothing while being worse than spaced study over the same total hours. What it does well is retrieval of facts, surface names and figures, which is often exactly what a short-answer question asks. It is a legitimate tool for a specific kind of short test and a poor substitute for a term."],"tl":"cramming","bis":"cram"}],"lang":"english","group":"study","sub":"exam-prep","title":"Exam prep","groupTitle":"Study","__file":"study/exam-prep.json"},
  {"topic":"study-memory","entries":[{"id":"stu-memory-000001","topic":"study-memory","intent":"definition","concept":"forgetting curve","keywords":["forgetting curve","decay","review","repeat","cheap","spaced","forgetting","curve","rate","unused","memories"],"patterns":["what is forgetting curve","forgetting curve","explain forgetting curve","definition of forgetting curve","forgetting curve meaning","what is the forgetting curve","is memory linear","how does review change forgetting","ano ang forgetting curve","unsa ang forgetting curve"],"answer":"The forgetting curve is the rate at which unused memories decay, and the finding is that the drop is fast at first and then flattens, so most of what is learned is gone within a day and the remainder persists for a long time. What makes the curve useful is that reviewing resets the position without resetting the shape, so each repetition makes the next one cheaper. That is the whole basis of spaced study, and it is why a single review session does so little.","variations":["What makes the curve useful is that reviewing resets the position without resetting the shape, so each repetition makes the next one cheaper. The forgetting curve is the rate at which unused memories decay, and the finding is that the drop is fast at first and then flattens, so most of what is learned is gone within a day and the remainder persists for a long time. That is the whole basis of spaced study, and it is why a single review session does so little.","That is the whole basis of spaced study, and it is why a single review session does so little. The forgetting curve is the rate at which unused memories decay, and the finding is that the drop is fast at first and then flattens, so most of what is learned is gone within a day and the remainder persists for a long time. What makes the curve useful is that reviewing resets the position without resetting the shape, so each repetition makes the next one cheaper."],"tl":"forgetting curve","bis":"forgetting curve"},{"id":"stu-memory-000002","topic":"study-memory","intent":"definition","concept":"mnemonic","keywords":["mnemonic","rhyme","attach","remember","order","capacity","device","makes","something","easier"],"patterns":["what is mnemonic","mnemonic","explain mnemonic","definition of mnemonic","mnemonic meaning","what is a mnemonic","are mnemonics effective","how to remember a list","ano ang mnemonic","unsa ang mnemonic"],"answer":"A mnemonic is a device that makes something easier to remember by attaching it to something already known, and the oldest form is a rhyme. It works for lists that are hard to order otherwise, so the order of the planets or the cranial nerves, and it is close to useless for anything requiring understanding. Its limitation is capacity: a mnemonic helps recall an item but does not explain why it is true, so it is a supplement rather than a replacement for learning the thing.","variations":["It works for lists that are hard to order otherwise, so the order of the planets or the cranial nerves, and it is close to useless for anything requiring understanding. A mnemonic is a device that makes something easier to remember by attaching it to something already known, and the oldest form is a rhyme. Its limitation is capacity: a mnemonic helps recall an item but does not explain why it is true, so it is a supplement rather than a replacement for learning the thing.","Its limitation is capacity: a mnemonic helps recall an item but does not explain why it is true, so it is a supplement rather than a replacement for learning the thing. A mnemonic is a device that makes something easier to remember by attaching it to something already known, and the oldest form is a rhyme. It works for lists that are hard to order otherwise, so the order of the planets or the cranial nerves, and it is close to useless for anything requiring understanding."],"tl":"mnemonic","bis":"mnemonic"},{"id":"stu-memory-000003","topic":"study-memory","intent":"definition","concept":"working memory","keywords":["working memory","capacity","chunk","hold","manipulate","expert","working","memory","small","amount","held","manipulated"],"patterns":["what is working memory","working memory","explain working memory","definition of working memory","working memory meaning","how many items can you hold","what is chunking","ano ang working memory","unsa ang working memory"],"answer":"Working memory is the small amount held and manipulated at once, and it is the bottleneck for almost every hard task, since you cannot reason over material you are not holding. Its capacity is famously small, around four items, though chunking lets a trained person hold far more by treating groups as single objects. This is why a phone number is harder than a familiar phone number, and why experts appear to remember more simply because their chunks are larger.","variations":["Its capacity is famously small, around four items, though chunking lets a trained person hold far more by treating groups as single objects. Working memory is the small amount held and manipulated at once, and it is the bottleneck for almost every hard task, since you cannot reason over material you are not holding. This is why a phone number is harder than a familiar phone number, and why experts appear to remember more simply because their chunks are larger.","This is why a phone number is harder than a familiar phone number, and why experts appear to remember more simply because their chunks are larger. Working memory is the small amount held and manipulated at once, and it is the bottleneck for almost every hard task, since you cannot reason over material you are not holding. Its capacity is famously small, around four items, though chunking lets a trained person hold far more by treating groups as single objects."],"tl":"working memory","bis":"working memory"},{"id":"stu-memory-000004","topic":"study-memory","intent":"definition","concept":"encoding","keywords":["encoding","store","retrieve","depth","process","repeat","turning","something","stored","learning"],"patterns":["what is encoding","encoding","explain encoding","definition of encoding","encoding meaning","what is encoding in memory","what is depth of processing","how do you encode information","ano ang encoding","unsa ang encoding"],"answer":"Encoding is the process of turning something into a form that can be stored, and it is where most learning is actually lost. Material that is understood can still fail to encode if it is encoded in a form you will never retrieve, which is the reason a shelf of unlabelled boxes is not a memory aid. Depth of processing is the reliable predictor of retention, so asking why something is true encodes better than repeating that it is.","variations":["Material that is understood can still fail to encode if it is encoded in a form you will never retrieve, which is the reason a shelf of unlabelled boxes is not a memory aid. Encoding is the process of turning something into a form that can be stored, and it is where most learning is actually lost. Depth of processing is the reliable predictor of retention, so asking why something is true encodes better than repeating that it is.","Depth of processing is the reliable predictor of retention, so asking why something is true encodes better than repeating that it is. Encoding is the process of turning something into a form that can be stored, and it is where most learning is actually lost. Material that is understood can still fail to encode if it is encoded in a form you will never retrieve, which is the reason a shelf of unlabelled boxes is not a memory aid."],"tl":"encoding","bis":"encoding"},{"id":"stu-memory-000005","topic":"study-memory","intent":"definition","concept":"retention","keywords":["retention","store","acquisition","retrieval","route","overlearn","long","something","stays","available","outcome"],"patterns":["what is retention","retention","explain retention","definition of retention","retention meaning","what is retention in memory","difference between retention and recall","why is forgetting not loss","ano ang retention","unsa ang retention"],"answer":"Retention is how long something stays available, and it is the outcome most people mean by learning a thing, so it is worth separating from the other two parts. Acquisition is getting it in, retention is keeping it, and retrieval is finding it, and a failure at any one looks identical from outside. The clinically important fact is that forgetting is not loss of the memory but loss of the route to it, so overlearning and varied practice both work by adding routes rather than by preventing decay.","variations":["Acquisition is getting it in, retention is keeping it, and retrieval is finding it, and a failure at any one looks identical from outside. Retention is how long something stays available, and it is the outcome most people mean by learning a thing, so it is worth separating from the other two parts. The clinically important fact is that forgetting is not loss of the memory but loss of the route to it, so overlearning and varied practice both work by adding routes rather than by preventing decay.","The clinically important fact is that forgetting is not loss of the memory but loss of the route to it, so overlearning and varied practice both work by adding routes rather than by preventing decay. Retention is how long something stays available, and it is the outcome most people mean by learning a thing, so it is worth separating from the other two parts. Acquisition is getting it in, retention is keeping it, and retrieval is finding it, and a failure at any one looks identical from outside."],"tl":"retention","bis":"retention"}],"lang":"english","group":"study","sub":"memory","title":"Memory","groupTitle":"Study","__file":"study/memory.json"},
  {"topic":"study-motivation","entries":[{"id":"stu-motivation-000001","topic":"study-motivation","intent":"definition","concept":"procrastination","keywords":["procrastination","delay","avoid","delaying","task","intend","primarily","time"],"patterns":["what is procrastination","procrastination","explain procrastination","definition of procrastination","procrastination meaning","why do i procrastinate","how do i stop procrastinating","ano ang procrastination","unsa ang procrastination"],"answer":"Procrastination is delaying a task you intend to do, and it is not primarily a time-management failure. The common cause is that the task gives immediate discomfort and no immediate reward, while avoidance gives immediate relief. That is why the hardest tasks get done last and the whole evening disappears. The workable counter is to lower the entry cost rather than to fight the feeling, so a two-minute start counts as having done it.","variations":["The common cause is that the task gives immediate discomfort and no immediate reward, while avoidance gives immediate relief. Procrastination is delaying a task you intend to do, and it is not primarily a time-management failure. That is why the hardest tasks get done last and the whole evening disappears. The workable counter is to lower the entry cost rather than to fight the feeling, so a two-minute start counts as having done it.","That is why the hardest tasks get done last and the whole evening disappears. Procrastination is delaying a task you intend to do, and it is not primarily a time-management failure. The common cause is that the task gives immediate discomfort and no immediate reward, while avoidance gives immediate relief. The workable counter is to lower the entry cost rather than to fight the feeling, so a two-minute start counts as having done it."],"tl":"procrastination","bis":"procrastination"},{"id":"stu-motivation-000002","topic":"study-motivation","intent":"definition","concept":"spaced repetition","keywords":["spaced repetition","memory","review","revision","interval","spaced","repetition","reviews","material","increasing","intervals"],"patterns":["what is spaced repetition","spaced repetition","explain spaced repetition","definition of spaced repetition","spaced repetition meaning","how to revise for an exam","ano ang spaced repetition","unsa ang spaced repetition"],"answer":"Spaced repetition reviews material at increasing intervals, timed so that each review happens just as you are about to forget it. It works because recall strengthens memory more than re-reading, and because the spacing is what forces the strengthening. Practically it means scheduling review for the next day, then three days, then a week, rather than reading everything the night before an exam.","variations":["It works because recall strengthens memory more than re-reading, and because the spacing is what forces the strengthening. Spaced repetition reviews material at increasing intervals, timed so that each review happens just as you are about to forget it. Practically it means scheduling review for the next day, then three days, then a week, rather than reading everything the night before an exam.","Practically it means scheduling review for the next day, then three days, then a week, rather than reading everything the night before an exam. Spaced repetition reviews material at increasing intervals, timed so that each review happens just as you are about to forget it. It works because recall strengthens memory more than re-reading, and because the spacing is what forces the strengthening."],"tl":"spaced repetition","bis":"spaced repetition"},{"id":"stu-motivation-000003","topic":"study-motivation","intent":"definition","concept":"pomodoro","keywords":["pomodoro","timer","focus","break","task","technique","simple","work"],"patterns":["what is pomodoro","pomodoro","explain pomodoro","definition of pomodoro","pomodoro meaning","what is the pomodoro technique","how do i focus while studying","ano ang pomodoro","unsa ang pomodoro"],"answer":"The Pomodoro technique is a simple timer: work on one task for twenty-five minutes, take five minutes off, and after four cycles take a longer break. The value is not the numbers but the fact that a large task becomes small enough to start, which is the step most people cannot take. It also produces a record of how long things actually take, which is usually more accurate than someone's guess.","variations":["The value is not the numbers but the fact that a large task becomes small enough to start, which is the step most people cannot take. The Pomodoro technique is a simple timer: work on one task for twenty-five minutes, take five minutes off, and after four cycles take a longer break. It also produces a record of how long things actually take, which is usually more accurate than someone's guess.","It also produces a record of how long things actually take, which is usually more accurate than someone's guess. The Pomodoro technique is a simple timer: work on one task for twenty-five minutes, take five minutes off, and after four cycles take a longer break. The value is not the numbers but the fact that a large task becomes small enough to start, which is the step most people cannot take."],"tl":"pomodoro","bis":"pomodoro"},{"id":"stu-motivation-000004","topic":"study-motivation","intent":"definition","concept":"active recall","keywords":["active recall","testing","flashcards","notes","revision","active","recall","means","yourself","rather"],"patterns":["what is active recall","active recall","explain active recall","definition of active recall","active recall meaning","is rereading better than testing","ano ang active recall","unsa ang active recall"],"answer":"Active recall means testing yourself rather than rereading, and it is consistently the highest-return study method. Rereading produces a feeling of familiarity that is easy to mistake for knowledge, and familiarity is not recall. Closing the notes and writing what you remember finds the gaps immediately, which is uncomfortable and is the point, because a gap found while studying is a gap found before the exam.","variations":["Rereading produces a feeling of familiarity that is easy to mistake for knowledge, and familiarity is not recall. Active recall means testing yourself rather than rereading, and it is consistently the highest-return study method. Closing the notes and writing what you remember finds the gaps immediately, which is uncomfortable and is the point, because a gap found while studying is a gap found before the exam.","Closing the notes and writing what you remember finds the gaps immediately, which is uncomfortable and is the point, because a gap found while studying is a gap found before the exam. Active recall means testing yourself rather than rereading, and it is consistently the highest-return study method. Rereading produces a feeling of familiarity that is easy to mistake for knowledge, and familiarity is not recall."],"tl":"active recall","bis":"active recall"},{"id":"stu-motivation-000005","topic":"study-motivation","intent":"definition","concept":"note taking","keywords":["note taking","notes","transcription","cornell","summary","study","note","taking","fails","becomes","copying"],"patterns":["what is note taking","note taking","explain note taking","definition of note taking","note taking meaning","how should i take notes while studying","is copying notes useful","ano ang note taking","unsa ang note taking"],"answer":"Note taking fails when it becomes transcription, because copying a paragraph feels productive and produces nothing retrievable later. The alternative is to write notes in your own words and in a form you can test from, so that a page of notes becomes a set of questions rather than a set of facts. Cornell-style splitting - cues on one side, notes on the other - exists for exactly this reason.","variations":["The alternative is to write notes in your own words and in a form you can test from, so that a page of notes becomes a set of questions rather than a set of facts. Note taking fails when it becomes transcription, because copying a paragraph feels productive and produces nothing retrievable later. Cornell-style splitting - cues on one side, notes on the other - exists for exactly this reason.","Cornell-style splitting - cues on one side, notes on the other - exists for exactly this reason. Note taking fails when it becomes transcription, because copying a paragraph feels productive and produces nothing retrievable later. The alternative is to write notes in your own words and in a form you can test from, so that a page of notes becomes a set of questions rather than a set of facts."],"tl":"note taking","bis":"note taking"},{"id":"stu-motivation-000006","topic":"study-motivation","intent":"definition","concept":"deep work","keywords":["deep work","focus","concentration","distraction","attention","interruption","deep","work","sustained","cognitively","demanding"],"patterns":["what is deep work","deep work","explain deep work","definition of deep work","deep work meaning","how do i stop getting distracted","ano ang deep work","unsa ang deep work"],"answer":"Deep work is sustained concentration on a cognitively demanding task while unproductive activity is out of reach. The reason it is rare is not laziness but that switching cost is low and every interruption has to be paid for twice, once to stop and again to restart. Batching communication rather than answering it continuously, and leaving a phone in another room, both work because they remove the choice rather than relying on willpower.","variations":["The reason it is rare is not laziness but that switching cost is low and every interruption has to be paid for twice, once to stop and again to restart. Deep work is sustained concentration on a cognitively demanding task while unproductive activity is out of reach. Batching communication rather than answering it continuously, and leaving a phone in another room, both work because they remove the choice rather than relying on willpower.","Batching communication rather than answering it continuously, and leaving a phone in another room, both work because they remove the choice rather than relying on willpower. Deep work is sustained concentration on a cognitively demanding task while unproductive activity is out of reach. The reason it is rare is not laziness but that switching cost is low and every interruption has to be paid for twice, once to stop and again to restart."],"tl":"deep work","bis":"deep work"}],"lang":"english","group":"study","sub":"motivation","title":"Motivation","groupTitle":"Study","__file":"study/motivation.json"},
  {"topic":"study-reading","entries":[{"id":"stu-reading-000001","topic":"study-reading","intent":"definition","concept":"skim","keywords":["skim","quickly","shape","structure","heading","decide","read","text","rather"],"patterns":["what is skim","skim","explain skim","definition of skim","skim meaning","what does skim mean","how to skim an article","is skimming the same as reading fast","ano ang skim","unsa ang skim"],"answer":"To skim is to read quickly to get the shape of a text rather than its content, and it is a real skill aimed at a real decision: whether this is worth an hour. What you look for is structure, so the first and last paragraphs, the headings, and the first sentence of each section, since writers put their claims there. Skimming badly means reading every line quickly, which is slower than reading properly and remembers less.","variations":["What you look for is structure, so the first and last paragraphs, the headings, and the first sentence of each section, since writers put their claims there. To skim is to read quickly to get the shape of a text rather than its content, and it is a real skill aimed at a real decision: whether this is worth an hour. Skimming badly means reading every line quickly, which is slower than reading properly and remembers less.","Skimming badly means reading every line quickly, which is slower than reading properly and remembers less. To skim is to read quickly to get the shape of a text rather than its content, and it is a real skill aimed at a real decision: whether this is worth an hour. What you look for is structure, so the first and last paragraphs, the headings, and the first sentence of each section, since writers put their claims there."],"tl":"skim","bis":"skim"},{"id":"stu-reading-000002","topic":"study-reading","intent":"definition","concept":"active reading","keywords":["active reading","question","annotate","summarise","gap","argument","active","reading","means","interacting","text","rather"],"patterns":["what is active reading","active reading","explain active reading","definition of active reading","active reading meaning","how to read actively","should you highlight while reading","ano ang active reading","unsa ang active reading"],"answer":"Active reading means interacting with the text rather than moving across it, and the three moves that matter are questioning, annotating and summarising. The questioning is the one people skip, and it is where the gaps are found: asking why the author said that before reading on is what exposes an argument you would otherwise have skimmed past. Annotating is only worth doing if the mark means something, since a dog-eared page is a record that you were there and nothing more.","variations":["The questioning is the one people skip, and it is where the gaps are found: asking why the author said that before reading on is what exposes an argument you would otherwise have skimmed past. Active reading means interacting with the text rather than moving across it, and the three moves that matter are questioning, annotating and summarising. Annotating is only worth doing if the mark means something, since a dog-eared page is a record that you were there and nothing more.","Annotating is only worth doing if the mark means something, since a dog-eared page is a record that you were there and nothing more. Active reading means interacting with the text rather than moving across it, and the three moves that matter are questioning, annotating and summarising. The questioning is the one people skip, and it is where the gaps are found: asking why the author said that before reading on is what exposes an argument you would otherwise have skimmed past."],"tl":"active reading","bis":"active reading"},{"id":"stu-reading-000003","topic":"study-reading","intent":"definition","concept":"reading speed","keywords":["reading speed","comprehension","argument","narrative","measure","trade","reading","speed","varies","several","fold","people"],"patterns":["what is reading speed","reading speed","explain reading speed","definition of reading speed","reading speed meaning","what is a good reading speed","can you read too fast","is speed reading effective","ano ang reading speed","unsa ang reading speed"],"answer":"Reading speed varies several-fold between people and, more usefully, between texts. A well-known argument at a familiar level is read slowly because it is being argued with, while a familiar narrative is read fast, so a single number says very little. The variable worth measuring is comprehension: speed gained at the cost of understanding is a loss, and this is why speed-reading courses that trade the first for the second tend to disappoint.","variations":["A well-known argument at a familiar level is read slowly because it is being argued with, while a familiar narrative is read fast, so a single number says very little. Reading speed varies several-fold between people and, more usefully, between texts. The variable worth measuring is comprehension: speed gained at the cost of understanding is a loss, and this is why speed-reading courses that trade the first for the second tend to disappoint.","The variable worth measuring is comprehension: speed gained at the cost of understanding is a loss, and this is why speed-reading courses that trade the first for the second tend to disappoint. Reading speed varies several-fold between people and, more usefully, between texts. A well-known argument at a familiar level is read slowly because it is being argued with, while a familiar narrative is read fast, so a single number says very little."],"tl":"reading speed","bis":"reading speed"},{"id":"stu-reading-000004","topic":"study-reading","intent":"definition","concept":"close reading","keywords":["close reading","passage","unsaid","notice","sentence","slow","close","reading","short","repeatedly","closely"],"patterns":["what is close reading","close reading","explain close reading","definition of close reading","close reading meaning","how do you close read","is close reading useful for exams","ano ang close reading","unsa ang close reading"],"answer":"Close reading is reading a short passage repeatedly and closely, and it exists because meaning in literature often sits in what is unsaid. The method is to notice specific words, to ask what the sentence does rather than what it says, and to hold several readings at once. It is slow by design and is the opposite of skimming, so it is used on a few pages rather than on a chapter, and it is a poor way to cover a textbook.","variations":["The method is to notice specific words, to ask what the sentence does rather than what it says, and to hold several readings at once. Close reading is reading a short passage repeatedly and closely, and it exists because meaning in literature often sits in what is unsaid. It is slow by design and is the opposite of skimming, so it is used on a few pages rather than on a chapter, and it is a poor way to cover a textbook.","It is slow by design and is the opposite of skimming, so it is used on a few pages rather than on a chapter, and it is a poor way to cover a textbook. Close reading is reading a short passage repeatedly and closely, and it exists because meaning in literature often sits in what is unsaid. The method is to notice specific words, to ask what the sentence does rather than what it says, and to hold several readings at once."],"tl":"close reading","bis":"close reading"},{"id":"stu-reading-000005","topic":"study-reading","intent":"definition","concept":"summary","keywords":["summary","compress","claim","structure","reconstruct","list","buod","compresses","text","main","supporting"],"patterns":["what is summary","summary","explain summary","definition of summary","summary meaning","how to write a good summary","how long should a summary be","is summarising the first sentence enough","ano ang summary","unsa ang buod"],"answer":"A summary compresses a text to its main claim and its supporting structure, and a good one can be three sentences while a bad one is three sentences of introduction. The test is whether somebody who has not read it could reconstruct the argument, and if the summary only lists sections then it has captured the order rather than the reasoning. Most people summarise by writing the first sentence of each paragraph, which is a reasonable approximation and misses the parts where the writer actually convinced themselves of something.","variations":["The test is whether somebody who has not read it could reconstruct the argument, and if the summary only lists sections then it has captured the order rather than the reasoning. A summary compresses a text to its main claim and its supporting structure, and a good one can be three sentences while a bad one is three sentences of introduction. Most people summarise by writing the first sentence of each paragraph, which is a reasonable approximation and misses the parts where the writer actually convinced themselves of something.","Most people summarise by writing the first sentence of each paragraph, which is a reasonable approximation and misses the parts where the writer actually convinced themselves of something. A summary compresses a text to its main claim and its supporting structure, and a good one can be three sentences while a bad one is three sentences of introduction. The test is whether somebody who has not read it could reconstruct the argument, and if the summary only lists sections then it has captured the order rather than the reasoning."],"tl":"summary","bis":"buod"}],"lang":"english","group":"study","sub":"reading","title":"Reading","groupTitle":"Study","__file":"study/reading.json"},
  {"topic":"study-research","entries":[{"id":"stu-research-000001","topic":"study-research","intent":"definition","concept":"hypothesis","keywords":["hypothesis","testable","falsify","null","evidence","propose","proposed","answer","word","carries"],"patterns":["what is hypothesis","hypothesis","explain hypothesis","definition of hypothesis","hypothesis meaning","what is a hypothesis","is a hypothesis the same as a theory","what is a null hypothesis","ano ang hypothesis","unsa ang hypothesis"],"answer":"A hypothesis is a testable proposed answer, and the word carries the requirement that evidence could count against it. A statement that cannot be falsified is not a weak hypothesis, it is not one, which is the most common way first research goes wrong. The useful discipline is to write the null alongside it, and the surprising part is how often people cannot state the null, which means they have not thought about what would show them to be wrong.","variations":["A statement that cannot be falsified is not a weak hypothesis, it is not one, which is the most common way first research goes wrong. A hypothesis is a testable proposed answer, and the word carries the requirement that evidence could count against it. The useful discipline is to write the null alongside it, and the surprising part is how often people cannot state the null, which means they have not thought about what would show them to be wrong.","The useful discipline is to write the null alongside it, and the surprising part is how often people cannot state the null, which means they have not thought about what would show them to be wrong. A hypothesis is a testable proposed answer, and the word carries the requirement that evidence could count against it. A statement that cannot be falsified is not a weak hypothesis, it is not one, which is the most common way first research goes wrong."],"tl":"hypothesis","bis":"hypothesis"},{"id":"stu-research-000002","topic":"study-research","intent":"definition","concept":"peer review","keywords":["peer review","assess","author","control","method","incomplete","peer","review","assessment","research","others","field"],"patterns":["what is peer review","peer review","explain peer review","definition of peer review","peer review meaning","why is peer review important","what are the limits of peer review","ano ang peer review","unsa ang peer review"],"answer":"Peer review is assessment of research by others in the same field who are not the author, and it is the main quality control on published work. What it reliably catches is error, bad method and unsupported claims; what it does not catch is a well-executed study of an uninteresting question. It is also incomplete as a system: review is usually done by a small number of people, sometimes by someone whose own work is being reviewed, and it checks the argument rather than the honesty of the data.","variations":["What it reliably catches is error, bad method and unsupported claims; what it does not catch is a well-executed study of an uninteresting question. Peer review is assessment of research by others in the same field who are not the author, and it is the main quality control on published work. It is also incomplete as a system: review is usually done by a small number of people, sometimes by someone whose own work is being reviewed, and it checks the argument rather than the honesty of the data.","It is also incomplete as a system: review is usually done by a small number of people, sometimes by someone whose own work is being reviewed, and it checks the argument rather than the honesty of the data. Peer review is assessment of research by others in the same field who are not the author, and it is the main quality control on published work. What it reliably catches is error, bad method and unsupported claims; what it does not catch is a well-executed study of an uninteresting question."],"tl":"peer review","bis":"peer review"},{"id":"stu-research-000003","topic":"study-research","intent":"definition","concept":"citation bias","keywords":["citation bias","striking","publish","negative","literature","protocol","bias","citation","tendency","studies","results"],"patterns":["what is citation bias","citation bias","explain citation bias","definition of citation bias","citation bias meaning","what is publication bias","how do systematic reviews help","ano ang citation bias","unsa ang bias"],"answer":"Citation bias is the tendency for studies with striking results to be cited more often than careful ones, and it has a measurable effect on what a field believes. Positive results get published and negative ones do not, which is the related problem called publication bias, and together they make the literature read as if every treatment works. The countermeasure is a systematic review protocol, which fixes the question and the search before the results are known and so cannot be quietly adjusted afterwards.","variations":["Positive results get published and negative ones do not, which is the related problem called publication bias, and together they make the literature read as if every treatment works. Citation bias is the tendency for studies with striking results to be cited more often than careful ones, and it has a measurable effect on what a field believes. The countermeasure is a systematic review protocol, which fixes the question and the search before the results are known and so cannot be quietly adjusted afterwards.","The countermeasure is a systematic review protocol, which fixes the question and the search before the results are known and so cannot be quietly adjusted afterwards. Citation bias is the tendency for studies with striking results to be cited more often than careful ones, and it has a measurable effect on what a field believes. Positive results get published and negative ones do not, which is the related problem called publication bias, and together they make the literature read as if every treatment works."],"tl":"citation bias","bis":"bias"},{"id":"stu-research-000004","topic":"study-research","intent":"definition","concept":"reproducibility","keywords":["reproducibility","repeat","method","data","software","version","degree","somebody","else","result"],"patterns":["what is reproducibility","reproducibility","explain reproducibility","definition of reproducibility","reproducibility meaning","is a study reproducible","why do scientists care about replication","ano ang reproducibility","unsa ang reproducibility"],"answer":"Reproducibility is the degree to which somebody else can repeat a result from what was written down, and it is now treated as a minimum rather than an achievement. It fails for mundane reasons most of the time: missing detail in the method, data held privately, and software that no longer runs. The last is why specifying versions is part of the method and not a footnote, since an analysis rerun on a current version of a library may not produce the published number.","variations":["It fails for mundane reasons most of the time: missing detail in the method, data held privately, and software that no longer runs. Reproducibility is the degree to which somebody else can repeat a result from what was written down, and it is now treated as a minimum rather than an achievement. The last is why specifying versions is part of the method and not a footnote, since an analysis rerun on a current version of a library may not produce the published number.","The last is why specifying versions is part of the method and not a footnote, since an analysis rerun on a current version of a library may not produce the published number. Reproducibility is the degree to which somebody else can repeat a result from what was written down, and it is now treated as a minimum rather than an achievement. It fails for mundane reasons most of the time: missing detail in the method, data held privately, and software that no longer runs."],"tl":"reproducibility","bis":"reproducibility"},{"id":"stu-research-000005","topic":"study-research","intent":"definition","concept":"methodology","keywords":["methodology","sample","collect","analyse","exclude","limit","study","describes","work","done","rather"],"patterns":["what is methodology","methodology","explain methodology","definition of methodology","methodology meaning","what does a methodology section contain","why is methodology important","ano ang methodology","unsa ang methodology"],"answer":"Methodology is the part of a study that describes how the work was done rather than what it found, and it is the section readers skip and the one that determines whether they can trust the result. It has to cover sampling, how data was collected, how it was analysed, and what was excluded, and the exclusions are the part that most often contains a choice made after seeing the data. Methodology is also where a study's limits are declared, and a paper without them is usually hiding something.","variations":["It has to cover sampling, how data was collected, how it was analysed, and what was excluded, and the exclusions are the part that most often contains a choice made after seeing the data. Methodology is the part of a study that describes how the work was done rather than what it found, and it is the section readers skip and the one that determines whether they can trust the result. Methodology is also where a study's limits are declared, and a paper without them is usually hiding something.","Methodology is also where a study's limits are declared, and a paper without them is usually hiding something. Methodology is the part of a study that describes how the work was done rather than what it found, and it is the section readers skip and the one that determines whether they can trust the result. It has to cover sampling, how data was collected, how it was analysed, and what was excluded, and the exclusions are the part that most often contains a choice made after seeing the data."],"tl":"methodology","bis":"methodology"}],"lang":"english","group":"study","sub":"research","title":"Research","groupTitle":"Study","__file":"study/research.json"},
  {"topic":"study-techniques","entries":[{"id":"stu-techniques-000001","topic":"study-techniques","intent":"definition","concept":"flashcard","keywords":["flashcard","question","answer","card","puts","side","whole"],"patterns":["what is flashcard","flashcard","explain flashcard","definition of flashcard","flashcard meaning","what is a flashcard","should you use flashcards","how to write a good flashcard","ano ang flashcard","unsa ang flashcard"],"answer":"A flashcard puts one question on one side and one answer on the other, and the whole method depends on you trying to answer before turning it over. Used that way it is a form of self-testing, which is why it works far better than reading a list of facts aloud. Used the other way, flipping through cards you already know, it becomes re-reading in a more expensive form. The design rule is one idea per card, and a card asking what are the three causes of the war will fail every time.","variations":["Used that way it is a form of self-testing, which is why it works far better than reading a list of facts aloud. A flashcard puts one question on one side and one answer on the other, and the whole method depends on you trying to answer before turning it over. Used the other way, flipping through cards you already know, it becomes re-reading in a more expensive form. The design rule is one idea per card, and a card asking what are the three causes of the war will fail every time.","Used the other way, flipping through cards you already know, it becomes re-reading in a more expensive form. A flashcard puts one question on one side and one answer on the other, and the whole method depends on you trying to answer before turning it over. Used that way it is a form of self-testing, which is why it works far better than reading a list of facts aloud. The design rule is one idea per card, and a card asking what are the three causes of the war will fail every time."],"tl":"flashcard","bis":"flashcard"},{"id":"stu-techniques-000002","topic":"study-techniques","intent":"definition","concept":"mind habit","keywords":["mind habit","routine","recall","session","frequency","small","consistency","motivation","gawi","study","habit","rather","burst","effort"],"patterns":["what is mind habit","mind habit","explain mind habit","definition of mind habit","mind habit meaning","what is a study habit","how do you build a study routine","is rereading effective","ano ang gawi","unsa ang study habit"],"answer":"A study habit is a routine rather than a burst of effort, and the reason it outperforms a single long session is that recall is strengthened by being tested rather than by being read, so what matters is how often you come back to the material. The useful unit is a short session with an attempt at recall at the end, since a session that only rereads feels productive and produces much less. Timing matters too, and the most reliable finding is that the same total study time spread across days beats the same time in one sitting before a test, because the effort of remembering it once is the thing that does the work. A habit that survives a bad week is built small, since a routine that can be abandoned easily will be, and a version that takes five honest minutes is worth more than an ambitious one that lasts a day. The common failure is building the habit around motivation, which is the wrong order: motivation follows visible progress rather than producing it.","variations":["The useful unit is a short session with an attempt at recall at the end, since a session that only rereads feels productive and produces much less. A study habit is a routine rather than a burst of effort, and the reason it outperforms a single long session is that recall is strengthened by being tested rather than by being read, so what matters is how often you come back to the material. Timing matters too, and the most reliable finding is that the same total study time spread across days beats the same time in one sitting before a test, because the effort of remembering it once is the thing that does the work. A habit that survives a bad week is built small, since a routine that can be abandoned easily will be, and a version that takes five honest minutes is worth more than an ambitious one that lasts a day. The common failure is building the habit around motivation, which is the wrong order: motivation follows visible progress rather than producing it.","Timing matters too, and the most reliable finding is that the same total study time spread across days beats the same time in one sitting before a test, because the effort of remembering it once is the thing that does the work. A study habit is a routine rather than a burst of effort, and the reason it outperforms a single long session is that recall is strengthened by being tested rather than by being read, so what matters is how often you come back to the material. The useful unit is a short session with an attempt at recall at the end, since a session that only rereads feels productive and produces much less. A habit that survives a bad week is built small, since a routine that can be abandoned easily will be, and a version that takes five honest minutes is worth more than an ambitious one that lasts a day. The common failure is building the habit around motivation, which is the wrong order: motivation follows visible progress rather than producing it."],"tl":"gawi","bis":"study habit"},{"id":"stu-techniques-000003","topic":"study-techniques","intent":"definition","concept":"revision","keywords":["revision","review","familiar","recall","gap","confidence","ulit","going","material","already","studied","word"],"patterns":["what is revision","revision","explain revision","definition of revision","revision meaning","how long should revision take","is rereading revision","ano ang revision","unsa ang ulit"],"answer":"Revision is going over material you have already studied, and the word covers everything from a five-minute look the night before to a full re-do of a term's work. The reason the night-before version feels effective is that familiarity is easy to mistake for recall, and the two are not related. Revision is worth doing in the sense that it finds gaps, and it is not worth doing as a way of feeling more confident, since confidence is the one thing reading reliably produces.","variations":["The reason the night-before version feels effective is that familiarity is easy to mistake for recall, and the two are not related. Revision is going over material you have already studied, and the word covers everything from a five-minute look the night before to a full re-do of a term's work. Revision is worth doing in the sense that it finds gaps, and it is not worth doing as a way of feeling more confident, since confidence is the one thing reading reliably produces.","Revision is worth doing in the sense that it finds gaps, and it is not worth doing as a way of feeling more confident, since confidence is the one thing reading reliably produces. Revision is going over material you have already studied, and the word covers everything from a five-minute look the night before to a full re-do of a term's work. The reason the night-before version feels effective is that familiarity is easy to mistake for recall, and the two are not related."],"tl":"revision","bis":"ulit"},{"id":"stu-techniques-000004","topic":"study-techniques","intent":"definition","concept":"mind map","keywords":["mind map","central","branch","classify","memory","reconstruct","mind","puts","idea","middle","branches"],"patterns":["what is mind map","mind map","explain mind map","definition of mind map","mind map meaning","what is a mind map","are mind maps effective","how to make a mind map","ano ang mind map","unsa ang mind map"],"answer":"A mind map puts a central idea in the middle with branches radiating out, and the value is that it forces you to decide what belongs where, which is a harder task than listing. That classification is the useful part; the drawing is not, and an hour on the layout is an hour not spent on the material. It works best as a reconstruction rather than a copy, so you map from memory and then check, and a map made by copying the chapter is a decorated outline.","variations":["That classification is the useful part; the drawing is not, and an hour on the layout is an hour not spent on the material. A mind map puts a central idea in the middle with branches radiating out, and the value is that it forces you to decide what belongs where, which is a harder task than listing. It works best as a reconstruction rather than a copy, so you map from memory and then check, and a map made by copying the chapter is a decorated outline.","It works best as a reconstruction rather than a copy, so you map from memory and then check, and a map made by copying the chapter is a decorated outline. A mind map puts a central idea in the middle with branches radiating out, and the value is that it forces you to decide what belongs where, which is a harder task than listing. That classification is the useful part; the drawing is not, and an hour on the layout is an hour not spent on the material."],"tl":"mind map","bis":"mind map"},{"id":"stu-techniques-000005","topic":"study-techniques","intent":"definition","concept":"elaborate","keywords":["elaborate","connect","example","attach","explain","detail","ipaliwanag","connection","something","already","half"],"patterns":["what is elaborate","elaborate","explain elaborate","definition of elaborate","elaborate meaning","what does elaborate mean","why does explaining help","how to elaborate on an idea","ano ang elaborate","unsa ang ipaliwanag"],"answer":"To elaborate is to add a connection or an example to something you already half know, and it works because the new material is attached to something existing rather than stored alone. This is why explaining a concept to somebody else is such a reliable test, since the gaps show up in the explanation rather than the knowledge. The trap is adding detail that is easy and not detail that matters, so a long explanation of the mechanism is not the same as understanding why it is asked.","variations":["This is why explaining a concept to somebody else is such a reliable test, since the gaps show up in the explanation rather than the knowledge. To elaborate is to add a connection or an example to something you already half know, and it works because the new material is attached to something existing rather than stored alone. The trap is adding detail that is easy and not detail that matters, so a long explanation of the mechanism is not the same as understanding why it is asked.","The trap is adding detail that is easy and not detail that matters, so a long explanation of the mechanism is not the same as understanding why it is asked. To elaborate is to add a connection or an example to something you already half know, and it works because the new material is attached to something existing rather than stored alone. This is why explaining a concept to somebody else is such a reliable test, since the gaps show up in the explanation rather than the knowledge."],"tl":"elaborate","bis":"ipaliwanag"},{"id":"stu-techniques-000006","topic":"study-techniques","intent":"definition","concept":"interleaving","keywords":["interleaving","mix","topic","blocking","method","retention","mixing","different","topics","practice","session"],"patterns":["what is interleaving","interleaving","explain interleaving","definition of interleaving","interleaving meaning","is blocked practice better","how to mix topics when revising","ano ang interleaving","unsa ang interleaving"],"answer":"Interleaving is mixing different topics in one practice session rather than blocking each one separately, and it feels harder and works better. Blocking, where all the fractions are done then all the decimals, produces a high score during practice and a poor one later, because the skill being tested is recognising which method applies. Interleaved practice is less comfortable and produces better retention, which is an uncomfortable combination that students consistently under-choose.","variations":["Blocking, where all the fractions are done then all the decimals, produces a high score during practice and a poor one later, because the skill being tested is recognising which method applies. Interleaving is mixing different topics in one practice session rather than blocking each one separately, and it feels harder and works better. Interleaved practice is less comfortable and produces better retention, which is an uncomfortable combination that students consistently under-choose.","Interleaved practice is less comfortable and produces better retention, which is an uncomfortable combination that students consistently under-choose. Interleaving is mixing different topics in one practice session rather than blocking each one separately, and it feels harder and works better. Blocking, where all the fractions are done then all the decimals, produces a high score during practice and a poor one later, because the skill being tested is recognising which method applies."],"tl":"interleaving","bis":"interleaving"}],"lang":"english","group":"study","sub":"techniques","title":"Study techniques","groupTitle":"Study","__file":"study/techniques.json"},
  {"topic":"study-time-management","entries":[{"id":"stu-time-management-000001","topic":"study-time-management","intent":"definition","concept":"prioritise","keywords":["prioritise","urgent","important","consequence","in-tray","quadrant","magbigay-priority","decide","comes","first","useful","version"],"patterns":["what is prioritise","prioritise","explain prioritise","definition of prioritise","prioritise meaning","how to prioritise","urgent vs important","what is the Eisenhower matrix","ano ang prioritise","unsa ang magbigay-priority"],"answer":"To prioritise is to decide what comes first, and the useful version decides by consequence rather than by urgency. Urgent and important are different axes: the urgent is whatever is loudest now, and the important is whatever changes the outcome later. Almost every prioritisation mistake is treating the two as the same, so the in-tray empties and nothing has moved. The important and not urgent is the only quadrant with room to work in, and it is the one that gets skipped.","variations":["Urgent and important are different axes: the urgent is whatever is loudest now, and the important is whatever changes the outcome later. To prioritise is to decide what comes first, and the useful version decides by consequence rather than by urgency. Almost every prioritisation mistake is treating the two as the same, so the in-tray empties and nothing has moved. The important and not urgent is the only quadrant with room to work in, and it is the one that gets skipped.","Almost every prioritisation mistake is treating the two as the same, so the in-tray empties and nothing has moved. To prioritise is to decide what comes first, and the useful version decides by consequence rather than by urgency. Urgent and important are different axes: the urgent is whatever is loudest now, and the important is whatever changes the outcome later. The important and not urgent is the only quadrant with room to work in, and it is the one that gets skipped."],"tl":"prioritise","bis":"magbigay-priority"},{"id":"stu-time-management-000002","topic":"study-time-management","intent":"definition","concept":"time block","keywords":["time block","schedule","defend","interruption","attention","visible","time","block","scheduled","period","reserved","work"],"patterns":["what is time block","time block","explain time block","definition of time block","time block meaning","what is time blocking","how do I block time","why do people avoid time blocking","ano ang time block","unsa ang time block"],"answer":"A time block is a scheduled period reserved for one kind of work, and it works because it removes the decision rather than relying on intention. Open time fills with whatever arrives, which is a reasonable default and a poor one for anything needing consecutive attention. A block has to be defended: one interruption does not ruin it, but the expectation that interruptions are acceptable is what collapses it. Blocking also makes the real cost of a request visible, since a two-hour block cannot absorb a two-hour interruption.","variations":["Open time fills with whatever arrives, which is a reasonable default and a poor one for anything needing consecutive attention. A time block is a scheduled period reserved for one kind of work, and it works because it removes the decision rather than relying on intention. A block has to be defended: one interruption does not ruin it, but the expectation that interruptions are acceptable is what collapses it. Blocking also makes the real cost of a request visible, since a two-hour block cannot absorb a two-hour interruption.","A block has to be defended: one interruption does not ruin it, but the expectation that interruptions are acceptable is what collapses it. A time block is a scheduled period reserved for one kind of work, and it works because it removes the decision rather than relying on intention. Open time fills with whatever arrives, which is a reasonable default and a poor one for anything needing consecutive attention. Blocking also makes the real cost of a request visible, since a two-hour block cannot absorb a two-hour interruption."],"tl":"time block","bis":"time block"},{"id":"stu-time-management-000003","topic":"study-time-management","intent":"definition","concept":"productivity system","keywords":["productivity system","productivity","system","capture","routine","list","choice","sistema","fixed","capturing","processing"],"patterns":["what is productivity system","productivity system","explain productivity system","definition of productivity system","productivity system meaning","what is a productivity system","how do I build one","is getting things done worth it","ano ang productivity system","unsa ang sistema"],"answer":"A productivity system is a fixed routine for capturing and processing what needs doing, and its value is in removing choices rather than in being efficient. Capture goes into one place and everything else follows from there; a system with a separate list for each context has multiplied the work rather than reduced it. The test of whether one works is whether you would still use it when nobody could see you using it, because most are maintained for the feeling of control rather than for the results.","variations":["Capture goes into one place and everything else follows from there; a system with a separate list for each context has multiplied the work rather than reduced it. A productivity system is a fixed routine for capturing and processing what needs doing, and its value is in removing choices rather than in being efficient. The test of whether one works is whether you would still use it when nobody could see you using it, because most are maintained for the feeling of control rather than for the results.","The test of whether one works is whether you would still use it when nobody could see you using it, because most are maintained for the feeling of control rather than for the results. A productivity system is a fixed routine for capturing and processing what needs doing, and its value is in removing choices rather than in being efficient. Capture goes into one place and everything else follows from there; a system with a separate list for each context has multiplied the work rather than reduced it."],"tl":"productivity system","bis":"sistema"},{"id":"stu-time-management-000004","topic":"study-time-management","intent":"definition","concept":"distraction","keywords":["distraction","attention","restart","absorb","resist","phone","abalang","anything","takes","away","important"],"patterns":["what is distraction","distraction","explain distraction","definition of distraction","distraction meaning","what is a distraction","why do interruptions cost twice","how to avoid distraction","ano ang distraction","unsa ang abalang"],"answer":"A distraction is anything that takes attention away, and the important part is that the cost is paid twice: once to stop what you were doing and once to return to it. That restarting cost is why four short checks of a phone can cost more than the checking itself, and it scales with how absorbing the original task was. Removing the choice beats resisting it, which is why a phone in another room outperforms willpower at a desk in a busy office.","variations":["That restarting cost is why four short checks of a phone can cost more than the checking itself, and it scales with how absorbing the original task was. A distraction is anything that takes attention away, and the important part is that the cost is paid twice: once to stop what you were doing and once to return to it. Removing the choice beats resisting it, which is why a phone in another room outperforms willpower at a desk in a busy office.","Removing the choice beats resisting it, which is why a phone in another room outperforms willpower at a desk in a busy office. A distraction is anything that takes attention away, and the important part is that the cost is paid twice: once to stop what you were doing and once to return to it. That restarting cost is why four short checks of a phone can cost more than the checking itself, and it scales with how absorbing the original task was."],"tl":"distraction","bis":"abalang"}],"lang":"english","group":"study","sub":"time-management","title":"Time management","groupTitle":"Study","__file":"study/time-management.json"},
  {"topic":"study-writing","entries":[{"id":"stu-writing-000001","topic":"study-writing","intent":"definition","concept":"thesis statement","keywords":["thesis statement","thesis","argue","falsify","topic","evidence","essay","statement","single","sentence","naming"],"patterns":["what is thesis statement","thesis statement","explain thesis statement","definition of thesis statement","thesis statement meaning","what is a thesis statement","how to write one","thesis vs topic sentence","ano ang thesis statement","unsa ang thesis"],"answer":"A thesis statement is a single sentence naming what the essay argues, and it belongs in the introduction where the reader can find it in one pass. Its job is to be falsifiable: if no evidence could count against it, it is a description rather than an argument. The common failure is a topic sentence instead, and I will discuss the causes of the war is the shape of it, since it could be argued about indefinitely without saying which way.","variations":["Its job is to be falsifiable: if no evidence could count against it, it is a description rather than an argument. A thesis statement is a single sentence naming what the essay argues, and it belongs in the introduction where the reader can find it in one pass. The common failure is a topic sentence instead, and I will discuss the causes of the war is the shape of it, since it could be argued about indefinitely without saying which way.","The common failure is a topic sentence instead, and I will discuss the causes of the war is the shape of it, since it could be argued about indefinitely without saying which way. A thesis statement is a single sentence naming what the essay argues, and it belongs in the introduction where the reader can find it in one pass. Its job is to be falsifiable: if no evidence could count against it, it is a description rather than an argument."],"tl":"thesis statement","bis":"thesis"},{"id":"stu-writing-000002","topic":"study-writing","intent":"definition","concept":"paragraph","keywords":["paragraph","claim","evidence","duplicate","develop","talata","unit","controlling","idea","rather"],"patterns":["what is paragraph","paragraph","explain paragraph","definition of paragraph","paragraph meaning","what is a paragraph","how long should a paragraph be","what is a topic sentence","ano ang paragraph","unsa ang talata"],"answer":"A paragraph is a unit with one controlling idea, and its job is to develop that idea rather than to introduce a new one. The topic sentence carries the claim and the rest of the paragraph supplies reasons, evidence or steps, and a paragraph that changes claim halfway has two paragraphs' worth of work at neither one's depth. This is why the middle of an essay is usually a list of things, and the fix is to write one sentence per paragraph first and check that they are not duplicates.","variations":["The topic sentence carries the claim and the rest of the paragraph supplies reasons, evidence or steps, and a paragraph that changes claim halfway has two paragraphs' worth of work at neither one's depth. A paragraph is a unit with one controlling idea, and its job is to develop that idea rather than to introduce a new one. This is why the middle of an essay is usually a list of things, and the fix is to write one sentence per paragraph first and check that they are not duplicates.","This is why the middle of an essay is usually a list of things, and the fix is to write one sentence per paragraph first and check that they are not duplicates. A paragraph is a unit with one controlling idea, and its job is to develop that idea rather than to introduce a new one. The topic sentence carries the claim and the rest of the paragraph supplies reasons, evidence or steps, and a paragraph that changes claim halfway has two paragraphs' worth of work at neither one's depth."],"tl":"paragraph","bis":"talata"},{"id":"stu-writing-000003","topic":"study-writing","intent":"definition","concept":"draft","keywords":["draft","revise","complete","edit","cut","improve","version","written","improved","rather"],"patterns":["what is draft","draft","explain draft","definition of draft","draft meaning","what is a draft","should you edit while writing","how to finish a draft","ano ang draft","unsa ang draft"],"answer":"A draft is a complete version written to be improved rather than to be right, and the reason it feels wrong to write is that it is being judged while it is being made. The rule is to finish before editing, because editing a half-finished draft produces a tidier half-finished draft. Time is the real constraint: a draft written quickly and then cut is faster than a draft polished sentence by sentence, and it is also easier to cut a finished thing than to imagine a finished thing.","variations":["The rule is to finish before editing, because editing a half-finished draft produces a tidier half-finished draft. A draft is a complete version written to be improved rather than to be right, and the reason it feels wrong to write is that it is being judged while it is being made. Time is the real constraint: a draft written quickly and then cut is faster than a draft polished sentence by sentence, and it is also easier to cut a finished thing than to imagine a finished thing.","Time is the real constraint: a draft written quickly and then cut is faster than a draft polished sentence by sentence, and it is also easier to cut a finished thing than to imagine a finished thing. A draft is a complete version written to be improved rather than to be right, and the reason it feels wrong to write is that it is being judged while it is being made. The rule is to finish before editing, because editing a half-finished draft produces a tidier half-finished draft."],"tl":"draft","bis":"draft"},{"id":"stu-writing-000004","topic":"study-writing","intent":"definition","concept":"citation","keywords":["citation","source","quote","reference","author","date","sanggunian","credits","idea","quotation","figure"],"patterns":["what is citation","citation","explain citation","definition of citation","citation meaning","what is a citation","author date vs numbered citation","do I need to cite a common fact","ano ang citation","unsa ang sanggunian"],"answer":"A citation credits the source of an idea, a quotation or a figure, and there are two systems with different logic. Author-date citation credits the person, while numbered citation credits the position in a list, and the second is more compact when there are many references. What a citation is not is a decoration added at the end, since a source cited but not discussed, or discussed but not cited, are the two most common faults and only one of them looks like an error.","variations":["Author-date citation credits the person, while numbered citation credits the position in a list, and the second is more compact when there are many references. A citation credits the source of an idea, a quotation or a figure, and there are two systems with different logic. What a citation is not is a decoration added at the end, since a source cited but not discussed, or discussed but not cited, are the two most common faults and only one of them looks like an error.","What a citation is not is a decoration added at the end, since a source cited but not discussed, or discussed but not cited, are the two most common faults and only one of them looks like an error. A citation credits the source of an idea, a quotation or a figure, and there are two systems with different logic. Author-date citation credits the person, while numbered citation credits the position in a list, and the second is more compact when there are many references."],"tl":"citation","bis":"sanggunian"},{"id":"stu-writing-000005","topic":"study-writing","intent":"definition","concept":"plagiarism","keywords":["plagiarism","copy","attribute","paraphrase","provenance","self","plagiarismo","presenting","somebody","else's","work","definition"],"patterns":["what is plagiarism","plagiarism","explain plagiarism","definition of plagiarism","plagiarism meaning","is paraphrasing plagiarism","what is self plagiarism","ano ang plagiarism","unsa ang plagiarismo"],"answer":"Plagiarism is presenting somebody else's work as your own, and the definition is stricter than most people expect, covering sentences, ideas and structure. Direct copying is the obvious case, but the ones that catch people out are paraphrasing with inadequate attribution and reusing your own earlier work without saying so, which is self-plagiarism. The reliable defence is not wording but provenance: write from a source while it is open, so the ideas arrive in your own structure, and record where each came from.","variations":["Direct copying is the obvious case, but the ones that catch people out are paraphrasing with inadequate attribution and reusing your own earlier work without saying so, which is self-plagiarism. Plagiarism is presenting somebody else's work as your own, and the definition is stricter than most people expect, covering sentences, ideas and structure. The reliable defence is not wording but provenance: write from a source while it is open, so the ideas arrive in your own structure, and record where each came from.","The reliable defence is not wording but provenance: write from a source while it is open, so the ideas arrive in your own structure, and record where each came from. Plagiarism is presenting somebody else's work as your own, and the definition is stricter than most people expect, covering sentences, ideas and structure. Direct copying is the obvious case, but the ones that catch people out are paraphrasing with inadequate attribution and reusing your own earlier work without saying so, which is self-plagiarism."],"tl":"plagiarism","bis":"plagiarismo"}],"lang":"english","group":"study","sub":"writing","title":"Academic writing","groupTitle":"Study","__file":"study/writing.json"},
  {"topic":"technology-ai","entries":[{"id":"tec-ai-000001","topic":"technology-ai","intent":"definition","concept":"ai","keywords":["ai","llm","model","neural","artificial","intelligence","software","performs","tasks","normally"],"patterns":["what is ai","ai","explain ai","definition of ai","ai meaning","artificial intelligence","machine learning","what is artificial intelligence","what is a language model"],"answer":"Artificial intelligence is software that performs tasks we would normally call intelligent - recognising images, translating, writing, deciding. Modern AI is mostly machine learning: instead of hand-written rules for every case, a model is shown many examples and learns the pattern. A large language model is trained on a very large amount of text to predict what comes next, which produces broad ability and a real tendency to state plausible things that are not true.","variations":["Modern AI is mostly machine learning: instead of hand-written rules for every case, a model is shown many examples and learns the pattern. Artificial intelligence is software that performs tasks we would normally call intelligent - recognising images, translating, writing, deciding. A large language model is trained on a very large amount of text to predict what comes next, which produces broad ability and a real tendency to state plausible things that are not true.","A large language model is trained on a very large amount of text to predict what comes next, which produces broad ability and a real tendency to state plausible things that are not true. Artificial intelligence is software that performs tasks we would normally call intelligent - recognising images, translating, writing, deciding. Modern AI is mostly machine learning: instead of hand-written rules for every case, a model is shown many examples and learns the pattern."]},{"id":"tec-ai-000002","topic":"technology-ai","intent":"definition","concept":"hallucinate","keywords":["hallucinate","hallucination","wrong","accuracy","reliable","trust","large","language","model","predicts","likely","continuation"],"patterns":["what is hallucinate","hallucinate","explain hallucinate","definition of hallucinate","hallucinate meaning","can ai be wrong","what is hallucination","is ai accurate"],"answer":"A large language model predicts the most likely continuation of text, which means it is optimised to sound plausible rather than to be true. When it does not know something, the same mechanism produces a fluent, well-formed and incorrect answer - called a hallucination. It is not a bug that will be patched away; it follows from how these systems are built. Anything that matters should be checked against a real source, and the model should be treated as a useful draft rather than an authority.","variations":["When it does not know something, the same mechanism produces a fluent, well-formed and incorrect answer - called a hallucination. A large language model predicts the most likely continuation of text, which means it is optimised to sound plausible rather than to be true. It is not a bug that will be patched away; it follows from how these systems are built. Anything that matters should be checked against a real source, and the model should be treated as a useful draft rather than an authority.","It is not a bug that will be patched away; it follows from how these systems are built. A large language model predicts the most likely continuation of text, which means it is optimised to sound plausible rather than to be true. When it does not know something, the same mechanism produces a fluent, well-formed and incorrect answer - called a hallucination. Anything that matters should be checked against a real source, and the model should be treated as a useful draft rather than an authority."]},{"id":"tec-ai-000003","topic":"technology-ai","intent":"definition","concept":"machine learning","keywords":["machine learning","machine","ml","ai","training","data","model","overfitting","supervised","parameters","learning","fits","adjusting"],"patterns":["what is machine learning","machine learning","explain machine learning","definition of machine learning","machine learning meaning","is machine learning the same as ai","ml meaning","what is overfitting","ano ang machine learning","unsa ang machine learning"],"answer":"Machine learning fits a model to data by adjusting parameters until its output matches the answers, rather than having the rules written by hand. It is split by what is supervised with labelled examples, unsupervised with none, and reinforcement where the system learns from reward. The failure mode to know about is overfitting, where a model memorises its training data and then performs badly on anything new it has not seen.","variations":["It is split by what is supervised with labelled examples, unsupervised with none, and reinforcement where the system learns from reward. Machine learning fits a model to data by adjusting parameters until its output matches the answers, rather than having the rules written by hand. The failure mode to know about is overfitting, where a model memorises its training data and then performs badly on anything new it has not seen.","The failure mode to know about is overfitting, where a model memorises its training data and then performs badly on anything new it has not seen. Machine learning fits a model to data by adjusting parameters until its output matches the answers, rather than having the rules written by hand. It is split by what is supervised with labelled examples, unsupervised with none, and reinforcement where the system learns from reward."],"tl":"machine learning","bis":"machine learning"},{"id":"tec-ai-000004","topic":"technology-ai","intent":"definition","concept":"neural network","keywords":["neural network","layer","node","weight","training","gradient","representation","dataset","network","neural","system","connected","units","strengths"],"patterns":["what is neural network","neural network","explain neural network","definition of neural network","neural network meaning","what is a neural network","how does a neural network learn","what is deep learning","ano ang neural network","unsa ang neural network"],"answer":"A neural network is a system of connected units whose strengths of connection are adjusted until its output matches the right answer, and the name is borrowed from the brain without implying it works anything like one. The units are arranged in layers: the first takes the raw input, the middle layers build up increasingly abstract features, and the last produces the answer. A network learns by comparing its wrong output to the right one and adjusting the connections slightly in the direction that would have reduced the error, repeated over very many examples, which is what training is. The layer idea is why a network can recognise a face without being told what a face is: early layers pick up edges, later ones combine them into shapes, and the last recognises the whole. The cost is that the internal representations are not designed by anyone, so a system can be accurate and still be impossible to reason about, and it can pick up a shortcut in the training data rather than the thing it appears to have learned. That is the practical reason a good dataset matters more than a complicated model.","variations":["The units are arranged in layers: the first takes the raw input, the middle layers build up increasingly abstract features, and the last produces the answer. A neural network is a system of connected units whose strengths of connection are adjusted until its output matches the right answer, and the name is borrowed from the brain without implying it works anything like one. A network learns by comparing its wrong output to the right one and adjusting the connections slightly in the direction that would have reduced the error, repeated over very many examples, which is what training is. The layer idea is why a network can recognise a face without being told what a face is: early layers pick up edges, later ones combine them into shapes, and the last recognises the whole. The cost is that the internal representations are not designed by anyone, so a system can be accurate and still be impossible to reason about, and it can pick up a shortcut in the training data rather than the thing it appears to have learned. That is the practical reason a good dataset matters more than a complicated model.","A network learns by comparing its wrong output to the right one and adjusting the connections slightly in the direction that would have reduced the error, repeated over very many examples, which is what training is. A neural network is a system of connected units whose strengths of connection are adjusted until its output matches the right answer, and the name is borrowed from the brain without implying it works anything like one. The units are arranged in layers: the first takes the raw input, the middle layers build up increasingly abstract features, and the last produces the answer. The layer idea is why a network can recognise a face without being told what a face is: early layers pick up edges, later ones combine them into shapes, and the last recognises the whole. The cost is that the internal representations are not designed by anyone, so a system can be accurate and still be impossible to reason about, and it can pick up a shortcut in the training data rather than the thing it appears to have learned. That is the practical reason a good dataset matters more than a complicated model."],"tl":"neural network","bis":"neural network"},{"id":"tec-ai-000005","topic":"technology-ai","intent":"definition","concept":"openai","keywords":["openai","gpt","token","llm","api","builds","large","language","models","sells"],"patterns":["what is openai","openai","explain openai","definition of openai","openai meaning","what is a language model","why do ai models hallucinate","ano ang openai","unsa ang openai"],"answer":"OpenAI builds large language models and sells access to them through an API, with the models being the product and the API the delivery. A language model predicts the next token, which produces broad ability and a real tendency to state things plausibly and incorrectly. That is the honest framing of what these systems are, and it is why verification matters more with them than with a lookup, which cannot invent an answer.","variations":["A language model predicts the next token, which produces broad ability and a real tendency to state things plausibly and incorrectly. OpenAI builds large language models and sells access to them through an API, with the models being the product and the API the delivery. That is the honest framing of what these systems are, and it is why verification matters more with them than with a lookup, which cannot invent an answer.","That is the honest framing of what these systems are, and it is why verification matters more with them than with a lookup, which cannot invent an answer. OpenAI builds large language models and sells access to them through an API, with the models being the product and the API the delivery. A language model predicts the next token, which produces broad ability and a real tendency to state things plausibly and incorrectly."],"tl":"openai","bis":"openai"}],"lang":"english","group":"technology","sub":"ai","title":"AI","groupTitle":"Technology","__file":"technology/ai.json"},
  {"topic":"technology-cloud","entries":[{"id":"tec-cloud-000001","topic":"technology-cloud","intent":"definition","concept":"cloud computing","keywords":["cloud computing","elasticity","infrastructure","capacity","metering","rented","cloud","computing","delivering","services","internet","instead"],"patterns":["what is cloud computing","cloud computing","explain cloud computing","definition of cloud computing","cloud computing meaning","what are the benefits of cloud","is cloud cheaper","ano ang cloud computing","unsa ang cloud computing"],"answer":"Cloud computing is delivering computing services over the internet instead of running them on hardware you own, and the three things that get rented are compute, storage and networking. The defining property is elasticity: capacity can be added when demand rises and released when it falls, so a site handles a traffic spike without buying a machine that is idle the rest of the week. The second property is that the resources live in someone else's data centre, which removes the capital cost and the maintenance work but also removes direct control over the hardware and the location of your data. The third is the billing model, where a service can be charged per hour of use or even per request, so the cost follows the usage rather than the largest machine you bought. The main criticism is the bill that surprises people: an under-optimised program is charged exactly as much as a well-written one, and a service left running costs money quietly every month.","variations":["The defining property is elasticity: capacity can be added when demand rises and released when it falls, so a site handles a traffic spike without buying a machine that is idle the rest of the week. Cloud computing is delivering computing services over the internet instead of running them on hardware you own, and the three things that get rented are compute, storage and networking. The second property is that the resources live in someone else's data centre, which removes the capital cost and the maintenance work but also removes direct control over the hardware and the location of your data. The third is the billing model, where a service can be charged per hour of use or even per request, so the cost follows the usage rather than the largest machine you bought. The main criticism is the bill that surprises people: an under-optimised program is charged exactly as much as a well-written one, and a service left running costs money quietly every month.","The second property is that the resources live in someone else's data centre, which removes the capital cost and the maintenance work but also removes direct control over the hardware and the location of your data. Cloud computing is delivering computing services over the internet instead of running them on hardware you own, and the three things that get rented are compute, storage and networking. The defining property is elasticity: capacity can be added when demand rises and released when it falls, so a site handles a traffic spike without buying a machine that is idle the rest of the week. The third is the billing model, where a service can be charged per hour of use or even per request, so the cost follows the usage rather than the largest machine you bought. The main criticism is the bill that surprises people: an under-optimised program is charged exactly as much as a well-written one, and a service left running costs money quietly every month."],"tl":"cloud computing","bis":"cloud computing"},{"id":"tec-cloud-000002","topic":"technology-cloud","intent":"definition","concept":"saas","keywords":["saas","subscription","browser","vendor","maintenance","licence","software","service","cloud","model","provider","runs"],"patterns":["what is saas","saas","explain saas","definition of saas","saas meaning","is gmail saas","difference between saas and on premise","ano ang saas","unsa ang SaaS"],"answer":"Software as a service is the cloud model where the provider runs the software and you access it through a browser or an app, with nothing to install and nothing to maintain. The provider handles the infrastructure, the updates, the backups and the uptime, and the customer manages only their own data and accounts. This is why a webmail or a spreadsheet no longer needs installing, and it is the reason a small organisation can run what used to require a server room. The pricing follows the same shape: a subscription per user per month, sometimes with tiers that limit features, and it is a predictable cost rather than a licence you buy and host. The trade is the loss of control, since the software lives on someone else's platform and access depends on a network connection, and the data is in a system you do not administer yourself. Most software a student or office uses now is SaaS, and the older pattern of a one-off purchase that you installed yourself is now the exception.","variations":["The provider handles the infrastructure, the updates, the backups and the uptime, and the customer manages only their own data and accounts. Software as a service is the cloud model where the provider runs the software and you access it through a browser or an app, with nothing to install and nothing to maintain. This is why a webmail or a spreadsheet no longer needs installing, and it is the reason a small organisation can run what used to require a server room. The pricing follows the same shape: a subscription per user per month, sometimes with tiers that limit features, and it is a predictable cost rather than a licence you buy and host. The trade is the loss of control, since the software lives on someone else's platform and access depends on a network connection, and the data is in a system you do not administer yourself. Most software a student or office uses now is SaaS, and the older pattern of a one-off purchase that you installed yourself is now the exception.","This is why a webmail or a spreadsheet no longer needs installing, and it is the reason a small organisation can run what used to require a server room. Software as a service is the cloud model where the provider runs the software and you access it through a browser or an app, with nothing to install and nothing to maintain. The provider handles the infrastructure, the updates, the backups and the uptime, and the customer manages only their own data and accounts. The pricing follows the same shape: a subscription per user per month, sometimes with tiers that limit features, and it is a predictable cost rather than a licence you buy and host. The trade is the loss of control, since the software lives on someone else's platform and access depends on a network connection, and the data is in a system you do not administer yourself. Most software a student or office uses now is SaaS, and the older pattern of a one-off purchase that you installed yourself is now the exception."],"tl":"saas","bis":"SaaS"},{"id":"tec-cloud-000003","topic":"technology-cloud","intent":"definition","concept":"paas","keywords":["paas","platform","runtime","deploy","container","serverless","managed","developer","service","middle","model","provider","manages"],"patterns":["what is paas","paas","explain paas","definition of paas","paas meaning","what is platform as a service","is heroku paas","ano ang paas","unsa ang PaaS"],"answer":"Platform as a service is the middle model, where the provider manages the operating system and the runtime and you deploy only your own code and configuration. The gain is that there is no server to patch and no operating system to install, so a developer can publish an application without ever seeing a machine, and the platform handles scaling, routing and often the database. The cost is that you work inside the provider's constraints: the supported language list, the configuration format, the deployment model and the pricing all belong to them. A container service sits near this end, with the platform scheduling the image and the application packaged inside it, and serverless sits furthest, where you supply a function and the platform runs it per request and bills only the time it used. The practical advice is to start with the smallest model that removes the work you do not want to do, because moving down a level later is easy and moving back up is not.","variations":["The gain is that there is no server to patch and no operating system to install, so a developer can publish an application without ever seeing a machine, and the platform handles scaling, routing and often the database. Platform as a service is the middle model, where the provider manages the operating system and the runtime and you deploy only your own code and configuration. The cost is that you work inside the provider's constraints: the supported language list, the configuration format, the deployment model and the pricing all belong to them. A container service sits near this end, with the platform scheduling the image and the application packaged inside it, and serverless sits furthest, where you supply a function and the platform runs it per request and bills only the time it used. The practical advice is to start with the smallest model that removes the work you do not want to do, because moving down a level later is easy and moving back up is not.","The cost is that you work inside the provider's constraints: the supported language list, the configuration format, the deployment model and the pricing all belong to them. Platform as a service is the middle model, where the provider manages the operating system and the runtime and you deploy only your own code and configuration. The gain is that there is no server to patch and no operating system to install, so a developer can publish an application without ever seeing a machine, and the platform handles scaling, routing and often the database. A container service sits near this end, with the platform scheduling the image and the application packaged inside it, and serverless sits furthest, where you supply a function and the platform runs it per request and bills only the time it used. The practical advice is to start with the smallest model that removes the work you do not want to do, because moving down a level later is easy and moving back up is not."],"tl":"paas","bis":"PaaS"},{"id":"tec-cloud-000004","topic":"technology-cloud","intent":"definition","concept":"load balancing","keywords":["load balancing","balancer","throughput","load","balancing","spreading","incoming","requests","across"],"patterns":["what is load balancing","load balancing","explain load balancing","definition of load balancing","load balancing meaning","what is a load balancer","difference between vertical and horizontal scaling","ano ang load balancing","unsa ang load balancing"],"answer":"Load balancing is spreading incoming requests across several server instances so that no single one becomes the bottleneck and so that a failure of one does not take the service down. The balancer sits in front of the servers, receives the connection, picks a target using a rule such as round robin, least connections or the source address, and forwards it. The benefit is twofold and both matter: it raises throughput, and it provides high availability because a health check can remove a dead instance from the pool without anyone noticing. The health check is the part that is often underestimated, because a server that is running but unable to serve is still receiving traffic unless something measures readiness rather than mere existence. Scaling is the related idea and it comes in two forms, vertical, which means a bigger machine, and horizontal, which means more machines, and only the second one is limited by the largest single machine you can buy.","variations":["The balancer sits in front of the servers, receives the connection, picks a target using a rule such as round robin, least connections or the source address, and forwards it. Load balancing is spreading incoming requests across several server instances so that no single one becomes the bottleneck and so that a failure of one does not take the service down. The benefit is twofold and both matter: it raises throughput, and it provides high availability because a health check can remove a dead instance from the pool without anyone noticing. The health check is the part that is often underestimated, because a server that is running but unable to serve is still receiving traffic unless something measures readiness rather than mere existence. Scaling is the related idea and it comes in two forms, vertical, which means a bigger machine, and horizontal, which means more machines, and only the second one is limited by the largest single machine you can buy.","The benefit is twofold and both matter: it raises throughput, and it provides high availability because a health check can remove a dead instance from the pool without anyone noticing. Load balancing is spreading incoming requests across several server instances so that no single one becomes the bottleneck and so that a failure of one does not take the service down. The balancer sits in front of the servers, receives the connection, picks a target using a rule such as round robin, least connections or the source address, and forwards it. The health check is the part that is often underestimated, because a server that is running but unable to serve is still receiving traffic unless something measures readiness rather than mere existence. Scaling is the related idea and it comes in two forms, vertical, which means a bigger machine, and horizontal, which means more machines, and only the second one is limited by the largest single machine you can buy."],"tl":"load balancing","bis":"load balancing"},{"id":"tec-cloud-000005","topic":"technology-cloud","intent":"definition","concept":"iaas","keywords":["iaas","instance","infrastructure","responsibility","tenancy","service","cloud","model","closest","renting"],"patterns":["what is iaas","iaas","explain iaas","definition of iaas","iaas meaning","is a cloud instance a virtual machine","difference between iaas and paas","ano ang iaas","unsa ang IaaS"],"answer":"Infrastructure as a service is the cloud model closest to renting a physical machine, where the provider supplies the computing power, storage and networking and you install whatever you want on top. That means you are responsible for the operating system, the security patches, the network configuration and everything in between, exactly as you would be on a server you bought. The usual comparison is with a virtual machine, and the two are not really different in kind: a virtual machine is software that emulates hardware, so a cloud instance is in practice a virtual machine running on someone else's larger machine, with the difference that it appeared in under a minute and disappears when you delete it. Because of that division of responsibility, the security failures in this model are almost always configuration mistakes by the customer rather than vulnerabilities in the platform. The economics work when utilisation is low or spiky, since you stop paying for hardware that sits idle.","variations":["That means you are responsible for the operating system, the security patches, the network configuration and everything in between, exactly as you would be on a server you bought. Infrastructure as a service is the cloud model closest to renting a physical machine, where the provider supplies the computing power, storage and networking and you install whatever you want on top. The usual comparison is with a virtual machine, and the two are not really different in kind: a virtual machine is software that emulates hardware, so a cloud instance is in practice a virtual machine running on someone else's larger machine, with the difference that it appeared in under a minute and disappears when you delete it. Because of that division of responsibility, the security failures in this model are almost always configuration mistakes by the customer rather than vulnerabilities in the platform. The economics work when utilisation is low or spiky, since you stop paying for hardware that sits idle.","The usual comparison is with a virtual machine, and the two are not really different in kind: a virtual machine is software that emulates hardware, so a cloud instance is in practice a virtual machine running on someone else's larger machine, with the difference that it appeared in under a minute and disappears when you delete it. Infrastructure as a service is the cloud model closest to renting a physical machine, where the provider supplies the computing power, storage and networking and you install whatever you want on top. That means you are responsible for the operating system, the security patches, the network configuration and everything in between, exactly as you would be on a server you bought. Because of that division of responsibility, the security failures in this model are almost always configuration mistakes by the customer rather than vulnerabilities in the platform. The economics work when utilisation is low or spiky, since you stop paying for hardware that sits idle."],"tl":"iaas","bis":"IaaS"}],"lang":"english","group":"technology","sub":"cloud","title":"Cloud","groupTitle":"Technology","__file":"technology/cloud.json"},
  {"topic":"technology-computers","entries":[{"id":"tec-computers-000001","topic":"technology-computers","intent":"definition","concept":"von neumann architecture","keywords":["von neumann architecture","memory","cache","bottleneck","arkitektura","neumann","architecture","stored","program","design","modern"],"patterns":["what is von neumann architecture","von neumann architecture","explain von neumann architecture","definition of von neumann architecture","von neumann architecture meaning","what does the cpu do","how does a computer execute a program","ano ang von neumann architecture","unsa ang arkitektura"],"answer":"The von Neumann architecture is the stored-program design that made the modern computer general purpose rather than a calculator built for one job, and its defining feature is that the same memory holds both the program and the data being worked on. That single idea is why the machine can be reprogrammed without rewiring anything, since the instructions are fetched from memory the same way the data is and a program is just data that happens to be interpreted. The processor runs a fetch-decode-execute cycle, taking an instruction, working out what it means, carrying it out and moving on, with a program counter deciding which instruction comes next. The parts of the machine that follow from this are the control unit for deciding, the arithmetic and logic unit for computing, registers for the handful of values being worked on right now, and memory for everything else. The bottleneck that emerges from the design is the gap between processor and memory speed, called the von Neumann bottleneck, and it is why performance work is usually about moving less data rather than doing less work.","variations":["That single idea is why the machine can be reprogrammed without rewiring anything, since the instructions are fetched from memory the same way the data is and a program is just data that happens to be interpreted. The von Neumann architecture is the stored-program design that made the modern computer general purpose rather than a calculator built for one job, and its defining feature is that the same memory holds both the program and the data being worked on. The processor runs a fetch-decode-execute cycle, taking an instruction, working out what it means, carrying it out and moving on, with a program counter deciding which instruction comes next. The parts of the machine that follow from this are the control unit for deciding, the arithmetic and logic unit for computing, registers for the handful of values being worked on right now, and memory for everything else. The bottleneck that emerges from the design is the gap between processor and memory speed, called the von Neumann bottleneck, and it is why performance work is usually about moving less data rather than doing less work.","The processor runs a fetch-decode-execute cycle, taking an instruction, working out what it means, carrying it out and moving on, with a program counter deciding which instruction comes next. The von Neumann architecture is the stored-program design that made the modern computer general purpose rather than a calculator built for one job, and its defining feature is that the same memory holds both the program and the data being worked on. That single idea is why the machine can be reprogrammed without rewiring anything, since the instructions are fetched from memory the same way the data is and a program is just data that happens to be interpreted. The parts of the machine that follow from this are the control unit for deciding, the arithmetic and logic unit for computing, registers for the handful of values being worked on right now, and memory for everything else. The bottleneck that emerges from the design is the gap between processor and memory speed, called the von Neumann bottleneck, and it is why performance work is usually about moving less data rather than doing less work."],"tl":"von neumann architecture","bis":"arkitektura"},{"id":"tec-computers-000002","topic":"technology-computers","intent":"definition","concept":"multitasking","keywords":["multitasking","process","thread","scheduler","parallel","letting","processor","appear","several","programs"],"patterns":["what is multitasking","multitasking","explain multitasking","definition of multitasking","multitasking meaning","difference between multitasking and multithreading","how does the os run two programs","ano ang multitasking","unsa ang multitasking"],"answer":"Multitasking is letting one processor appear to run several programs at once by switching between them rapidly, and on a single core it is an illusion created by scheduling rather than genuine parallelism. The operating system gives each running program a slice of time, and when the slice runs out it saves the program state, switches to another, and returns later, which is why a browser tab can look frozen while audio keeps playing and the clock keeps ticking. Each program is isolated in its own address space so it cannot read another's memory, which is a security benefit as much as an organisational one. The context switch between programs is more expensive than between threads of one program, because more of the state has to be saved, which is why a machine with many cores changes the picture: work can then genuinely run in parallel rather than interleaved. The term is also used for a user perception rather than a mechanism, since switching a window on screen looks like multitasking even though only one thing was ever running.","variations":["The operating system gives each running program a slice of time, and when the slice runs out it saves the program state, switches to another, and returns later, which is why a browser tab can look frozen while audio keeps playing and the clock keeps ticking. Multitasking is letting one processor appear to run several programs at once by switching between them rapidly, and on a single core it is an illusion created by scheduling rather than genuine parallelism. Each program is isolated in its own address space so it cannot read another's memory, which is a security benefit as much as an organisational one. The context switch between programs is more expensive than between threads of one program, because more of the state has to be saved, which is why a machine with many cores changes the picture: work can then genuinely run in parallel rather than interleaved. The term is also used for a user perception rather than a mechanism, since switching a window on screen looks like multitasking even though only one thing was ever running.","Each program is isolated in its own address space so it cannot read another's memory, which is a security benefit as much as an organisational one. Multitasking is letting one processor appear to run several programs at once by switching between them rapidly, and on a single core it is an illusion created by scheduling rather than genuine parallelism. The operating system gives each running program a slice of time, and when the slice runs out it saves the program state, switches to another, and returns later, which is why a browser tab can look frozen while audio keeps playing and the clock keeps ticking. The context switch between programs is more expensive than between threads of one program, because more of the state has to be saved, which is why a machine with many cores changes the picture: work can then genuinely run in parallel rather than interleaved. The term is also used for a user perception rather than a mechanism, since switching a window on screen looks like multitasking even though only one thing was ever running."],"tl":"multitasking","bis":"multitasking"},{"id":"tec-computers-000003","topic":"technology-computers","intent":"definition","concept":"virtual memory","keywords":["virtual memory","paging","translation","isolation","swap","virtual","memory","technique","program","believe","continuous"],"patterns":["what is virtual memory","virtual memory","explain virtual memory","definition of virtual memory","virtual memory meaning","what is paging","why does my computer slow down with many tabs","ano ang virtual memory","unsa ang virtual memory"],"answer":"Virtual memory is the technique that lets each program believe it has its own continuous block of memory, even when the physical memory is shared, smaller, and non-contiguous. Each program gets its own address space, and the operating system maps the addresses it actually uses onto physical pages, so two programs can both believe they are at address 4000 without interfering. The mapping table is the page table, and the hardware caches its most recent use in a translation lookaside buffer, which is why enabling that hardware feature is such a large speedup. When a program touches a page that is not in memory the operation blocks and the operating system loads it from storage, which is paging and is the mechanism behind the freeze when memory has run out. The illusion works because most code touches its own memory in a localised way, so the scheme is affordable, and a separate address space is also what lets the operating system keep programs isolated from each other.","variations":["Each program gets its own address space, and the operating system maps the addresses it actually uses onto physical pages, so two programs can both believe they are at address 4000 without interfering. Virtual memory is the technique that lets each program believe it has its own continuous block of memory, even when the physical memory is shared, smaller, and non-contiguous. The mapping table is the page table, and the hardware caches its most recent use in a translation lookaside buffer, which is why enabling that hardware feature is such a large speedup. When a program touches a page that is not in memory the operation blocks and the operating system loads it from storage, which is paging and is the mechanism behind the freeze when memory has run out. The illusion works because most code touches its own memory in a localised way, so the scheme is affordable, and a separate address space is also what lets the operating system keep programs isolated from each other.","The mapping table is the page table, and the hardware caches its most recent use in a translation lookaside buffer, which is why enabling that hardware feature is such a large speedup. Virtual memory is the technique that lets each program believe it has its own continuous block of memory, even when the physical memory is shared, smaller, and non-contiguous. Each program gets its own address space, and the operating system maps the addresses it actually uses onto physical pages, so two programs can both believe they are at address 4000 without interfering. When a program touches a page that is not in memory the operation blocks and the operating system loads it from storage, which is paging and is the mechanism behind the freeze when memory has run out. The illusion works because most code touches its own memory in a localised way, so the scheme is affordable, and a separate address space is also what lets the operating system keep programs isolated from each other."],"tl":"virtual memory","bis":"virtual memory"},{"id":"tec-computers-000004","topic":"technology-computers","intent":"definition","concept":"firmware","keywords":["firmware","bios","uefi","boot","bootloader","post","software","stored","volatile","memory","device"],"patterns":["what is firmware","firmware","explain firmware","definition of firmware","firmware meaning","what is bios","what is uefi","how does a computer boot","ano ang firmware","unsa ang firmware"],"answer":"Firmware is software stored in non-volatile memory on the device itself and running before any operating system is involved, and its job is to bring the hardware to a state where it can accept an operating system. On a desktop the familiar example is the BIOS or its modern replacement UEFI, which initialises the memory, detects the connected devices, runs a power-on self test, reads the boot order and then loads the operating system from disk or network. On a phone, a router or a graphics card the same role is played by lower-level firmware that initialises that specific component, and on an embedded board it is often the only software present at all. The reason the replacement happened is that BIOS stored its configuration in a small chip with a limited space and a slow 16-bit interface, so large disks, fast boot and graphical menus were not practical, while UEFI loads a program from a much larger partition and is written in ordinary languages. Getting stuck in the firmware setup screen is the most common reason a machine that used to boot suddenly does not.","variations":["On a desktop the familiar example is the BIOS or its modern replacement UEFI, which initialises the memory, detects the connected devices, runs a power-on self test, reads the boot order and then loads the operating system from disk or network. Firmware is software stored in non-volatile memory on the device itself and running before any operating system is involved, and its job is to bring the hardware to a state where it can accept an operating system. On a phone, a router or a graphics card the same role is played by lower-level firmware that initialises that specific component, and on an embedded board it is often the only software present at all. The reason the replacement happened is that BIOS stored its configuration in a small chip with a limited space and a slow 16-bit interface, so large disks, fast boot and graphical menus were not practical, while UEFI loads a program from a much larger partition and is written in ordinary languages. Getting stuck in the firmware setup screen is the most common reason a machine that used to boot suddenly does not.","On a phone, a router or a graphics card the same role is played by lower-level firmware that initialises that specific component, and on an embedded board it is often the only software present at all. Firmware is software stored in non-volatile memory on the device itself and running before any operating system is involved, and its job is to bring the hardware to a state where it can accept an operating system. On a desktop the familiar example is the BIOS or its modern replacement UEFI, which initialises the memory, detects the connected devices, runs a power-on self test, reads the boot order and then loads the operating system from disk or network. The reason the replacement happened is that BIOS stored its configuration in a small chip with a limited space and a slow 16-bit interface, so large disks, fast boot and graphical menus were not practical, while UEFI loads a program from a much larger partition and is written in ordinary languages. Getting stuck in the firmware setup screen is the most common reason a machine that used to boot suddenly does not."],"tl":"firmware","bis":"firmware"}],"lang":"english","group":"technology","sub":"computers","title":"Computers","groupTitle":"Technology","__file":"technology/computers.json"},
  {"topic":"technology-cybersecurity","entries":[{"id":"tec-cybersecurity-000001","topic":"technology-cybersecurity","intent":"definition","concept":"phishing","keywords":["phishing","scam","email","password","link","impersonate","message","impersonates","something","trusted","person"],"patterns":["what is phishing","phishing","explain phishing","definition of phishing","phishing meaning","how do i avoid phishing","why did my password leak from phishing","ano ang phishing","unsa ang phishing"],"answer":"Phishing is a message that impersonates something trusted to get a person to reveal a password or click a link. It works because it attacks the person rather than the software, which is why a strong password alone does not help. The reliable defence is to check the actual sender and the actual destination rather than what the message claims, and to use a password manager that will not autofill on the wrong domain.","variations":["It works because it attacks the person rather than the software, which is why a strong password alone does not help. Phishing is a message that impersonates something trusted to get a person to reveal a password or click a link. The reliable defence is to check the actual sender and the actual destination rather than what the message claims, and to use a password manager that will not autofill on the wrong domain.","The reliable defence is to check the actual sender and the actual destination rather than what the message claims, and to use a password manager that will not autofill on the wrong domain. Phishing is a message that impersonates something trusted to get a person to reveal a password or click a link. It works because it attacks the person rather than the software, which is why a strong password alone does not help."],"tl":"phishing","bis":"phishing"},{"id":"tec-cybersecurity-000002","topic":"technology-cybersecurity","intent":"definition","concept":"malware","keywords":["malware","virus","worm","trojan","spyware","ransomware","software","written","something","intend"],"patterns":["what is malware","malware","explain malware","definition of malware","malware meaning","difference between virus and worm","how do i avoid malware","ano ang malware","unsa ang malware"],"answer":"Malware is any software written to do something the user did not intend, and the categories differ by what they do rather than how they spread. Viruses attach to files, worms spread on their own, trojans look legitimate, ransomware encrypts and demands payment, and spyware quietly records. The defence is layered, and the single most effective layer is not running what you did not deliberately install.","variations":["Viruses attach to files, worms spread on their own, trojans look legitimate, ransomware encrypts and demands payment, and spyware quietly records. Malware is any software written to do something the user did not intend, and the categories differ by what they do rather than how they spread. The defence is layered, and the single most effective layer is not running what you did not deliberately install.","The defence is layered, and the single most effective layer is not running what you did not deliberately install. Malware is any software written to do something the user did not intend, and the categories differ by what they do rather than how they spread. Viruses attach to files, worms spread on their own, trojans look legitimate, ransomware encrypts and demands payment, and spyware quietly records."],"tl":"malware","bis":"malware"},{"id":"tec-cybersecurity-000003","topic":"technology-cybersecurity","intent":"definition","concept":"sql injection","keywords":["sql injection","injection","parameterised","query","escape","database","defect","pasted"],"patterns":["what is sql injection","sql injection","explain sql injection","definition of sql injection","sql injection meaning","how do i prevent sql injection","what is a parameterised query","ano ang sql injection","unsa ang sql injection"],"answer":"SQL injection is a defect where input from a user is pasted into a database query instead of being passed as data, so the input can change the meaning of the statement. The fix is not escaping, which is easy to get subtly wrong, but parameterised queries, where the database treats the input as a value no matter what it contains. It is still common because string-concatenated SQL is quick to write and looks correct.","variations":["The fix is not escaping, which is easy to get subtly wrong, but parameterised queries, where the database treats the input as a value no matter what it contains. SQL injection is a defect where input from a user is pasted into a database query instead of being passed as data, so the input can change the meaning of the statement. It is still common because string-concatenated SQL is quick to write and looks correct.","It is still common because string-concatenated SQL is quick to write and looks correct. SQL injection is a defect where input from a user is pasted into a database query instead of being passed as data, so the input can change the meaning of the statement. The fix is not escaping, which is easy to get subtly wrong, but parameterised queries, where the database treats the input as a value no matter what it contains."],"tl":"sql injection","bis":"sql injection"},{"id":"tec-cybersecurity-000004","topic":"technology-cybersecurity","intent":"definition","concept":"xss","keywords":["xss","encoding","csp","injection","markup","cross","site","scripting","defect"],"patterns":["what is xss","xss","explain xss","definition of xss","xss meaning","how do i prevent xss","what is a content security policy","ano ang xss","unsa ang xss"],"answer":"Cross-site scripting is a defect where user input is inserted into a page as markup rather than as text, so the browser runs it. The standard defence is output encoding matched to context, since escaping for HTML is not the same as escaping for a script block or an attribute. A content security policy is the second layer, because it limits what a page is allowed to execute even if something slips through.","variations":["The standard defence is output encoding matched to context, since escaping for HTML is not the same as escaping for a script block or an attribute. Cross-site scripting is a defect where user input is inserted into a page as markup rather than as text, so the browser runs it. A content security policy is the second layer, because it limits what a page is allowed to execute even if something slips through.","A content security policy is the second layer, because it limits what a page is allowed to execute even if something slips through. Cross-site scripting is a defect where user input is inserted into a page as markup rather than as text, so the browser runs it. The standard defence is output encoding matched to context, since escaping for HTML is not the same as escaping for a script block or an attribute."],"tl":"xss","bis":"xss"},{"id":"tec-cybersecurity-000005","topic":"technology-cybersecurity","intent":"definition","concept":"two factor authentication","keywords":["two factor authentication","2fa","mfa","authenticator","otp","security","factor","authentication","requires","second","piece","evidence"],"patterns":["what is two factor authentication","two factor authentication","explain two factor authentication","definition of two factor authentication","two factor authentication meaning","what is 2fa","is sms two factor secure","what is an authenticator app","ano ang two factor authentication","unsa ang two factor authentication"],"answer":"Two-factor authentication requires a second piece of evidence beyond the password, and the types are not equal in strength. Something you know is the password, something you have is a code from your phone, and something you are is a fingerprint. The second is much stronger than the first because the phone cannot be phished into revealing it, which is why a rotating app code beats an SMS code.","variations":["Something you know is the password, something you have is a code from your phone, and something you are is a fingerprint. Two-factor authentication requires a second piece of evidence beyond the password, and the types are not equal in strength. The second is much stronger than the first because the phone cannot be phished into revealing it, which is why a rotating app code beats an SMS code.","The second is much stronger than the first because the phone cannot be phished into revealing it, which is why a rotating app code beats an SMS code. Two-factor authentication requires a second piece of evidence beyond the password, and the types are not equal in strength. Something you know is the password, something you have is a code from your phone, and something you are is a fingerprint."],"tl":"two factor authentication","bis":"two factor authentication"},{"id":"tec-cybersecurity-000006","topic":"technology-cybersecurity","intent":"definition","concept":"hashing","keywords":["hashing","hash","salt","bcrypt","argon2","irreversible","turns","fixed","length","fingerprint"],"patterns":["what is hashing","hashing","explain hashing","definition of hashing","hashing meaning","why are passwords hashed","what is salting","why is bcrypt slow","ano ang hashing","unsa ang hashing"],"answer":"A hash turns input into a fixed-length fingerprint, and unlike encryption it cannot be reversed, which is exactly why passwords are stored as hashes. Salting means giving every user a different random value before hashing, so two people with the same password do not produce the same stored value and precomputed tables become useless. Deliberately slow algorithms such as bcrypt or argon2 are used for passwords, because speed helps an attacker and not a defender.","variations":["Salting means giving every user a different random value before hashing, so two people with the same password do not produce the same stored value and precomputed tables become useless. A hash turns input into a fixed-length fingerprint, and unlike encryption it cannot be reversed, which is exactly why passwords are stored as hashes. Deliberately slow algorithms such as bcrypt or argon2 are used for passwords, because speed helps an attacker and not a defender.","Deliberately slow algorithms such as bcrypt or argon2 are used for passwords, because speed helps an attacker and not a defender. A hash turns input into a fixed-length fingerprint, and unlike encryption it cannot be reversed, which is exactly why passwords are stored as hashes. Salting means giving every user a different random value before hashing, so two people with the same password do not produce the same stored value and precomputed tables become useless."],"tl":"hashing","bis":"hashing"},{"id":"tec-cybersecurity-000007","topic":"technology-cybersecurity","intent":"definition","concept":"password strength","keywords":["password strength","password","passphrase","length","reuse","breach","strength","mostly","rather","mixture"],"patterns":["what is password strength","password strength","explain password strength","definition of password strength","password strength meaning","how long should a password be","is length or complexity better","why does password reuse matter","ano ang password strength","unsa ang password strength"],"answer":"Password strength is mostly about length rather than the mixture of character types, and the current guidance is to prefer several unrelated words over a short scrambled string. A long passphrase survives a brute-force attempt that a complex short password does not, because length multiplies the work. Reuse is the actual risk, since one breached site is enough to attack every account sharing that password, which is why a unique password per site matters more than a strong one reused.","variations":["A long passphrase survives a brute-force attempt that a complex short password does not, because length multiplies the work. Password strength is mostly about length rather than the mixture of character types, and the current guidance is to prefer several unrelated words over a short scrambled string. Reuse is the actual risk, since one breached site is enough to attack every account sharing that password, which is why a unique password per site matters more than a strong one reused.","Reuse is the actual risk, since one breached site is enough to attack every account sharing that password, which is why a unique password per site matters more than a strong one reused. Password strength is mostly about length rather than the mixture of character types, and the current guidance is to prefer several unrelated words over a short scrambled string. A long passphrase survives a brute-force attempt that a complex short password does not, because length multiplies the work."],"tl":"password strength","bis":"password strength"},{"id":"tec-cybersecurity-000008","topic":"technology-cybersecurity","intent":"howto","concept":"password","keywords":["password","security","hack","2fa","mfa","account","phishing","long","beats","complicated","short","length"],"patterns":["what is password","password","explain password","definition of password","password meaning","how to make a strong password","how to secure my account","what is two factor authentication"],"answer":"A long password beats a complicated short one - length matters more than symbols, and a four-word phrase is both stronger and easier to remember. Use a different one everywhere, because breaches are reused. Turn on two-factor authentication wherever it is offered, and prefer an authenticator app or a hardware key over SMS. Never act on an unexpected link: go to the site yourself instead.","variations":["Use a different one everywhere, because breaches are reused. A long password beats a complicated short one - length matters more than symbols, and a four-word phrase is both stronger and easier to remember. Turn on two-factor authentication wherever it is offered, and prefer an authenticator app or a hardware key over SMS. Never act on an unexpected link: go to the site yourself instead.","Turn on two-factor authentication wherever it is offered, and prefer an authenticator app or a hardware key over SMS. A long password beats a complicated short one - length matters more than symbols, and a four-word phrase is both stronger and easier to remember. Use a different one everywhere, because breaches are reused. Never act on an unexpected link: go to the site yourself instead."]}],"lang":"english","group":"technology","sub":"cybersecurity","title":"Cybersecurity","groupTitle":"Technology","__file":"technology/cybersecurity.json"},
  {"topic":"technology-data","entries":[{"id":"tec-data-000001","topic":"technology-data","intent":"definition","concept":"why is my phone slow","keywords":["why is my phone slow","storage","memory","space","apps","background","update","battery","processor","hiktay","phone","gets","slow","reasons","amount","work"],"patterns":["what is why is my phone slow","why is my phone slow","explain why is my phone slow","definition of why is my phone slow","why is my phone slow meaning","how to speed up my phone","phone running slow","ano ang dahan dahan","unsa ang hiktay"],"answer":"A phone gets slow for reasons that are usually about the amount of work being asked of limited memory, and the first thing to check is how full the storage is, because a nearly full drive is a genuinely common cause and is free to fix. A phone keeps a running list of everything running, including things assumed to be idle, and a small amount of unused memory is what it needs to do ordinary things like opening a list. Background activity is the other half: apps left switched on for messages, location and synchronisation keep the processor awake, and on an older device that is the difference between smooth and unusable. Updates help more than people expect, because manufacturers fix performance problems in them, and the opposite advice, to install a cleaner app, is mostly a way to create a second problem. The honest summary is that a phone from several years ago will not be made fast by anything except less running at once.","variations":["A phone keeps a running list of everything running, including things assumed to be idle, and a small amount of unused memory is what it needs to do ordinary things like opening a list. A phone gets slow for reasons that are usually about the amount of work being asked of limited memory, and the first thing to check is how full the storage is, because a nearly full drive is a genuinely common cause and is free to fix. Background activity is the other half: apps left switched on for messages, location and synchronisation keep the processor awake, and on an older device that is the difference between smooth and unusable. Updates help more than people expect, because manufacturers fix performance problems in them, and the opposite advice, to install a cleaner app, is mostly a way to create a second problem. The honest summary is that a phone from several years ago will not be made fast by anything except less running at once.","Background activity is the other half: apps left switched on for messages, location and synchronisation keep the processor awake, and on an older device that is the difference between smooth and unusable. A phone gets slow for reasons that are usually about the amount of work being asked of limited memory, and the first thing to check is how full the storage is, because a nearly full drive is a genuinely common cause and is free to fix. A phone keeps a running list of everything running, including things assumed to be idle, and a small amount of unused memory is what it needs to do ordinary things like opening a list. Updates help more than people expect, because manufacturers fix performance problems in them, and the opposite advice, to install a cleaner app, is mostly a way to create a second problem. The honest summary is that a phone from several years ago will not be made fast by anything except less running at once."],"tl":"dahan dahan","bis":"hiktay"},{"id":"tec-data-000002","topic":"technology-data","intent":"definition","concept":"backup","keywords":["backup","data","copy","cloud","restore","photos","phone","lost","sync","second","somewhere","else"],"patterns":["what is backup","backup","explain backup","definition of backup","backup meaning","how do i back up my data","what is a backup","how do i back up my phone","ano ang backup","unsa ang backup"],"answer":"A backup is a second copy of your data somewhere else, and the one detail that makes it a backup rather than a copy is that it is somewhere else. Data that is on one device and also on the machine it is plugged into has not been protected from the failure that actually destroys it, which is usually theft, a drop or a flood, and those take the plugged-in machine too. What matters most to protect is rarely the device and is the account: a phone can be replaced and a library of photographs cannot. Automatic backup to a cloud account is the default for most phones and solves most of the problem for someone who does not think about it, and the check worth doing is whether it is actually running rather than whether it has been enabled. The old advice about checking a backup by restoring it remains the only way to be certain, since a backup that fails silently looks exactly like one that is working.","variations":["Data that is on one device and also on the machine it is plugged into has not been protected from the failure that actually destroys it, which is usually theft, a drop or a flood, and those take the plugged-in machine too. A backup is a second copy of your data somewhere else, and the one detail that makes it a backup rather than a copy is that it is somewhere else. What matters most to protect is rarely the device and is the account: a phone can be replaced and a library of photographs cannot. Automatic backup to a cloud account is the default for most phones and solves most of the problem for someone who does not think about it, and the check worth doing is whether it is actually running rather than whether it has been enabled. The old advice about checking a backup by restoring it remains the only way to be certain, since a backup that fails silently looks exactly like one that is working.","What matters most to protect is rarely the device and is the account: a phone can be replaced and a library of photographs cannot. A backup is a second copy of your data somewhere else, and the one detail that makes it a backup rather than a copy is that it is somewhere else. Data that is on one device and also on the machine it is plugged into has not been protected from the failure that actually destroys it, which is usually theft, a drop or a flood, and those take the plugged-in machine too. Automatic backup to a cloud account is the default for most phones and solves most of the problem for someone who does not think about it, and the check worth doing is whether it is actually running rather than whether it has been enabled. The old advice about checking a backup by restoring it remains the only way to be certain, since a backup that fails silently looks exactly like one that is working."],"tl":"backup","bis":"backup"}],"lang":"english","group":"technology","sub":"data","title":"Data","groupTitle":"Technology","__file":"technology/data.json"},
  {"topic":"technology-hardware","entries":[{"id":"tec-hardware-000001","topic":"technology-hardware","intent":"definition","concept":"cpu","keywords":["cpu","processor","core","ghz","clock","throttle","executes","instructions","speed","gigahertz","measure"],"patterns":["what is cpu","cpu","explain cpu","definition of cpu","cpu meaning","what is a cpu","what does ghz mean","what is cpu throttling","ano ang cpu","unsa ang cpu"],"answer":"The CPU executes instructions, and the clock speed in gigahertz is one measure of how many it can retire per second, though architecture matters at least as much. Modern CPUs have multiple cores, so total throughput is cores times per-core speed in the best case. The practical limit is usually thermal rather than electronic, which is why a faster generation can throttle itself back under sustained load.","variations":["Modern CPUs have multiple cores, so total throughput is cores times per-core speed in the best case. The CPU executes instructions, and the clock speed in gigahertz is one measure of how many it can retire per second, though architecture matters at least as much. The practical limit is usually thermal rather than electronic, which is why a faster generation can throttle itself back under sustained load.","The practical limit is usually thermal rather than electronic, which is why a faster generation can throttle itself back under sustained load. The CPU executes instructions, and the clock speed in gigahertz is one measure of how many it can retire per second, though architecture matters at least as much. Modern CPUs have multiple cores, so total throughput is cores times per-core speed in the best case."],"tl":"cpu","bis":"cpu"},{"id":"tec-hardware-000002","topic":"technology-hardware","intent":"definition","concept":"ram","keywords":["ram","memory","swap","volatile","megabyte","gigabyte","working","computer","uses","right","fast"],"patterns":["what is ram","ram","explain ram","definition of ram","ram meaning","why is my computer slow","what is swapping","ano ang ram","unsa ang ram"],"answer":"RAM is the working memory a computer uses right now, and it is fast but volatile, so everything in it is lost when power goes. Its size decides what can be open at once rather than how fast a program runs, which is why too little RAM shows up as swapping to disk and feels like a hard freeze. Disk is not a substitute: it is orders of magnitude slower, so anything pushed out of RAM is felt immediately.","variations":["Its size decides what can be open at once rather than how fast a program runs, which is why too little RAM shows up as swapping to disk and feels like a hard freeze. RAM is the working memory a computer uses right now, and it is fast but volatile, so everything in it is lost when power goes. Disk is not a substitute: it is orders of magnitude slower, so anything pushed out of RAM is felt immediately.","Disk is not a substitute: it is orders of magnitude slower, so anything pushed out of RAM is felt immediately. RAM is the working memory a computer uses right now, and it is fast but volatile, so everything in it is lost when power goes. Its size decides what can be open at once rather than how fast a program runs, which is why too little RAM shows up as swapping to disk and feels like a hard freeze."],"tl":"ram","bis":"ram"},{"id":"tec-hardware-000003","topic":"technology-hardware","intent":"definition","concept":"ssd","keywords":["ssd","flash","speed","wear","storage","moving","makes","dramatically","faster"],"patterns":["what is ssd","ssd","explain ssd","definition of ssd","ssd meaning","difference between ssd and hdd","why is an ssd faster","do ssds wear out","ano ang ssd","unsa ang ssd"],"answer":"An SSD is flash storage with no moving parts, which makes it dramatically faster and more shock-resistant than a spinning disk. The speed comes from there being no seek time, since data is addressed directly rather than located on a platter. It also changes how you maintain it, because flash wears out after a limited number of writes even though nothing shows that in a health readout.","variations":["The speed comes from there being no seek time, since data is addressed directly rather than located on a platter. An SSD is flash storage with no moving parts, which makes it dramatically faster and more shock-resistant than a spinning disk. It also changes how you maintain it, because flash wears out after a limited number of writes even though nothing shows that in a health readout.","It also changes how you maintain it, because flash wears out after a limited number of writes even though nothing shows that in a health readout. An SSD is flash storage with no moving parts, which makes it dramatically faster and more shock-resistant than a spinning disk. The speed comes from there being no seek time, since data is addressed directly rather than located on a platter."],"tl":"ssd","bis":"ssd"},{"id":"tec-hardware-000004","topic":"technology-hardware","intent":"definition","concept":"binary","keywords":["binary","bit","byte","counting","symbols","normally","every","piece"],"patterns":["what is binary","binary","explain binary","definition of binary","binary meaning","how many bits in a byte","why do computers use binary","ano ang binary","unsa ang binary"],"answer":"Binary is counting with two symbols, normally 0 and 1, and every piece of data in a computer is ultimately a pattern of them. One digit is a bit, and eight bits make a byte, which is enough to represent one character. The reason computers are built this way is that a two-state physical system is far more reliable and cheaper to read than one with many distinguishable states.","variations":["One digit is a bit, and eight bits make a byte, which is enough to represent one character. Binary is counting with two symbols, normally 0 and 1, and every piece of data in a computer is ultimately a pattern of them. The reason computers are built this way is that a two-state physical system is far more reliable and cheaper to read than one with many distinguishable states.","The reason computers are built this way is that a two-state physical system is far more reliable and cheaper to read than one with many distinguishable states. Binary is counting with two symbols, normally 0 and 1, and every piece of data in a computer is ultimately a pattern of them. One digit is a bit, and eight bits make a byte, which is enough to represent one character."],"tl":"binary","bis":"binary"},{"id":"tec-hardware-000005","topic":"technology-hardware","intent":"definition","concept":"cache","keywords":["cache","locality","memory","speed","hit","small","fast","store","placed","front"],"patterns":["what is cache","cache","explain cache","definition of cache","cache meaning","what is a cache","what is cpu cache","what is locality","ano ang cache","unsa ang cache"],"answer":"A cache is a small fast store placed in front of something large and slow, and the design problem is deciding what to keep. Caches work on locality, so the assumption is that recent access predicts future access, which holds well in practice. There are several at different speeds, from the CPU core outward, and the more aggressive the placement the smaller and faster it has to be.","variations":["Caches work on locality, so the assumption is that recent access predicts future access, which holds well in practice. A cache is a small fast store placed in front of something large and slow, and the design problem is deciding what to keep. There are several at different speeds, from the CPU core outward, and the more aggressive the placement the smaller and faster it has to be.","There are several at different speeds, from the CPU core outward, and the more aggressive the placement the smaller and faster it has to be. A cache is a small fast store placed in front of something large and slow, and the design problem is deciding what to keep. Caches work on locality, so the assumption is that recent access predicts future access, which holds well in practice."],"tl":"cache","bis":"cache"}],"lang":"english","group":"technology","sub":"hardware","title":"Hardware","groupTitle":"Technology","__file":"technology/hardware.json"},
  {"topic":"technology-internet","entries":[{"id":"tec-internet-000001","topic":"technology-internet","intent":"definition","concept":"internet","keywords":["internet","packet","router","web","decentralized","fibre","global","network","networks","word","matters"],"patterns":["what is internet","internet","explain internet","definition of internet","internet meaning","what is the internet","is the internet the same as the web","how does the internet work","ano ang internet","unsa ang internet"],"answer":"The internet is a global network of networks, and the word network is the part that matters: no single organisation runs it, and it works because independently owned networks agree to pass each other's traffic. A packet is the unit of transfer, split at the source, routed independently through intermediate routers, and reassembled at the destination, so a conversation can survive one broken link by taking a different path. The physical layer is cable, fibre optic and radio, and the addressing that makes it usable is the IP address, with domain names layered on top through DNS because people remember words and routers need numbers. What the internet is not is the web: the web is one service running on it, alongside email, video calling and file transfer, and they are routinely confused. Its governance is genuinely decentralised, with the naming function run by a multi-party arrangement rather than a single government, which is one reason it grew without a central plan.","variations":["A packet is the unit of transfer, split at the source, routed independently through intermediate routers, and reassembled at the destination, so a conversation can survive one broken link by taking a different path. The internet is a global network of networks, and the word network is the part that matters: no single organisation runs it, and it works because independently owned networks agree to pass each other's traffic. The physical layer is cable, fibre optic and radio, and the addressing that makes it usable is the IP address, with domain names layered on top through DNS because people remember words and routers need numbers. What the internet is not is the web: the web is one service running on it, alongside email, video calling and file transfer, and they are routinely confused. Its governance is genuinely decentralised, with the naming function run by a multi-party arrangement rather than a single government, which is one reason it grew without a central plan.","The physical layer is cable, fibre optic and radio, and the addressing that makes it usable is the IP address, with domain names layered on top through DNS because people remember words and routers need numbers. The internet is a global network of networks, and the word network is the part that matters: no single organisation runs it, and it works because independently owned networks agree to pass each other's traffic. A packet is the unit of transfer, split at the source, routed independently through intermediate routers, and reassembled at the destination, so a conversation can survive one broken link by taking a different path. What the internet is not is the web: the web is one service running on it, alongside email, video calling and file transfer, and they are routinely confused. Its governance is genuinely decentralised, with the naming function run by a multi-party arrangement rather than a single government, which is one reason it grew without a central plan."],"tl":"internet","bis":"internet"},{"id":"tec-internet-000002","topic":"technology-internet","intent":"howto","concept":"how the internet works","keywords":["how the internet works","cdn","parsing","render","learning","internet","actually","works","means","following"],"patterns":["what is how the internet works","how the internet works","explain how the internet works","definition of how the internet works","how the internet works meaning","how does the internet work step by step","what happens when i open a website","what is a dns lookup","ano ang how the internet works","unsa ang paagi sa internet"],"answer":"Learning how the internet actually works means following one request from typed name to displayed page, and the steps are the same for every site you have ever opened. The browser first checks its own cache, then asks a DNS resolver to turn a domain name into an IP address, and the resolver answers from its cache or works outward through the hierarchy until an authoritative server is reached. A connection is then opened to that address using TCP, which agrees on sequence numbers and retransmits anything lost, or QUIC, which does the same job over UDP with encryption built in. The browser sends a request, the server responds, and a middle layer such as a content delivery network may answer from a location nearer than the origin, which is why a popular site can be fast from Manila despite originating on another continent. The page is then built as the browser parses the markup, requests the stylesheet and scripts, lays out the boxes, paints, and finally runs the script that fetches whatever it needs next.","variations":["The browser first checks its own cache, then asks a DNS resolver to turn a domain name into an IP address, and the resolver answers from its cache or works outward through the hierarchy until an authoritative server is reached. Learning how the internet actually works means following one request from typed name to displayed page, and the steps are the same for every site you have ever opened. A connection is then opened to that address using TCP, which agrees on sequence numbers and retransmits anything lost, or QUIC, which does the same job over UDP with encryption built in. The browser sends a request, the server responds, and a middle layer such as a content delivery network may answer from a location nearer than the origin, which is why a popular site can be fast from Manila despite originating on another continent. The page is then built as the browser parses the markup, requests the stylesheet and scripts, lays out the boxes, paints, and finally runs the script that fetches whatever it needs next.","A connection is then opened to that address using TCP, which agrees on sequence numbers and retransmits anything lost, or QUIC, which does the same job over UDP with encryption built in. Learning how the internet actually works means following one request from typed name to displayed page, and the steps are the same for every site you have ever opened. The browser first checks its own cache, then asks a DNS resolver to turn a domain name into an IP address, and the resolver answers from its cache or works outward through the hierarchy until an authoritative server is reached. The browser sends a request, the server responds, and a middle layer such as a content delivery network may answer from a location nearer than the origin, which is why a popular site can be fast from Manila despite originating on another continent. The page is then built as the browser parses the markup, requests the stylesheet and scripts, lays out the boxes, paints, and finally runs the script that fetches whatever it needs next."],"tl":"how the internet works","bis":"paagi sa internet"},{"id":"tec-internet-000003","topic":"technology-internet","intent":"definition","concept":"bandwidth","keywords":["bandwidth","megabit","megabyte","throughput","latency","capacity","data","pass","connection","second","measured"],"patterns":["what is bandwidth","bandwidth","explain bandwidth","definition of bandwidth","bandwidth meaning","is bandwidth the same as internet speed","what is the difference between mb and mbps","ano ang bandwidth","unsa ang bandwidth"],"answer":"Bandwidth is how much data can pass over a connection in a given second, measured in bits per second, and it is the maximum the link can carry rather than the speed of anything on it. The two are different quantities, which is why a fast connection can still make a page feel slow: the data rate is the data actually moving, and the other half of the capacity may simply be idle. A megabit is a million bits, and this is the single most common unit confusion in the field, because service providers advertise megabits per second while downloads show megabytes per second. There are eight bits to a byte, so a 100 megabit connection moves at most about 12.5 megabytes per second, and the ceiling is reached long before the connection is saturated because overhead takes some of the capacity. The other thing that determines the number is latency, the delay before the first byte arrives: a connection with huge bandwidth across a great distance can still feel unusable for anything that involves waiting for a reply, and that is the problem satellite internet and long-distance calls both hit.","variations":["The two are different quantities, which is why a fast connection can still make a page feel slow: the data rate is the data actually moving, and the other half of the capacity may simply be idle. Bandwidth is how much data can pass over a connection in a given second, measured in bits per second, and it is the maximum the link can carry rather than the speed of anything on it. A megabit is a million bits, and this is the single most common unit confusion in the field, because service providers advertise megabits per second while downloads show megabytes per second. 5 megabytes per second, and the ceiling is reached long before the connection is saturated because overhead takes some of the capacity. The other thing that determines the number is latency, the delay before the first byte arrives: a connection with huge bandwidth across a great distance can still feel unusable for anything that involves waiting for a reply, and that is the problem satellite internet and long-distance calls both hit.","A megabit is a million bits, and this is the single most common unit confusion in the field, because service providers advertise megabits per second while downloads show megabytes per second. Bandwidth is how much data can pass over a connection in a given second, measured in bits per second, and it is the maximum the link can carry rather than the speed of anything on it. The two are different quantities, which is why a fast connection can still make a page feel slow: the data rate is the data actually moving, and the other half of the capacity may simply be idle. 5 megabytes per second, and the ceiling is reached long before the connection is saturated because overhead takes some of the capacity. The other thing that determines the number is latency, the delay before the first byte arrives: a connection with huge bandwidth across a great distance can still feel unusable for anything that involves waiting for a reply, and that is the problem satellite internet and long-distance calls both hit."],"tl":"bandwidth","bis":"bandwidth"},{"id":"tec-internet-000004","topic":"technology-internet","intent":"definition","concept":"url","keywords":["url","scheme","domain","path","fragment","dns","resource","uniform","locator","address","names","internet"],"patterns":["what is url","url","explain url","definition of url","url meaning","what is a url","what does url stand for","difference between url and uri","ano ang url","unsa ang url"],"answer":"A uniform resource locator is the address that names a resource on the internet, and its structure explains all of it: a scheme, a host, a port, a path, a query and a fragment, separated in that order. The scheme is the protocol, http or https, and the host is a domain name that DNS has to resolve before anything else can happen. The path names a resource on that server, the query string carries parameters as key and value pairs joined by ampersands, and the fragment after a hash is handled entirely inside the browser and never sent to the server at all, which is why a single page application can change it without a request. A uniform resource locator and a uniform resource identifier are different things: the first must include how to reach it and the second is a name that may not, and the shorthand of a web address for the first is why the second term exists. The parts that change between one link and the next are exactly the path, the query and the fragment, since the scheme and host are usually the same within a site.","variations":["The scheme is the protocol, http or https, and the host is a domain name that DNS has to resolve before anything else can happen. A uniform resource locator is the address that names a resource on the internet, and its structure explains all of it: a scheme, a host, a port, a path, a query and a fragment, separated in that order. The path names a resource on that server, the query string carries parameters as key and value pairs joined by ampersands, and the fragment after a hash is handled entirely inside the browser and never sent to the server at all, which is why a single page application can change it without a request. A uniform resource locator and a uniform resource identifier are different things: the first must include how to reach it and the second is a name that may not, and the shorthand of a web address for the first is why the second term exists. The parts that change between one link and the next are exactly the path, the query and the fragment, since the scheme and host are usually the same within a site.","The path names a resource on that server, the query string carries parameters as key and value pairs joined by ampersands, and the fragment after a hash is handled entirely inside the browser and never sent to the server at all, which is why a single page application can change it without a request. A uniform resource locator is the address that names a resource on the internet, and its structure explains all of it: a scheme, a host, a port, a path, a query and a fragment, separated in that order. The scheme is the protocol, http or https, and the host is a domain name that DNS has to resolve before anything else can happen. A uniform resource locator and a uniform resource identifier are different things: the first must include how to reach it and the second is a name that may not, and the shorthand of a web address for the first is why the second term exists. The parts that change between one link and the next are exactly the path, the query and the fragment, since the scheme and host are usually the same within a site."],"tl":"url","bis":"url"}],"lang":"english","group":"technology","sub":"internet","title":"Internet","groupTitle":"Technology","__file":"technology/internet.json"},
  {"topic":"technology-networking","entries":[{"id":"tec-networking-000001","topic":"technology-networking","intent":"definition","concept":"ip address","keywords":["ip address","ipv4","ipv6","address","subnet","private","public","identifies","device","network","uses"],"patterns":["what is ip address","ip address","explain ip address","definition of ip address","ip address meaning","what is an ip address","difference between ipv4 and ipv6","what is a private ip","ano ang ip address","unsa ang ip address"],"answer":"An IP address identifies a device on a network, and IPv4 uses four numbers from 0 to 255 while IPv6 uses a far larger space. The important part is the split into network and host portions, because that split is what a router uses to decide whether to forward a packet or answer it itself. Private ranges are reserved for internal networks and are not routable publicly, which is why a home router always has one.","variations":["The important part is the split into network and host portions, because that split is what a router uses to decide whether to forward a packet or answer it itself. An IP address identifies a device on a network, and IPv4 uses four numbers from 0 to 255 while IPv6 uses a far larger space. Private ranges are reserved for internal networks and are not routable publicly, which is why a home router always has one.","Private ranges are reserved for internal networks and are not routable publicly, which is why a home router always has one. An IP address identifies a device on a network, and IPv4 uses four numbers from 0 to 255 while IPv6 uses a far larger space. The important part is the split into network and host portions, because that split is what a router uses to decide whether to forward a packet or answer it itself."],"tl":"ip address","bis":"ip address"},{"id":"tec-networking-000002","topic":"technology-networking","intent":"definition","concept":"tcp","keywords":["tcp","handshake","reliable","ordered","retransmit","transport","protocol","delivers","every","byte","order"],"patterns":["what is tcp","tcp","explain tcp","definition of tcp","tcp meaning","why is tcp slower than udp","what is the three way handshake","ano ang tcp","unsa ang tcp"],"answer":"TCP is a transport protocol that delivers every byte in order and retransmits anything lost, which is what makes it reliable. It establishes a connection with a handshake before any data flows, and that setup cost is why it is slower than UDP where a late packet is worse than a lost one. HTTP, SSH and most database traffic run over it.","variations":["It establishes a connection with a handshake before any data flows, and that setup cost is why it is slower than UDP where a late packet is worse than a lost one. TCP is a transport protocol that delivers every byte in order and retransmits anything lost, which is what makes it reliable. HTTP, SSH and most database traffic run over it.","HTTP, SSH and most database traffic run over it. TCP is a transport protocol that delivers every byte in order and retransmits anything lost, which is what makes it reliable. It establishes a connection with a handshake before any data flows, and that setup cost is why it is slower than UDP where a late packet is worse than a lost one."],"tl":"tcp","bis":"tcp"},{"id":"tec-networking-000003","topic":"technology-networking","intent":"definition","concept":"udp","keywords":["udp","fast","connectionless","dns","live","sends","data","without","connecting","ordering","retransmitting"],"patterns":["what is udp","udp","explain udp","definition of udp","udp meaning","when to use udp instead of tcp","is udp reliable","ano ang udp","unsa ang udp"],"answer":"UDP sends data without connecting, ordering or retransmitting, which makes it fast and cheap but not reliable. That trade is correct for live things where a late packet is worthless, such as a video call, a game or a DNS lookup. A packet lost in a video call is invisible, while the same packet lost in a file transfer means a corrupt file.","variations":["That trade is correct for live things where a late packet is worthless, such as a video call, a game or a DNS lookup. UDP sends data without connecting, ordering or retransmitting, which makes it fast and cheap but not reliable. A packet lost in a video call is invisible, while the same packet lost in a file transfer means a corrupt file.","A packet lost in a video call is invisible, while the same packet lost in a file transfer means a corrupt file. UDP sends data without connecting, ordering or retransmitting, which makes it fast and cheap but not reliable. That trade is correct for live things where a late packet is worthless, such as a video call, a game or a DNS lookup."],"tl":"udp","bis":"udp"},{"id":"tec-networking-000004","topic":"technology-networking","intent":"definition","concept":"dns","keywords":["dns","domain","resolve","nameserver","mx","cname","translates","human","name","address","machine"],"patterns":["what is dns","dns","explain dns","definition of dns","dns meaning","how does dns work","what is an mx record","ano ang dns","unsa ang dns"],"answer":"DNS translates a human name like a domain into an IP address a machine can route to, which is why it is called the phonebook of the internet. The lookup is hierarchical, so a resolver asks a root, which points at a top-level domain server, which points at the authoritative one. Other record types matter too, as MX points at mail servers and CNAME aliases one name to another.","variations":["The lookup is hierarchical, so a resolver asks a root, which points at a top-level domain server, which points at the authoritative one. DNS translates a human name like a domain into an IP address a machine can route to, which is why it is called the phonebook of the internet. Other record types matter too, as MX points at mail servers and CNAME aliases one name to another.","Other record types matter too, as MX points at mail servers and CNAME aliases one name to another. DNS translates a human name like a domain into an IP address a machine can route to, which is why it is called the phonebook of the internet. The lookup is hierarchical, so a resolver asks a root, which points at a top-level domain server, which points at the authoritative one."],"tl":"dns","bis":"dns"},{"id":"tec-networking-000005","topic":"technology-networking","intent":"definition","concept":"router","keywords":["router","nat","gateway","wireless","forwarding","forwards","packets","different","networks","home"],"patterns":["what is router","router","explain router","definition of router","router meaning","what is a router","what does nat do","what is a home router","ano ang router","unsa ang router"],"answer":"A router forwards packets between different networks, and a home router also assigns addresses, runs the wireless access point, bridges to the wired ports, and usually runs NAT. NAT is the part people forget, and it is why a home network can share one public address among many devices by rewriting them all to look like the same machine.","variations":["NAT is the part people forget, and it is why a home network can share one public address among many devices by rewriting them all to look like the same machine. A router forwards packets between different networks, and a home router also assigns addresses, runs the wireless access point, bridges to the wired ports, and usually runs NAT."],"tl":"router","bis":"router"},{"id":"tec-networking-000006","topic":"technology-networking","intent":"definition","concept":"firewall","keywords":["firewall","filter","rule","stateful","block","enforces","connections","allowed","filtering"],"patterns":["what is firewall","firewall","explain firewall","definition of firewall","firewall meaning","what is a firewall","what is a stateful firewall","can a firewall stop all attacks","ano ang firewall","unsa ang firewall"],"answer":"A firewall enforces a rule set about which connections are allowed in or out, filtering by address, port and sometimes by inspecting the traffic. Stateful firewalls remember which connections were opened from inside and let the returning traffic through, which is why an unsolicited incoming connection is usually dropped. It is a perimeter control rather than a guarantee, because traffic that is allowed can still carry something harmful.","variations":["Stateful firewalls remember which connections were opened from inside and let the returning traffic through, which is why an unsolicited incoming connection is usually dropped. A firewall enforces a rule set about which connections are allowed in or out, filtering by address, port and sometimes by inspecting the traffic. It is a perimeter control rather than a guarantee, because traffic that is allowed can still carry something harmful.","It is a perimeter control rather than a guarantee, because traffic that is allowed can still carry something harmful. A firewall enforces a rule set about which connections are allowed in or out, filtering by address, port and sometimes by inspecting the traffic. Stateful firewalls remember which connections were opened from inside and let the returning traffic through, which is why an unsolicited incoming connection is usually dropped."],"tl":"firewall","bis":"firewall"},{"id":"tec-networking-000007","topic":"technology-networking","intent":"definition","concept":"latency","keywords":["latency","ping","delay","bandwidth","before","data","begins","arrive"],"patterns":["what is latency","latency","explain latency","definition of latency","latency meaning","difference between latency and bandwidth","what is a good ping","ano ang latency","unsa ang latency"],"answer":"Latency is the delay before data begins to arrive, and it is not the same as bandwidth, which is how much can arrive at once. A fast connection with high latency feels worse for interactive work than a slow one with low latency, because every round trip is felt. Physical distance sets a floor, since light cannot beat its own speed in glass.","variations":["A fast connection with high latency feels worse for interactive work than a slow one with low latency, because every round trip is felt. Latency is the delay before data begins to arrive, and it is not the same as bandwidth, which is how much can arrive at once. Physical distance sets a floor, since light cannot beat its own speed in glass.","Physical distance sets a floor, since light cannot beat its own speed in glass. Latency is the delay before data begins to arrive, and it is not the same as bandwidth, which is how much can arrive at once. A fast connection with high latency feels worse for interactive work than a slow one with low latency, because every round trip is felt."],"tl":"latency","bis":"latency"},{"id":"tec-networking-000008","topic":"technology-networking","intent":"definition","concept":"vpn","keywords":["vpn","tunnel","encrypted","privacy","remote","builds","points","traffic","appears"],"patterns":["what is vpn","vpn","explain vpn","definition of vpn","vpn meaning","what is a vpn","does a vpn make you anonymous","what is a vpn tunnel","ano ang vpn","unsa ang vpn"],"answer":"A VPN builds an encrypted tunnel between two points so that traffic appears to come from somewhere else, which hides it from the local network but not from the endpoints. A full VPN shifts trust from your internet provider to the VPN operator, which is a real trade rather than a pure gain. It does not make you anonymous, because the service you connect to can still see you.","variations":["A full VPN shifts trust from your internet provider to the VPN operator, which is a real trade rather than a pure gain. A VPN builds an encrypted tunnel between two points so that traffic appears to come from somewhere else, which hides it from the local network but not from the endpoints. It does not make you anonymous, because the service you connect to can still see you.","It does not make you anonymous, because the service you connect to can still see you. A VPN builds an encrypted tunnel between two points so that traffic appears to come from somewhere else, which hides it from the local network but not from the endpoints. A full VPN shifts trust from your internet provider to the VPN operator, which is a real trade rather than a pure gain."],"tl":"vpn","bis":"vpn"}],"lang":"english","group":"technology","sub":"networking","title":"Networking","groupTitle":"Technology","__file":"technology/networking.json"},
  {"topic":"technology-operating-systems","entries":[{"id":"tec-operating-systems-000001","topic":"technology-operating-systems","intent":"definition","concept":"operating system","keywords":["operating system","kernel","process","memory","filesystem","linux","operating","system","layer","hardware","programs","real"],"patterns":["what is operating system","operating system","explain operating system","definition of operating system","operating system meaning","what is an operating system","what does a kernel do","what is the difference between kernel and os","ano ang operating system","unsa ang operating system"],"answer":"An operating system is the layer between hardware and programs, and its real job is making one piece of hardware look like many usable resources. It schedules the CPU across running processes, manages memory between them, owns the filesystem, and provides the networking and graphics interfaces applications call instead of touching hardware. The kernel is the part running in the privileged mode, and everything else is a user process with far less authority.","variations":["It schedules the CPU across running processes, manages memory between them, owns the filesystem, and provides the networking and graphics interfaces applications call instead of touching hardware. An operating system is the layer between hardware and programs, and its real job is making one piece of hardware look like many usable resources. The kernel is the part running in the privileged mode, and everything else is a user process with far less authority.","The kernel is the part running in the privileged mode, and everything else is a user process with far less authority. An operating system is the layer between hardware and programs, and its real job is making one piece of hardware look like many usable resources. It schedules the CPU across running processes, manages memory between them, owns the filesystem, and provides the networking and graphics interfaces applications call instead of touching hardware."],"tl":"operating system","bis":"operating system"},{"id":"tec-operating-systems-000002","topic":"technology-operating-systems","intent":"definition","concept":"process","keywords":["process","thread","memory","isolation","deadlock","scheduling","running","program","address","space","operating"],"patterns":["what is process","process","explain process","definition of process","process meaning","what is a process","difference between a process and a thread","what is a deadlock","ano ang process","unsa ang process"],"answer":"A process is a running program with its own address space, and the operating system keeps processes isolated so one cannot read or corrupt another's memory. Threads are separate streams of execution inside a single process, which share that memory and are therefore cheap to create and dangerous to use. A deadlock is when threads each wait on something the other holds, and no amount of waiting resolves it.","variations":["Threads are separate streams of execution inside a single process, which share that memory and are therefore cheap to create and dangerous to use. A process is a running program with its own address space, and the operating system keeps processes isolated so one cannot read or corrupt another's memory. A deadlock is when threads each wait on something the other holds, and no amount of waiting resolves it.","A deadlock is when threads each wait on something the other holds, and no amount of waiting resolves it. A process is a running program with its own address space, and the operating system keeps processes isolated so one cannot read or corrupt another's memory. Threads are separate streams of execution inside a single process, which share that memory and are therefore cheap to create and dangerous to use."],"tl":"process","bis":"process"},{"id":"tec-operating-systems-000003","topic":"technology-operating-systems","intent":"definition","concept":"kernel","keywords":["kernel","process","interrupt","driver","privilege","core","operating","system","runs","full"],"patterns":["what is kernel","kernel","explain kernel","definition of kernel","kernel meaning","what is a kernel","what does the kernel do","kernel vs operating system","ano ang kernel","unsa ang kernel"],"answer":"The kernel is the core of an operating system and the only part that runs with full privilege, which means it can touch any memory and any device. It manages the things no application can be trusted to do: scheduling which process gets the processor, mapping virtual memory, handling the interrupt a keypress generates, and giving processes a controlled way to reach the hardware. User space is everything else, and the reason the split exists is fault containment and safety, since a program that crashes takes down its own process and not the machine. The path from a keypress to a program is the clearest illustration of the design: the keyboard raises an interrupt, the kernel decides which program should be told, and that program is switched to so the keystroke appears to go straight to the application. Linux and the Windows family are both kernels with a large set of user-space tools around them, and the switch between the two is a system call, which is the carefully controlled doorway between the two halves.","variations":["It manages the things no application can be trusted to do: scheduling which process gets the processor, mapping virtual memory, handling the interrupt a keypress generates, and giving processes a controlled way to reach the hardware. The kernel is the core of an operating system and the only part that runs with full privilege, which means it can touch any memory and any device. User space is everything else, and the reason the split exists is fault containment and safety, since a program that crashes takes down its own process and not the machine. The path from a keypress to a program is the clearest illustration of the design: the keyboard raises an interrupt, the kernel decides which program should be told, and that program is switched to so the keystroke appears to go straight to the application. Linux and the Windows family are both kernels with a large set of user-space tools around them, and the switch between the two is a system call, which is the carefully controlled doorway between the two halves.","User space is everything else, and the reason the split exists is fault containment and safety, since a program that crashes takes down its own process and not the machine. The kernel is the core of an operating system and the only part that runs with full privilege, which means it can touch any memory and any device. It manages the things no application can be trusted to do: scheduling which process gets the processor, mapping virtual memory, handling the interrupt a keypress generates, and giving processes a controlled way to reach the hardware. The path from a keypress to a program is the clearest illustration of the design: the keyboard raises an interrupt, the kernel decides which program should be told, and that program is switched to so the keystroke appears to go straight to the application. Linux and the Windows family are both kernels with a large set of user-space tools around them, and the switch between the two is a system call, which is the carefully controlled doorway between the two halves."],"tl":"kernel","bis":"kernel"},{"id":"tec-operating-systems-000004","topic":"technology-operating-systems","intent":"definition","concept":"file system","keywords":["file system","directory","block","mount","partition","permissions","journal","metadata","file","system","method","drive","uses","record"],"patterns":["what is file system","file system","explain file system","definition of file system","file system meaning","what is a file system","what is journaling","what is the difference between ntfs and ext4","ano ang file system","unsa ang file system"],"answer":"A file system is the method a drive uses to record where files are, what they are named, and which blocks belong to them, and it is the layer that turns a stream of bytes into something with names. Files are stored in a tree of directories, but the real structure underneath is a table or a map linking each name to the location of its data and to metadata such as size, owner and timestamps. The important properties are what happens when the system stops cleanly and what happens when it does not, and this is the whole difference between the two main families: one writes changes through immediately so a crash loses little, and the other writes them to a log and replays it on the next start, which is slower and safer. Permissions attach per user and group, which is where a file that one person cannot open but can delete comes from, since the ability to remove a file is governed by the directory rather than by the file. Storage is organised in blocks, and a filesystem map turns a logical file offset into the physical block that holds it.","variations":["Files are stored in a tree of directories, but the real structure underneath is a table or a map linking each name to the location of its data and to metadata such as size, owner and timestamps. A file system is the method a drive uses to record where files are, what they are named, and which blocks belong to them, and it is the layer that turns a stream of bytes into something with names. The important properties are what happens when the system stops cleanly and what happens when it does not, and this is the whole difference between the two main families: one writes changes through immediately so a crash loses little, and the other writes them to a log and replays it on the next start, which is slower and safer. Permissions attach per user and group, which is where a file that one person cannot open but can delete comes from, since the ability to remove a file is governed by the directory rather than by the file. Storage is organised in blocks, and a filesystem map turns a logical file offset into the physical block that holds it.","The important properties are what happens when the system stops cleanly and what happens when it does not, and this is the whole difference between the two main families: one writes changes through immediately so a crash loses little, and the other writes them to a log and replays it on the next start, which is slower and safer. A file system is the method a drive uses to record where files are, what they are named, and which blocks belong to them, and it is the layer that turns a stream of bytes into something with names. Files are stored in a tree of directories, but the real structure underneath is a table or a map linking each name to the location of its data and to metadata such as size, owner and timestamps. Permissions attach per user and group, which is where a file that one person cannot open but can delete comes from, since the ability to remove a file is governed by the directory rather than by the file. Storage is organised in blocks, and a filesystem map turns a logical file offset into the physical block that holds it."],"tl":"file system","bis":"file system"}],"lang":"english","group":"technology","sub":"operating-systems","title":"Operating systems","groupTitle":"Technology","__file":"technology/operating-systems.json"},
  {"topic":"technology-smartphones","entries":[{"id":"tec-smartphones-000001","topic":"technology-smartphones","intent":"definition","concept":"android","keywords":["android","manufacturer","permission","sandbox","update","framework","operating","system","runs","majority","world's"],"patterns":["what is android","android","explain android","definition of android","android meaning","is android linux","is android better than ios","ano ang android","unsa ang android"],"answer":"Android is the operating system that runs on the majority of the world's phones, and the detail that shapes everything about it is that it is licensed to manufacturers rather than sold by one company. The source code is published, each manufacturer adapts it for its own hardware and adds its own interface on top, and that is why two Android phones from different brands can behave quite differently while still running the same applications. Applications are written in a managed language, compile down to bytecode that a runtime executes, and are distributed as a package containing the code, the resources and a manifest declaring the permissions the app wants. The system is built on a Linux kernel and sandboxes each app, so one app normally cannot read the files of another, and recent versions ask again for sensitive access rather than deciding it once at install. The main complaints are update fragmentation, where support for a given model ends at whatever the manufacturer chose, and the number of pre-installed applications that cannot be fully removed.","variations":["The source code is published, each manufacturer adapts it for its own hardware and adds its own interface on top, and that is why two Android phones from different brands can behave quite differently while still running the same applications. Android is the operating system that runs on the majority of the world's phones, and the detail that shapes everything about it is that it is licensed to manufacturers rather than sold by one company. Applications are written in a managed language, compile down to bytecode that a runtime executes, and are distributed as a package containing the code, the resources and a manifest declaring the permissions the app wants. The system is built on a Linux kernel and sandboxes each app, so one app normally cannot read the files of another, and recent versions ask again for sensitive access rather than deciding it once at install. The main complaints are update fragmentation, where support for a given model ends at whatever the manufacturer chose, and the number of pre-installed applications that cannot be fully removed.","Applications are written in a managed language, compile down to bytecode that a runtime executes, and are distributed as a package containing the code, the resources and a manifest declaring the permissions the app wants. Android is the operating system that runs on the majority of the world's phones, and the detail that shapes everything about it is that it is licensed to manufacturers rather than sold by one company. The source code is published, each manufacturer adapts it for its own hardware and adds its own interface on top, and that is why two Android phones from different brands can behave quite differently while still running the same applications. The system is built on a Linux kernel and sandboxes each app, so one app normally cannot read the files of another, and recent versions ask again for sensitive access rather than deciding it once at install. The main complaints are update fragmentation, where support for a given model ends at whatever the manufacturer chose, and the number of pre-installed applications that cannot be fully removed."],"tl":"android","bis":"android"},{"id":"tec-smartphones-000002","topic":"technology-smartphones","intent":"definition","concept":"app store","keywords":["app store","marketplace","distribution","listing","update","commission","install","review","store","software","distributed","phones","model"],"patterns":["what is app store","app store","explain app store","definition of app store","app store meaning","what is an app store","how do apps get on phones","why does an app need so much storage","ano ang app store","unsa ang app store"],"answer":"An app store is the marketplace through which software is distributed to phones, and the store model is what turned the phone from a device with a fixed feature set into a general computer. The provider hosts the applications, handles discovery, payment, updates and in some regions review, so software reaches a phone without the user copying files around, and because updates are pushed centrally a fix can reach millions of devices in days. Each listing carries a minimum operating system version, which is how old devices end up unable to run anything new even though the hardware is not broken, and the accumulated size of applications is what fills the storage people then delete photos to free. The economics run on a commission taken from sales, which is why developers tend to offer things free with a paid upgrade rather than a plain price, and why the storefronts that allow alternative payment methods are such a contested area. For a user the practical facts are the same everywhere: an app asks for permissions, and the decision to grant them is the real contract.","variations":["The provider hosts the applications, handles discovery, payment, updates and in some regions review, so software reaches a phone without the user copying files around, and because updates are pushed centrally a fix can reach millions of devices in days. An app store is the marketplace through which software is distributed to phones, and the store model is what turned the phone from a device with a fixed feature set into a general computer. Each listing carries a minimum operating system version, which is how old devices end up unable to run anything new even though the hardware is not broken, and the accumulated size of applications is what fills the storage people then delete photos to free. The economics run on a commission taken from sales, which is why developers tend to offer things free with a paid upgrade rather than a plain price, and why the storefronts that allow alternative payment methods are such a contested area. For a user the practical facts are the same everywhere: an app asks for permissions, and the decision to grant them is the real contract.","Each listing carries a minimum operating system version, which is how old devices end up unable to run anything new even though the hardware is not broken, and the accumulated size of applications is what fills the storage people then delete photos to free. An app store is the marketplace through which software is distributed to phones, and the store model is what turned the phone from a device with a fixed feature set into a general computer. The provider hosts the applications, handles discovery, payment, updates and in some regions review, so software reaches a phone without the user copying files around, and because updates are pushed centrally a fix can reach millions of devices in days. The economics run on a commission taken from sales, which is why developers tend to offer things free with a paid upgrade rather than a plain price, and why the storefronts that allow alternative payment methods are such a contested area. For a user the practical facts are the same everywhere: an app asks for permissions, and the decision to grant them is the real contract."],"tl":"app store","bis":"app store"},{"id":"tec-smartphones-000003","topic":"technology-smartphones","intent":"definition","concept":"battery","keywords":["battery","charging","capacity","heat","power","baterya","phone","rechargeable","lithium","cell","life"],"patterns":["what is battery","battery","explain battery","definition of battery","battery meaning","how long does a phone battery last","what is battery health","why does my phone die fast","ano ang battery","unsa ang baterya"],"answer":"A phone battery is a rechargeable lithium ion cell, and its life is governed by capacity in milliamp hours together with how much power the device actually draws while you use it. The display is usually the largest single consumer, which is why brightness, refresh rate and screen size are the settings people are told to lower, and the other large draws are the radio, the camera and any app that keeps location active in the background. Charging follows a curve rather than being a simple fill: a lithium cell is stressed by sitting at a very high charge, so phones typically charge quickly to about 80 percent and then taper, and the overnight-to-full habit is what the charger manages. Heat shortens lifespan more than the number of cycles does, so leaving a phone in a hot car is worse than charging it often. Battery health in the system settings refers to the maximum capacity the cell can now hold compared with when it was new, and it is the number that predicts whether the day will still last.","variations":["The display is usually the largest single consumer, which is why brightness, refresh rate and screen size are the settings people are told to lower, and the other large draws are the radio, the camera and any app that keeps location active in the background. A phone battery is a rechargeable lithium ion cell, and its life is governed by capacity in milliamp hours together with how much power the device actually draws while you use it. Charging follows a curve rather than being a simple fill: a lithium cell is stressed by sitting at a very high charge, so phones typically charge quickly to about 80 percent and then taper, and the overnight-to-full habit is what the charger manages. Heat shortens lifespan more than the number of cycles does, so leaving a phone in a hot car is worse than charging it often. Battery health in the system settings refers to the maximum capacity the cell can now hold compared with when it was new, and it is the number that predicts whether the day will still last.","Charging follows a curve rather than being a simple fill: a lithium cell is stressed by sitting at a very high charge, so phones typically charge quickly to about 80 percent and then taper, and the overnight-to-full habit is what the charger manages. A phone battery is a rechargeable lithium ion cell, and its life is governed by capacity in milliamp hours together with how much power the device actually draws while you use it. The display is usually the largest single consumer, which is why brightness, refresh rate and screen size are the settings people are told to lower, and the other large draws are the radio, the camera and any app that keeps location active in the background. Heat shortens lifespan more than the number of cycles does, so leaving a phone in a hot car is worse than charging it often. Battery health in the system settings refers to the maximum capacity the cell can now hold compared with when it was new, and it is the number that predicts whether the day will still last."],"tl":"battery","bis":"baterya"},{"id":"tec-smartphones-000004","topic":"technology-smartphones","intent":"definition","concept":"touchscreen","keywords":["touchscreen","capacitive","digitiser","stylus","glass","resistance","coordinates","input","display","senses","finger"],"patterns":["what is touchscreen","touchscreen","explain touchscreen","definition of touchscreen","touchscreen meaning","how does a touchscreen work","is a touchscreen capacitive or resistive","why did my screen stop working","ano ang touchscreen","unsa ang touch screen"],"answer":"A touchscreen is a display that senses input from a finger or a stylus, and the technology underneath it is what makes a phone feel direct. The capacitive approach, used in essentially every modern phone, senses the change in capacitance of a grid of electrodes when a conductor touches it, and because the human body is conductive it works without pressure. That is the difference from the resistive screens before it, which needed a firm stylus and a two-layer film, and the durability improvement is a large part of why the older approach disappeared. A digitiser then converts the touch point into coordinates, and the operating system's software decides what that means, so the same panel can scroll one interface and tap another. The real limitations are practical rather than technical: water and gloves interfere with capacitive sensing, and because the screen is glass and the digitiser sits under it, a badly cracked display often cannot be replaced without the whole assembly.","variations":["The capacitive approach, used in essentially every modern phone, senses the change in capacitance of a grid of electrodes when a conductor touches it, and because the human body is conductive it works without pressure. A touchscreen is a display that senses input from a finger or a stylus, and the technology underneath it is what makes a phone feel direct. That is the difference from the resistive screens before it, which needed a firm stylus and a two-layer film, and the durability improvement is a large part of why the older approach disappeared. A digitiser then converts the touch point into coordinates, and the operating system's software decides what that means, so the same panel can scroll one interface and tap another. The real limitations are practical rather than technical: water and gloves interfere with capacitive sensing, and because the screen is glass and the digitiser sits under it, a badly cracked display often cannot be replaced without the whole assembly.","That is the difference from the resistive screens before it, which needed a firm stylus and a two-layer film, and the durability improvement is a large part of why the older approach disappeared. A touchscreen is a display that senses input from a finger or a stylus, and the technology underneath it is what makes a phone feel direct. The capacitive approach, used in essentially every modern phone, senses the change in capacitance of a grid of electrodes when a conductor touches it, and because the human body is conductive it works without pressure. A digitiser then converts the touch point into coordinates, and the operating system's software decides what that means, so the same panel can scroll one interface and tap another. The real limitations are practical rather than technical: water and gloves interfere with capacitive sensing, and because the screen is glass and the digitiser sits under it, a badly cracked display often cannot be replaced without the whole assembly."],"tl":"touchscreen","bis":"touch screen"}],"lang":"english","group":"technology","sub":"smartphones","title":"Smartphones","groupTitle":"Technology","__file":"technology/smartphones.json"},
  {"topic":"technology-software","entries":[{"id":"tec-software-000001","topic":"technology-software","intent":"definition","concept":"crypto","keywords":["crypto","bitcoin","blockchain","ethereum","wallet","decentralized","cryptocurrency","digital","record","keeping","system","central"],"patterns":["what is crypto","crypto","explain crypto","definition of crypto","crypto meaning","what is bitcoin","what is blockchain","what is cryptocurrency"],"answer":"A cryptocurrency is a digital record-keeping system with no central authority. Bitcoin introduced the blockchain: a public list of transactions, each linked cryptographically to the one before, which makes altering history require redoing all the work since. It solves a real problem around trust without a middleman, and the trade-off is that it is slow, publicly visible, and irreversible once confirmed.","variations":["Bitcoin introduced the blockchain: a public list of transactions, each linked cryptographically to the one before, which makes altering history require redoing all the work since. A cryptocurrency is a digital record-keeping system with no central authority. It solves a real problem around trust without a middleman, and the trade-off is that it is slow, publicly visible, and irreversible once confirmed.","It solves a real problem around trust without a middleman, and the trade-off is that it is slow, publicly visible, and irreversible once confirmed. A cryptocurrency is a digital record-keeping system with no central authority. Bitcoin introduced the blockchain: a public list of transactions, each linked cryptographically to the one before, which makes altering history require redoing all the work since."]},{"id":"tec-software-000002","topic":"technology-software","intent":"definition","concept":"offline","keywords":["offline","local","privacy","server","cloud","install","app","real","difference","software","runs","machine","calls"],"patterns":["what is offline","offline","explain offline","definition of offline","offline meaning","can this work offline","do you need internet","what is a local model"],"answer":"There is a real difference between software that runs on your machine and software that calls a company. The first keeps your data where it is and keeps working without a connection; the second is often better at its job and knows who you are. Local tools such as a browser app or Ollama never send anything anywhere, at the cost of needing capable hardware and no live data.","variations":["The first keeps your data where it is and keeps working without a connection; the second is often better at its job and knows who you are. There is a real difference between software that runs on your machine and software that calls a company. Local tools such as a browser app or Ollama never send anything anywhere, at the cost of needing capable hardware and no live data.","Local tools such as a browser app or Ollama never send anything anywhere, at the cost of needing capable hardware and no live data. There is a real difference between software that runs on your machine and software that calls a company. The first keeps your data where it is and keeps working without a connection; the second is often better at its job and knows who you are."]},{"id":"tec-software-000003","topic":"technology-software","intent":"definition","concept":"open source","keywords":["open source","licence","mit","apache","gpl","repository","open","source","means","code","available","read"],"patterns":["what is open source","open source","explain open source","definition of open source","open source meaning","what does open source mean","difference between mit and gpl","is public github open source","ano ang open source","unsa ang open source"],"answer":"Open source means the source code is available to read, modify and redistribute, and the licence is what makes it a right rather than a gift. MIT, Apache and GPL are the common licences, and the difference that matters is whether a derivative must also be open. GPL requires it and MIT does not, which is why the same codebase can be commercial under one and not the other. Public on GitHub is not the same as open source, because a public repository can carry a proprietary licence.","variations":["MIT, Apache and GPL are the common licences, and the difference that matters is whether a derivative must also be open. Open source means the source code is available to read, modify and redistribute, and the licence is what makes it a right rather than a gift. GPL requires it and MIT does not, which is why the same codebase can be commercial under one and not the other. Public on GitHub is not the same as open source, because a public repository can carry a proprietary licence.","GPL requires it and MIT does not, which is why the same codebase can be commercial under one and not the other. Open source means the source code is available to read, modify and redistribute, and the licence is what makes it a right rather than a gift. MIT, Apache and GPL are the common licences, and the difference that matters is whether a derivative must also be open. Public on GitHub is not the same as open source, because a public repository can carry a proprietary licence."],"tl":"open source","bis":"open source"},{"id":"tec-software-000004","topic":"technology-software","intent":"definition","concept":"blockchain","keywords":["blockchain","ledger","tamper","hash","consensus","distributed","list","records","linked","changing","earlier"],"patterns":["what is blockchain","blockchain","explain blockchain","definition of blockchain","blockchain meaning","what is a blockchain","what problem does blockchain solve","is blockchain secure","ano ang blockchain","unsa ang blockchain"],"answer":"A blockchain is a list of records linked so that changing an earlier one means recomputing every link after it, which is what makes tampering expensive rather than impossible. The guarantee is about integrity of the record, not about the truth of what was written, so a majority agreement on a false entry is still a majority agreement. The cost of that design is that every node keeps a copy and every write is visible, which is why it scales badly compared with simply having one database.","variations":["The guarantee is about integrity of the record, not about the truth of what was written, so a majority agreement on a false entry is still a majority agreement. A blockchain is a list of records linked so that changing an earlier one means recomputing every link after it, which is what makes tampering expensive rather than impossible. The cost of that design is that every node keeps a copy and every write is visible, which is why it scales badly compared with simply having one database.","The cost of that design is that every node keeps a copy and every write is visible, which is why it scales badly compared with simply having one database. A blockchain is a list of records linked so that changing an earlier one means recomputing every link after it, which is what makes tampering expensive rather than impossible. The guarantee is about integrity of the record, not about the truth of what was written, so a majority agreement on a false entry is still a majority agreement."],"tl":"blockchain","bis":"blockchain"}],"lang":"english","group":"technology","sub":"software","title":"Software","groupTitle":"Technology","__file":"technology/software.json"},
  {"topic":"words-uncommon","entries":[{"id":"wor-uncommon-000001","topic":"words-uncommon","intent":"definition","concept":"serendipity","keywords":["serendipity","chance","accident","fortunate","discovery","walpole","horace","lucky","auspicious","good","outcome"],"patterns":["what is serendipity","serendipity","explain serendipity","definition of serendipity","serendipity meaning","what does serendipity mean","how to use serendipity","ano ang auspicious","unsa ang serendipity"],"answer":"Serendipity is a fortunate accident, a good outcome that came about by chance rather than by planning, and the detail that makes the word interesting is who it was made for. It was coined in 1754 by Horace Walpole, who wrote it after a happy discovery of a Prince of Wales story he had not been looking for, and he credited it to a Persian phrase meaning the three princes of Serendip, a place name. The word is used in two slightly different ways and the difference is worth knowing: a serendipitous discovery is one where the thing found was better than the thing searched for, while a purely lucky outcome is not serendipity at all, since serendipity specifically involves a person being observant enough to recognise the lucky find. That is the part people miss when they use the word as a synonym for any luck at all.","variations":["It was coined in 1754 by Horace Walpole, who wrote it after a happy discovery of a Prince of Wales story he had not been looking for, and he credited it to a Persian phrase meaning the three princes of Serendip, a place name. Serendipity is a fortunate accident, a good outcome that came about by chance rather than by planning, and the detail that makes the word interesting is who it was made for. The word is used in two slightly different ways and the difference is worth knowing: a serendipitous discovery is one where the thing found was better than the thing searched for, while a purely lucky outcome is not serendipity at all, since serendipity specifically involves a person being observant enough to recognise the lucky find. That is the part people miss when they use the word as a synonym for any luck at all.","The word is used in two slightly different ways and the difference is worth knowing: a serendipitous discovery is one where the thing found was better than the thing searched for, while a purely lucky outcome is not serendipity at all, since serendipity specifically involves a person being observant enough to recognise the lucky find. Serendipity is a fortunate accident, a good outcome that came about by chance rather than by planning, and the detail that makes the word interesting is who it was made for. It was coined in 1754 by Horace Walpole, who wrote it after a happy discovery of a Prince of Wales story he had not been looking for, and he credited it to a Persian phrase meaning the three princes of Serendip, a place name. That is the part people miss when they use the word as a synonym for any luck at all."],"tl":"auspicious","bis":"serendipity"},{"id":"wor-uncommon-000002","topic":"words-uncommon","intent":"definition","concept":"ephemeral","keywords":["ephemeral","brief","short","temporary","fleeting","transient","moment","opposite","pansamantala","means","lasting","time"],"patterns":["what is ephemeral","ephemeral","explain ephemeral","definition of ephemeral","ephemeral meaning","what does ephemeral mean","opposite of ephemeral","ano ang pansamantala","unsa ang ephemeral"],"answer":"Ephemeral means lasting a very short time, and its opposite is a word most people know well: permanent. It describes a thing that exists briefly by its nature rather than a thing that merely happens to be brief, so an ephemeral flower, a fading fashion and a very short speech are all properly described, while a long film is not. The word comes from a root meaning for a day or a short span, which is the whole of its history and the whole of its sense. It is worth knowing because it does not simply mean temporary, since a temporary thing might last a year and an ephemeral one lasts a morning, and a person looking for a strong word for brief is looking for this one. It also has an antonym that is easy to confuse with it, since permanent is about duration and eternal is about having no end at all.","variations":["It describes a thing that exists briefly by its nature rather than a thing that merely happens to be brief, so an ephemeral flower, a fading fashion and a very short speech are all properly described, while a long film is not. Ephemeral means lasting a very short time, and its opposite is a word most people know well: permanent. The word comes from a root meaning for a day or a short span, which is the whole of its history and the whole of its sense. It is worth knowing because it does not simply mean temporary, since a temporary thing might last a year and an ephemeral one lasts a morning, and a person looking for a strong word for brief is looking for this one. It also has an antonym that is easy to confuse with it, since permanent is about duration and eternal is about having no end at all.","The word comes from a root meaning for a day or a short span, which is the whole of its history and the whole of its sense. Ephemeral means lasting a very short time, and its opposite is a word most people know well: permanent. It describes a thing that exists briefly by its nature rather than a thing that merely happens to be brief, so an ephemeral flower, a fading fashion and a very short speech are all properly described, while a long film is not. It is worth knowing because it does not simply mean temporary, since a temporary thing might last a year and an ephemeral one lasts a morning, and a person looking for a strong word for brief is looking for this one. It also has an antonym that is easy to confuse with it, since permanent is about duration and eternal is about having no end at all."],"tl":"pansamantala","bis":"ephemeral"},{"id":"wor-uncommon-000003","topic":"words-uncommon","intent":"definition","concept":"ubiquitous","keywords":["ubiquitous","everywhere","common","omnipresent","present","all","abundant","network","kahawig","means","noticing"],"patterns":["what is ubiquitous","ubiquitous","explain ubiquitous","definition of ubiquitous","ubiquitous meaning","what does ubiquitous mean","is ubiquitous a rare word","ano ang kahawig","unsa ang ubiquitous"],"answer":"Ubiquitous means present everywhere, or so common that noticing it is odd, and the word is used far more than the situation usually deserves. It began as a technical term, meaning present in a fluid at the same concentration everywhere, and it moved into ordinary speech as a strong word for something that is simply very widespread. That is the risk: a thing being common is not the same as a thing being everywhere, so using the word claims more than is usually meant. It is also an excellent word for technology, since the number of things that quietly depend on a network connection or a data centre has grown to the point where the honest answer to many how-does-this-work questions is that it does not work alone. A related family exists for the opposite, from rare, and the same exaggeration applies in the other direction.","variations":["It began as a technical term, meaning present in a fluid at the same concentration everywhere, and it moved into ordinary speech as a strong word for something that is simply very widespread. Ubiquitous means present everywhere, or so common that noticing it is odd, and the word is used far more than the situation usually deserves. That is the risk: a thing being common is not the same as a thing being everywhere, so using the word claims more than is usually meant. It is also an excellent word for technology, since the number of things that quietly depend on a network connection or a data centre has grown to the point where the honest answer to many how-does-this-work questions is that it does not work alone. A related family exists for the opposite, from rare, and the same exaggeration applies in the other direction.","That is the risk: a thing being common is not the same as a thing being everywhere, so using the word claims more than is usually meant. Ubiquitous means present everywhere, or so common that noticing it is odd, and the word is used far more than the situation usually deserves. It began as a technical term, meaning present in a fluid at the same concentration everywhere, and it moved into ordinary speech as a strong word for something that is simply very widespread. It is also an excellent word for technology, since the number of things that quietly depend on a network connection or a data centre has grown to the point where the honest answer to many how-does-this-work questions is that it does not work alone. A related family exists for the opposite, from rare, and the same exaggeration applies in the other direction."],"tl":"kahawig","bis":"ubiquitous"}],"lang":"english","group":"words","sub":"uncommon","title":"Uncommon words","groupTitle":"Words","__file":"words/uncommon.json"}
